window.B79_BANK2M = {
"b1":"Commercial messages now appear on phones, buses, clothing and even school equipment, so that few waking hours pass without one. Advertising has legitimate uses, but I believe its spread into almost every part of daily life is a negative development.\n\nThe strongest objection is that constant advertising takes attention people have not agreed to give. A poster in a shop window can be ignored, whereas a video that interrupts a film or a notification that appears during a conversation cannot. Because each company has to shout louder than its rivals, the messages become more frequent and more personal, drawing on records of what a person has searched for or bought. The cumulative effect is to encourage a feeling that what one owns is not quite good enough. This pressure falls most heavily on teenagers, who are still forming their sense of what they need, and on households whose budgets leave no room for impulse purchases.\n\nDefenders of advertising point out that it pays for a great deal that people value. Search engines, news sites and television channels, for example, are free to use only because advertisers cover the cost, and a new business has few other ways of telling the public it exists. These are genuine benefits. They do not, however, depend on advertising being everywhere. A newspaper can be funded by a limited number of clearly marked advertisements, and a small firm needs to reach likely customers rather than follow them from one screen to the next. The useful part of advertising, in other words, could survive with far less of it.\n\nAdvertising informs consumers and funds services, yet these gains were available when it occupied a smaller place in people's lives. Its steady expansion, in my view, costs the public more in attention and contentment than it returns.",
"b2":"The weeks after a birth are among the most demanding in any family's life, yet in many workplaces only the mother is expected to be at home for them. I agree that fathers should have a legal right to paid leave at this time, although its length has to be realistic for employers.\n\nThe case for it begins with practical need. A woman recovering from childbirth, sometimes from surgery, needs rest, while a newborn needs attention day and night. A father who is at home can share the feeding, washing and broken sleep, and can care for older children whose routine has been disturbed. His presence in these weeks also shapes what follows. Men who learn to look after a baby from the start tend to remain more involved later, and the couple is less likely to assume that childcare belongs to the mother alone. This in turn helps women to return to work, since the responsibility at home is shared.\n\nThe usual objection is the burden on business. A large firm can cover a two-week absence without much difficulty, but a workshop with four employees may struggle when a skilled member of staff is away. This concern deserves an answer rather than dismissal. In countries such as Sweden, parental leave is paid for through the public insurance system rather than by the individual employer, which spreads the cost. A reasonable notice period also allows managers to plan ahead. Provided that such arrangements are in place, the disruption is modest and temporary compared with the benefit to the family.\n\nFor these reasons I agree that paternity leave should be a right for every working father and not a favour granted by generous companies. A short, publicly funded period would protect families without placing an unfair load on small firms.",
"b3":"Big employers shape the towns in which they operate: they bring jobs, but also traffic, noise and pressure on housing. Some argue that they should give something back by building gyms, pitches or meeting halls for local residents. I think this is admirable where it happens, but I disagree that it should be expected of every large company.\n\nThere is a sound argument for such provision. A firm with a large site often has land and money that a local council lacks, and opening a sports hall or a community room to neighbours costs little compared with its annual profits. In return, it gains goodwill and a healthier pool of future employees. Some well-known manufacturers did exactly this in the past. Cadbury, for example, built playing fields and swimming baths for the village around its factory near Birmingham. Where a company chooses this path, both sides benefit.\n\nMaking it a general duty is a different matter. Large companies differ greatly: a bank with scattered city offices has no natural community to serve, and a firm with thin margins may be large in staff but not in spare funds. One rule would overburden some and barely touch others. Public facilities also need to be planned according to what residents need rather than where a business is located. Otherwise, towns with a generous employer would have fine facilities while others had none. Taxation is a fairer mechanism, since companies pay according to their profits and elected councils decide how the money is spent. A requirement might make sense only where one employer dominates an isolated town.\n\nCompany-funded facilities are therefore welcome but should remain voluntary. In my view, large firms meet their obligation to the community by paying their taxes in full, and local government is better placed to turn that money into pitches and halls.",
"b4":"Children grow up among many voices: parents and siblings first, then teachers, classmates and the media. The statement holds that these later, external voices matter more to a child's development than the family does. I disagree, because in my view the home shapes the way all other influences are received.\n\nThere is no denying that the world outside has considerable power. From about the age of five, children spend most of their weekdays at school, where teachers set standards and classmates set fashions. In adolescence the opinion of friends can seem to count for more than that of any adult. A boy whose friends all play football, for instance, is likely to take it up whatever his parents think of the sport. Although influence of this kind is highly visible, much of it concerns tastes and pastimes, which change repeatedly as a child grows.\n\nThe family's influence lies deeper and arrives first. Long before a child enters a classroom, he or she has learned from parents how to speak, how to treat other people and what to expect from them. Attitudes to effort, honesty and learning are absorbed in the same way, through years of daily example. These early lessons act as a filter. While two pupils may sit in the same class with the same teacher, the one whose parents read with her and ask about her day usually gains more from it. Parents also choose, to a large degree, which school and neighbourhood a child encounters. Only where parents are absent or uninterested does this filter fail, and outsiders then fill the gap.\n\nFriends, schools and the media all leave their mark, particularly on the surface of a young person's life. The values and habits underneath are formed at home, however, which is why I believe the family remains the stronger force.",
"b5":"Ambition, the desire to achieve more than one's present position, is widely admired in modern societies. I believe it is close to essential for anyone hoping to succeed, and that it is a positive trait as long as it is balanced by regard for other people.\n\nIts importance for success lies in the effort it sustains. Most worthwhile achievements, whether qualifying as a surgeon or building a business, require years of work with little immediate reward, and talent alone seldom carries a person through them. An ambitious person sets a distant goal and accepts present discomfort for its sake. A junior employee who wants to lead a department, for instance, will volunteer for difficult projects and study in the evenings, while an equally able colleague without that aim stays where he is. Although ambition is not sufficient, since opportunity and luck also matter, ability tends to go unused without it.\n\nAs a characteristic, ambition is mostly beneficial, to the individual and to those around them. It gives life a sense of direction, and the companies, discoveries and works of art that ambitious people produce are enjoyed by others. Problems arise when the goal becomes more important than the means of reaching it. Someone determined to rise at any price may take credit for colleagues' work, neglect their family or damage their own health through overwork. Ambition of this kind also brings little satisfaction, because each success is soon replaced by a larger target. The quality itself is not at fault in such cases. What is missing is a sense of proportion.\n\nFor people who wish to succeed, then, ambition is one of the most valuable qualities they can have. I think it deserves its good reputation, provided that it is pursued honestly and leaves room for the other things that make a life worth living.",
"b6":"Many species are disappearing, and since conservation budgets are limited, it is sometimes argued that only animals of practical value to people deserve protection. I disagree, because usefulness is far harder to judge than this view assumes, and because it is not the only reason for protecting wildlife.\n\nThe main weakness of the argument is that we rarely know which animals are useful. Species depend on one another, so the loss of a creature with no apparent value can damage others that people rely on. Bees are an obvious case of usefulness, but the insects, birds and small mammals that share their habitat help to keep that habitat healthy, and their contribution is mostly invisible. Wolves, for instance, were long treated as pests, yet where they have returned they have kept deer numbers down and allowed young forests to recover. A policy that saved only the species we currently value would therefore risk removing the supports on which those species stand.\n\nThere is also a moral objection. To protect an animal only when it serves us is to treat the natural world as a store of goods, although most people sense that a tiger or a whale matters in itself. Few would let a species vanish because it has no use. Responsibility matters too. Much of the threat comes from human activity such as farming and logging, so those who cause the harm should help to limit it. That duty remains when the animal has no market value. Funds are finite, though, so it is sensible to favour animals whose survival supports many others rather than spreading money thinly.\n\nSetting priorities, however, is very different from abandoning whatever fails to benefit us directly. For these reasons I disagree with the proposal and think that protection should extend well beyond the animals we happen to find useful.",
"b7":"Painting and drawing are often the first lessons to be cut when a school timetable is under pressure. I agree that they deserve a protected place in secondary education, but I do not think they should be compulsory for the whole of high school.\n\nArt contributes to a child's development in ways that other subjects seldom do. A pupil drawing a still life has to look closely, make decisions for which there is no single correct answer, and accept that the first attempt will probably fail. These habits of observation and persistence are useful well beyond the art room. Art lessons also give some children their only experience of success at school. A boy who struggles with algebra, for instance, may discover that he can draw, and the confidence this brings often carries over into his other work. For these reasons, the subject deserves the same respect as science or history in the early years of secondary school, when pupils are still finding out what they are good at.\n\nMaking it compulsory until graduation is harder to justify. Older students are preparing for examinations that decide their university places, and their hours are limited. Requiring a seventeen-year-old who intends to study medicine to spend several periods a week painting would take time from the subjects she needs, and she might come to resent it. Art taught to unwilling teenagers tends to be poor art, however good the teacher. Although the same objection could be made about mathematics, numeracy is a practical necessity in adult life in a way that drawing is not.\n\nI believe, therefore, that art should be required for younger secondary pupils and offered, rather than imposed, in the final years. This arrangement recognises its value without forcing it on students who have already chosen another direction.",
"b8":"Few needs are as basic as a roof over one's head, and it is reasonable to expect the state to step in when the market leaves people without one. I agree that governments are responsible for housing those who cannot pay, but I do not think that giving homes away free of charge is the right way to meet that duty.\n\nThe case for public responsibility is strong. Without a stable address, it is difficult to hold a job, keep children in school or recover from illness, so homelessness tends to turn a temporary setback into a permanent one. The cost falls on society as well, through emergency shelters, hospital admissions and lost work. For people with no realistic means of supporting themselves, such as the severely disabled or the elderly poor, a home provided at no charge is both humane and sensible. This group is small, though, and policy for other households should be designed differently.\n\nFor most low-income families, housing that is subsidised rather than free works better. If a flat costs nothing as long as a tenant's income stays below a certain level, then accepting a pay rise or a full-time job may mean losing it. Such a rule discourages exactly the effort the state wants to encourage. Free provision is also expensive, so fewer homes can be built and waiting lists grow. Singapore's public housing, for example, is sold or rented at prices linked to what families can afford, and it covers most of the population. Charging a modest rent based on income allows a limited budget to reach far more people.\n\nIn my view, governments should guarantee that no citizen is left without shelter, while reserving free housing for those who cannot work. Other households are better served by affordable rents that rise gradually with their earnings.",
"b9":"Rising rents and crowded roads have led some governments to offer tax breaks and grants to companies willing to leave the city for the countryside. Such a policy has clear drawbacks for the firms involved, but I believe its benefits are greater, as long as it is applied to the right kinds of business.\n\nThe principal advantage is that jobs move to where people already live, instead of people moving to where the jobs are. When a food-processing plant or a call centre opens in a small town, local school-leavers can find work without joining the queue for a flat in the capital. The city gains too, since every employer that leaves takes commuters off its trains and tenants out of its housing market. Rural areas, meanwhile, receive wages that are spent in local shops and taxes that pay for better roads and schools. This relief is gradual rather than dramatic, yet it tackles the cause of congestion, which is the concentration of work in one place.\n\nThe disadvantages fall mainly on the companies themselves. A firm in a remote district is further from its customers, suppliers and ports, so transport costs rise and deliveries take longer. For a furniture maker, this can cancel the saving from cheaper land. Recruitment is the other main difficulty. Specialists such as engineers or accountants are often reluctant to leave city life behind, and a firm that cannot fill these posts may have to pay higher salaries. Although these problems are serious for some industries, they can be reduced if governments invest in fast internet and good roads first.\n\nOn balance, I think the advantages outweigh the disadvantages because the policy spreads employment more evenly and eases pressure on cities at the same time. It will succeed, however, only where rural infrastructure is ready for the businesses being invited.",
"b10":"Problems such as climate change, pandemics and financial crises pay no attention to borders. I strongly agree that this makes cooperation between states more valuable now than at any earlier time, although good relations alone are not enough to solve such problems.\n\nNo government can deal with a global threat by acting alone. A country may cut its own emissions sharply, but if its neighbours go on burning coal, the climate it lives in will continue to warm. The same logic applies to disease. A virus that appears in one city can reach another continent within days, so early warning, shared data and a fair supply of vaccines depend on governments that trust one another enough to be open. Where relations are hostile, information is withheld and each state protects itself first, which usually leaves all of them worse off.\n\nGood relations also make it possible to share the burden fairly. Poorer nations often suffer most from problems they did little to cause, and they cannot afford flood defences or clean energy without outside help. Agreements such as the Paris climate accord show that when countries are on reasonable terms they can settle who pays and who acts first. Friendly relations are only a starting point, however. Promises made at international meetings are easily broken once they become costly at home, and a handshake between leaders achieves little unless it is followed by binding targets and regular checks. Diplomacy matters, in other words, because of the practical commitments it makes possible rather than for its own sake.\n\nThe scale of today's problems has outgrown the nation state, which is why, in my view, sound relationships between countries have become more important than ever. They are the condition on which shared solutions rest.",
"b11":"A company exists to earn a return for its owners, and one that fails to do so will not survive long enough to help anybody. That does not mean profit is its only obligation. I agree that businesses also owe something to the society they operate in, though I think this duty is narrower than is sometimes suggested.\n\nThe basic reason is that firms do not make their money alone. They rely on roads built with public funds, on employees educated in state schools and on courts that enforce their contracts. Having drawn on these shared resources, a business can reasonably be expected not to damage them. A factory that saves money by releasing waste into a river, for instance, is passing its costs to the farmers and families downstream. Paying fair wages, keeping workplaces safe and selling products that do what they claim are responsibilities of the same kind. They concern how profit is made rather than what is done with it afterwards.\n\nIt is less clear that companies are obliged to go further and take on social problems unrelated to their work. Although donations to charity or sponsorship of local events are welcome, they are paid for with shareholders' money, and managers are not elected to decide which causes deserve support. Choices of that kind belong to the owners themselves, or to governments that answer to voters. Even so, the distinction should not be drawn too sharply. A firm whose skills fit a public need, such as a pharmaceutical company during an epidemic, has a stronger duty to act than others.\n\nMaking money and behaving responsibly are therefore compatible aims. In my view, a business has met its social obligations when it earns its profit without harming employees, customers or the environment, and anything beyond that is commendable but optional.",
"b12":"The idea that some pupils simply cannot learn a second language is widely held. I disagree that this possibility justifies making languages optional, because the premise is weak and the cost of acting on it would fall on children too young to judge.\n\nThe claim about natural ability does not survive much scrutiny. A child who can hold a conversation has already mastered one language without formal teaching, which suggests that the basic capacity is close to universal. In places such as Switzerland or Malaysia, ordinary children grow up using two or three languages because their surroundings require it, not because they are unusually gifted. Where pupils fail, the cause is more often a few short lessons a week, built around grammar tables, than any lack of aptitude. Children do differ in how quickly they progress, as they do in mathematics, but slow progress is a reason to teach differently rather than to stop.\n\nMaking the subject voluntary would also close doors before pupils know they exist. A twelve-year-old who is allowed to drop French because it is difficult cannot foresee that she may later want to work abroad or study in another country. Languages are far harder to begin in adulthood, when time is short and embarrassment is greater. Schools already insist on subjects that many children find hard, and they do so because the benefit arrives years afterwards. This does not mean that all pupils must sit a demanding examination. A school can require the lessons while adjusting its expectations, so that a struggling learner aims at simple conversation instead of advanced writing.\n\nDoubts about aptitude are, in my view, a poor reason to exempt children from language lessons. The sensible response to uneven progress is better and more flexible teaching, which keeps the opportunity open for all pupils.",
"b13":"Almost any painting, tool or manuscript can now be viewed on a phone, together with more commentary than a gallery label could hold. Some conclude that museums have had their day. I disagree, because a museum does several things that a website cannot, and the internet itself relies on them.\n\nThe first of these is the experience of the real object. A photograph of an Egyptian coffin or a medieval sword gives the facts, but it does not convey size, weight or texture. Nor does it convey the knowledge that this thing was made and used by people long dead. Visitors standing in front of a dinosaur skeleton, for instance, understand its scale in a way that no screen can communicate. That encounter tends to stay in the memory, which is why school visits to museums remain so valuable. For people who live far from any major collection, a virtual tour is far better than nothing, though it remains a substitute for the visit.\n\nMuseums are also where knowledge about the past is produced and protected. Objects have to be stored at the right temperature, repaired by trained conservators and studied by specialists, and much of the reliable information online about history comes from this work. If museums closed, collections would be dispersed or would decay, and the internet would be left repeating what was already known. Digital access is best seen, then, as an extension of the museum. Many institutions now publish their collections online, which widens their audience without replacing the building, although smaller museums may lack the funds to do so.\n\nThe internet has made information easier to find, but it has not made the places that preserve and interpret the evidence redundant. I believe museums remain necessary, both for the experience they offer and for the knowledge they safeguard.",
"b14":"A proposal to excuse apparently weak learners from language lessons sounds kind, since it would spare them repeated failure. I disagree with it, because it relies on a judgement that schools cannot make reliably and it would harm the very pupils it is meant to protect.\n\nThe difficulty lies in the word 'seem'. A teacher deciding which eleven-year-olds lack linguistic ability has little to go on beyond a term or two of test results, and those results reflect many things other than aptitude. A shy child who dislikes speaking in front of classmates, for instance, may look like a poor learner while understanding nearly as much as her neighbours. So may a pupil whose family cannot afford extra tuition. If such children are removed from the class, the early impression becomes permanent, and they are told that languages are for other people. Labels of this kind tend to be believed, both by the pupils and by their later teachers.\n\nExemption would also deepen the gap it responds to. The pupils who continue would gain a skill valued by universities and employers, while those excused, who are often already behind in other subjects, would fall further back. A better response is to change what is asked of them. Smaller groups, more spoken practice and targets set at a modest level allow slower learners to achieve something real, even if it is only the ability to hold a simple conversation. There are rare cases, admittedly, in which a diagnosed learning difficulty makes a second language an unreasonable burden. Those decisions should follow a professional assessment rather than a classroom hunch.\n\nIn my view, therefore, schools should not release pupils from language study merely because they appear to struggle. Apparent lack of talent is too uncertain a basis for a decision that narrows a child's future.",
"b15":"Pupils who lose focus within minutes of a lesson beginning are a familiar sight to teachers in many countries. The causes lie partly in children's lives outside school and partly in the way lessons are organised, and both need to change if attention is to improve.\n\nMuch of the problem begins before the school day starts. Children who own a smartphone often stay awake late watching short videos or playing games, and a tired mind cannot follow an explanation of fractions. These forms of entertainment also train the brain to expect something new every few seconds, so that an ordinary lesson feels slow by comparison. The classroom can make matters worse. Pupils are frequently required to sit still and listen for most of the day, with few chances to move or talk. Although some children cope well with this routine, younger ones are not built for it, and their restlessness is often mistaken for bad behaviour.\n\nSolutions must therefore involve both families and schools. Parents can set a fixed bedtime and keep phones out of the bedroom, which costs nothing and improves alertness the next morning. A child who has slept well follows a lesson far more easily than one who watched videos until midnight. Schools, for their part, can break lessons into shorter stages, so that listening is followed by discussion or a practical task. A science class in which pupils test an idea themselves, for example, holds attention far better than one in which they copy notes from the board. Such measures will not be enough, however, for pupils with a diagnosed attention disorder, who need specialist support.\n\nPoor concentration is less a fault in children than a product of too little sleep, too much screen time and lessons that ask them to be passive. It improves when parents and teachers address those conditions together.",
"b16":"Selling more is the constant concern of every business, from a street stall to an international brand. Companies rely on three broad approaches: advertising, pricing and reputation. Of these, I believe the last, which means earning the recommendation of existing customers, is the most effective.\n\nThe most visible techniques aim to attract attention. Firms advertise on television and, increasingly, on social media, where they can pay well-known personalities to be seen using a product. Online advertising can be aimed at people of a particular age or interest, so less money is spent on those unlikely to buy. The other common tool is price. Supermarkets offer two items for the cost of one, online shops hold seasonal sales, and airlines reward frequent travellers with points. Although such methods work quickly, which explains their popularity, their effect is short-lived: sales tend to fall again when the campaign ends, and customers who came for a discount often leave for a cheaper rival.\n\nA more lasting method is to make buyers themselves the source of new sales. Most people trust the opinion of a friend, or of other purchasers, far more than a company's own claims, and many now read reviews before choosing a restaurant, a hotel or a phone. Businesses therefore invest in the things that produce good reviews, such as reliable products, generous return policies and helpful staff, and then encourage satisfied customers to share their experience. This approach is slower and cannot rescue a poor product. For a company whose goods are sound, however, it brings customers who cost nothing to attract and who are likely to return.\n\nAdvertising and discounts will remain necessary, particularly for launching something new. In my view, though, a strong reputation among existing customers does more than either to raise sales over time, because it persuades in a way that paid promotion cannot.",
"b17":"Places at well-regarded universities are now far harder to win than they were a generation ago. This is mainly because many more young people are applying for a number of places that has grown slowly, and I believe the result is, on the whole, a negative development.\n\nThe main reason for the change is that a degree has become a basic requirement for jobs that once needed none. As more school-leavers obtain one, employers use it as a first filter, and families conclude that their children cannot afford to be without it. Supply has not kept up. The best-known institutions have expanded slowly, since their reputation depends partly on being selective, while a new university takes decades to earn the same standing. Demand has therefore risen much faster than the number of places at the top.\n\nSome effects of this pressure are welcome. Applicants work harder, and universities can fill their courses with able and motivated students. Yet the costs seem to me greater. Teenagers in highly competitive systems often spend their evenings and weekends in private tuition, with little time left for sport, friendship or rest, and anxiety among them is common. Years of this can leave a young person exhausted before university begins. The contest is also unequal. A candidate whose parents can pay for tutors and exam coaching, for instance, has a clear advantage over an equally bright one who cannot, so selection ends up rewarding wealth as well as ability. Competition would be healthier if it were spread across many good institutions rather than concentrated on a few famous names.\n\nFiercer competition for places is thus the predictable outcome of rising demand meeting limited supply. While it raises effort, in my view it does more harm than good, because it damages young people's wellbeing and favours those who are already privileged.",
"b18":"In schools, workplaces and even leisure, people are increasingly encouraged to measure themselves against others. In my view, this competitive spirit improves performance and character up to a point, but it becomes harmful once every part of life is treated as a contest.\n\nThe clearest effect of competitiveness on individuals is that it raises the standard they set for themselves. A runner training alone rarely pushes as hard as one who has a rival beside her, and the same is true of students and sales staff. Competing also teaches people to prepare carefully, to perform under pressure and to recover from defeat, which are habits that serve them long after the particular contest is forgotten. A student who fails one examination and passes the next is better prepared for later setbacks. These gains depend, however, on the person having a realistic chance of doing well. Someone who loses repeatedly tends to decide that effort is pointless and may stop trying altogether.\n\nWhether the trend is welcome therefore depends on how far it extends. Within limits, rivalry between companies produces better products, and rivalry between colleagues can keep a team alert. The damage begins when competition replaces cooperation in settings that rely on trust. In an office where staff are ranked against one another, for example, employees may hide useful information from their peers, and the organisation as a whole performs worse. The personal cost can be high as well. People who judge their worth only by comparison are seldom content, since there is someone richer or more successful in almost any room they enter.\n\nCompetitiveness sharpens effort and builds resilience, and for that reason I regard it as a largely positive quality. It remains so, I believe, only while people can also work together and can value what they do without needing to beat someone else.",
"b19":"Shoppers today meet rival advertisements at every stage of a purchase, from the first search to the supermarket shelf. I believe these messages influence consumers considerably, chiefly by deciding which brand they choose rather than whether they buy at all. Sensible regulation can limit the harm that results.\n\nAdvertising is most powerful when products are hard to tell apart. Few people can judge the difference between two brands of washing powder or bottled water, so they reach for the name they recognise, and recognition is what repeated advertising buys. The effect goes largely unnoticed. Although most shoppers would say they chose freely, the familiar brand often costs more than an identical supermarket alternative. Influence is weaker for expensive purchases such as cars or laptops, where buyers tend to compare specifications and read reviews first. It is strongest among those least able to judge a claim, particularly children and people in financial difficulty who are offered easy credit.\n\nProtection should therefore begin with honesty. Regulators need the power to remove misleading claims quickly and to fine the companies behind them, since a correction published months later repairs little. The same principle applies online, where paid promotion by well-known personalities ought to be labelled clearly, because followers may otherwise mistake it for personal advice. Rules alone are not enough, however. Schools can teach pupils how persuasion works, for example by asking them to analyse an advertisement and identify what it leaves out. Children who have practised this are more likely to question a claim before they spend their money. A consumer who understands the technique is harder to mislead.\n\nAdvertising rarely forces a purchase, but it steers a great many of them. Consumers are best protected, in my view, by strict rules on truthfulness combined with an education that makes them sceptical.",
"b20":"Recent interruptions to world trade have revived the idea that a nation ought to feed itself from its own fields. The instinct is understandable, but I disagree that imports should be kept to a minimum. In my view, a country should protect its ability to grow basic foods while continuing to buy freely from abroad.\n\nSome degree of self-reliance is prudent. A state that buys nearly all its grain from a few exporters is exposed when one of them suffers a drought, goes to war or decides to keep its harvest at home. When wheat shipments from Ukraine were disrupted, for instance, bread became dearer in several North African countries that depended on them. Maintaining farmland, skills and reserves for staple crops is therefore a sensible form of insurance. It is insurance, though, rather than a reason to grow everything, and it applies to essentials such as rice or wheat far more than to coffee or pineapples.\n\nPursuing self-sufficiency beyond that point would make food more expensive and less secure. Climate, soil and water differ so widely that many crops can be produced abroad with a fraction of the resources they would require at home. Tomatoes raised in heated greenhouses in northern Europe can consume more energy than those shipped from Spain. A purely domestic food supply is also fragile, because a single failed harvest would leave nothing to fall back on, whereas a trading nation can turn to other suppliers. Although imports do carry risks, spreading purchases across many countries reduces them more reliably than closing the border.\n\nFeeding a population is too important to leave entirely to foreign suppliers, yet it is equally unwise to refuse what other regions grow better. That is why I believe the aim should be a secure supply of staples, not the smallest possible volume of imports.",
"b21":"New tools such as security cameras, DNA analysis and digital tracking have given the police abilities that were unimaginable a generation ago. I agree that these advances are reducing certain kinds of crime, but I disagree that overall crime rates are likely to fall as a result.\n\nTechnology has clearly made some offences harder to commit and easier to solve. Modern cars cannot be started without an electronic key, which has made them far more difficult to steal, and homes fitted with alarms and cameras are less inviting to burglars. When a crime does occur, investigators can match DNA from the scene, trace a suspect's phone or follow a vehicle through a network of cameras. A greater chance of being caught discourages those who weigh the risks before acting. For traditional crimes against property, therefore, a continued decline seems probable.\n\nThe same progress, however, has opened opportunities that did not exist before. Fraud, identity theft and blackmail can now be carried out from another continent, against thousands of victims at once, by people the local police have little hope of reaching. A thief who once broke into houses may find it safer to send deceptive messages, such as fake requests from a bank for account details. Crime of this kind is changing form rather than disappearing, and much of it goes unreported. Technology also leaves the causes of offending untouched. Poverty, addiction and lack of opportunity lead people into crime whatever tools the police possess, and while cameras may move drug dealing from one street to another, they do not end it.\n\nTechnological progress will go on making particular crimes rarer, which is a real benefit. Total crime, in my view, will fall only if online offences are policed as seriously as physical ones and the social causes are addressed.",
"b22":"The world needs considerably more food each decade than it did in the one before. This pressure comes from a larger and richer global population, and meeting it will require action both by individual governments and by countries working together.\n\nThe most direct cause is population growth. Families remain large in parts of Africa and South Asia, and better healthcare means that far more children now survive into adulthood, so there are more people to feed each year. A less visible cause is rising income. As families in countries such as China and Vietnam move out of poverty, they eat more meat and dairy products. Producing these requires far more grain and land than a diet based on rice or vegetables does. Demand therefore grows faster than the number of people.\n\nNational governments are best placed to raise production at home. They can fund irrigation and rural roads, give small farmers access to better seeds and credit, and build storage facilities so that harvests do not rot before reaching the market. The international community has a different role, since some problems cross borders. Wealthy states and bodies such as the United Nations can share agricultural research, finance projects in poorer regions and keep trade in basic foods open during shortages, when countries are tempted to ban exports. More output alone will not be enough, however, unless it is achieved without exhausting soil and water, so support should favour farming methods that can be sustained.\n\nGrowing populations and changing diets explain why the world's appetite keeps increasing. It can be satisfied if governments invest in their own farmers while international partners supply the knowledge, funding and open trade that single countries cannot provide alone.",
"b23":"Schooling now reaches far more people than it once did, yet a considerable number of adults still cannot read a simple notice or sign their name. These adults are held back mainly in work and in their dealings with officials, and governments can help by taking teaching to them.\n\nThe clearest disadvantage is economic. Almost every job that pays a reasonable wage now involves some reading, whether it is a safety instruction, a delivery list or a message from a supervisor. People who cannot manage this are confined to casual manual labour, where earnings are low and work may vanish without warning. They are also poorly placed to defend their interests. A labourer who is handed an employment contract, for example, has to trust the person offering it, and may agree to terms he would have refused had he understood them. Although many such adults develop impressive memories and rely on trusted relatives, this dependence leaves them exposed whenever that help is unavailable.\n\nGovernments are in a position to reduce these difficulties, provided that they design programmes around adult lives rather than school timetables. Free classes held in the evening, in familiar places such as community halls, temples or large workplaces, are far easier to attend than courses at a distant college. Lessons should use the materials learners need to handle, including payslips, medicine labels and bus timetables, so that progress is felt quickly. Teaching takes time, though, so public services should become less dependent on print in the meantime. Offices can offer spoken help at counters and use clear symbols on signs, so that an adult who is still learning can claim a benefit unaided.\n\nIlliteracy narrows an adult's choices at work and weakens his or her hand in formal transactions. Practical, local and free teaching would do much to widen those choices again.",
"b24":"Being unable to read as an adult shapes far more than a person's job prospects. It affects health, family life and self-respect on an ordinary day, and state help needs to reach people who have often spent years hiding the problem.\n\nIn daily life, the effects are constant and sometimes dangerous. A mother who cannot read the dosage on a bottle of children's medicine must guess or ask a neighbour, and a patient may leave hospital without understanding the written instructions he has been given. Ordinary tasks such as taking the right bus, choosing food by its label or replying to a text message require planning and help from others. This dependence is tiring, and it is frequently accompanied by shame. Many adults invent excuses, saying they have forgotten their glasses, rather than admit the truth. The damage also passes to the next generation, because parents who cannot read are unable to share a storybook or check homework, however much they want their children to succeed.\n\nSince embarrassment keeps many of these adults away from classrooms, governments should begin by making help easy to accept. Campaigns on television and radio, which do not depend on print, can show that the difficulty is common and nothing to be ashamed of. When a well-known footballer or singer describes learning to read late, others find it easier to come forward. Family programmes are especially promising. In these, parents attend the primary school with their young children and learn alongside them, so that the motive is the child's progress and not the adult's own weakness. Such programmes reach only parents of young children, so evening classes remain necessary.\n\nThe inability to read leaves adults dependent, anxious and less able to support their children. Governments can change this most effectively by removing the stigma and by bringing discreet, family-centred teaching within reach.",
"b25":"In most companies and public bodies, the people at the top are in their fifties or older, having spent decades climbing to that position. Some argue that organisations would be better run by younger leaders. I agree only to a limited extent, because I believe ability and judgement matter more than the age at which someone is appointed.\n\nYounger leaders do bring qualities that many organisations lack. They have grown up with digital technology and tend to recognise sooner how it is changing what customers want. Many large technology companies, such as Facebook, were built by founders in their twenties who saw possibilities that older executives had dismissed. The young are also less attached to established ways of working, which makes them more willing to abandon a product or process that no longer succeeds. Those who did not design the old system lose less by replacing it. This advantage is greatest in fast-moving industries, though, and counts for less where mistakes are costly and conditions change slowly.\n\nExperience, which only time provides, is the strength of older managers. A director who has lived through a recession, a failed merger or a strike has learned what such events demand and is less likely to panic when the next one arrives. Such a person also remembers the warning signs of the last crisis and can act earlier. A hospital or an airline, for example, needs steady judgement at the top more than bold experiment. Seniority is not a guarantee of wisdom, however, and a leader who has stopped learning can hold an organisation back for years.\n\nNeither youth nor age is therefore a qualification in itself. In my view, the best arrangement is to promote people on merit and to build leadership teams in which the energy of younger members is balanced by the experience of older ones.",
"b26":"Phones and handheld consoles have made games and other entertainment available at any moment, on the train, in a queue or in bed. Critics regard this as damaging to both the players and their communities. I disagree with so sweeping a judgement, since the harm comes from excessive use and not from the entertainment itself.\n\nFor the individual, mobile games are in most cases a harmless way of relaxing. A commuter who spends the journey on a puzzle game is doing nothing worse than a passenger of an earlier generation who read a magazine. Some games exercise memory, planning or quick reactions, and many are played with friends, which keeps people in touch. Trouble begins, however, when play displaces other things. A teenager who plays late into the night, for example, will sleep badly and struggle at school, and games designed around endless rewards make it hard for some users to stop.\n\nThe effect on society follows the same pattern. It is often claimed that portable entertainment isolates people, and a family sitting in silence over four separate screens is not an encouraging sight. Even so, there is little reason to think that games have made communities less lawful or less productive, and the industry employs large numbers of designers, artists and programmers. The real social cost falls on a minority, particularly children and those drawn into spending heavily on in-game purchases. This is an argument for regulation and parental limits rather than for condemning a whole form of leisure. Rules restricting how such purchases are sold to minors would address much of the damage.\n\nPortable games can injure those who lose control of their use, and such users need protection. For the majority, I believe they are an ordinary pastime that does no significant harm to players or to society.",
"b27":"It is tempting to think that protecting the environment is a job for those in power, since one person's efforts look tiny beside the scale of the problem. I agree that politicians carry the greater responsibility, but I do not accept that individuals can be excused on the grounds that they achieve too little.\n\nPoliticians are rightly held responsible because they control the tools that matter most. Only a government can set limits on what factories release, ban harmful chemicals or decide whether public money goes to motorways or railways. Decisions of this kind reach an entire population. When leaded petrol was banned, for instance, the air improved for all city residents, including those who had given the matter no thought. Voluntary action could not have produced the same result, because a minority of careful citizens cannot make up for a majority who see no reason to change.\n\nIt does not follow, though, that individual action is worthless. Small contributions become significant when they are repeated across millions of households, as the spread of recycling and the decline of single-use plastic bags have shown. A habit that looks trivial in one kitchen removes a great deal of waste across a whole city. Individuals also influence politicians. Elected leaders rarely introduce green taxes or stricter rules unless they believe voters will accept them, so a public that sorts its waste, saves energy and demands cleaner air makes such policies possible. If citizens treat the environment as someone else's business, even well-designed laws tend to be ignored. Responsibility is therefore shared, although not in equal measure.\n\nPoliticians should lead, because only they can change the rules under which society operates. I believe, however, that their efforts succeed only where ordinary people support them and act accordingly, so neither group can leave the task to the other.",
"b28":"Universal schooling has not ended illiteracy among adults, some of whom left school early or learnt little there. Their difficulties have grown as daily life has moved onto screens, and governments need both to teach them and to stop excluding them in the meantime.\n\nThe challenges facing these adults are sharper today than they were for earlier generations. Services that once involved speaking to a clerk, such as paying a bill, booking a medical appointment or applying for a job, now tend to require typing into a website. Someone who cannot read is locked out of these systems, or must hand passwords to another person. A second challenge is the risk of being cheated. Anyone who has to sign papers without reading them depends on the honesty of the person explaining them. A dishonest lender, for example, can describe a document in one way and have it signed in another. Not all illiterate adults are isolated, but even those with supportive families lose a measure of privacy and independence.\n\nSupport should begin with free courses that adults can take up. Few will come forward unprompted, so staff at job centres and clinics, who notice when a form is repeatedly left blank, could offer a place without fuss. Paid study leave or a small allowance would then allow learners to afford the time. The other step is to stop shutting these adults out while they learn. Public bodies ought to keep a telephone line or a staffed counter open as an alternative to online forms. This costs more than a website alone, but it lets someone book an appointment or pay a bill without sharing private details.\n\nAdults who cannot read face exclusion from services and a higher risk of being cheated. Funded courses that are offered tactfully, together with non-written channels, would reduce both problems.",
"b29":"Many countries allow teenagers to leave education at sixteen, and some young people are glad to go. I agree that the leaving age should be eighteen, on condition that 'school' is understood to include vocational training and not only academic study.\n\nThe case for the later age is that sixteen-year-olds who leave have little to offer an employer. Most of the jobs open to them are low-paid and insecure, and without further qualifications they often remain in such work for decades. Two more years of learning change this picture considerably. A teenager who completes a course in electrical installation or nursing care, for example, enters the labour market with a recognised skill and is far less likely to be unemployed later. Those extra years matter most for pupils from poorer families, who are the likeliest to leave early and the least able to recover from a bad start.\n\nThe objection is that some teenagers are plainly unhappy in a classroom, and keeping them there helps neither them nor their classmates. This is a fair criticism of forcing all pupils through the same academic programme. A sixteen-year-old who has failed repeatedly at written examinations will gain little from two more years of them, and may disrupt lessons for others. It is not, however, an argument for letting such a pupil leave altogether. Systems such as Germany's, in which apprentices divide their week between a workplace and a college, show that practical learners can be kept in education while doing work they find worthwhile.\n\nStaying in education until eighteen gives young people a much stronger start in adult life, and for that reason I believe it should be required. The requirement will succeed, though, only where schools offer practical routes alongside academic ones.",
"b30":"Every winter and summer, accidents on mountains and in the air prompt calls for the most hazardous sports to be outlawed. I disagree with this proposal. Adults are entitled to accept risks that fall mainly on themselves, and sensible regulation would protect them better than prohibition.\n\nThe main objection to a ban is that it would remove a freedom without a strong enough reason. People who take up sky diving or skiing know that injury is possible, and they judge that the excitement, fitness and sense of achievement are worth it. The danger is also easy to exaggerate. Skiing is a family holiday for a great many people, and most first parachute jumps are made attached to a trained instructor with reserve equipment. Ordinary pastimes such as horse riding and cycling in traffic injure people too, yet few would suggest forbidding them. This freedom has a limit, however. It does not extend to conduct that endangers others, as when skiers leave marked runs and risk setting off an avalanche.\n\nRegulation deals with that limit far more effectively than a ban could. If these sports were illegal, enthusiasts would be likely to continue in remote places, without instructors, safety checks or anyone to call for help. A licensing system achieves the opposite. Operators can be required to employ qualified staff and maintain their equipment, and participants can be obliged to carry insurance so that the cost of rescue does not fall on taxpayers. Although such rules will not prevent every accident, they reduce the number and ensure that those who choose the risk also bear its cost.\n\nExtreme sports are risky, and the people who practise them know it. In my view, the proper response is careful supervision, since a ban would restrict responsible adults while making the activities themselves less safe.",
"b31":"Many adults now go for weeks without writing more than a signature or a shopping list by hand. The causes of this shift are easy to identify, and while it has made everyday communication more efficient, I believe it is a loss overall, especially where children are concerned.\n\nHandwriting has declined chiefly because typing is quicker and its results are more useful. Text produced on a keyboard can be corrected without mess, copied, searched and sent across the world, none of which is true of a page of ink. As a result, workplaces now expect reports, forms and messages in digital form. The spread of phones is the other cause. A person who once kept a diary or wrote notes to friends is far more likely to use an app, which is always at hand.\n\nIn practical terms little has been lost, and few would wish to return to handwritten business letters. The cost appears in learning and in personal expression. Forming letters by hand is slow, and that slowness obliges a writer to select and summarise rather than record every word. Students who take notes on paper, for instance, generally have to decide what matters as they listen, and they tend to remember the lesson better for it. Young children also seem to recognise letters more readily when they have drawn them themselves. Beyond the classroom, a handwritten card or letter carries something of the sender that a typed message, however kind, does not. That personal effort is why people still keep old letters.\n\nThe move from pen to keyboard is the natural result of faster and more convenient tools, and for most adult tasks it is harmless. In education, though, the decline seems to me a negative one, which is why I think schools should go on teaching children to write by hand.",
"b32":"Enrolments in physics, chemistry and biology have fallen in several countries as students turn to courses in programming, data and software design. The shift is easy to explain, but I believe it is a worrying development if it continues unchecked.\n\nStudents are responding rationally to what they see around them. Technology companies are among the most visible and best-paying employers, and a graduate in software engineering can often expect a job offer before finishing the course. A degree in chemistry appears to promise a longer route through postgraduate study towards a less certain salary. Computing is also more familiar. Teenagers have grown up with phones and games, and writing a simple program gives quick, visible results, whereas school science can seem a matter of memorising formulae for an examination.\n\nAlthough the move brings some benefit, since economies do need programmers, its long-term effects are likely to be harmful. Computing is a tool, and many of the problems it is applied to belong to the natural sciences. Developing a vaccine, designing a better battery or understanding a changing climate requires people who know biology, chemistry and physics in depth. A country that trains too few of them will have to import that knowledge or go without it. Science teaching would suffer as well, because fewer science graduates means fewer qualified teachers. Weaker lessons would then give the next generation even less reason to choose the subject. The trend would matter less if students combined the two fields, as those in areas such as bioinformatics do, but most are choosing one in place of the other.\n\nThe drift away from science reflects better pay and greater familiarity on the computing side, not a lack of ability among students. In my view it is a negative trend, and schools should respond by teaching science in a more practical way.",
"b33":"Violent films and video games attract huge audiences, and their popularity regularly prompts calls for the state to restrict them. Others reply that fictional violence is simply a way to unwind. I believe that neither view is quite right: such content is not harmless for children, but age limits, not censorship, are the proper response.\n\nThose who want censorship fear that repeated exposure to violence changes behaviour. In many games the player is rewarded for killing, and critics worry that this makes cruelty seem normal, particularly to young players who are still forming their sense of right and wrong. There is also a concern that hours spent in front of brutal images leave viewers less sensitive to real suffering. These worries deserve attention where children are concerned, although they are much weaker in the case of adults, who are generally able to separate a story from reality.\n\nDefenders of this entertainment point out that most people who enjoy it live peaceful lives. An office worker who plays a shooting game after work, for example, is seeking excitement and release, much as readers of crime novels have long done. Stories about violence are as old as literature, and many respected films depict war honestly precisely because they do not hide its horror. Censorship would remove such works along with the crude ones, and it would hand officials the power to decide what adults may watch. That said, the claim of harmlessness holds only if the audience is mature enough, which is not the case when a ten-year-old plays a game designed for adults.\n\nViolent entertainment is neither a serious threat to society nor suitable for all ages. In my view, governments should leave adults free to choose while enforcing age ratings strictly and helping parents to apply them at home.",
"b34":"Supermarket shelves in many cities now carry mangoes in winter and salmon caught on the other side of the world. I believe this is a positive development on the whole, because it improves diets and supports farmers in poorer countries, although the way some food travels needs to change.\n\nThe clearest gain is to the people who eat this food and those who grow it. Before long-distance trade, the diet of a cold or dry country narrowed sharply for much of the year, whereas fresh fruit and vegetables are now available in every season at prices most households can manage. This variety makes a healthy diet easier to follow. Producers benefit as well. Farmers who grow green beans in Kenya or coffee in Vietnam, for example, reach customers far wealthier than those in their local market, and the income pays for schooling and better equipment. These benefits are real, though they depend on growers receiving a fair share of the final price.\n\nThis trade does have an environmental cost, although it is concentrated in one part of the business. Food sent by ship adds relatively little to its carbon footprint, since vessels carry vast quantities at once. Perishable goods flown in overnight are a different matter, and berries or asparagus delivered by air cause emissions out of proportion to their value. Shoppers can seldom tell how a product travelled, so clear labelling would help them avoid the worst cases. The problem is not trivial, but it argues for better choices about transport and season rather than for an end to the trade.\n\nGlobal food transport has made good nutrition more widely available and given distant farmers a living. Despite its environmental cost, I think the development is clearly beneficial, provided that air freight becomes the exception.",
"b35":"Coal, oil and gas still supply most of the world's energy, at considerable cost to the atmosphere, and many governments are now promoting cleaner alternatives. I believe this encouragement is a clearly positive development, despite the practical difficulties that wind and similar sources still present.\n\nThe strongest argument in favour is environmental. Burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide, which is the main driver of global warming, together with smoke and gases that damage people's lungs. A wind turbine produces neither once it has been built. Replacing a coal-fired power station with a wind farm therefore improves the air in nearby towns as well as reducing a country's contribution to climate change. Official encouragement matters because clean energy needs heavy investment at the start. Subsidies and favourable prices persuade companies to build turbines sooner than they otherwise would, and as more are built, the cost of each one falls.\n\nPromoting alternatives also makes a country more secure. Oil and gas are concentrated in a few regions, so importers are exposed to sudden price rises whenever supplies are interrupted, whereas wind is available at home and cannot be cut off by another state. This benefit has limits, however. The wind does not blow to order, and a grid that depends heavily on it needs storage or reserve power stations for calm days. Turbines are sometimes opposed by local residents, too, who object to their noise and their effect on the landscape. Although such problems are real, they are matters of engineering and planning, such as better batteries and offshore sites, rather than reasons to keep burning coal.\n\nAlthough fossil fuels will be needed for some years yet, I believe that encouraging wind and other clean sources is a welcome step, one that reduces pollution and strengthens energy security.",
"b36":"Most countries still run their power stations, vehicles and factories on fuels taken from the ground. The decision by some nations to turn instead to sunlight and wind is, I think, a positive one, both for those countries and for the states that follow them.\n\nOne reason is that fossil fuels will not last. Reserves of oil and gas are finite, and those that remain are increasingly difficult and expensive to extract. A country that begins to build solar and wind capacity now is preparing for a future that will arrive in any case, and it does so while it still has time to make mistakes. A second reason is that solar power can reach places that the national grid does not. In rural parts of India and East Africa, for instance, small rooftop panels now light homes that previously relied on kerosene lamps. Connecting such villages by power line would have taken far longer and cost far more.\n\nThe early adopters also do a service to others. Germany and China invested in solar panels when they were costly, and the scale of their demand has made the technology affordable for much poorer nations. This progress should not be overstated. Solar farms occupy large areas of land, output falls at night and in winter, and a country may still need gas as a reserve while storage remains expensive. The transition can be painful for mining regions as well, unless governments retrain the workers whose jobs disappear. These costs, though real, are temporary, whereas the damage caused by continued dependence on coal and oil would be lasting.\n\nRenewable energy is not yet a full replacement for conventional fuel. Even so, I believe the countries now adopting it are moving in the right direction, and the sooner others join them the better.",
"b37":"Sending rockets into orbit while people on the ground lack clean water strikes many taxpayers as a poor use of public money. I understand this objection, but I disagree with most of it, since space programmes cost less and return more than their critics tend to assume.\n\nThe claim that space budgets are wasted overlooks what they have produced. Weather forecasting, satellite navigation and long-distance communication all grew out of publicly funded space research, and each now serves ordinary life on Earth. Satellites are also among the best tools available for the very problems that critics want addressed. They track deforestation, measure the melting of polar ice and warn coastal communities of approaching storms, such as the cyclones that regularly strike Bangladesh. Research of this kind is slow and uncertain, which is why private firms were for decades unwilling to pay for it and governments had to.\n\nIt is also doubtful that cancelling these programmes would do much for poverty or disease. Space agencies receive a very small share of national spending compared with health, defence or pensions, so the sum released would make little difference to problems of that size. Such problems persist largely because of poor administration and political choices rather than a shortage of funds that astronomy has absorbed. The critics are right in one respect, however. Prestige projects, such as a crewed mission whose main purpose is national pride, are hard to defend in a country where many citizens lack basic services. Priorities within a space budget should therefore favour work with clear practical or scientific value.\n\nA government need not choose between the sky and the ground. Provided that spending is modest and directed at useful research, I believe space exploration is a sound investment that also helps to solve problems on Earth.",
"b38":"Public budgets for sport and culture are limited, so every grant to a national team or a theatre is money not spent on a school playing field. I agree that school sport deserves priority over professional sport, but I do not think the same reasoning applies to the arts.\n\nThe case for funding children's sport is that the benefit reaches those who need it and cannot buy it themselves. A school with a decent pitch, equipment and a trained coach gives every pupil regular exercise, including those whose parents could not afford club fees. Habits formed at that age tend to last, so the spending is repaid later in better health. Professional sport, by contrast, has other sources of income. Leading football clubs, for example, earn large sums from television rights, sponsors and ticket sales, and it is hard to justify adding taxpayers' money to that. The exception is minority sports with small audiences, where elite athletes may depend on modest public grants to compete at all.\n\nThe arts are in a different position, because most of them cannot survive on what audiences pay. An orchestra or a regional theatre has high costs and a limited number of seats, and without a subsidy it must either close or charge prices that exclude ordinary families. Public money is what keeps such institutions open to the general public rather than to a wealthy few. Although some subsidised work attracts small audiences, that is a reason to fund it more carefully, not to withdraw support.\n\nGovernments should therefore move money from professional sport to school sport, where it does more good. I believe, however, that the arts should keep their funding, since they rely on it in a way that commercial sport does not.",
"b39":"Children and adults learn a great deal from how they spend their time outside formal lessons, whether in an orchestra or alone with a book. I agree that activities shared with others teach the more important lessons for adult life, although solitary pursuits develop qualities that no team can provide.\n\nGroup activities matter most because nearly everything adults do depends on other people. In a football team or a school drama production, a young person has to listen, accept a role that may not be the one they wanted, and continue cooperating with someone they dislike. They also discover that their own mistakes affect others, which is a strong incentive to be reliable. These abilities are difficult to acquire in any other way, and they are what employers, families and communities later require. A brilliant engineer who cannot explain an idea to colleagues or accept criticism, for example, will achieve less than a more modest one who can.\n\nActivities done alone build a different set of strengths. Practising the piano, running long distances or learning a language from a book demands self-discipline, since there are no teammates to notice if the work is skipped. Such pursuits teach concentration and the capacity to be content in one's own company, both of which are scarce and valuable. They are, however, strengths that mainly improve what a person can do individually. Unless that person can also negotiate, compromise and share credit, much of this ability will be wasted in a world organised around offices, committees and households.\n\nBoth kinds of activity deserve a place in a balanced upbringing, and neither should be neglected. Since working with others is the skill on which most of adult life depends, I believe team activities offer the more important preparation.",
"b40":"Many of the world's great museums display treasures that were made thousands of miles away, and the countries they came from increasingly want them back. I agree that such objects should generally be returned, particularly those removed by force, although a few exceptions are justified.\n\nThe strongest argument for return is one of ownership. A large number of famous objects left their homelands during periods of conquest or colonial rule, when local people were in no position to refuse. The Benin Bronzes, for example, were seized by British soldiers and later sold to museums across Europe. Keeping such items is difficult to defend. They also mean most in the place where they were created, where people can see them as part of their own history rather than as curiosities in a foreign capital. Schoolchildren there can see the work of their own ancestors without travelling abroad.\n\nOpponents of return raise two objections. They argue that large international museums allow visitors from every country to compare civilisations under one roof. They also warn that some objects would be at risk if sent to regions affected by war or lacking proper facilities. The second point is reasonable, and return should wait where an object's safety cannot be assured. Items that were legally bought or given as gifts are also a separate case. These exceptions are narrower than museums tend to suggest, however. Most countries requesting their heritage now have modern museums of their own, and long-term loans or high-quality copies can keep the objects known to a wider public.\n\nObjects taken without consent belong, in my view, in the countries that produced them, and the burden should be on the holder to justify keeping them. Only where an item was fairly acquired, or could not be kept safely, is there good reason to delay.",
"b41":"Forests have been cleared, seas overfished and countless habitats built over, and few would deny that people are responsible. Opinion divides on whether this harm can still be repaired. I believe it can, at least in part, although the pessimists are right about how difficult the task is.\n\nThose who think nothing can be done point first to the forces behind the damage. A farmer or a logging company gains immediately from clearing forest, while the benefit of leaving it standing is distant and shared. As the human population continues to grow and needs more food, housing and energy, that incentive to clear land only becomes stronger. The other argument is that some losses are final. A species that has died out cannot be brought back, and a rainforest that took thousands of years to form will not regrow within a human lifetime. On this view, conservation only delays the inevitable.\n\nThe opposing view rests on evidence that determined action does work. Whale populations recovered in many oceans after commercial hunting was banned, and rivers such as the Thames, once too polluted to support fish, now hold many species again. These successes share certain features. A specific cause was identified, a law was passed against it, and that law was enforced. Where such conditions are met, nature tends to recover faster than expected, because most species breed quickly once the pressure on them is removed. Recovery is far less likely, however, where governments are weak or where local people have no other way of earning a living.\n\nThe damage, then, is neither fully reversible nor beyond repair. While some of what has been lost will not return, I side with those who hold that well-enforced measures can halt the decline and restore a good deal of it.",
"b42":"As populations age, more elderly people reach a point at which they can no longer manage alone, and the question of who must look after them becomes pressing. Some hold that the law should place this duty on their children and grandchildren. While I accept that families have a strong moral obligation, I disagree that it should be made a legal one.\n\nThe appeal of such a law is understandable. Parents spend years feeding, housing and educating their children, and it seems only fair that this care should be returned when they become frail. On this view, abandoning a frail parent means refusing to repay a debt. In societies where the state provides little for the old, relatives are in practice the only protection against poverty and neglect. Countries such as China and Singapore have accordingly passed laws requiring adult children to support their parents. These laws express a widely shared value, although they can do no more than compel payment or visits, and care given under threat of prosecution is seldom given well.\n\nThe difficulty is that a legal duty takes no account of circumstances. Looking after someone with advanced dementia is skilled, exhausting work, and a daughter with young children and a full-time job may be unable to do it however much she wishes to. Others have good reasons for keeping their distance, having been neglected or mistreated by the parent who now needs help. A single rule would treat all these cases alike. Public care services, funded by taxation, spread the burden more fairly, provided that families remain involved where they are able.\n\nMost people will want to care for their ageing relatives, and society should make that easier through paid leave and financial support. I believe, though, that compelling them by law would be both unjust and ineffective.",
"b43":"In a number of countries the bicycle, once the ordinary way of getting to work or school, is steadily giving way to motor vehicles. This shift has more to do with rising incomes and the design of cities than with any change in the bicycle's merits, and reversing it requires changes to both.\n\nThe decline is largely a product of growing prosperity. As people earn more, they can afford a motorbike or a car, which is faster, keeps the rider dry and carries passengers and shopping with ease. In cities such as Hanoi or Beijing, where bicycles once filled the streets, owning a motor vehicle has also become a sign of success, while cycling is associated with those who cannot afford anything better. The increase in traffic then makes matters worse. Roads crowded with cars are dangerous and unpleasant for cyclists, so even people who would like to ride feel unable to.\n\nEncouraging people back onto bicycles means making cycling safe and convenient rather than simply praising it. The most effective step is to build lanes that are physically separated from motor traffic, since fear of accidents is the main deterrent. Parents in particular are unlikely to let children ride to school unless the route keeps them away from cars. Convenience matters too. Secure parking at stations and offices, together with public bike-sharing schemes, allows cycling to be combined with trains and buses on longer journeys. The Netherlands shows what such policies achieve, since cycling there is common among all ages and incomes. Although campaigns about health and clean air may help, they have little effect unless the roads feel safe.\n\nPeople have abandoned the bicycle because other vehicles became affordable and the roads became hostile. They are likely to return once riding is a safe and convenient choice.",
"b44":"Most justice systems respond to crime by locking offenders up, yet prisons in many countries are full and crime persists. In my view, better education would do more to reduce offending, although it cannot take the place of prison for those who are dangerous.\n\nEducation is more effective chiefly because it acts before a crime is committed, whereas prison acts afterwards. Many prisoners left school early, with weak reading skills and no qualifications, and so had few legal ways of earning a decent living. A teenager who leaves school able to train as an electrician, for example, has something to lose by breaking the law and little reason to do so. Prison offers nothing comparable. It removes an offender from society for a time and then returns him with a criminal record that makes honest work harder to find.\n\nThis does not mean that education could replace imprisonment. Its effects take a generation to appear, and they depend on the quality of teaching in the poorest districts rather than on the number of years pupils spend in a classroom. Better schools for today's children also do nothing about adults who are already committing serious offences. While a violent offender remains a threat, the public has a right to be protected from him now, not in twenty years. Prison therefore keeps a necessary role, though a narrower one than it currently has, and it works best when inmates are taught while they are inside.\n\nPrison deals with the results of crime, while education removes many of its causes. For that reason I agree that spending more on schools, particularly in deprived areas, would make societies safer than building more cells, provided that custody remains available for serious offenders.",
"b45":"Cities such as Jakarta, Lagos and Manila have grown far beyond what their roads, housing and water supplies were built to serve. I believe this overcrowding is mainly the result of opportunity being concentrated in one place, and that the remedy is to spread that opportunity more widely.\n\nPeople crowd into a major city because that is where the rewards are. In many countries the capital contains the best universities, the head offices of large companies, the main hospitals and the national government, so anyone with ambition has little choice but to go there. Each arrival strengthens the pattern, since employers prefer to set up where skilled workers are already gathered. Natural growth is a second cause. Migrants are mostly young adults who then raise families in the city, so its population keeps rising even in years when few newcomers arrive.\n\nSince concentration is the cause, the most promising measure is to build up other centres. Governments can move ministries and public agencies to regional cities, as South Korea has done with Sejong, and offer tax incentives to companies that follow. Jobs created in this way give graduates a reason to settle outside the capital. Public services need to be spread as well. Many families move simply because the best hospitals and universities are in one city, so investment in provincial ones removes a common reason for leaving. Such policies are slow and costly, and they succeed only if the new centres offer careers and not merely office buildings. Better planning of housing and transport within the big city helps too, but it treats the symptoms rather than the cause.\n\nAs long as they hold most of a nation's good jobs and services, large cities will, in my view, keep growing. Easing the pressure therefore means making several other cities attractive enough to compete.",
"b46":"The gap between the pay of senior executives and that of ordinary employees is now widely debated. Some regard very large salaries as a sign of a healthy country, whereas others want the law to set a ceiling. In my view, a legal limit would do more harm than good, and taxation is the better instrument.\n\nThose who defend high earnings argue that they reward scarce ability and attract it. A surgeon with a rare skill, or the head of a large company, makes decisions whose value is far greater than the salary paid. If such people can earn more abroad, many will leave, and the country loses both their talent and their taxes. The prospect of exceptional reward also encourages individuals to start businesses that create jobs for others. Few would risk their savings on a new company if success were capped. This argument is sound where pay reflects performance, although it is weaker when executives are rewarded generously while their firms are failing.\n\nSupporters of a salary cap are concerned with fairness and social stability. They find it hard to accept that a chief executive can receive many times what a nurse earns, and they fear that such contrasts breed resentment and weaken people's sense of belonging to one community. A fixed maximum seems a simple remedy. In practice, however, it would be easy to avoid, since companies could pay in other forms, such as shares or bonuses, and the most mobile earners would move elsewhere. The concern is legitimate, but the proposal itself is unlikely to work.\n\nVery high salaries bring benefits to a country, yet they also strain its sense of fairness. I believe the sensible response is to leave pay uncapped and to tax the largest incomes at a higher rate, so that the wider public shares in the rewards.",
"b47":"Rising rates of childhood obesity have led many to look to the state for a remedy. I agree that governments carry part of the responsibility, since they control much of the environment in which children eat and play. The larger share, however, belongs to parents.\n\nThere are parts of this problem that only public authorities can address. Children spend much of their day at school, where the state decides what is served at lunch and how much time is given to physical education. Governments also set the rules for businesses. They can, for example, restrict advertisements for sweets and fried food during children's television programmes, or prevent fast-food outlets from opening beside school gates. An individual family could not achieve these changes alone. Such measures shape the choices available, though they cannot determine which of them a child actually makes.\n\nWhat a child eats and does each day is decided mainly at home. Parents choose what goes into the shopping basket, whether meals are cooked or ordered, and how many hours are spent in front of a screen. Their own example counts as well, as children of active parents are more likely to enjoy sport themselves. A government that tried to regulate these private habits would be both intrusive and ineffective. Parents cannot simply be left alone with the problem, however. Those with little money or time may need practical help, such as free school meals or affordable sports clubs, before good intentions can become daily practice.\n\nOverweight children are the product of both public conditions and private habits. For that reason I agree only partly with the statement: the state should make healthy choices easier, but it is parents who must make them.",
"b48":"A child born in a remote village often has a less qualified teacher and a more distant doctor than one born in the capital. Some would correct this by posting new graduates to the countryside, while others defend their right to choose. I believe a short period of rural service is reasonable, but only as a condition of publicly funded training and with proper support.\n\nThose who favour compulsory postings start from the scale of the need. Rural schools and clinics struggle to recruit because salaries are lower, facilities are basic and young professionals prefer city life, so vacancies stay open for years. Where the state has paid most of the cost of a degree, it seems fair to ask for something in return. Two or three years early in a career is a modest request. Thailand, for example, has long required newly qualified doctors to serve in provincial hospitals, and many districts gained physicians as a result.\n\nOpponents reply that people forced into a job seldom do it well. A resentful teacher counting the days until her transfer is of limited use to her pupils, and an inexperienced doctor working alone may make serious mistakes. There are personal costs as well, since a graduate with a partner or elderly parents in the city cannot easily move. Two years apart can strain a marriage. These objections carry weight, although they are arguments about how a scheme is run rather than whether it should exist. Decent housing, extra pay, regular supervision and a guaranteed return date would answer most of them. Graduates who paid for their own education, however, should remain free to decline.\n\nFreedom of choice matters, but so does the claim of rural families to basic services. I think a brief, well-supported posting for state-funded graduates is a fair compromise between the two.",
"b49":"Shoppers in many countries now fill their baskets with fruit, meat and dairy products from abroad while local farmers struggle to sell their own. This preference has clear explanations in price, availability and trust, and each suggests a way in which domestic produce could win customers back.\n\nThe simplest reason for buying imported food is that it is often cheaper and more reliably available. Large producers overseas benefit from scale, favourable climates and lower labour costs, and modern shipping allows their goods to reach distant supermarkets all year round. Local growers, tied to a short season, cannot compete on the same terms. Perception matters as much as price. In countries where food safety scandals have occurred, foreign goods such as Australian beef or New Zealand milk powder are widely regarded as cleaner and better controlled. This reputation is not necessarily deserved, but it is powerful, and imported brands may also carry prestige.\n\nEncouraging people to buy locally therefore depends on making domestic food both trustworthy and affordable. Governments could introduce a credible certification scheme, with regular inspection and a clear label, so that shoppers can recognise safe local produce. A parent choosing milk powder, for instance, is more likely to pick a domestic brand if an independent body has tested it. Shortening the route from farm to customer would help with price. Farmers' markets and direct delivery schemes remove several intermediaries, which lets growers charge less while earning more. Tariffs on imports are a less attractive tool, since they raise prices for consumers rather than improving what local farmers offer.\n\nPeople choose imported food because it appears cheaper, safer or more varied, not out of indifference to their own farmers. Local produce will regain its place only when it can match those qualities, and reliable labelling and shorter supply chains are the most promising means of achieving this.",
"b50":"Leaving school without finding work has become a common experience for young people in many parts of the world. The consequences reach far beyond a temporary lack of income, and I believe they can be reduced only if education and employers are brought much closer together.\n\nFor the individual, the damage is both financial and personal. A school-leaver with no wage remains dependent on parents, cannot rent a home and has to postpone plans such as marriage. The longer the search lasts, the harder it becomes, because skills fade and employers grow suspicious of an empty record. Many young people lose confidence as a result, and some stop looking altogether. Society pays as well. The state collects less tax while spending more on benefits, and businesses lose the energy and new ideas that young recruits bring. Areas with many unemployed young people also tend to see more petty crime and unrest, although unemployment is rarely the only cause.\n\nThe most useful measures are those that address the gap between what schools teach and what jobs require. Vocational courses and apprenticeships, such as those long established in Germany, allow teenagers to train inside a company while still studying, so that they finish with experience as well as a certificate. Governments can encourage this by reducing taxes for firms that take on first-time workers. Careers advice should also begin earlier. A pupil who learns at fourteen that local firms need electricians or nurses can choose subjects accordingly. Such schemes cannot create jobs where the economy itself is weak, however, and public investment is needed there too.\n\nYouth unemployment wastes individual potential and weakens the wider economy. In my view, the surest way to reduce it is to combine practical training with incentives for employers, so that leaving school leads directly to a first job.",
"b51":"A visitor walking down the main shopping street of a large city may now struggle to tell which country it is in, as the same chains sell the same goods almost everywhere. This uniformity has clear attractions for shoppers, but I believe that on balance it leaves places poorer.\n\nPeople who welcome this trend point to reliability and price. A customer who enters a branch of IKEA or Uniqlo knows in advance what the products will be like and roughly what they will cost, whether the shop is in Hanoi or Madrid. Because such companies buy and manufacture in enormous quantities, they can sell a decent chair or coat for less than a local producer could. For families on a limited budget, this is a real improvement in living standards. The benefit is narrower than it appears, though, because low prices often depend on distant factories where wages and conditions are poor.\n\nCritics are more concerned with what disappears. When a global chain opens, small independent shops nearby tend to lose customers and close, and with them go products made to local taste, such as regional foods, handmade furniture or traditional clothing. Over time, streets in different cities come to look alike, and travelling between them loses some of its interest. Choice, too, becomes an illusion. A shopper may face dozens of brands that are owned by a handful of companies and differ little from one another.\n\nStandardised shops offer convenience and low prices, and those gains should not be dismissed. In my view, however, they are outweighed by the loss of local businesses and of the variety that gives a place its character, so the development is mainly a negative one.",
"b52":"In the business district of almost any large city, people dress in much the same way: suits in the office and jeans at the weekend. This uniformity has practical causes, and I believe it is a largely harmless development, as long as traditional dress keeps its place on the occasions that matter.\n\nWestern clothing has spread mainly because it is practical. Jeans and T-shirts are hard-wearing, easy to wash and suited to cycling, factory work or sitting at a desk. Many traditional garments, by contrast, were designed for a slower way of life and take time to put on and maintain. Because they are made in huge quantities, western clothes also cost less than anything sewn by a local tailor. Convention matters too. The suit has become the accepted dress of international business, so a manager in Lagos or Seoul wears one in order to be taken seriously by partners abroad.\n\nOn the whole, this change does little damage and brings some benefit. Clothing that is affordable and practical makes daily life easier, and people are free to choose it. The risk is that traditional clothing, and the weaving and embroidery skills behind it, will disappear if it is no longer worn at all. This has not generally happened, however. In Japan and Vietnam, for instance, the kimono and the ao dai are still worn at weddings, festivals and graduation ceremonies, where they carry more meaning precisely because they are no longer everyday wear. The outcome is less happy where local textile workers lose their living, although governments and designers can do something to support them.\n\nMost people have adopted western dress for reasons of practicality and convention. Because traditional clothes tend to survive as dress for special occasions, I think the shift is a broadly positive one, though the crafts that produce them need protection.",
"b53":"Large quantities of edible food are discarded every day by supermarkets, cafes and restaurants. I believe this happens chiefly because, for a business, throwing food away is cheaper than running out of it. Changing that calculation is the key to reducing the waste.\n\nWaste in shops and restaurants is largely the result of commercial habits. Retailers know that full shelves attract customers, so they order more bread, fruit and ready meals than they expect to sell. Much of the surplus is perfectly edible, but it is removed once the date on the label has passed or its appearance is no longer flawless. Restaurants face a similar pressure. They cannot predict how many diners will arrive, and they prepare generously because disappointing a customer costs more than discarding a few portions. Oversized servings add to the problem. In each case the waste is rational from the firm's point of view, although it is costly for society.\n\nReducing waste therefore means making surplus food worth saving. Shops can lower prices steadily as the sell-by date approaches, which many customers welcome, and whatever remains can go to charities. In France, for example, large supermarkets are obliged by law to donate unsold food instead of destroying it. Restaurants need a different approach, because their waste is left on the plate as well as in the kitchen. They could offer smaller portions at a lower price and routinely invite diners to take leftovers home, which is already normal practice in the United States. Such measures will not eliminate waste, since demand can be forecast only roughly. They would, however, cut it substantially.\n\nFood is wasted in shops and restaurants mainly because abundance sells and disposal costs little. Once discounting, donation and realistic portions become normal practice, I think far less of it will end up in the bin.",
"b54":"The bins outside most homes fill more quickly than they did a generation ago, and much of what goes into them is wrapping rather than rubbish in the traditional sense. The growth of this waste reflects changes in how people shop and eat, and it can be reduced only by involving producers as well as consumers.\n\nPackaging has multiplied because food is now bought and eaten differently. Shoppers once took loose vegetables from a market stall, whereas supermarkets sell them sealed in plastic so that they survive long journeys and stay fresh on the shelf. Because plastic is so cheap, retailers have little incentive to use less of it, and a single cucumber, for example, may come in its own wrapper. The second cause is the demand for convenience. Busy working lives have made ready meals and takeaway food popular, and each portion arrives in its own tray, film and bag. A family that orders dinner a few times a week can fill a bin with containers alone.\n\nSince the problem starts before the product reaches the home, so should the solution. Governments could require companies to pay for collecting and recycling the packaging they put on the market. A firm that is charged for every tray and wrapper has a direct reason to use fewer of them, or to switch to materials that are easier to recycle. Consumers have less control, but they are not powerless. Charges on carrier bags have shown that a small fee changes habits quickly, and deposit schemes for bottles and cans work similarly. Such measures succeed, however, only when the alternative is as convenient as the packaged product.\n\nHousehold waste is rising because modern shopping wraps almost everything. It will fall when producers bear the cost of that wrapping and shoppers are given a reason to avoid it.",
"b55":"Bus and rail fares have climbed faster than wages in many countries, and passengers who have no alternative are paying a growing share of their income simply to reach work. The increase has two main sources, the rising cost of running services and the shrinking contribution of the state, and both can be addressed.\n\nPublic transport is expensive to operate, and its costs have grown. Fuel and electricity prices have risen sharply in recent years, drivers and maintenance staff must be paid competitive wages, and much of the track and rolling stock in older systems needs replacing. Operators could once rely on public subsidy to absorb such pressures. Many governments, however, have cut this support in order to balance their budgets, so the gap has been filled by the passenger. Where services are run by private companies, the need to return a profit to shareholders pushes prices higher still, although this factor matters less than the loss of subsidy.\n\nThe most effective solution is to restore stable public funding and to be clear about where the money will come from. One fair source is the motorist. Cities such as Singapore and Milan charge drivers to enter congested central areas, and the revenue can be directed to buses and trains, which lowers fares while reducing traffic. Operators should also be expected to control their own costs. Replacing diesel buses with electric ones, for instance, costs more at first, but the vehicles are cheaper to fuel and maintain. Such measures take time to pay off, so governments may need to cap annual fare rises in the meantime.\n\nFares are rising because the cost of providing transport has grown while public support for it has fallen. Reversing that trend depends on treating buses and trains as a service worth funding rather than a business expected to pay for itself.",
"b56":"Public money is limited, and each sum given to a theatre or gallery is a sum not given to a hospital or school. Both kinds of spending can be defended, but in my view health and education deserve the larger share, with the arts receiving modest and stable support.\n\nSupporters of arts funding argue that culture is part of what makes a country worth living in. Museums, orchestras and traditional crafts preserve a nation's memory, and most of them could not survive on ticket sales alone. Once a craft is no longer taught, it is very hard to recover. State support also keeps them open to people who could not otherwise afford to attend. The British Museum, for example, charges nothing for entry, so a family on a low income can see its collections as easily as a wealthy tourist can. These benefits are real, although they are rarely urgent.\n\nUrgency is the heart of the opposing view. A patient waiting for an operation or a child in an overcrowded classroom suffers a loss that cannot be made good later, whereas a delayed exhibition harms few people seriously. Health and schooling are also the conditions for enjoying everything else, because people who are ill or unable to read well are in no position to benefit from a concert hall. A healthy, educated population is also the audience the arts depend on. This reasoning is persuasive, but it should not be pushed too far. Arts budgets are usually tiny beside those of hospitals, so abolishing them would barely improve public services while destroying institutions that take generations to build.\n\nBecause medical care and education meet needs that are more basic and more pressing than those served by culture, I believe they should come first. A small, protected arts budget is nevertheless a sensible use of public funds.",
"b57":"Across much of the world, wild animals and plants are becoming scarcer, and species that were once common are now rarely seen. I think the principal reason is the loss of the places where they live, and it follows that protecting and restoring habitat is the most promising remedy.\n\nWildlife declines chiefly because its living space is taken for human use. Forests are felled for timber and cattle, wetlands are drained for housing, and grassland is ploughed for crops. In Southeast Asia, for example, large areas of rainforest have been replaced by palm oil plantations, which support only a small proportion of the species that lived there before. What remains is often divided into fragments too small for animals to find food or mates. Modern farming puts further pressure on the survivors, since pesticides kill the insects that birds and wild flowers depend on. Hunting and overfishing matter in some regions, though they tend to affect particular species rather than nature as a whole.\n\nIf lost habitat is the main cause, the solution must be to keep what is left and to return some of what has gone. Governments can create protected areas and link them with corridors of woodland, so that animals are able to move between them. Such reserves work only if they are properly guarded, however, and if local people gain from them through tourism or employment instead of losing their livelihoods. Farming must change as well. Paying farmers to leave hedges, ponds and field margins uncultivated allows wildlife to survive alongside food production.\n\nThe decline of plants and animals is, in my view, mainly a consequence of shrinking habitat. It can be slowed, and in places reversed, provided that enough land is set aside and farmed with wildlife in mind.",
"b58":"Paper, glass and metal can all be turned into new products, yet in many countries the bulk of these materials is still buried or burned. The reasons are practical and financial more than a matter of attitude, which means that the problem can be solved with fairly ordinary measures.\n\nPeople recycle little mainly because it is made difficult for them. Where there is one bin for everything and the nearest glass container is a car journey away, even well-intentioned residents give up. Confusion plays a part too, as rules about what is accepted differ from one district to the next. Behind this lies a financial obstacle. Collecting, sorting and processing waste is expensive, and when new raw materials are cheap, local authorities and companies find that recycled paper or glass is hardly worth producing. Recycling therefore remains a low priority, although few people oppose it in principle.\n\nThe most direct solution is to make recycling the easiest choice. Separate, clearly labelled bins should be collected from every home as regularly as ordinary waste, and the rules ought to be the same across the country. A household that can recycle at its own front door has little reason not to. Financial incentives are the other useful tool. In Germany, for instance, shoppers pay a small deposit on bottles and cans, which is refunded when the empty container is returned, and very few are thrown away as a result. Public information campaigns are useful, but they work best once the facilities are in place rather than as a substitute for them.\n\nLow recycling rates are largely the result of inconvenient systems and weak economic incentives. With simple collection and deposit schemes of this kind, most households would recycle as a matter of routine.",
"b59":"International chains selling burgers, pizza and fried chicken have spread to cities where they were unknown a generation ago. Many conclude that local cuisine, and with it family and social life, is being damaged. I agree only in part, because I think the harm to society is real while the harm to families is often exaggerated.\n\nThe effect on society is the more convincing half of the claim. Global fast-food companies sell a standard menu that is richer in fat and sugar than most traditional diets. Where such meals become a habit, obesity and the medical bills that accompany it tend to rise. Local restaurants and street vendors may also lose trade to competitors with larger advertising budgets. When they close, a district loses jobs and familiar dishes. Even here the picture is mixed, though. In Vietnam, for example, noodle stalls remain crowded at breakfast, and foreign chains have expanded more slowly than they expected.\n\nThe supposed damage to families is harder to demonstrate. It is said that relatives no longer gather round a home-cooked dinner, yet the cause is usually long working hours and commuting, which leave little time to shop and cook. A burger restaurant is a response to that shortage of time rather than its source. An outing to such a place is, for many households, an occasional shared treat. The risk arises only when purchased meals replace cooking altogether, so that children grow up without learning the dishes of their own culture. That outcome, while possible, depends on how parents use fast food and not on its mere presence.\n\nInternational fast food has brought poorer diets and tougher competition for local cooks, and to that extent the criticism is fair. I disagree, however, that it is weakening families, whose shared meals are threatened far more by lack of time.",
"b60":"The large household in which grandparents, parents, children and cousins share a roof has become rare in the world's biggest cities. In its place are couples with one or two children and a growing number of people living alone. Although this shift gives individuals more freedom, I believe it is, on balance, a negative development.\n\nIts advantages should not be dismissed. Young adults gain privacy and the freedom to make their own decisions about careers, partners and how to raise their children, without constant supervision from older relatives. For many women, leaving the extended family has meant escaping a heavy share of domestic work. Small households also suit urban life, where flats are compact and jobs may require moving from one city to another. A couple can then accept a post elsewhere without moving a dozen relatives. These are real gains, though they are enjoyed mainly by healthy adults of working age.\n\nThe costs are borne by those at either end of life, and by anyone who meets misfortune. In an extended family, help is close at hand: a grandmother minds the baby, and an uncle lends money in a difficult month. A couple living alone must pay for such help or manage without it. Elderly people are affected most. A widower in a high-rise flat in Tokyo or Seoul, for example, may go for days without speaking to anyone. Loneliness of this kind harms health, and the state must then provide the care that relatives once gave. Although technology allows scattered families to stay in touch, a video call cannot collect a child from school.\n\nIndependence is valuable, and few city dwellers would return to a crowded family home. In my view, however, the decline of the extended family has removed a source of everyday support that cities have not yet managed to replace.",
"b61":"Offences once associated with adults, from theft to serious assault, are increasingly being committed by people who are still at school. I believe this reflects how early children now enter an adult world through their phones, and that the right response is punishment designed to correct rather than simply to hurt.\n\nChildhood is far less sheltered than it used to be, and this seems to me the main reason for the trend. A twelve-year-old with a smartphone can watch violent material, be contacted by strangers and see crime presented as glamorous and profitable, all without leaving the bedroom. Criminal groups have noticed this. Drug dealers in some cities, for instance, recruit schoolchildren online as couriers, knowing that the young attract less suspicion and lighter sentences. Technology is not the whole explanation, since children in stable homes with firm rules are much less likely to be drawn in, but it has made the route into crime shorter.\n\nPunishment for young offenders should be certain and prompt, but it should aim at reform. For most offences this means penalties served in the community: repairing the damage done, meeting the victim, unpaid work at weekends and a period of close supervision. Such sanctions make a teenager face what he has done while keeping him at school and away from hardened criminals. Custody is a different matter. Although it is sometimes unavoidable for grave crimes such as armed robbery, it should take place in separate institutions where education continues. A child sent to an adult prison is likely to leave it more dangerous.\n\nChildren are offending more because adult influences now reach them earlier and with less supervision. They should be held to account, in my view, through firm community penalties that teach responsibility, with detention reserved for the gravest cases.",
"b62":"Children in many societies now have a greater say over how they dress, what they study and how they spend their free time than their grandparents had at the same age. I believe this change is largely positive, provided that freedom is extended gradually and parents remain involved.\n\nThe main benefit of greater freedom is that it teaches children to make decisions. A child who is allowed to choose between activities, manage a small amount of pocket money or settle a disagreement with a friend learns what the consequences of a choice feel like. These lessons are difficult to teach by instruction alone. Young people raised in this way also tend to speak more openly with their parents, because they expect to be listened to rather than simply corrected. A teenager who can admit a mistake at home is in a far safer position than one who hides it out of fear.\n\nFreedom does, however, carry risks when it arrives before the judgement needed to use it. This is most evident online. A ten-year-old with an unsupervised smartphone, for example, can encounter strangers, violent material and games designed to hold attention, and has little ability to resist any of them. Similar problems arise when young children are left to decide their own bedtimes or diets. Although these dangers are real, they are an argument against neglect, not against freedom itself. Parents who set a few firm limits, and widen them as a child shows responsibility, can avoid most of the harm while keeping the benefits.\n\nChildren who are trusted to make choices suited to their age generally grow into more capable and honest adults than those who are tightly controlled. This is why, in my view, the trend is a welcome one, as long as independence is matched to a child's maturity.",
"b63":"Tracing one's ancestors, once the pastime of a few enthusiasts, has become widely popular now that old records can be searched online. Some regard it as a worthwhile activity, while others feel that attention is better given to the living and to those not yet born. I believe the research has real value, provided that it remains a supplement to present concerns.\n\nPeople who favour looking forward have a simple argument: the past cannot be changed, whereas the future can. Time and money are limited, and hours spent in archives could be devoted to children's education or to an elderly relative who needs company now. They also note that discoveries about remote ancestors rarely alter anything. Learning that a great-great-grandfather was a blacksmith is interesting, but it does not pay a bill or solve a family quarrel. This view is reasonable, although it assumes that the two interests compete, which in most families they do not.\n\nThose who value family history point to benefits that are less obvious. Knowing where one's family came from, and what it endured, can give a person a stronger sense of identity. This matters especially to the descendants of migrants, who may feel that they belong fully to neither country. A grandparent's account of leaving home can explain customs and attitudes that otherwise seem arbitrary. There are practical uses too. A record of illnesses in earlier generations, such as heart disease or certain cancers, can warn living members to seek tests early. Knowledge of this kind protects children as well as adults, so it serves the future rather than competing with it.\n\nPresent and future generations deserve most of our energy. Understanding earlier ones takes little away from them, though, and in my view it is a useful activity that benefits the very generations it is supposed to distract us from.",
"b64":"A broken kettle or a torn jacket was once taken to someone who could mend it, whereas today it is more likely to go straight into the bin. This shift has clear economic causes, and its consequences for the environment and for household skills are serious.\n\nThe principal reason is that repair no longer makes financial sense. Mass production has pushed the price of new goods down, while the cost of a skilled repairer's time has risen. Paying someone to fix a toaster may therefore cost more than buying another one. Design is a further obstacle. Many modern products are glued or sealed shut, so that even a simple fault cannot be reached without special tools. A smartphone with a fixed battery, for example, often has to be sent back to the maker, and spare parts for older models may no longer be sold.\n\nThe most serious problem this habit causes is waste. Discarded appliances contain plastics and metals that do not break down, and when they are buried or burned, toxic substances can enter the soil and air. Each replacement also requires fresh raw materials and energy, so the true cost of a cheap product is paid by the environment rather than by the buyer. Recycling eases this burden only partly, because many items combine materials that are hard to separate. A less visible loss is that of skill. As fewer people learn to sew on a button or change a fuse, and as repair shops close, households come to depend on buying new even when a fault is minor.\n\nPeople replace things mainly because the market has made replacement the cheaper and easier choice. Unless repair becomes affordable again, the result will be ever larger volumes of waste and a population that has forgotten how to mend what it owns.",
"b65":"Long working days and crowded timetables have changed not only what people eat but when and how they eat it. The health consequences, from weight gain to digestive and heart problems, stem largely from a shortage of time, and the most effective responses are those that give some of that time back.\n\nThe first cause is that eating has been pushed to the margins of the day. An office worker who leaves home early and returns late has little opportunity to shop or cook, so breakfast is skipped and lunch is eaten at the desk. Irregular hours make matters worse, since a heavy meal late at night is followed by sleep rather than activity. The second cause is the kind of food that fills the gap. Delivery apps and convenience stores make a meal available within minutes, but most of what they sell is processed and high in salt, sugar and fat.\n\nBecause the problem begins with the working day, employers are well placed to ease it. A protected lunch break and a canteen serving simple, balanced meals would improve the diet of staff more than any leaflet. Some large companies, for instance, already subsidise fresh food on site, and they benefit from fewer days lost to illness. Governments can act on the food itself by requiring clear nutritional labels on ready meals and delivery menus. A simple colour code for salt, sugar and fat, like the traffic-light labels used in Britain, lets a customer in a hurry compare two dishes in seconds. Although neither step lengthens the day, together they make a decent meal possible within it.\n\nPoor eating habits among busy people reflect a lack of time more than a lack of knowledge. They will improve when workplaces make room for proper meals and when the convenient option is also a reasonably healthy one.",
"b66":"Tourists are increasingly aware that their holidays affect the places they visit, and many now try to limit the harm. Some critics argue that this effort is pointless because travel is damaging by its nature. I disagree, although I accept that no trip is free of impact.\n\nThe sceptics are right about one thing: the journey itself. A long-haul flight produces a large amount of carbon however carefully the passenger behaves on arrival, and no amount of reusing hotel towels cancels it. Popular destinations also suffer simply from numbers. Venice, for instance, is strained by the sheer volume of visitors, each of whom may be perfectly considerate. If responsibility means leaving no trace at all, then it is indeed out of reach. That definition, however, sets a standard that no human activity could meet, including staying at home.\n\nA more reasonable test is whether a visitor's choices make a real difference, and they clearly do. Someone who stays in a family-run guesthouse, eats in local restaurants and hires a local guide leaves most of their money in the community. A package booked through a foreign chain, by contrast, sends much of it abroad. The environment benefits from careful choices as well. Travelling by train where possible and staying longer in one place cut the emissions of a holiday, while visiting outside the peak season spreads the pressure on paths, beaches and water supplies. These choices depend on tourists being willing to pay a little more and to plan ahead, which not all of them are, but that is a matter of will rather than possibility.\n\nResponsible tourism cannot remove the cost of travel, yet it can greatly reduce it. For this reason I believe the claim is mistaken: a perfect tourist may not exist, but a responsible one certainly can.",
"b67":"From the age at which pupils first choose their own subjects, girls are more likely to pick languages and literature and boys to pick physics or computing. This difference owes more to upbringing than to ability, and I think it ought to be reduced, though by encouragement and not by pressure.\n\nThe trend begins long before any subject is chosen. Small boys are commonly given construction kits and toy cars, and girls dolls and storybooks, so each group arrives at school with more practice in one kind of thinking. Later influences reinforce this start. Textbooks and films still tend to present scientists and engineers as men, and a teenage girl who sees few women in those roles may conclude that she would not belong. Individual taste plays some part, of course, but the pattern varies so much between countries that biology cannot be the main explanation.\n\nBecause the causes are social, I think the tendency should be changed, for the sake of both sexes. Girls who avoid science rule themselves out of many secure and well-paid occupations, while boys who avoid the arts are seldom drawn to professions such as primary teaching or nursing, where more men are needed. The most effective steps are early and modest. Primary schools can make sure that all pupils build, experiment, read and perform, and secondary schools can invite female engineers and male writers to talk about their work. Compelling pupils to take subjects they dislike would be a mistake, however, since resentment is a poor basis for learning.\n\nGirls and boys divide along these lines mainly because of the toys, images and expectations they grow up with. In my view the divide should be narrowed, so that each pupil chooses according to interest and not according to sex.",
"b68":"City councils in several countries have begun handing over sections of parkland to residents who want to grow their own food. Although something is lost whenever open space changes its purpose, I believe the benefits of these gardens are greater, as long as they replace only part of a park.\n\nThe main advantage is that a shared garden gives people a reason to use a public space together. Neighbours who tend adjoining plots talk to one another, exchange seeds and advice, and gradually come to know the street they live on. This matters most where residents live in flats. The other benefit is to health. Digging, planting and watering provide regular gentle exercise, which suits older residents who would not join a gym. Gardeners also eat more fresh vegetables, although a few square metres will not feed a household.\n\nThe cost is borne by people who do not garden. Open grass serves many purposes, such as a football game, a picnic or a dog walk, without asking anything in return, whereas a vegetable plot is useful mainly to the person who holds it. If the plots are fenced and allocated by waiting list, a space that belonged to the whole neighbourhood is in effect handed to a few households. Gardens also depend on steady commitment, and neglected beds quickly make a park look abandoned. Once plot holders lose interest, the council is left with weeds to clear. These objections are serious, however, only when a council converts an entire park rather than an underused corner of it.\n\nOn balance, I think community gardens add more to city life than they take away, because they turn passive green space into a place where neighbours meet and stay active. That judgement holds provided that enough open ground remains for those who simply want somewhere to walk or play.",
"b69":"In a number of developed countries, the average age at which people have their first child has risen steadily, and many now become parents in their late thirties or forties. This trend reflects the way education, work and housing have changed, and its consequences are felt both in the home and across society.\n\nThe reasons are largely economic. Young adults spend longer in education than their parents did, and afterwards need several years to establish a career. Women in particular may fear that an early break would cost them promotion, so many wait until their position is secure. Housing is a further obstacle. In expensive cities such as London or Sydney, a couple may be well into their thirties before they can afford a home large enough for a family. A small rented flat feels too insecure for raising a child, and many postpone the decision until they have bought.\n\nWithin the family, the results are mixed. Older parents tend to be more financially secure and more patient, and their children often grow up in stable homes. They may, however, have less energy for a toddler, and they are more likely to face medical difficulties in conceiving. Their children may have fewer years with their grandparents, who are elderly by the time they are born. For society, the main effect is demographic. People who start late usually have fewer children, so the population ages and a smaller workforce must support a growing number of pensioners. Schools may close in some areas while demand for elderly care rises, although immigration can soften this imbalance to some extent.\n\nLater parenthood is therefore a rational response to modern pressures, with benefits for individual families. Its wider cost is a shrinking younger generation, which governments can address only by making it easier to combine children with work and affordable housing.",
"b70":"Being told that one is too old, or too young, for a post is a frustrating experience, since age is the one thing no candidate can change. A number of countries have now banned the practice. I think this is a positive development, because it obliges recruiters to look at the person and not the date of birth.\n\nAt its heart, the reform is about fairness. Assumptions based on age are often mistaken: there are people of sixty who learn new systems eagerly, and people of twenty-five who are more reliable than colleagues twice their age. When a recruiter discards applications by birth year, capable candidates lose their chance before anyone has considered what they can do. A middle-aged parent who loses a job and is repeatedly refused interviews, for example, may face years without income long before any pension is available. Outlawing this treatment places age alongside sex and race as a factor that should not decide a person's prospects.\n\nThe law has limits, and the most serious is enforcement. Discrimination is easy to disguise, since an employer can say that another applicant was a better fit. Few rejected candidates have the money or evidence to go to court, so most cases go untested. Although this weakens the law, it does not make it pointless, because legislation changes behaviour over time. Advertisements asking for young applicants disappear, managers are trained to justify their choices, and mixed-age teams become normal. A recruiter who knows that a decision could be challenged is more likely to read the whole application rather than stopping at the date of birth.\n\nNo statute can guarantee that every decision is free of bias. Even so, I believe that making age discrimination illegal is a clear improvement, as it gives applicants of any age the right to be judged on their merits.",
"b71":"Supermarkets that close at midnight, or not at all, have become familiar in many cities where shops once shut in the early evening. Longer opening hours bring convenience and employment but place a burden on shop workers and small traders, and I think the change is welcome only where those groups are protected.\n\nCustomers whose own working days are no longer regular gain the most. A nurse finishing a late shift, for example, or an office worker who leaves at eight, can buy food on the way home instead of sacrificing part of the weekend. Because shopping is spread across more hours, stores are also less crowded at peak times. Extended trading creates work as well, particularly part-time evening jobs that suit students and parents who are occupied during the day. This benefit should not be overstated, though, since people do not spend more simply because shops are open longer, and total sales may rise very little.\n\nThe costs fall mainly on those who staff the tills. Employees asked to work late evenings and Sundays see less of their families, and those in junior positions may feel unable to refuse. Travelling home after midnight, when most buses have stopped running, adds expense and some risk. Small independent shops face a different problem. A family-run grocery cannot stay open from early morning until midnight without exhausting its owners, so it loses customers to chains that can afford several shifts. Where late work is voluntary and properly paid, however, the first of these objections largely disappears.\n\nLonger hours suit the way many people now live and work, while making life harder for retail staff and small competitors. In my view, the development is positive on the whole, as long as labour laws guarantee fair pay and a real choice over late shifts.",
"b72":"By most measurable standards, the young in developed countries enjoy better lives than their grandparents did, yet many report feeling anxious or dissatisfied. I believe the explanation lies in constant comparison and in the pressure to succeed, and that schools and families can do much to ease both.\n\nMaterial comfort does not produce contentment once basic needs are met, because people judge their lives against those around them. In the past that meant classmates and neighbours. A teenager today compares herself with thousands of carefully edited lives on a phone, and almost anyone will appear plain, poor or unpopular beside such images. Expectations have risen in other ways too. Young people who have been told that any career is open to them tend to treat an ordinary result as a failure, and the competition for university places and good jobs begins early. Safety and wealth, in other words, have removed old hardships while adding new sources of worry.\n\nMuch of the remedy lies in changing what young people are encouraged to value. Schools could give less weight to rankings and more to activities in which success is not measured against others, such as team sports, music or community projects. A pupil who spends an afternoon rehearsing a play with classmates has less time, and less reason, to compare herself with strangers. Parents can help by praising effort rather than results, and by setting limits on phone use in the evenings, particularly for younger children. Rules alone will not work, however, unless adults follow them too.\n\nProsperity has given young people security without giving them peace of mind, largely because they measure themselves against impossible standards. In my view, the most useful response is to reduce that comparison both at home and at school.",
"b73":"Older people in many societies now see less of their relatives and friends than previous generations did. In my view, the main cause is the way work and housing are organised rather than any decline in affection, and the remedies must therefore be practical ones.\n\nThe root of the problem is that adult children seldom live or work near their parents. Jobs are concentrated in large cities, so a graduate from a small town usually has to move away, and visits home then depend on long journeys and scarce holidays. Those who do stay close are often no freer. Both partners in a couple typically work full time, and the evenings that remain are taken up by their own children. Although most people in this position would like to do more, the hours are simply not there, and friends of the elderly person are often too frail to fill the gap.\n\nBecause the obstacle is time and distance, the most useful measures are those that give carers more of the first and reduce the second. Employers could be required to offer paid leave for the care of a parent, as many already do for a newborn child, and to allow flexible hours where the job permits. A son who can work from his home town for one week a month, for example, sees far more of his mother. Local authorities can help as well by running day centres where older residents eat together and are visited by a nurse. Although such services cannot replace a daughter's company, they ensure that somebody notices when help is needed.\n\nLoneliness in old age, I believe, is a by-product of modern working life rather than a sign of neglect. It will ease only when employers and communities make it easier for willing relatives to be present.",
"b74":"As populations grow older and careers grow longer, several governments have made it unlawful to turn a candidate down on account of age. I believe this is a positive step, mainly because economies with ageing workforces can no longer afford to waste experienced people.\n\nThe economic argument is straightforward. In many developed countries the number of people in their fifties and sixties is rising while fewer young workers enter the labour market. If employers automatically exclude older candidates, these people are pushed into early retirement, where they draw pensions and benefits instead of paying tax. A law against age discrimination keeps them productive. Someone made redundant at fifty-five, for instance, may have a decade of useful work ahead and skills that take years to acquire. Firms benefit too, since an accountant or engineer with long experience has usually seen problems that younger colleagues are meeting for the first time.\n\nOpponents argue that such a law interferes with an employer's freedom to choose. They point out that an older recruit may be slower to learn new software, may expect a higher salary and will leave sooner, which makes training less worthwhile. These concerns are not unreasonable, but they describe individuals rather than age groups, and a fair selection process can test them directly. The legislation does not oblige a company to hire an unsuitable person. It simply requires the decision to rest on ability, and it normally permits exceptions where age is relevant, as in physically demanding work such as firefighting. Its real weakness is enforcement, because a rejected candidate can seldom prove the true reason.\n\nAlthough the law cannot remove prejudice by itself, it sets a standard that most employers will follow. In my view, it is a welcome development for older workers and for the economies that depend on them.",
"b75":"In wealthier parts of the world, an ordinary household can cook with ingredients drawn from several continents in a single week. This abundance has drawbacks for local farmers and for the climate. Even so, I believe the advantages are greater, chiefly because a varied supply is also a more dependable one.\n\nThe principal benefit is stability, not merely pleasure. A country that eats only what it grows is at the mercy of its own weather, so a wet summer or a crop disease can empty the shelves and push prices up sharply. When food arrives from many regions, a shortage in one is covered by another, and shoppers rarely notice. Choice matters in its own right as well. Immigrant families can cook the dishes they grew up with, and their neighbours come to know cuisines they might otherwise have met only abroad. Such gains do depend on income, since imported goods are of little use to those who cannot afford them.\n\nAgainst this must be set the effect on domestic agriculture. Local growers often cannot match the price of produce from countries with lower wages or longer seasons, and some give up farming as a result. Apple orchards in Britain, for instance, have declined as cheaper fruit has arrived from abroad. Transport is the other cost. Shoppers now expect strawberries in midwinter, and flying such fruit thousands of kilometres burns far more fuel than a lorry from a nearby farm. These costs can be limited, however, by clear labelling of origin and by support for farmers who sell directly.\n\nImported variety puts pressure on local producers and adds to transport emissions, and neither should be ignored. On the whole, though, I believe a diverse food supply leaves a country better fed and less vulnerable, so its benefits outweigh its costs.",
"b76":"Family history, once the hobby of a few enthusiasts, now attracts a wide following in a number of countries. This interest has grown because records are now easy to reach and because people feel less rooted than they once did, and I believe it is, on the whole, a healthy development.\n\nTwo changes explain the trend. The practical one is access. Census returns, parish registers and passenger lists that once had to be consulted in distant archives can now be searched from home. Inexpensive genetic tests, meanwhile, can link people to relatives they did not know existed. The deeper reason is a sense of disconnection. Many people live far from where they were born, in small households, and seldom hear the stories that grandparents used to tell. Discovering where a family came from offers a feeling of belonging that daily life no longer supplies, particularly for the descendants of migrants.\n\nThe effects of this curiosity are mostly beneficial. Someone who learns, for instance, that a great-grandmother worked in a textile mill or fled a war tends to see history as something that happened to real people. Events that seemed remote in a textbook become personal. The search also brings generations together, since older relatives are usually the first source of names and photographs. Grandparents asked about the past often share memories that would otherwise be lost. There are, admittedly, risks. A genetic test may reveal an unknown half-sibling or an adoption that was kept secret, and such discoveries can be painful. These outcomes are uncommon, however, and depend on how the knowledge is used.\n\nIn my view, then, the growing interest in family history deserves to be welcomed. It is driven by easier access and a wish to belong, and it generally leaves people with a richer understanding of both their relatives and the past.",
"b77":"Across much of the developed world, the average age at which women have their first child has risen steadily. This shift is largely the result of longer education and more demanding careers, and I believe that, for most families, what it offers outweighs what it costs.\n\nPeople delay parenthood chiefly because the years in which they used to start families are now occupied by other things. Many remain at university until their mid-twenties and then need several more years to secure a stable job and somewhere to live. Reliable contraception allows couples to wait until these conditions are met, and women in particular are reluctant to interrupt a career before it is established. In cities such as Seoul or London, where housing is expensive, a couple may feel unable to raise a child until both are earning well.\n\nWaiting has two main benefits. Although age is no guarantee of wealth, older parents usually earn more, so their children grow up in a secure home with money for good childcare and schooling. They also tend to be calmer and more settled as a couple, which makes family life steadier. The disadvantages are mainly physical. Fertility declines with age, so some couples find that they cannot conceive when they are finally ready, and pregnancy carries greater medical risks for older mothers. Energy is a further cost. Parents who start late may tire more easily with small children and have fewer healthy years to share with them later. These are real losses, although they weigh most heavily on those who wait until their forties.\n\nLater parenthood is a reasonable response to the way education and work are now arranged. Provided that couples understand the limits that biology imposes, I think the stability it brings to children matters more than the risks it adds.",
"b78":"Several large cities now charge drivers for entering the centre at the busiest times of day. I believe this is a positive step, because a price changes drivers' behaviour more reliably than appeals or new roads do, although its fairness depends on what the money is spent on.\n\nA charge works because it makes drivers weigh a cost they previously ignored. Each additional car slows all the others, yet the motorist pays nothing for that delay unless a fee is attached to it. Once it is, people whose journeys are flexible begin to travel earlier, share a car or take the train, and the road is left to those who really need it. London and Singapore, for instance, both saw noticeably lighter traffic in their central districts after introducing such schemes. Buses run faster as a result, deliveries become more reliable and the air is cleaner for residents. Building more roads, by contrast, tends to attract more vehicles.\n\nThe usual objection is that a flat fee hurts people on low incomes more than the wealthy, for whom it is a minor expense. This is a fair point where no alternative exists. A cleaner who starts work before the first bus has little choice but to drive, and charging her achieves nothing except to make her poorer. The answer, however, is to design the scheme well rather than to abandon it. Revenue should be spent on public transport, so that leaving the car at home is a realistic option, and exemptions can be offered to disabled drivers and essential workers.\n\nOn balance, I think congestion charging is a development to be welcomed, since it reduces traffic and pollution in a way that few other policies manage. Its fairness, though, rests on returning the income to the public through better buses and trains.",
"b79":"A rapidly growing number of children is a feature of several countries, particularly in parts of Africa and South Asia. The consequences are felt first in homes and classrooms, and later in the labour market and the wider environment, where they may be either a burden or an opportunity.\n\nAt present, the main effect is pressure on the services that children depend on. Schools built for one generation have to accommodate a much larger one, so classes become crowded and teachers are stretched. Clinics face the same strain, and governments with modest tax revenues struggle to pay for vaccinations, school meals and new buildings at once. Families feel it too. Where parents have many children to feed, older siblings are often taken out of school to work or to mind the younger ones, which limits their own prospects. These difficulties are not the same everywhere, however, since a country with a growing economy can expand its services far more easily than a poor one.\n\nThe longer-term effects depend on what happens when these children reach working age. If they have been educated and there are jobs for them, a large young workforce can drive rapid growth, as it did in countries such as South Korea. Without enough jobs, though, the same generation becomes a source of unemployment, migration and political unrest. The other lasting effect is on land and resources. More people will need housing, water and food, and cities like Lagos are already expanding faster than their roads and drains can cope with. Farmland on their edges is built over, which makes food harder to supply.\n\nA young and expanding population therefore strains public services today and will shape a country's economy for decades. Whether that future is prosperous or unstable rests largely on how well the present generation of children is educated and employed.",
"b80":"Parents who withdraw their children from school and teach them at the kitchen table were once a rarity, but their numbers have risen in several countries. Although home education has real strengths, I believe that for most families its drawbacks are greater.\n\nThe appeal of teaching a child at home is that the lessons can fit the learner. A parent working with one or two children can slow down when a topic is difficult, move ahead when it is easy, and build lessons around what interests them. A girl fascinated by astronomy, for example, can spend a whole week on it in a way no school timetable would allow. Home is also a refuge for children who have been bullied or whose needs a crowded classroom cannot meet. Away from that pressure, an anxious child can often recover confidence and enjoy learning again. These benefits depend heavily on the parent, however, and few households can spare an adult's full income for years.\n\nThe disadvantages affect more families and are harder to repair. Few parents can teach chemistry, a foreign language and advanced mathematics to the standard of specialist teachers. Gaps therefore tend to appear as children grow older, and a home has no laboratory for practical work. The larger loss is social. At school, children learn to cooperate with people they did not choose and to hear opinions unlike those of their own family. Clubs and neighbourhood friendships offer some of this experience, although they seldom provide the daily mixture of backgrounds found in a classroom. A child educated only at home may reach adulthood well taught but poorly prepared for a workplace.\n\nHome education can work well for a particular child with a capable and available parent. In general, though, I think its limits in specialist teaching and social experience outweigh the advantage of individual attention.",
"b81":"Longer lives and higher incomes were expected to make each generation more content than the last, but in several wealthy countries the young say otherwise. The causes are social rather than material, and they call for responses that money alone cannot provide.\n\nOne cause is loneliness. Young adults increasingly live alone, move cities for work and keep in touch with friends through screens, which means they have many contacts but few people to rely on in a crisis. Earlier generations were poorer, but they usually belonged to something, whether an extended family, a religious community or a neighbourhood in which people knew one another. A second cause is uncertainty about the future. Although salaries have risen, a graduate in a city such as London or Seoul may see little prospect of buying a home or holding a permanent job, and feeling unable to plan is deeply discouraging.\n\nAddressing the first problem means rebuilding the settings in which friendships form. Local authorities can fund sports clubs, libraries and youth centres, and universities and employers can organise shared activities rather than leaving people to work alone at a laptop. Such efforts cost little, though they succeed only if young people are willing to turn up. The second problem requires government action on a larger scale. Building affordable housing and discouraging insecure short-term contracts would give young adults a stable base from which to start families and careers. In Vienna, for example, a large stock of publicly owned flats keeps rents within reach of people in their twenties.\n\nYoung people have gained wealth and health while losing the close relationships and the secure prospects that make life satisfying. Their wellbeing is likely to improve only when communities and governments work to restore both.",
"b82":"Researching the lives of earlier generations has become a common pastime in many countries. People are drawn to it mainly by a wish to understand where they come from, and I believe the trend is, on the whole, a healthy one.\n\nThe appeal lies partly in how easy the search has become and partly in what modern life lacks. Census records, passenger lists and parish registers can now be consulted online, and inexpensive DNA tests promise to connect customers with distant cousins. At the same time, many people live far from where they grew up and know little about their grandparents' lives. Learning that a great-grandfather worked in a coal mine or crossed an ocean as a teenager gives them a story to belong to. For the descendants of migrants, for instance, such research may be the only link to a country and language their family left behind.\n\nThis curiosity seems to me beneficial, chiefly because it brings generations together. The natural first step is to ask elderly relatives about their memories while they are still able to share them. Such conversations give grandparents an audience and younger people a reason to visit. What they hear tends to stay with them, because a war or a famine means more when a named relative lived through it. The hobby does have limits, however. Records sometimes reveal painful facts, such as an unknown half-sibling or a relative's crime, and genetic data handed to a private company may not stay private. These risks are real, although they affect a small minority and can be reduced by treating discoveries with discretion.\n\nInterest in family history grows out of a natural desire for roots in a mobile age. Despite the occasional unwelcome discovery, in my view it enriches people's sense of who they are and strengthens the ties between generations.",
"b83":"It is now feasible to earn a salary, buy groceries and keep in touch with friends without leaving home or meeting another person. Each of these services is useful in itself, but I believe that the kind of life they make possible, one with no contact in person, is a negative development.\n\nThe case in favour should be acknowledged, because for some people these tools remove real barriers. Someone with a disability, or a carer who cannot leave the house, can now hold a job and order what they need without relying on others. Online contact also keeps relationships alive across distances that would once have ended them. The value here, however, lies in having an additional way of reaching people rather than a replacement for meeting them. A video call with a sister abroad is a gain, while a video call instead of visiting a friend who lives nearby is a loss.\n\nWhat disappears when all contact is remote is the unplanned and physical side of human company. People read one another through tone, posture and shared surroundings, and much of that is flattened on a screen. Ordinary encounters matter too. A brief conversation with a shopkeeper or a colleague in a corridor, for instance, asks little of anyone, yet it gives a person the sense of belonging somewhere. Those who work, shop and socialise alone at a desk may not notice the absence at first, although loneliness tends to build slowly. Communities suffer as well, since local shops and meeting places close when too few people use them. An emptied high street then offers fewer places to find company.\n\nThe technology itself is not the problem. My view is that using it to avoid people altogether leaves individuals more isolated and neighbourhoods emptier, which makes this way of living a change for the worse.",
"b84":"Advances in farming, textiles and medical science have led some to argue that humans can now live well without exploiting animals at all. I agree that this is true of clothing and, for many people, of food. I disagree, however, that it yet applies to medicine or to every community.\n\nIn wealthy, urban societies the need for animal products has shrunk. Cotton and modern synthetic fibres keep people as warm as fur or leather, so wearing animal skins has become a matter of fashion rather than survival. Diet is moving in the same direction. A city resident can obtain enough protein from beans, nuts, grains and fortified foods, and a well-planned vegetarian diet is widely accepted as healthy. For such consumers, eating meat is a preference. It is reasonable to ask them to reduce it, although cost and habit still make a full change difficult for some.\n\nThe claim fails when it is extended to all people and all purposes. Many communities live on land where crops will not grow, such as herders on the Mongolian steppe or fishing villages in the Arctic, and animals are their only dependable source of food and clothing. Importing substitutes would be costly and leave them dependent on distant suppliers. In medicine, new drugs are generally required to be tested on animals before they are given to patients, and some established treatments, including the blood thinner heparin, are derived from animal tissue. Computer models and laboratory-grown cells are beginning to replace certain tests. Until they can do so reliably, however, abandoning animal use would put human lives at risk.\n\nThe use of animals is no longer essential for clothing or, in prosperous countries, for a healthy diet. For medical research and for communities with no alternative, though, I believe it remains necessary, so the statement is only partly correct.",
"b85":"The internet has placed lectures, textbooks and practice exercises within reach of any child with a connection. I disagree, however, that this makes schools unnecessary, because access to information is only a small part of what an education consists of.\n\nChildren seldom learn well from information alone. A ten-year-old faced with a video on long division needs someone to notice where she has gone wrong, explain the step differently, and insist that she tries again when she would prefer to give up. Online material, by contrast, cannot tell a wrong answer caused by carelessness from one caused by misunderstanding, and it is constantly competing with games and entertainment on the same screen. The experience of school closures during the pandemic was instructive here. In many countries, pupils who were given online lessons at home fell behind, and those from poorer households fell furthest. Motivated older students can study effectively online, but they are the exception among children.\n\nSchool also provides things that have little to do with information. It is where children learn to take turns, work in a group, lose a game gracefully and get along with classmates whose families differ from their own. A child studying alone in a bedroom misses these lessons, however good the website. There are practical functions too. Schools supervise children while parents work, and for some pupils they supply a hot meal and an adult who notices when something is wrong at home. The internet is a valuable addition to all of this, for instance when a class uses a simulation to explore the solar system, though it works best under a teacher's direction.\n\nOnline resources have enriched learning without replacing the institution that organises it. I believe schools remain necessary, since children need guidance, structure and one another's company as well as facts.",
"b86":"International travel once promised streets, buildings and shops unlike anything at home, yet a visitor to Shanghai, Dubai or Toronto now meets the same skyline. This convergence has clear causes, and although something is lost through it, I believe its advantages are greater for the people who live there.\n\nCities resemble one another because they are solving the same problems with the same tools. Land in a centre is scarce, so developers everywhere build upwards rather than outwards, and the tall glass tower is the cheapest way to fit thousands of workers onto a small site. The architects who design these towers work for the same few international firms and repeat one style across continents. Trade is the second reason. International brands such as Starbucks and Zara open branches wherever customers can afford them, so the same cafes and clothes shops fill main streets everywhere.\n\nFor residents, this sameness mostly reflects rising standards. A city that looks like other modern cities usually has reliable electricity, clean water, safe buildings and efficient transport. It is also easier to settle in. A newcomer who has used a metro and a bank in one city can do so in another, which helps firms and migrants alike. The disadvantages are felt chiefly by those who care about heritage and by visitors. Old districts are sometimes demolished to make room for towers, and cannot be rebuilt. Tourists, meanwhile, find fewer surprises than their grandparents did, and a long journey seems less worthwhile when the destination resembles home. Although these losses are real, they are limited, since language, food and customs change more slowly than skylines.\n\nCities have become alike mainly because they have become better places to live. That gain, I believe, outweighs the loss of visual variety, provided that each city preserves the older areas that give it an identity.",
"b87":"Almost any fact about the past, from the dates of a dynasty to a photograph of an ancient vase, can now be found online within seconds. Some conclude that museums have therefore lost their purpose. I disagree, because a museum does far more than store information.\n\nWhat a museum offers above all is the object itself. Standing in front of a bronze drum or a soldier's worn uniform gives a sense of scale, texture and age that a screen cannot reproduce. This physical encounter is often what makes people care about history. Schoolchildren who visit the British Museum, for example, tend to remember the Egyptian mummies long after they have forgotten the textbook chapter. Museums are also where such objects are preserved and studied. The images and descriptions that circulate online exist largely because curators have conserved, dated and interpreted the originals, so the internet depends on these institutions rather than replacing them.\n\nIt is true that the web has taken over part of the museum's former role. A student who simply needs to know when a temple was built has no reason to travel to an exhibition, and people in remote areas can now see collections that were once out of reach. Online material is, however, of uneven quality, and a reader has little means of telling a careful account from a careless one. Museums provide a trusted standard against which other sources can be judged. The two therefore complement each other, with websites spreading knowledge that museums create and verify.\n\nThe internet has changed how people look up historical facts, but it has not removed the need for places that keep and explain the real evidence. In my view the claim is mistaken, although museums should use digital tools to reach a wider public.",
"b88":"For most of history, a person could expect to die within a few miles of where they were born. That expectation has largely disappeared, and I believe the freedom to relocate is a positive change on the whole, despite what it costs families and neighbourhoods.\n\nPeople move more often today mainly because work and education require it. Farming and local trades once tied families to a particular piece of land, whereas modern jobs are concentrated in cities and may shift from one city to another during a career. Universities draw school-leavers away from their home towns, and many do not return. Travel has also become cheap and communication effortless, so that leaving no longer means losing touch. An engineer from Manila working in Singapore, for example, can speak to her parents every evening and fly home for a festival, which makes the decision to go far easier.\n\nThe benefits of this mobility fall mainly to the individual. Someone who can move is able to follow opportunity, escape a declining region and choose a place that suits their temperament, rather than accepting whatever their birthplace offers. A young person from a town with one failing factory, for instance, is no longer bound to share its fate. What suffers is the web of long acquaintance that settled communities provide. Grandparents see little of their grandchildren, neighbours remain strangers, and elderly people may be left without relatives nearby when they need care. Although technology softens this separation, a video call cannot collect a child from school or sit with someone who is ill.\n\nGreater mobility is the product of changing work, wider education and easier travel. In my view its advantages are larger than its drawbacks, because it lets people shape their own lives, though it obliges them to make a deliberate effort to keep family ties alive.",
"b89":"Human knowledge used to sit on shelves, and it now sits largely on servers. In my view, storing information digitally has more advantages than drawbacks, although its weaknesses are serious enough that printed copies should not be abandoned.\n\nWhat digital storage offers above all is the ability to find things. A large library may hold the answer to a question, but locating it can take days of searching through catalogues and indexes. The same material held online can be searched in seconds, by any word it contains, from any place. Storage itself is also cheaper and less limited. The contents of a national library, for instance, occupy kilometres of shelving, yet the same texts fit in a space the size of a cupboard once they are digitised. Copies can be kept in several countries at once, so a fire or flood of the kind that destroyed many historic collections need not mean permanent loss.\n\nDigital storage is, however, more fragile than it looks. A book printed two centuries ago can still be opened and read, while a file saved a few decades ago may require software or equipment that no longer exists. Documents kept on floppy disks, for example, are already beyond the reach of most modern computers. Ownership is the second weakness. Much of what is stored online belongs to private companies, and a website disappears when the organisation behind it closes or stops paying for it. Readers then lose material that no library holds on paper. These risks do not cancel the benefits, but they do mean that preservation cannot be left to chance.\n\nStoring knowledge online has made it easier to keep, copy and search than paper allowed. I believe these gains outweigh the dangers, provided that libraries and governments take responsibility for maintaining digital archives and keep printed copies of what matters most.",
"b90":"A traveller who steps off a plane today may struggle to tell from the view which country they have landed in. The same hotel towers, shopping streets and resort beaches appear on every continent, and I believe that this growing resemblance costs the world more than it gives.\n\nTwo forces lie behind it. Global companies expand by repeating a formula that has already worked, so the same coffee shops, fashion stores and fast-food signs line main streets from Bangkok to Barcelona. Local businesses that cannot match their prices or afford the rising rents close, and the street loses what was particular to it. Tourism reinforces the process. Although some travellers seek out difference, most arrive expecting familiar comforts, and developers respond with concrete hotels, paved promenades and souvenir stalls that look alike wherever they are built.\n\nThis similarity has its uses. Travellers can find their way around easily, and they can rely on a known standard of hygiene and service, which matters greatly to families and older visitors. Local people gain too, since international hotels and shops bring steady jobs and modern facilities to places that had few of either. Most of these benefits could be delivered, though, without erasing what made a place distinctive. What is lost is harder to replace. A fishing village rebuilt as a row of identical resorts, for instance, has given up the character that first attracted visitors, and its traditional buildings and skills seldom return. Money is lost as well. When the hotels and restaurants belong to international chains, much of what tourists spend leaves the area instead of reaching local owners.\n\nIn my view, the convenience of a familiar landscape does not make up for the loss of variety. Places should modernise, but they benefit most when new development respects the scenery and architecture that set them apart.",
"b91":"A lifetime spent in one town and a lifetime divided among several are very different experiences, and each has its attractions. Neither pattern is simply better, since what one gains in belonging the other gains in opportunity.\n\nRemaining in one place offers, above all, stability. A person who grows up, works and grows old in the same community is surrounded by relatives and old friends. Practical help is close at hand, such as a neighbour who minds the children or a cousin who knows of a job. Familiar surroundings also give a strong sense of identity, because a person's history is visible in the streets around them. The weakness of this way of life is that it limits choice. If the local economy depends on a single industry, residents must take the work that exists or go without, and customs may go unquestioned because few outsiders arrive to challenge them. Stability can become stagnation, especially for the young and ambitious.\n\nMoving from place to place reverses these strengths and weaknesses. Those who relocate can pursue better education and employment, and each new city exposes them to different people, food and ways of thinking. A nurse who has worked in three countries, for instance, usually brings broader skills and a more open outlook than one who has known a single hospital. These gains come at a price, however. Friendships are repeatedly interrupted, children change schools and lose classmates, and it is difficult to feel responsible for a neighbourhood one expects to leave. Although modern communication helps people stay in contact, it does not replace relatives who live close enough to help in an emergency.\n\nStaying put provides security and deep relationships but narrows opportunity, while frequent moves widen horizons and weaken roots. Which matters more depends on the person, and on the stage of life they have reached.",
"b92":"Hospitals, banks, employers and retailers now keep detailed electronic records about almost every adult. I believe that this practice does more good than harm, but only where the law limits what organisations may collect and how long they may keep it.\n\nComputerised records allow services to work faster and with fewer mistakes. When a patient arrives at an emergency department, for example, doctors can see at once which medicines he is allergic to and what treatment he has already received. Paper files, kept in one building and often incomplete, could not provide that. The other main benefit comes from analysing many records together. A bank that can compare each payment with a customer's usual spending is able to block a stolen card within minutes. These gains depend on the information being accurate, and an error in a digital record can follow a person for years unless it is easy to correct.\n\nThe dangers arise from the scale on which data is now held. A stolen filing cabinet exposed a few hundred people, whereas a single breach of a company database can reveal the addresses and account details of millions. Criminals use such details to open accounts or make purchases in another person's name. A quieter risk is misuse by the organisation itself, which may sell customer profiles to advertisers or use them in ways the customer did not agree to. Few customers ever learn that this has happened, so they cannot object. Although no system can be made entirely secure, both risks shrink considerably when firms are required to store only what they need and are fined for carelessness.\n\nElectronic records have made healthcare and banking far more efficient than paper allowed. For that reason I think the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, on the condition that regulators hold organisations responsible for the data they keep.",
"b93":"Science has explained a great deal about the physical world, from the structure of the atom to the causes of disease. Yet people continue to turn to novels, songs and paintings, because the arts address aspects of life that measurement and experiment are not designed to reach.\n\nThe first of these is what an experience is like from the inside. A scientist can describe grief in terms of hormones and brain activity. Such an account is accurate, however, without conveying what it feels like to lose a parent. A novel or a piece of music can. Readers of a war novel such as All Quiet on the Western Front come to understand the fear and boredom of a young soldier in a way that casualty figures do not allow. By letting us occupy another person's point of view, the arts widen our sympathy for lives we will not lead ourselves.\n\nThe arts also deal with questions of value, which science deliberately sets aside. Research can tell us how to extend life, but not what makes a life worth living, whether loyalty matters more than honesty, or how we should face death. Plays and poems do not settle such questions either, although they allow us to examine them through particular people in particular situations rather than through abstract rules. A tragedy in which a decent character makes a ruinous choice teaches something about responsibility that a formula could not express. While this kind of understanding cannot be tested or proved, it is the kind on which most personal decisions depend.\n\nScience tells us how the world works, and the arts show us how it is experienced and what in it deserves our care. The two are not rivals, and a society that valued only one of them would understand itself far less well.",
"b94":"Much of the world's scientific work is now funded and carried out by corporations, with public laboratories playing a smaller part than they once did. Commercial investment has clear strengths, but I believe the drawbacks of this imbalance are greater, because profit is a poor guide to what most needs to be studied.\n\nPrivate funding deserves credit for two things. One is speed, since a company can commit large sums as soon as it sees an opportunity, without waiting for a parliament to approve a budget. The other is practical use. Firms have every reason to turn a discovery into something people can buy, and many modern medicines and most consumer electronics reached the public because a business paid for years of costly development. These strengths apply, however, mainly to projects that promise a return within a few years, which is a narrow part of science.\n\nThe weakness of a commercial system lies in what it leaves out. Firms tend to avoid questions with no obvious market, such as diseases that mainly affect poor countries or basic physics whose applications may be decades away. Yet a great deal of later technology rests on exactly this kind of unprofitable early work. Ownership is a further concern. Whereas a university would publish its findings, a company often keeps them secret or patents them, so other scientists cannot build on them and prices remain high.\n\nCorporate laboratories are good at developing products, and they should continue to do so. My view, nonetheless, is that the disadvantages prevail when they do most of the work, since areas with no commercial value are neglected and knowledge that ought to be shared is kept private.",
"b95":"Faced with melting ice caps and polluted oceans, it is easy to assume that ordinary people are powerless and that only states and corporations count. I disagree with the first half of this claim. Governments and big businesses do have far greater influence, but individual behaviour is one of the things that shapes what they do.\n\nThe belief that individuals can do nothing ignores how markets work. Companies produce what customers are willing to buy, and when enough of them change their preferences, firms follow. The growth of electric cars, plant-based food and refillable packaging began with a minority of buyers who were prepared to pay more, and their demand persuaded manufacturers that such products were worth developing. People also act directly on their surroundings. A neighbourhood group that clears a riverbank or plants trees along a street, for instance, improves that place whatever national policy happens to be. Although each such effort is small, the effect across a whole population is considerable.\n\nIt is true, however, that the largest sources of harm lie beyond personal choice. A household can lower its heating and sort its rubbish, but it cannot decide how the national grid generates electricity or what a steelworks releases into the air. Only legislation can impose standards on a whole industry, and only large companies have the capital to replace polluting equipment. This is where the statement has some force. Individual effort that is not supported by regulation tends to remain marginal, because the careful few are outnumbered by those who see no reason to change. The two levels, in other words, depend on each other.\n\nGovernments and corporations hold the most powerful tools, yet they use them when citizens and customers insist. That is why I believe individuals can make a real difference to the environment.",
"b96":"Leaders of countries, companies and teams are frequently praised for being clever. I disagree, however, that intelligence is the quality that matters most in such people, because a leader's task is less to solve problems personally than to bring others to solve them together.\n\nIntelligence certainly has its place. Anyone in charge of a large organisation must absorb complicated information, see the likely consequences of a decision and recognise when an adviser is mistaken. A prime minister who could not follow an economic argument, for instance, would be at the mercy of whoever spoke to her last. Even so, a leader does not need to be the cleverest person in the room. Specialist knowledge can be hired, and a sensible chief executive surrounds himself with engineers, lawyers and accountants who know more than he does. Beyond a certain level, extra brilliance adds little.\n\nWhat cannot be delegated is character, and in particular integrity and the ability to earn trust. People follow willingly only when they believe that the person directing them is honest, listens to them and will share both credit and blame. A manager with these qualities can draw the best from a talented team, whereas a brilliant but arrogant one tends to silence disagreement and so loses the very advice that might prevent a mistake. Trust is also what holds a group together in a crisis, when instructions must be acted on before they can be fully explained. Although character without competence is not enough, it is the scarcer of the two and the harder to replace.\n\nA good leader has to be intelligent enough to understand the issues and to judge the advice received. In my view, though, honesty and the capacity to inspire confidence count for more, since these are what turn a group of able individuals into an effective whole.",
"b97":"Budget airlines and online booking have turned foreign travel from a luxury into something that ordinary families can plan for. I believe this is a positive development overall, since it spreads both income and understanding, although the pressure it places on popular destinations and on the climate needs to be managed.\n\nExperience of other cultures is no longer reserved for the rich, and this is the greatest benefit of cheaper travel. A student who spends two weeks in another country hears a different language, eats unfamiliar food and discovers that people abroad live sensibly by other rules. Such contact tends to reduce prejudice in a way that books and films seldom do. The money travellers spend matters too. In countries such as Thailand and Greece, tourism supports hotels, restaurants, guides and drivers, and provides work in regions that have little other industry. These gains are smaller, admittedly, when visitors stay inside international resorts and meet local people only as staff.\n\nCheap travel has costs that fall on places and on the environment. Cities such as Venice and Barcelona receive so many visitors that streets are crowded, rents rise as flats are let to tourists, and residents feel pushed out of their own neighbourhoods. Flying is also a major source of carbon emissions, and low fares encourage frequent short trips. These problems are serious. They are, however, problems of volume rather than of travel itself, and they can be eased by limiting visitor numbers at fragile sites, taxing flights fairly and improving rail links.\n\nAffordable travel has opened the world to millions of people and brought income to places that need it. Its drawbacks are real but manageable, and for that reason I think the change should be welcomed and regulated, not regretted.",
"b98":"A car, a television and a refrigerator have long been the marks of a comfortable life, and it is often said that people in every country now hope to own all three. If that hope were fulfilled, the planet would bear a heavy cost, yet I believe the benefits to ordinary households would still be greater.\n\nThe advantages are easy to overlook in countries where these goods are taken for granted. The first is better health at home. A fridge keeps food and medicines safe, so a family wastes less and falls ill less often. The second is access to the wider world. A television brings news and education into homes that newspapers may not reach, and a car puts jobs, markets and hospitals within range. A farmer who can drive produce to a town market, for example, is no longer dependent on the price a passing trader offers.\n\nThe drawbacks arise from scale rather than from the goods themselves. Billions of additional appliances would require far more electricity, and much of it is still generated by burning coal and gas, so carbon emissions would climb. Cars are the larger concern, since they burn fuel directly, crowd roads and pollute city air. Cities such as Delhi and Jakarta already suffer from heavy traffic and smog. These are serious problems, although they are not fixed features of ownership. Modern refrigerators use a fraction of the power that older models did, and electric vehicles charged from renewable sources produce far lower emissions. The damage depends on how the goods are made and powered, which governments and manufacturers can change.\n\nThese goods lift living standards in ways that wealthier nations already enjoy, so the disadvantages do not, in my view, outweigh the advantages. The task is to supply them cleanly, not to wish that fewer people wanted them.",
"b99":"Retirement at a fixed age was designed for a time when most work was physical and few people lived long beyond sixty. Neither condition holds in many countries today. I agree that carrying on working is a good idea for those who are able to, as long as it remains a choice.\n\nOlder people themselves stand to benefit most. A job provides structure, purpose and daily contact with others, and those who lose all three at once often find retirement lonelier than they expected. Staying active also seems to help people remain healthy for longer, particularly when the work is part time and not too demanding. Society gains too. Experienced employees continue to pay tax instead of drawing a pension, and they can pass on knowledge that would otherwise leave with them. A retired teacher who works part time as a mentor to new staff, for instance, offers something no training course can supply.\n\nThere are limits to this argument. Heavy manual work, such as construction or farming, wears the body out, and although a lawyer may continue comfortably into her seventies, a bricklayer usually cannot. Any policy on later retirement has to allow for such differences, perhaps by offering lighter duties or earlier pensions in physical trades. The more serious risk is that governments treat longer working lives as a reason to reduce pensions. People who had planned to stop at sixty-five would then have to carry on whether or not they were fit to. Unless the right to retire is protected, an opportunity becomes an obligation.\n\nWorking later in life suits many people and benefits the economy. For that reason, I believe it should be encouraged through flexible and part-time arrangements, but not forced on those whose health or occupation makes it unsuitable.",
"b100":"Species have appeared and vanished throughout the history of life, long before humans existed. From this fact some people conclude that conservation is pointless interference. I disagree, because the extinctions taking place today are for the most part not natural, and because their consequences fall on us as well.\n\nThis reasoning confuses two different processes. Natural extinction is slow, typically resulting from gradual changes in climate or competition, and new species evolve over time to replace those that disappear. The present losses are of another kind. Animals are dying out because their forests are cleared, their rivers polluted and their numbers reduced by hunting, and this is happening too quickly for evolution to compensate. The dodo, for example, did not fail in some natural contest. It was wiped out within a century of sailors arriving on its island. Since people are the cause, it is hard to describe the result as nature taking its course.\n\nSelf-interest points in the same direction. Ecosystems supply services that human societies depend on, including the pollination of crops, clean water and fertile soil, and each of these relies on many interacting species. Removing one may have little visible effect, while removing several can cause a system to fail unpredictably. Wild plants and animals have also been the source of many medicines. A species lost before it is studied takes that possibility with it. This does not mean that every species can or should be saved at any cost. Resources are limited, and where a population is declining for reasons unrelated to human activity, there is a fair case for letting events unfold.\n\nSuch cases are, however, the exception. Because most modern extinctions are caused by people and threaten human welfare, I believe we have both a duty and a practical interest in preventing them.",
"b101":"No government has the means to end poverty and suffering across the globe, and each is elected to serve its own citizens first. I agree that domestic needs should take priority, but I disagree with the conclusion that help for people elsewhere should stop, since a modest share of aid is both affordable and wise.\n\nThe argument for putting one's own population first is strong. A government collects taxes from its citizens and answers to them, and it understands their problems better than those of distant societies. It would be hard to justify funding schools abroad while children at home are taught in crumbling buildings. Voters in that position would rightly ask why their own needs came second. This reasoning shows where most public spending should go. It does not show that the remainder must be zero, particularly for wealthy states whose basic needs are largely met.\n\nLimited assistance to other countries is justified on two grounds. The first is that a small sum can achieve far more in a poor country than at home. The cost of one hospital scanner in Europe, for instance, could pay for vaccines or clean water for a whole district elsewhere. The second is self-interest. Diseases, refugees and the effects of climate change do not stop at borders, so helping a neighbouring region to remain stable and healthy protects one's own people as well. The fact that helping all is impossible is no reason to help none, although aid must be carefully targeted to be worth giving.\n\nAlthough governments are right to devote the bulk of their budgets to their own citizens, I believe well-directed aid to poorer nations should continue alongside this. It relieves great hardship at little cost and serves the donor's long-term interests.",
"b102":"History lessons have limited hours, and curriculum planners must decide how many of them to give to a pupil's own town or region and how many to the wider world. I disagree that local history deserves priority, since each kind of knowledge does a different job and children need both.\n\nLocal history has a strong claim on the early years of schooling. Young children understand the past most easily when they can see and touch it, and a visit to an old market or a nearby battlefield makes events real in a way a textbook cannot. Pupils who have stood in the place where something happened tend to ask questions and to remember the answers. Its weakness is its narrowness. Taught alone, the story of one place can leave pupils with the impression that their community's experience is the measure of all others.\n\nWorld history corrects that impression, and it becomes more necessary as pupils grow older. The forces that shape their lives, including trade, migration, technology and war, have rarely respected borders. A teenager in Vietnam, for instance, cannot make sense of her own country's twentieth century without knowing something about colonial empires and the Cold War. Knowledge of other societies matters for the future as well. Today's pupils will work with foreign colleagues and vote on international questions, and they will judge these better if they know how other nations see the past. World history can become a list of distant dates, admittedly, unless teachers connect it to places and people the class already knows.\n\nThe two are best treated as stages of one subject instead of rivals. In my view neither is more important: local history is the natural starting point, and world history gives it the context without which it cannot be understood.",
"b103":"Health budgets are limited, and every sum spent on campaigns against smoking or obesity is a sum not spent on hospitals. Prevention deserves a larger share than it usually receives, but I disagree that it is more important than treatment. In my view, the two are complementary and neither can be sacrificed to the other.\n\nSpending on prevention is attractive because it addresses illness at its source. Many of the conditions that fill hospital wards, such as heart disease, type 2 diabetes and several cancers, are closely linked to diet, inactivity, alcohol and tobacco. Persuading people to change these habits costs far less than years of medication and surgery. Vaccination programmes and anti-smoking campaigns, for example, have spared large numbers of people from serious disease at modest expense. The limits of this approach should be recognised, though. Its results take decades to appear, and they depend on individuals choosing to act on the advice they are given.\n\nTreatment cannot be given a lower priority, because the people who need it are suffering now. A patient with cancer or a child injured in a road accident cannot be helped by a campaign, however well designed. A great deal of illness is also beyond the reach of prevention. Inherited conditions, infections and the ordinary effects of ageing affect people who have lived carefully all their lives. A health service that reduced care for them in order to fund advice for the healthy would be failing in its most basic duty. While prevention may lower future demand, it does not remove the present need.\n\nPromoting healthy living is a wise investment, and governments should expand it. It should be funded alongside the care of the sick and not at its expense, which is why I disagree that prevention matters more than treatment.",
"b104":"The claim that mass higher education is both unaffordable and pointless has two parts, and they deserve separate answers. I disagree with the first and only partly accept the second. A country can educate a large share of its young people at university, though the benefit depends on what they study and how well they are taught.\n\nOn the question of possibility, the evidence is in front of us. Countries such as South Korea and Canada already send the majority of their school-leavers into some form of higher education, and they have not been bankrupted by it. They manage this by spreading the cost between taxpayers and graduates, and by offering shorter and more applied courses alongside traditional degrees. Expansion on this scale is harder for a poorer nation, which may have to build schools before it builds campuses. That is a matter of timing, however, rather than proof that the goal is out of reach.\n\nWhether such expansion is useful is a harder question. Modern economies need large numbers of nurses, programmers, engineers and teachers, and these are occupations for which school alone no longer provides enough preparation. A hospital cannot be staffed, or a power network maintained, by people who left education at eighteen. The benefit is not automatic, though. If places multiply faster than suitable jobs, graduates end up in work that does not require a degree, and both they and the state have paid for a qualification that brings little return. Wide access is therefore valuable only when courses are of real quality and at least loosely connected to what the country needs.\n\nA high rate of university attendance is clearly achievable, as several nations have shown. I believe it is also worthwhile, provided that governments expand good courses rather than simply counting the number of students enrolled.",
"b105":"In many countries, physics, chemistry and mathematics departments struggle to fill their places while courses in business or media are oversubscribed. This pattern has its roots in school and in the job market, and its consequences are likely to be felt well beyond the universities.\n\nScience is avoided mainly because it is widely seen as difficult and poorly rewarded for the effort it demands. Pupils often meet these subjects at school as a series of formulae to memorise, and many decide by sixteen that they are not scientific. Those who remain then compare their prospects. A physics degree involves long hours in laboratories and no guarantee of well-paid work, whereas a graduate in finance or law, for example, can expect a clearer and more lucrative career. Although some young people choose a subject purely out of interest, most weigh effort against reward, and on that calculation science tends to lose.\n\nThe effects on society would emerge slowly but would be serious. The first is economic. A country that trains too few scientists must either import them or go without, and industries that depend on research, such as pharmaceuticals and energy, may move their laboratories abroad. The well-paid jobs and tax revenue those firms provide go with them. Public debate would suffer too. Decisions about vaccines, climate or food safety require citizens who can judge evidence, and this ability weakens when few adults have studied science beyond a basic level. Rumour then competes with research on equal terms. These outcomes are not inevitable, but each year of delay makes the gap harder to close.\n\nThe decline in science enrolment is therefore the product of discouraging lessons and unattractive careers rather than a lack of talent. Left unaddressed, it threatens a nation's industries and the quality of its public decisions.",
"b106":"The idea that every household ought to possess a car, a television and a refrigerator treats three very different objects as a single measure of progress. Looking at their effects on society as a whole, I believe that the advantages are greater for the fridge and the television, whereas for the car the disadvantages prevail.\n\nThe two household appliances benefit the community as well as their owners. When every home can store food safely, cases of food poisoning fall and less is thrown away, which eases pressure on health services and on farmland. Universal access to television means that public information, such as health campaigns or storm warnings, reaches almost the whole population at once, including people who cannot read. Television has its critics, and hours spent in front of a screen may weaken family conversation and physical fitness. This harm depends on how much people watch, though, rather than on whether they own a set.\n\nThe car is a different matter, because its drawbacks multiply as ownership spreads. One car gives its owner freedom, but a city in which each adult drives has slower journeys, dirtier air and streets given over to parking. Los Angeles, for instance, was built around private vehicles and is known for its congestion in spite of its wide motorways. Public transport also declines when passengers desert it. Bus routes with too few users are cut, which leaves the elderly and the young with fewer ways to travel. In remote rural districts a car is close to a necessity. Elsewhere, society gains more when people share buses, trains and hired vehicles than when each family owns its own.\n\nIn my view, a fridge and a television in every home would leave society better off, while a car for every household would not. Where universal car ownership is concerned, the disadvantages outweigh the advantages.",
"b107":"Parents are a child's first teachers of right and wrong, but they are not the only ones. I agree that schools share this duty, because much of what we call good behaviour can only be learned among other children, although teachers cannot be expected to replace what is missing at home.\n\nSchool is the first place where a child has to get along with people who are not family. At home, a boy or girl is loved without conditions and usually has to share attention with a brother or sister at most. In a classroom of thirty, the same child must wait for a turn, listen to opinions he or she dislikes and accept decisions that seem unfair. These habits cannot be taught in a living room, however conscientious the parents are. They are learned through daily practice, such as working in a group with a classmate one would not have chosen, and teachers are the adults who are present to guide it.\n\nIt would be a mistake, though, to treat this as the school's job alone. Children arrive at five or six with their basic attitudes to honesty and kindness already formed, and they return to the home where those attitudes are either reinforced or undermined. A teacher who insists on politeness will achieve little if rudeness is tolerated at home. Lessons on behaviour also take time from reading and mathematics. Few parents could teach these themselves, so each hour lost matters. Their moral role should therefore be woven into ordinary school life rather than added as a separate subject.\n\nGood conduct is taught most effectively when home and school give the same message. For that reason I believe schools must accept their part in this task, while recognising that it complements the work of parents and cannot stand in for it.",
"b108":"Major tournaments are now surrounded by corporate logos, from the boards around the pitch to the names of the stadiums themselves. Some regard this commercial presence as damaging to sport, but I disagree, because without it most large events could not take place in their present form.\n\nStaging an international competition is expensive, and sponsors bear much of the cost. The Olympic Games, for example, depend on a small group of global companies whose payments help to fund venues, the travel of athletes from poorer nations and worldwide broadcasting. If that income disappeared, the bill would fall on taxpayers in the host country or on spectators through higher ticket prices. Corporate money therefore widens access to sport, both for competitors and for audiences. The same is true on a smaller scale. A city marathon backed by a bank or a sportswear brand can offer low entry fees and proper medical support, which a volunteer committee could not afford alone.\n\nCritics are right that commercial interests sometimes pull events away from their sporting purpose. Matches may be scheduled at times that suit television advertisers rather than players, and a large share of seats at a final can go to corporate guests instead of loyal supporters. Promotion of fast food or betting at an athletic contest also sits uneasily with the values sport is meant to represent. These are faults of regulation, however, not of sponsorship as such. Governing bodies can refuse unsuitable sponsors, as many did with tobacco companies, and can keep control over fixtures and tickets when they negotiate contracts.\n\nCorporate promotion has brought some unwelcome changes, yet they are minor beside the events and opportunities it pays for. On balance, I think its effect on sport is positive as long as organisers, not sponsors, make the sporting decisions.",
"b109":"Senior positions in most companies and public bodies are held by people in their fifties or sixties, and some argue that younger leaders would do the job better. I disagree with this as a general claim, because the qualities that leadership requires are not tied to a particular age.\n\nThe case for younger leaders is strongest in fields that change quickly. Someone in their thirties is likely to be at ease with new technology and less attached to established habits. This is one reason why many software companies, such as Facebook in its early years, were run by people in their twenties. Younger managers may also bring energy and a willingness to take risks that an organisation needs when its market is shifting. These strengths are real, but they are not unique to the young, and plenty of older executives adapt readily.\n\nWhat youth cannot provide is experience, and for most organisations this matters more. A leader who has lived through a recession, a failed product or a serious dispute with staff has learned things that no course teaches, including how to stay calm when plans collapse. Judging people is a similar skill. Deciding whom to promote or trust improves with years of watching colleagues succeed and fail. A hospital or an airline, where mistakes are costly, has good reason to prefer directors who have seen many situations before. Experience can harden into caution, admittedly, and an older board that ignores younger voices will miss opportunities. The sensible answer is a mixed leadership team rather than a wholesale change of generation.\n\nLeaders should be chosen for their judgement, knowledge and openness to new ideas, whatever their date of birth. While younger people deserve more chances than they currently receive, I believe that age alone would not make them better at the job.",
"b110":"A single long-haul flight burns an enormous quantity of fuel, and this fact has led some people to argue that holiday air travel, not everyday driving, should be the target of environmental policy. I disagree with this view to a large extent, because the car remains the greater problem and the easier one to solve.\n\nThe comparison between one flight and one car is misleading. Most people take a long-distance flight rarely, whereas cars are used daily by a large share of the world's adults, so road transport produces considerably more emissions than aviation. Limiting car use is practical as well. A commuter can often switch to a train, a bus or a bicycle, and cities such as Paris have reduced traffic by closing streets and adding cycle lanes. Leaving cars untouched would therefore mean ignoring the largest source of harm, and the one for which alternatives already exist.\n\nDiscouraging tourist flights, by contrast, would bring heavy costs for a smaller gain. Countries such as Thailand and Greece depend on foreign visitors for a large part of their income, and many of the people employed in their hotels and restaurants have few other prospects. There is rarely a substitute for flying across an ocean. A commuter can leave the car at home, whereas a traveller between continents has no train to take. Aviation should not escape responsibility, however. A tax on frequent flyers would be reasonable, since those who fly several times a year cause a disproportionate share of the damage. Such measures should accompany limits on driving rather than replace them.\n\nRestricting leisure flights in place of car use would, in my view, be a mistake. Both forms of travel must become cleaner, but the car, which pollutes more in total and can more easily be replaced, deserves the closer attention.",
"b111":"A single long-haul flight burns an enormous quantity of fuel, and much air travel is for pleasure, so it may seem logical to target holidays abroad and leave motorists alone. I agree that unnecessary flying should be discouraged, but I disagree that this can replace limits on car use.\n\nThere is a strong case for reducing leisure flights. A return journey between continents can add more to a traveller's emissions than months of ordinary driving, and aircraft release their exhaust high in the atmosphere, where its warming effect is greater. Aviation fuel is also lightly taxed in most countries, which keeps fares artificially low and encourages frequent short breaks. Raising these taxes, or charging more to those who fly several times a year, would be a fair way of reflecting the true cost. Such measures would not prevent tourism, but they would prompt people to fly less often, to stay longer, or to take a train where one is available, as is common between Paris and London.\n\nRestricting flights instead of cars would nevertheless miss the larger problem. Flying is an occasional activity for most people, while driving is a daily one, and road traffic as a whole produces considerably more greenhouse gas than aviation. Even a sharp fall in holiday flights would therefore leave most transport emissions untouched. Car use is also the easier habit to change. Unlike long-distance flights, many car journeys have practical alternatives, such as buses, cycling or walking, so limiting them asks less of people. Some trips by car are unavoidable, particularly in rural areas, but that is an argument for careful policy rather than for exemption.\n\nBoth forms of transport pollute, and neither should be protected at the expense of the other. On balance, I think governments should discourage non-essential flights while continuing to restrict cars where good alternatives exist.",
"b112":"Wide differences in earnings are often blamed for the tension and discontent in prosperous societies. I agree that narrowing this gap would make many communities happier, but I do not think it is the single best method, since what people need most is security, not equality for its own sake.\n\nLarge inequalities do damage wellbeing. When a minority lives in visible luxury while others struggle to pay rent, those at the bottom feel that their effort counts for little, and trust between social groups declines. Wealth also buys advantages, such as private schooling and better medical care, that pass from one generation to the next and make the division seem permanent. Countries with relatively even incomes, such as Denmark and Norway, are regularly described as among the most contented in the world. It would be a mistake, however, to assume that equal incomes are the cause, since these societies also offer strong public services and safe streets.\n\nThis points to what matters more. A household is unlikely to be made miserable by the existence of millionaires. It will be, however, by the fear of losing its home or being unable to afford medical treatment. A government that guarantees decent housing, health care and schooling removes these fears, even if some citizens remain far richer than others. Forcing incomes closer together carries a cost of its own. Very heavy taxes, for example, could weaken the incentive to work and invest, and a smaller economy would leave less to spend on the services that give people security.\n\nA fairer distribution of income would ease resentment and is worth pursuing in moderation. In my view, though, a society becomes happier chiefly by ensuring that its poorest members live securely, and narrowing the gap is one means to that end, not the goal itself.",
"b113":"Rapid growth has lifted living standards in many nations, but it has also changed how people relate to their families, neighbours and traditions. Although something of value is lost in this process, I believe the benefits of economic development are greater, because poverty damages social life more severely than prosperity does.\n\nThe advantages are concrete. Growth pays for clean water, hospitals, schools and electricity, which change lives more deeply than almost anything else. A farming family that once lost children to preventable illness and could not afford to keep a daughter in school has gained something that no tradition could compensate for. Higher incomes also bring choice, since people can decide where to live and what work to do instead of inheriting their parents' occupation. Vietnam offers a clear illustration: within a generation, hunger became rare and university became a realistic ambition for ordinary households. These gains are spread unevenly, admittedly, and rural areas often wait longest for them.\n\nWhat is lost is harder to measure. As young people leave villages for jobs in cities, the extended family breaks up, and elderly parents may be left with money but little company. Community life weakens as well. Long working hours leave less time for neighbours, and festivals that once involved a whole village are attended by fewer people each year. These are serious costs. They are not, however, an inevitable price of growth, since wealthy societies such as Japan have kept many customs alive. A country can protect public holidays, support local traditions and limit working hours while its economy continues to expand.\n\nEconomic development weakens some of the bonds that held poorer communities together, and this deserves attention. On balance, I think its advantages outweigh that loss, since health, education and freedom of choice are the foundations on which a good social life can be rebuilt.",
"b114":"In a number of countries, particularly in Africa and parts of South Asia, children under fifteen now form a large and growing share of the population. This places immediate strain on families and public services, while its long-term consequences could be either very favourable or very damaging.\n\nThe present effect is mainly one of pressure. Children consume resources without yet producing any, so a society with many of them must spend heavily on schools, vaccinations and maternity care while relatively few adults are earning and paying tax. Where budgets are small, the result is visible in classrooms. A primary teacher in a fast-growing city such as Lagos may face a class so large that individual attention is impossible. Households feel the same strain, as parents with several young children have less to spend on each one and mothers often remain outside paid work. Older daughters may be kept at home to help, at the expense of their own schooling. Some businesses do benefit, however, since demand rises for baby products, clothing and private tuition.\n\nThe future effects depend on what happens when this generation reaches working age. If these young people have been well educated and can find jobs, the country will have an unusually large labour force supporting comparatively few dependants. A similar situation helped several East Asian economies to grow quickly. Tax revenue would rise and savings would increase. The opposite outcome is equally possible. Millions of school-leavers with poor skills and no work would mean widespread unemployment, migration to overcrowded cities or abroad, and possibly unrest.\n\nA rising number of children currently stretches schools, clinics and family incomes. Whether it later becomes an economic advantage or a source of instability will be decided by how much is invested in those children now.",
"b115":"Doctors routinely tell patients in their sixties and seventies to stay active, yet the advice is more often heard than followed. I believe the reason is rarely laziness. Older people face real obstacles to exercise, and the solution is to make the advice specific, safe and sociable.\n\nFear, combined with uncertainty, is the strongest barrier. Many elderly patients live with stiff joints, poor balance or heart conditions, and they worry that exertion will cause a fall or make their symptoms worse. Although the recommendation is medically sound, a general instruction to exercise more does little to reassure them, because it does not say which activities are safe. The lack of a suitable place is a second reason. Most gyms are designed for the young, with loud music and heavy equipment, and their fees may be high for a pensioner. An older person who exercises alone at home has nobody to encourage them and soon gives up.\n\nAdvice is more likely to be followed when it comes as a clear plan rather than a general recommendation. Doctors could refer patients to a named class or a physiotherapist who sets gentle exercises suited to their condition, and ask about progress at the next appointment. Company matters as much as instruction. In Chinese cities, for instance, groups of retired people gather in parks each morning to practise tai chi or dance, and the pleasure of meeting friends keeps them coming. Local councils elsewhere could support similar walking or swimming groups at little cost. Such schemes will not reach every patient, but they remove the obstacles that stop most of those who are willing.\n\nMost older patients ignore the recommendation because it seems risky, vague and lonely. When exercise is prescribed in detail and organised as a shared local activity, I think many more of them will take it up and continue.",
"b116":"Recruiters increasingly look beyond certificates and ask how a candidate will get on with colleagues and clients. I agree with the thinking behind this trend: in most jobs, the ability to deal with people matters as much as formal knowledge. It cannot, however, take the place of that knowledge where technical competence is essential.\n\nVery little work is now done alone. Projects are shared among teams, and even specialists have to explain their ideas to people outside their field. An engineer who cannot listen to a customer's needs may design the wrong product, however accurate the calculations. In service roles the point is clearer. A hotel receptionist or a sales representative, for example, succeeds through patience, tact and clear speech far more than through anything learnt for an examination. Employers also know that knowledge can be taught on the job within months, whereas a difficult manner is much harder to change. A course can teach a firm's software, but not patience. Hiring for social skills is therefore rational.\n\nThis reasoning has a limit, which lies in the kind of work involved. A hospital would be unwise to appoint a charming surgeon with weak training, and the same applies to pilots, pharmacists and structural engineers. In such professions qualifications are a guarantee of safety, and good communication is valuable only in addition to them. There is also a risk in the selection process. Interviews tend to favour confident talkers, so a firm that gives too much weight to first impressions may overlook a quiet but capable candidate. Social skills should be assessed with the same care as technical ones, through references and trial tasks.\n\nEmployers are right, in my view, to treat social skills as equal to qualifications for the majority of posts. The exception is work in which expertise protects lives, where qualifications must come first.",
"b117":"Scientific discovery now depends on laboratories that are costly to run, so it matters who owns and directs them. I disagree that this work should be left to the state alone. Governments are indispensable for some kinds of research, but excluding companies would slow progress rather than protect the public.\n\nPublic control is most defensible where research offers no prospect of profit. Basic science, such as the study of particles or distant galaxies, may take decades to produce anything that can be sold, and no firm answerable to shareholders will pay for it for long. The same is true of diseases that mainly affect poor countries, whose patients cannot afford expensive drugs. In these fields the state is the only realistic sponsor, and its findings can be published openly rather than kept as commercial secrets. However, this is an argument for public funding where the market fails, not for a public monopoly.\n\nPrivate companies, for their part, are far better at turning knowledge into things people can use. Competition gives them a strong reason to work quickly and to abandon projects that are going nowhere, which officials spending public money have less incentive to do. Much of the progress in computing, mobile phones and new medicines has come from firms that risked their own capital in the hope of a return. Their motives are commercial, of course, and profit can tempt a company to hide unwelcome results. That danger is better met by regulation, for instance by requiring independent safety trials before a drug is approved, than by removing companies from science.\n\nI therefore disagree with the statement in its strong form. In my view, governments should pay for the research that business will not undertake and set the rules for the rest, while leaving companies free to do what they do well.",
"b118":"For millions of households the car is the means by which shopping is done, friends are visited and work is reached. This dependence is understandable, but when driving is unrestricted it damages health and wastes time. Reducing it requires both better alternatives and a fairer price for road use.\n\nThe problems of heavy car use are felt most sharply in towns and cities. The most serious is air pollution. Although modern engines are cleaner than older ones, exhaust fumes still contain gases and fine particles that aggravate asthma and heart disease, and children who live beside busy roads are among the worst affected. Congestion is the other main cost. Each driver adds to the delay of the others, so at busy times the car fails to deliver the speed that made it attractive. A commuter in a city such as Bangkok or Los Angeles can lose hours of every working day to slow traffic.\n\nCar use will fall only when people have a realistic alternative and a reason to choose it. The alternative, for most journeys to work, is frequent and affordable public transport. Where buses and trains run every few minutes and have their own lanes or tracks, they are often quicker than driving, and many commuters switch willingly. The reason to choose it comes from price. Charges for driving into city centres, such as London's congestion charge, persuade people to think before making a journey, and the income can be spent on better bus services. These charges are fair, however, only where good public transport already exists, since otherwise they penalise people who have no other way to travel.\n\nUnlimited driving pollutes the air and slows traffic for all road users. It can be reduced by improving public transport and charging for road use, so that the car becomes one option among several.",
"b119":"The morning paper and the evening bulletin have given way, for a growing number of people, to a phone screen. I believe this shift is positive on balance, since it gives readers more information and more control. It also demands more judgement from them than the old media did.\n\nThe great strength of online news is range. A reader is no longer limited to one newspaper's view or to the handful of stories chosen for a half-hour broadcast. Someone following an election abroad, for example, can read local reporters, compare foreign coverage and consult the original documents within minutes, all at little or no cost. Those with specialist interests, from local planning decisions to scientific discoveries, can find detailed coverage that mass media rarely had space for. News also arrives as it happens. During floods, strikes or public health emergencies, people who learn at once which roads are closed can act in time.\n\nThe weakness is that the internet removes the editor along with the limits. Newspapers and broadcasters, whatever their faults, check facts before publication and can be held responsible for errors, whereas a false story shared on social media may reach millions before anyone corrects it. By then the correction, if it comes, is seen by far fewer people. This risk is serious, though it depends on how people read rather than on the medium itself. Those who go directly to established news organisations online get the reliability of the old system with the speed and breadth of the new.\n\nReading news online offers wider and faster access than print or television could provide, while exposing careless readers to rumour. Because that danger can be managed by choosing sources well, I think the change is a welcome one.",
"b120":"Typing a home address or a card number into a website has become so routine that most people do it without a second thought. I believe this habit is, on balance, a positive development, because the convenience it buys is large and the risks can be kept small by sensible precautions.\n\nSharing these details is what makes modern services possible. A retailer cannot deliver a parcel without an address, and a payment cannot be taken without an account. In return for this information, people save a remarkable amount of time and effort. Someone who lives far from a city, for example, can order medicine, pay a bill and book a train ticket in a few minutes, with none of the journeys these tasks used to involve. Elderly and disabled people gain the most, since errands that were physically difficult can now be done from a chair. These benefits depend on a degree of trust, and they are greatest when the company on the other side is well established and regulated.\n\nThe danger is that information, once given, can travel further than its owner intended. Card numbers are stolen when a company's systems are broken into, and a phone number passed to advertisers leads to a stream of unwanted calls. Victims of fraud can lose money and spend weeks putting matters right. This risk should not be exaggerated, however. Banks usually refund payments their customers did not make, and simple habits such as using strong passwords and avoiding unfamiliar websites prevent a great many problems. Handing a card to a stranger in a restaurant was hardly safer.\n\nPutting private details online carries a real but manageable risk, and in exchange it has made everyday life considerably easier. That is why, in my view, the trend should be welcomed rather than feared.",
"b121":"Low-cost airlines have turned flying from a luxury into something that a student or a pensioner can afford. Whether this should be welcomed or discouraged through higher prices is disputed, and I believe that fares ought to rise moderately to reflect the damage flying causes.\n\nSupporters of cheap flights emphasise what they have made possible for people on ordinary incomes. A family that could once afford only a domestic holiday can now see another country, and migrants working abroad can return home for a wedding or a funeral. Journeys of this kind were once so costly that many families made them only once in several years. To price these travellers out, it is argued, would return flying to the wealthy and leave the rest behind. This is a fair objection, though it applies with less force to those who take several short breaks a year simply because the tickets cost so little.\n\nThe opposing view is that low fares hide the true cost of a flight. Aircraft emit large quantities of carbon, and in many countries aviation fuel is taxed lightly or not at all, so passengers pay far less than the harm they cause. When a flight costs less than the train, people choose it even for journeys such as London to Paris, where rail is almost as fast. A higher price would not stop people flying, but it would make them think about how often they do so. The revenue could also be invested in railways, giving travellers a real alternative rather than just a penalty.\n\nAffordable travel is a real gain, and it should not be reversed altogether. On balance, though, I think air fares should include a tax that reflects their environmental cost, ideally one that rises for those who fly most often.",
"b122":"The move from school to higher education is often described as liberating, yet a large number of first-year students find their studies more difficult than before. I believe this happens chiefly because structure disappears overnight, and that universities and students can each do a good deal to ease the change.\n\nAt school, someone else organises the learner's time. Lessons fill the day, homework is set for a fixed date, and a teacher who knows each pupil by name notices when work is missing. University removes nearly all of this at once. An undergraduate may have only a few lectures a week, an essay due in six weeks and a reading list longer than can be finished, with nobody checking on progress. Many are also living away from home for the first time and have to cook, budget and make new friends while they adjust. Although the academic content is harder too, it is usually this sudden independence, not the material, that causes students to fall behind.\n\nSince the difficulty lies in the transition, that is where help should be concentrated. Universities can build more structure into the first term, for example through weekly small-group tutorials and short assignments that give early feedback before any major deadline. Workshops on planning, note-taking and academic writing are equally valuable, provided that they are offered at the start and not after the first failures. Students have responsibilities of their own. Those who treat their course like a job, with fixed hours in the library, tend to cope far better than those who wait for pressure to motivate them.\n\nUniversity study feels harder mainly because support that pupils took for granted is withdrawn without warning. In my view, a more gradual handover, combined with disciplined routines on the student's side, would prevent most of the early struggles.",
"b123":"Students who did well at school are frequently surprised by how demanding higher education feels, since they are suddenly expected to manage large amounts of work with little guidance. The problem can be reduced considerably if they are better prepared before they arrive and better supported once they have.\n\nPreparation should start in the final years of school. If older pupils are given tasks that resemble university work, such as an extended research project with a distant deadline, they meet independent study while a familiar teacher is still available to advise them. Universities can continue this in the first weeks by explaining plainly what is expected. New undergraduates often lose marks not through lack of ability but because they have not been shown what a good essay or laboratory report looks like. Sample answers and clear marking criteria remove much of this uncertainty, although they cannot replace the practice that only writing several pieces of work provides.\n\nSupport during the course matters just as much, and much of it can come from other students. Many institutions pair each newcomer with a second- or third-year mentor. Because the mentor has recently faced the same difficulties, the advice is practical and newcomers are less embarrassed to ask. Study groups are the other form of peer support. Explaining a concept to a classmate is a reliable test of whether one has understood it, and a weekly meeting sets a deadline for reading that nobody else checks. Such schemes help only those who use them, however, so tutors should contact students who miss classes.\n\nNo measure will make a degree as easy as school, nor should it, since greater difficulty is part of its value. What can be removed is the unnecessary struggle caused by poor preparation and isolation, through earlier practice, clearer expectations and steady support from tutors and peers.",
"b124":"For a large number of employees, the working week leaves little room for the people who matter most to them. The reasons lie in the way jobs are now structured, and the consequences can be seen both inside homes and in the life of communities.\n\nLong hours are partly a matter of economics. Where wages have not kept pace with rents and prices, people take on overtime or a second job to cover their bills. A delivery driver whose rent rises each year, for instance, may work six days a week simply to stay in the same flat. Technology is the other main reason, because it has removed the boundary that once ended the working day. An accountant who answers emails on her phone after dinner is still at work even though she has left the office, and her employer comes to expect a reply. Some people choose long hours out of ambition, yet most have little real power to refuse.\n\nThe first casualty is family life. Parents who arrive home after their children are asleep miss the ordinary moments, such as shared meals and help with homework, through which closeness is built. Over several years the children learn to take their worries to someone else, and the distance becomes hard to close. Society pays as well, because community life depends on people with spare time. Those with no free evenings do not coach youth teams, join local committees or check on elderly neighbours, so these tasks are left undone and neighbourhoods become collections of strangers. Countries with strict limits on working hours suffer less from this, which suggests that the damage is not inevitable.\n\nShortage of free time is therefore produced mainly by financial pressure and constant connection. Its price is paid in weaker families and in neighbourhoods in which fewer people take part.",
"b125":"Each year large numbers of school-leavers exchange a village or small town for a university place or a job in the city. Their departure is painful for the families and communities they leave, but I believe that, taken as a whole, the gains are greater than the losses.\n\nThe benefits to the young people themselves are considerable. Rural areas can seldom offer higher education or a choice of careers, so staying often means accepting farm work or a single local employer whatever one's abilities. In a city, a capable student can train as an engineer, a nurse or a designer and earn a salary to match. Their families share in the gains. A daughter working in an urban hospital, for example, may pay for a younger brother's schooling or for repairs to the family home, which farm income alone could not cover. City life can be lonely and expensive at first, although most newcomers adjust in time.\n\nThe cost is borne mainly by the places they leave. When the most energetic members of a community go, farms lack labour and local shops lose customers, so some villages slowly decline. Ageing parents are the other losers. A couple in their seventies whose children all live in a distant city may have nobody to drive them to a clinic. Although these are real losses, they are not permanent in every case. Some migrants return with savings and skills to open a business or modernise the family farm. The alternative is hardly attractive, since talented young people who remain in a place with no work for them benefit neither themselves nor their neighbours.\n\nOn the whole, I believe the opportunities that cities give to young rural people outweigh the strain their absence causes at home. The balance improves further when governments help rural areas offer reasons to come back.",
"b126":"Homes today contain far more equipment than those of a few decades ago, including washing machines, microwave ovens and air purifiers. I believe this growth in ownership is a positive development on the whole, although it has an environmental cost that should not be ignored.\n\nThe strongest reason to welcome it is the labour that household goods remove. Washing clothes by hand, cooking over a fire and keeping food fresh without a refrigerator once occupied most of the day, and in most families this burden fell on women. Appliances have freed that time for paid work, study and rest. In rural India, for example, a family that acquires a refrigerator and a gas stove no longer has to shop and collect fuel daily, which changes how the whole household lives. Girls who once stayed at home to help with such chores can remain at school instead.\n\nThe picture is less favourable once purchases go beyond what is useful. Many kitchens hold gadgets that were bought on impulse and are seldom used, and cheaper goods are often designed to be replaced rather than repaired. Each item consumes metal, plastic and energy in its manufacture and usually ends as waste, some of it difficult to recycle. A kettle that fails after two years and costs more to mend than to replace is thrown away along with the resources it contains. Although these drawbacks are serious, they stem from careless buying and poor design, not from the goods themselves. Durable, efficient products that are mended when they break deliver the benefits at a fraction of the cost.\n\nHousehold goods have relieved millions of people of exhausting daily work, and that gain seems to me far greater than the harm. The trend should be welcomed, in my view, while consumers and manufacturers are encouraged to favour quality over quantity.",
"b127":"International travel, once limited to the wealthy and to business travellers, is now a normal part of life for a large share of the world's population. This growth has clear economic and practical causes, and I believe that it is, on the whole, a positive development.\n\nThe principal cause is cost. Budget airlines and online booking have lowered the price of a foreign trip to the point where it competes with a holiday at home. Rising incomes in countries such as China and India have meanwhile created a large new group of travellers. Travel has also become easier to arrange. A person with a smartphone can book a room, translate a menu and find the way around an unfamiliar city without help from an agent. Many people who once found a foreign journey too daunting to plan now feel able to attempt one.\n\nThe effects of this movement are mostly beneficial. Tourism brings income and jobs to places with few other industries, and a small island or mountain village may depend on it almost entirely. Travellers gain too, since spending time in another society tends to weaken the prejudices that come from knowing it only through the news. These benefits are not automatic, however. A visitor who stays inside a resort learns little about the country outside it, and destinations that receive more people than they can absorb suffer from crowding, rising rents and damaged coastlines. Whether travel does good therefore depends on how it is managed, through measures such as limits on visitor numbers at fragile sites.\n\nCheaper and simpler journeys explain why so many people now go abroad. Despite the pressure this places on popular destinations, I think the trend benefits both hosts and visitors, as long as growth is kept within what each place can bear.",
"b128":"Creams, treatments and cosmetic procedures that promise younger-looking skin have become a major item of personal spending. The desire behind them is understandable, since youth is widely rewarded, but I believe the scale of this spending is a negative development.\n\nPeople want to look younger mainly because appearance affects how they are treated. In many workplaces a youthful face is associated with energy and adaptability, and older applicants, especially women, may feel that visible ageing counts against them. For someone who fears losing a promotion, an expensive cream can seem a sensible investment. Social life adds to this pressure. Photographs are now shared constantly and often edited, so people compare their own faces with images that no real skin could match. The cosmetics industry reinforces the comparison by presenting wrinkles as a problem to be corrected, not as a normal part of life.\n\nCaring for one's skin is harmless in itself, and protection from the sun, for instance, has real medical value. The trend becomes harmful, however, when large sums are spent on products whose effects are slight or temporary. Money that could go towards savings or experiences is used to chase a result that time will reverse, and more invasive procedures carry medical risks. The wider cost is social. The more people conceal their age, the more unusual an ageing face appears, and the stronger the pressure on others to do the same. A society that treats looking old as a failure makes growing older harder for all its members, despite the fact that ageing is the one experience most of them will share.\n\nPeople pursue a younger appearance because they expect to be judged by it, and they are often right. In my view, though, heavy spending on this goal strengthens the very prejudice that causes it, and is therefore an unwelcome trend.",
"b129":"Each year, large numbers of young people leave home to take a degree in another country, often at great expense to their families. Despite the cost and the loneliness this can involve, I believe the gains are greater for students who go well prepared.\n\nThe chief benefit is academic. An overseas university may offer a stronger department or facilities that the student's own country lacks, so a student of medicine or engineering can train with equipment and teachers unavailable at home. The other main benefit is personal. Studying and living in a second language produces a fluency that classroom lessons rarely achieve, and dealing with landlords, banks and classmates in a foreign culture builds confidence. A Vietnamese graduate who has studied in Melbourne, for instance, returns with working English and an understanding of how Australian companies operate. These gains depend, however, on mixing with local people rather than staying within a circle of compatriots.\n\nThe drawbacks are real and fall mainly in the first year. Fees for international students are high, and living costs in cities such as London or Sydney can exhaust a family's savings. Many students therefore take part-time jobs, which leaves them less time and energy for their courses. Isolation is the other serious drawback. Homesickness and language difficulties affect academic performance, so that an able student may initially get lower marks than at home. These problems are serious, although most of them diminish with time or can be reduced through scholarships, language preparation and the support services that universities now provide.\n\nStudying abroad is expensive and, at first, isolating, and it does not suit every student. On balance, though, I think the academic, linguistic and personal benefits last far longer than the difficulties, and they therefore outweigh them.",
"b130":"Lecture theatres are giving way to video platforms as universities move a growing share of their teaching onto the internet. Although online delivery has clear uses, I believe that replacing classroom teaching with it, as opposed to adding to it, is a negative development.\n\nWhat is lost first is the exchange that makes a class more than a broadcast. In a seminar room, a lecturer can see confusion on faces and stop to explain, and one student's question often clarifies a point for twenty others. Online, most participants keep their cameras off and say nothing, so misunderstandings go unnoticed until the examination. Practical subjects suffer more directly. A chemistry undergraduate, for example, cannot learn to handle laboratory equipment by watching someone else do so on a screen. Although recorded lectures allow students to pause and repeat difficult sections, this advantage can be had without closing the classroom.\n\nThe wider experience of university is also reduced. Much of what graduates remember, and much of what employers value, comes from working with others: arguing in a study group, organising a society, or learning to live alongside people from different backgrounds. A student who completes a degree from a bedroom misses most of this and is more likely to lose motivation and withdraw. The change does widen access, however, for those who could not otherwise attend, such as people with jobs or young children. For them an online course is far better than none, yet that is an argument for offering a choice, not for removing the alternative.\n\nUniversities often adopt online teaching because it is cheaper, not because it teaches better. In my view the trend is harmful where it substitutes for contact between students and lecturers, and welcome only where it supplements that contact.",
"b131":"Messages, emails and video calls now carry a large share of the conversations that once took place in person. It is sometimes argued that people are, as a result, forgetting how to talk to one another directly. I agree with this only to a limited extent, since the danger applies mainly to the young.\n\nFor most adults, the claim overstates the case. People who learned to converse before these devices existed have not lost the skill by sending messages, any more than writing letters made earlier generations unable to speak. Digital contact frequently leads to meetings rather than replacing them. Friends arrange a dinner through a group chat, and colleagues who exchange emails all week still negotiate, persuade and disagree in meeting rooms. What has changed for adults is the amount of practice, not the ability itself. Someone who works remotely for years may become less comfortable in a crowded room, although the discomfort usually fades once regular contact resumes.\n\nThe concern is better founded for children and teenagers. Speaking face to face is learned through repetition, and it involves reading expressions, tolerating silences and responding without time to edit. A teenager who conducts most friendships by text has fewer opportunities to practise these things. A difficult conversation, such as an apology or a disagreement, is easier to type than to say, so the harder skill may go undeveloped. Teachers and employers sometimes notice that young people avoid telephone calls and interviews. This is a risk rather than a certainty, however, and it depends on how much direct contact school and family life continue to provide.\n\nTechnology has altered how often people speak in person more than whether they are able to. I believe the fear is justified only for those growing up with screens, and even for them it can be prevented.",
"b132":"Managing money, buying goods and keeping up with friends increasingly require people to hand over private details to websites. The services themselves are valuable, but I believe the habit of disclosure that has grown up around them is a negative development, because individuals have lost control over what is known about them.\n\nSharing has ceased to be a real choice. Many banks have closed branches, shops offer their lowest prices online, and social events are organised on platforms that demand registration. A person who refuses to supply an address or card number is therefore excluded from much of ordinary life. Each disclosure seems small, yet together they add up. Over several years, a typical adult leaves details with dozens of companies, most of which he will not remember. He cannot know how well each one guards its records, or to whom it passes them, unless he reads lengthy terms that few people have time for.\n\nThe consequences of this loss of control are becoming familiar. When any one of those companies is attacked, card numbers and addresses are sold to criminals, who use them to steal money or to impersonate the victim. Information shared for one purpose is also used for another. Details given to a social network, for example, may shape the advertisements and even the prices that a user is later shown. It is true that online banking and shopping save a great deal of time, and that banks often compensate victims of fraud. These benefits could, however, be delivered with far less data than is currently collected.\n\nConvenience has been bought at the price of privacy, and most people were not asked whether they accepted the bargain. Until companies are required to collect less and protect it better, this trend is, in my view, more harmful than beneficial.",
"b133":"Scientific knowledge lies behind much of what makes a country prosperous, from its hospitals to its factories. Even so, I disagree that the greater part of the education budget should go to science teaching, because development depends on a wider range of abilities than laboratories alone can produce.\n\nThe case for generous science funding is a strong one. Laboratories, equipment and qualified teachers are expensive, and without them a country cannot train the engineers and medical researchers on whom modern industry relies. Nations that invested heavily in technical education, such as Singapore and South Korea, moved from poverty to wealth within a few decades. It is reasonable, then, for science to receive more per student than subjects that need only a classroom and books. Receiving more per student is not the same, however, as receiving most of the total.\n\nProgress requires other kinds of expertise that would wither under such a policy. A new vaccine is of little use without lawyers to draft contracts, managers to run organisations and linguists to deal with foreign partners. A pharmaceutical company, for instance, needs people who can negotiate licences and sell its products abroad as much as it needs chemists. Countries also develop in ways that are not economic. A society's understanding of its own history, its ability to debate public questions and the quality of its arts all depend on the humanities and social sciences. If these were starved of money, a country would gain technicians while losing the people who decide how technology should be used. Balanced funding is slower to show results, although its results are more durable.\n\nScience teaching merits substantial public investment and probably a larger share than it receives in many countries. Giving it most of the budget, though, would in my view weaken the very progress the policy is meant to achieve.",
"b134":"National museums and galleries are often seen as guardians of a country's heritage, and some people conclude that foreign works have little place in them. I agree that a nation's own heritage should come first, but I do not accept that collections should concentrate on it at the expense of art and artefacts from elsewhere.\n\nThe case for giving priority to national material is a strong one. A country's museums are usually the only institutions with the motive and the expertise to look after its past, and if they neglect it, others are unlikely to step in. A foreign gallery may buy a famous painting, but it will rarely pay to conserve local pottery or farm tools. This reasoning justifies a generous share of space and funding for local work. It does not, however, justify excluding everything else.\n\nCultures have seldom developed in isolation, and a purely national display would hide this. French painting of the late nineteenth century, for example, was deeply influenced by Japanese woodblock prints, and a gallery that hung only French pictures could not explain why they look as they do. Foreign works therefore help the public to understand their own tradition. They also serve the many people who cannot afford to travel. For a school pupil or a pensioner, a museum in their own city may be the only chance to see the art of other civilisations. A small regional museum with a limited budget may reasonably specialise, although the same restriction would make little sense for a large national institution.\n\nMuseums should give their own country's heritage the largest share of attention without closing themselves to the rest of the world. On balance, I think a collection that sets national works beside foreign ones serves its public better than one that looks only inward.",
"b135":"Many countries have long treated a university degree as the natural goal for able young people and practical training as a second-best alternative. I agree that this balance should shift and that vocational education deserves a larger share of public spending, though not to the point of starving universities.\n\nThe strongest reason is that the labour market needs skilled practical workers and cannot find enough of them. Electricians, care workers, welders and mechanics are in short supply in many economies, while some graduates struggle to find work that uses their degree. Money spent on well-equipped colleges and apprenticeships therefore meets a real demand. Germany offers a well-known illustration. Its system, in which teenagers divide the week between a company and a college, has long been associated with low youth unemployment. Apprentices there earn a wage while they train and are often kept on by the same firm, so public money leads directly to employment.\n\nThis does not mean that universities are a poor investment. Doctors, teachers, scientists and engineers can only be educated there, and the research carried out in universities produces the technologies that practical workers later install and maintain. A country that cut university budgets sharply would soon depend on others for both. The question is therefore one of proportion. Where universities have expanded for decades while technical colleges have been neglected, additional money should go to the latter. In a different setting, such as a developing nation with too few doctors and teachers, the priority may well be the reverse.\n\nPractical training has been undervalued for too long, and correcting that will require more generous funding. Governments should, I believe, direct a greater proportion of new spending to vocational routes, while maintaining the universities on which advanced skills depend.",
"b136":"Children who would once have spent an afternoon outdoors or with a box of toys are now more likely to spend it with a tablet or a games console. I believe that, as things stand, the disadvantages of this change outweigh the advantages, mainly because of what screen time has pushed aside.\n\nScreens have brought children genuine benefits, which should not be dismissed. Technology gives them access to information that earlier generations could not have imagined. A ten-year-old with an interest in animals, for instance, can watch documentaries, look up any species and even learn to edit her own short films. The other benefit is social. Online games and video calls let children keep in contact with cousins or friends who live far away, and a shy child may join a group more easily there than in a playground. These gains depend on what the child is doing, however. They are greatest when she is making or learning something and much smaller when she is simply watching one video after another.\n\nWhat worries me is what children no longer do. Hours spent sitting indoors are hours not spent running, climbing or cycling. Inactive habits formed early tend to last, and a child who rarely plays outside is more likely to become an overweight teenager. Free play with other children matters just as much. When a group invents a game in a playground, its members have to agree on rules, settle arguments and cope with losing, and no application teaches those lessons as well. Although parents can restore some balance by setting limits, many find this difficult once every classmate has a device.\n\nUnless families deliberately protect time for active and shared play, new technology leaves children's leisure richer in content but poorer in movement and company. The losses, in my view, are therefore greater than the gains.",
"b137":"Most adults check the news at least once a day, and much of what they find is troubling. News matters because people rely on it to make decisions, and bad news dominates for understandable reasons. I believe it should not be replaced by good news, although the balance could be improved.\n\nNews is important chiefly because it connects private lives to public events. A rise in interest rates, a change in school policy or an approaching storm affects what households should do, and people can respond only if they are told. A family that hears a flood warning in time, for instance, can move its belongings and leave. In a democracy the news also allows citizens to judge those who govern them. Voters who follow reports on how public money is spent can reward careful officials and remove wasteful ones at the next election, which official statements alone would not allow.\n\nBad news fills so much space because it is usually more urgent and more unusual than good news. A bridge that collapses is an event, whereas thousands of bridges that remain standing are not. Readers also pay closer attention to threats, and editors competing for an audience know this. Replacing such coverage with cheerful stories would be a mistake, since a public that hears little about corruption, disease or conflict cannot press for remedies. The real weakness of current reporting is not that it covers problems but that it rarely follows them to their resolution. Journalists could report progress, for example falling crime in a city once known for violence, without ignoring what remains wrong.\n\nNews earns its place by telling people what they need to know, and that often means what has gone wrong. I believe it should go on doing so, while giving solutions and improvements the attention they deserve.",
"b138":"Diets in much of the world have shifted from fresh, home-cooked dishes towards processed products rich in sugar, fat and salt, and obesity has spread as a result. People eat badly less from ignorance than because unhealthy food is cheap, heavily promoted and hard to avoid. Better habits will require changing that environment as well as informing individuals.\n\nPoor eating is common mainly because the least healthy food is the easiest to choose. Manufacturers design crisps, biscuits and fizzy drinks to be as appealing as possible, sell them at low prices and display them at every checkout and petrol station. Advertising reinforces the message, particularly to children. Fresh ingredients, by contrast, cost more per meal and require time and skill to prepare. Many young adults have not learned to cook, so a packet or a takeaway feels like the only realistic option after work. Although most people know roughly what a balanced diet looks like, this knowledge has little force when everything around them pulls the other way.\n\nImprovement therefore depends on making good food the convenient choice. Governments can begin by limiting promotion where temptation is strongest. In Britain, for example, restrictions on advertising fast food during children's television and on displaying sweets at supermarket checkouts mean that families meet fewer prompts to buy on impulse. Schools can supply the missing skills. Practical cooking lessons, in which pupils prepare simple meals from fresh ingredients, would show the next generation that a healthy dinner need not cost more or take longer than a takeaway. While individuals remain responsible for what they eat, they choose better when the choice is made easier.\n\nUnhealthy diets are the predictable outcome of cheap processed food, constant marketing and declining cooking skills. Restrictions on promotion and early practical education offer the best prospect of changing what ends up on the plate.",
"b139":"Obtaining a credit card now takes little more than a few minutes and an online form. Although easy credit leads some borrowers into serious difficulty, I believe that the benefits of these cards are greater than the drawbacks for most people who hold them.\n\nThe chief advantage of a credit card is flexibility. It allows a household to meet an unexpected expense, such as a car repair or an urgent flight, without waiting for the next salary. The cost of a large purchase can also be spread over several months. Security is the other main benefit. A stolen wallet of cash is simply lost, whereas a stolen card can be cancelled and the bank usually refunds money taken by fraud. This protection makes cards particularly useful for online shopping and travel abroad. Provided that the balance is cleared each month, these services cost nothing.\n\nThe danger arises when the balance is not cleared. Interest on card debt is far higher than on most loans, and a cardholder who makes only the minimum payment can find that the amount owed hardly falls. A debt left for a year or two can therefore grow well beyond the original purchase. Cards also encourage overspending. Because the money does not feel real, some people buy more than they would with notes in their hand. A young worker with several cards may accumulate debts that take years to repay. This harm is, however, largely the result of cards being issued without proper checks, and it could be reduced if lenders were obliged to confirm that applicants can afford their limit.\n\nUsed with discipline, a credit card is a convenient and secure tool, while misused it can be ruinous. On balance, I think the advantages prevail, since the problems fall on a minority and can be limited by stricter lending rules.",
"b140":"Waiting has become unusual. A film begins the moment it is chosen, a question is answered in seconds, and a parcel ordered at breakfast may arrive by evening. This appetite for immediacy has clear origins in technology, and I believe that, despite its convenience, it is doing more harm than good.\n\nPeople expect instant results mainly because they have been taught to. Once one company delivers overnight or replies within a minute, its competitors must match it, and customers quickly treat the faster standard as normal. Smartphones have extended this expectation to every hour of the day, since the device that makes a request is in the hand already. Busier lives add to the pressure. Someone who works long hours and commutes in heavy traffic has little spare time, and therefore values any service that saves a few minutes.\n\nMuch of this speed is a real improvement, and few people miss queuing at a bank or waiting days for a document. The harm lies in what the habit does when it spreads beyond routine tasks. Skills such as playing an instrument or speaking a foreign language cannot be acquired quickly, and people used to immediate rewards tend to give up when progress is slow. A learner who expects results within a week may abandon the piano long before the months of practice begin to pay off. There is a human cost as well. Same-day delivery, for instance, depends on drivers and warehouse staff working under intense pressure, although the customer rarely sees it. Convenience for one person is frequently paid for by another.\n\nThe demand for instant results has grown out of competition between businesses, mobile technology and crowded schedules. I welcome it for simple transactions, but in my view it becomes a negative force wherever patience or other people's welfare is sacrificed to speed.",
"b141":"The bicycle was once the ordinary way to get to school, to work and to the shops, but in many places it has become something people use at weekends, if at all. This decline has less to do with the bicycle itself than with the roads and distances riders now face, and it can be reversed by making cycling safe and convenient again.\n\nPeople cycle less mainly because the conditions for it have worsened. As car ownership has grown, roads have become faster and more crowded, and riding among lorries and impatient drivers feels dangerous, particularly to parents deciding how their children should travel. Cities have also spread outwards. A worker whose office is on the far side of a large city cannot reasonably cycle there, and motorbikes, cars and cheap taxis booked by phone offer speed and shelter from the weather. Affordable cars are part of the explanation, although fear of traffic is what keeps many willing cyclists off the road.\n\nEncouraging people back onto bicycles begins with safety. Lanes separated from traffic by a kerb, and not merely by a painted line, give nervous riders confidence, and lower speed limits on residential streets help further. Cities such as Copenhagen and Amsterdam have built networks of this kind, and people of all ages cycle there daily. Distance is the second obstacle to remove. Secure parking at stations allows cycling to be combined with rail for longer journeys, while electric bicycles extend the practical range and flatten hills. Subsidies for them may therefore attract older or less fit riders who would not otherwise consider cycling. Publicity alone achieves little unless these physical changes come first.\n\nCycling has declined because roads became hostile and journeys longer. People return to it where they are given protected space and a practical way to cover greater distances.",
"b142":"Passengers in many countries have grown used to an annual announcement that tickets for buses and trains will cost more than the year before. I believe the main explanation is a cycle in which higher fares and falling passenger numbers feed each other, and that it can be broken only by making travel cheaper and better.\n\nA transport network has high fixed costs. A bus needs a driver, fuel and maintenance whether it carries few passengers or many, so when passengers are lost, the same bill is divided among fewer of them. Operators respond by raising fares, which persuades more travellers to buy a car or a motorbike, and the next increase follows. Growing car ownership and the recent shift to home working have reduced the number of regular commuters, making this cycle harder to escape. General inflation in wages and energy adds to the pressure, although it does not explain why fares so often rise faster than other prices.\n\nBreaking the cycle means winning passengers back, and the surest way is a simple, low price. Germany, for example, introduced a monthly ticket valid on local and regional services nationwide, and many people who had rarely used public transport began to do so. Such a scheme requires public money at first, but fuller vehicles bring in more revenue per journey and fewer cars reduce spending on roads. Reliability matters as much as price. Bus lanes and priority at traffic lights shorten journeys and allow one vehicle to complete more trips a day, which lowers the cost of each. Cheap tickets will not fill buses, however, unless the service is frequent enough to depend on.\n\nFares keep rising, in my view, because networks are losing the passengers who pay for them. Governments can reverse this by funding lower, simpler tickets and faster services until numbers recover.",
"b143":"Young children have a naturally short attention span, yet many teachers feel that it has become shorter still. Two causes stand out, the fast-paced entertainment children consume at home and the way the school day itself is organised, and each points towards a practical remedy.\n\nMuch of the problem begins before a child enters the classroom. Cartoons and video games change scene every few seconds and reward the viewer instantly, so a child who spends hours with them grows used to a level of stimulation that no teacher can match. Reading a page or listening to a story then feels slow by comparison. The organisation of the school day is a second cause. In many systems, pupils of seven or eight are expected to sit still through long lessons with few breaks, and those who cannot are described as inattentive when they are simply restless.\n\nEach cause has a practical remedy. At home, parents can set firm limits on screen time and fill the gap with activities that train patience, such as reading together, drawing or playing a board game to the end. A child who regularly finishes a long task learns that effort is rewarded, though more slowly than on a screen. At school, lessons for this age group can be divided into short tasks that alternate listening with movement, and regular outdoor play allows children to return to their desks calmer. While such changes will not help the small number of children with a medical condition affecting attention, who need specialist support, they would benefit most of the class.\n\nChildren's concentration has weakened largely because their leisure trains them to expect constant novelty and their classrooms ask for long periods of stillness. Less screen time at home and shorter, more active lessons at school would address both causes.",
"b144":"Screens occupy a large share of many children's free time, and it is often claimed that this weakens their ability to think. I agree only to a limited extent, since the harm depends far more on how long children watch or play, and on what, than on the screen itself.\n\nThere are genuine grounds for concern when viewing is heavy and passive. A child who spends every evening in front of cartoons is not reading, building, drawing or talking, and these are the activities through which vocabulary and imagination develop. Fast-moving programmes and games also deliver constant rewards, so a page of text or a teacher's explanation can seem dull by comparison. Teachers frequently report that pupils who play late into the night find it hard to concentrate the next morning. Much of this damage, though, comes from lost sleep and lost reading time rather than from anything the screen does to the brain directly.\n\nIt would be wrong, however, to treat all screen time as mentally empty. Well-made documentaries introduce children to animals, countries and historical events they would otherwise not encounter. A programme about the deep ocean can prompt questions and further reading that a textbook page rarely does. Many games demand planning and problem-solving. A child playing a building game such as Minecraft, for example, has to manage resources, design structures and often cooperate with other players. These gains should not be exaggerated, as skill at a game does not necessarily transfer to schoolwork, but they show that the effect of screens is not uniformly negative.\n\nTelevision and computer games can hold back a child's mental development when they crowd out sleep, reading and conversation. In moderate amounts and with suitable content they do little harm and some good, which is why I believe the claim is only partly true.",
"b145":"Typing symptoms into a search engine has become, for many people, the first response to feeling unwell. The habit is easy to explain, since online advice is instant and free. I believe it is a negative development when it replaces a consultation, though a useful one when it prepares for it.\n\nPeople turn to the Internet mainly because it is so much easier than seeing a doctor. An appointment may involve days of waiting, time off work and, in many countries, a fee, whereas a website answers within seconds. For a minor complaint such as a sore throat, the effort of a visit can seem out of proportion to the problem. Privacy is another attraction. Someone troubled by a condition they find embarrassing, such as a skin complaint or a mental health problem, may prefer to read about it alone before speaking to anyone. Searching at home involves no waiting room and no need to describe the symptoms aloud.\n\nAs a substitute for professional care, this habit does more harm than good. A website cannot examine a patient, take a history or order a test, so it can only list possibilities. A person with a persistent headache, for instance, will find explanations ranging from dehydration to a brain tumour. Some readers are falsely reassured and delay seeking help for a serious illness, while others are frightened without cause. Self-treatment with medicines bought on the strength of such reading carries further risks. Reliable sites do have a place, however, in helping people manage minor complaints and recognise when a visit to the doctor is needed.\n\nOnline medical advice is popular because it is quick, private and costs nothing. I think it is beneficial as a first source of information but harmful as a replacement for diagnosis, so the growing tendency to bypass doctors is a worrying one.",
"b146":"It is sometimes claimed that people today are so particular about their work that they would sooner have no job at all than one they dislike. I disagree with this as a general description. It is true of a small group who can afford to wait, but most people still take whatever work pays the bills.\n\nThe claim holds for those who have something to fall back on. A graduate whose parents live in the capital, for example, can stay in the family home for a year and turn down offers that do not match her degree. In countries with generous unemployment benefits, a similar choice is open to people who have recently lost a job. For this group, waiting is often a sensible decision rather than a sign of laziness, since accepting an unsuitable post can make it harder to find a better one later. Their position is nevertheless a privileged one, and it lasts only as long as the savings or the family support does.\n\nFor the majority, enjoyment is not the deciding factor. Rent, food and children's school fees have to be paid, and few households can go long without a wage. This is why millions of people clean offices at night, drive delivery vans or work on production lines, jobs that are rarely chosen for pleasure. Many of them would change occupation if they could, yet they do not resign in order to look. Although younger workers do appear more willing to leave a post that makes them unhappy, they usually move to another employer, not into unemployment.\n\nThe idea that many people now prefer idleness to unpleasant work therefore seems to me exaggerated. In my view, it describes a fortunate minority, while for most workers the need for an income still outweighs the wish for a job they enjoy.",
"b147":"Parents in their late thirties or forties were once the exception, yet in many societies they are becoming the norm. The reasons are mostly economic and medical, and in my view the gains from waiting are greater than the drawbacks, though only up to a certain age.\n\nTwo developments explain the trend. One is insecurity in early adult life. Young workers frequently move between short contracts and rented flats, and a couple in this position, for example two teachers on temporary posts, will hesitate to add a child to an uncertain budget. The other development is medical. Treatments such as IVF have persuaded many people that conception can safely be left until later, even though these procedures fail more often than is commonly assumed.\n\nWaiting brings two clear advantages. Parents who are established in their careers have more money and a secure home, so the child is less exposed to financial stress. They are usually more settled as people too, and less likely to resent the freedom they have given up. Against these stand the drawbacks of age. Conception becomes harder and complications in pregnancy are more common, which can mean years of costly treatment or no child at all. Older parents also have less energy, and a father who is sixty when his child leaves school may not live to help with the next generation. For those who start in their early or mid-thirties these costs remain modest, whereas the benefits of a stable home last for years.\n\nInsecure work and confidence in fertility treatment have both pushed parenthood later. On balance, I think this serves families well, provided that the wait is not prolonged to the point where having a child becomes unlikely.",
"b148":"Professional sport has grown into a global industry in which leading players, clubs and broadcasters earn very large sums. In my view, this has been a positive development for sport itself, because money has raised standards, but it remains so only while competitions stay open and honest.\n\nCommercial success has improved the quality of what happens on the field. High earnings allow athletes to train full time from a young age, supported by specialist coaches, doctors and analysts whom amateur clubs could not afford. The result is faster, more skilful play than earlier generations saw. Investment also spreads the game. Television income has paid for modern stadiums and youth academies, and the recent growth of women's football, for example, owes much to broadcasters and sponsors who decided it was worth backing. These gains are greatest, admittedly, in the few sports that draw large audiences.\n\nThe danger is that money begins to decide results. In European football, the wealthiest clubs can buy the best players year after year, so the same handful of teams win most titles. A league whose winner can be guessed in advance loses much of the tension that draws spectators. Honesty is threatened as well. Where so much depends on winning, the temptation to cheat through banned drugs or arranged matches grows stronger, and each scandal makes supporters doubt the results they see. None of this is an inevitable consequence of wealth, however. Leagues that share broadcasting income evenly and limit spending, as American football does, remain both rich and competitive.\n\nBig business has given sport better athletes, better facilities and wider reach, and for those reasons I think it is a positive change. Its benefits will last, though, only if governing bodies prevent wealth from deciding who wins.",
"b149":"Crises, crimes and disasters occupy far more of the news than steady improvements in health, safety or living standards. I agree that this imbalance does harm, to individuals more clearly than to society, though the answer is not for the media to look away from problems.\n\nFor the individual, a constant diet of alarming reports distorts the sense of risk. Violent crime, for example, has fallen over several decades in many countries, yet people who follow the news closely often believe their streets are becoming more dangerous. This gap between impression and reality has practical effects. Parents who fear abduction may stop their children walking to school, and elderly viewers may become reluctant to go out after dark. Rolling coverage of distant emergencies can also produce a persistent anxiety about events the viewer has no power to influence. The harm is greatest for those who check headlines many times a day, while an occasional reader is far less affected.\n\nThe effect on society is more mixed. Publicity given to failures is often what gets them fixed, as when reports of unsafe buildings or polluted rivers force authorities to act, and in this respect critical coverage serves the public. Damage arises, however, when negative stories are all that people hear. Citizens who are told only of scandals may conclude that every institution is corrupt and that voting or volunteering is pointless. Such cynicism weakens the cooperation on which public life depends. A fair account would report what is working as well as what is not, because knowledge of past successes is what convinces people that present problems can be solved.\n\nReporting on problems is a necessary function of the press, and I would not wish it reduced. I agree, however, that the present emphasis leaves many people more fearful and distrustful than the facts justify.",
"b150":"Actors, singers and footballers occupy a large place in the imagination of teenagers, including some stars whose public behaviour is far from admirable. I believe this admiration is a negative development when it attaches to fame itself, although the picture is not as bleak as the statement suggests.\n\nThe concern is that admiration leads to imitation. Adolescents are working out who they want to be, and they borrow attitudes from the people they look up to. When a celebrated player abuses a referee, or a singer displays extravagant wealth, followers may come to see aggression and excess as marks of success. Social media has made this influence more direct, since fans now see a star's daily life and opinions, not just performances. There is a quieter cost as well. A teenager who measures achievement by fame may undervalue the nurses, engineers and teachers whose work is less visible, and may neglect study for an ambition that very few achieve.\n\nIt would be wrong, however, to treat all such admiration as harmful. Many well-known figures set an excellent example, and what young people admire in them is often discipline and skill. The footballer Marcus Rashford, for instance, used his reputation to campaign for free school meals for children from poor families, and athletes in general show what years of patient training can achieve. Young fans are also more discerning than adults assume. Most can enjoy a musician's songs while disapproving of the same person's conduct. Whether admiration does harm depends largely on parents and teachers, who can help teenagers separate talent from character.\n\nAdmiring a star for dedication or generosity can inspire young people, whereas admiring one simply for being famous teaches them little of value. Since the second kind is common, the trend is in my view more negative than positive.",
"b151":"Starting school hands a large part of a child's day to adults outside the family for the first time. I agree that teachers then become the main influence on how a child's mind develops, but I disagree that the same is true of social development, where parents continue to matter most.\n\nIn intellectual terms, the teacher's influence quickly overtakes that of the home. Reading, writing and arithmetic are taught systematically by someone trained to do so, and few parents have the time or the skill to match this. A good teacher also shapes how a child feels about learning. A pupil who is praised for a clever question in the first years of primary school may come to see himself or herself as capable, with effects that last for decades. Parents still contribute, particularly by reading with their children and showing interest in homework, although the direction and pace of learning are now set in the classroom.\n\nSocial development is a different matter. Teachers certainly establish rules about sharing, taking turns and resolving quarrels, and for some children these are stricter than the rules at home. Even so, a teacher divides attention among a whole class and changes each year, whereas parents remain constant. Children absorb their deepest habits by imitation, such as how to treat a waiter, how to react to disappointment or how to speak about people who are different. They copy these from the adults they love and watch most closely. A child told to be polite at school but who hears rudeness at home is likely to follow the example.\n\nTeachers do become the stronger force once formal lessons begin, but only in one of the two areas mentioned. Parents remain, in my view, the more important guide to how children behave towards others, however much time is spent at school.",
"b152":"Aviation and motoring are frequently compared when governments decide where to cut emissions, and one proposal is to leave drivers alone while making holiday flights less attractive. I agree that leisure flying deserves to be discouraged, although I do not accept that this removes the need for some limits on cars.\n\nThere are good reasons to look at tourist flights first. A long-distance flight burns far more fuel than a car uses in months of driving, and it is, by definition, a journey the passenger could do without. Driving is different in kind. A rural family may need a car to reach work, school and the doctor, so restricting its use imposes real hardship. Technology points in the same direction. Electric cars are already on sale and becoming cheaper, whereas no clean aircraft capable of crossing an ocean exists yet, so reduced demand is the only way to cut aviation emissions soon. A levy that rises with each additional flight a person takes in a year, for example, would fall on frequent travellers while sparing the family that goes abroad once.\n\nThe proposal goes too far, however, in treating cars as if they needed no restraint. Much car use is no more essential than a holiday. Short trips to the shops or the school gate could often be made on foot, and in dense cities these journeys cause congestion and dirty air that no measure on aviation will improve. Modest controls, such as charges for entering a city centre, are justified on those grounds alone. Limits of this kind differ from penalising drivers who have no alternative, which would be unfair.\n\nGovernments are therefore right, in my view, to discourage non-essential flights more firmly than everyday driving. This priority should sit alongside sensible restrictions on urban car use rather than take their place.",
"b153":"Places that were once reached only by determined travellers, from Himalayan villages to Arctic settlements, now receive groups on packaged itineraries. In my view, this trend is broadly positive for the people who live there but harmful to their surroundings, unless visitor numbers are firmly limited.\n\nFor local residents, organised tourism brings income that few other activities in an isolated region can offer. Farming or herding in such places tends to provide a bare living, whereas guiding, cooking and renting out rooms pay in cash and allow families to keep their children at school. A village on a trekking route in Nepal, for instance, can earn enough from its lodges to pay for a clinic. These gains depend on who runs the tour, however. When a company based in a distant city supplies the guides, the transport and even the food, very little of the money stays in the community. Residents are then left with the crowds but not the profit.\n\nThe effect on the environment is harder to defend. Remote areas are usually remote because they are fragile: thin mountain soils, coral reefs and tundra recover slowly from damage, and they have no systems for dealing with waste. A large group of visitors produces more rubbish and uses more water and firewood than the land can readily supply. The roads and airstrips built to bring them in also open the area to further development. Although a well-run tour can teach visitors to respect what they see, education does not remove the physical pressure of their presence.\n\nOrganised travel to isolated regions is therefore a mixed development. On balance, I believe it improves the lives of local people where they control the business themselves, while the damage to the natural environment can be contained only by strict limits on numbers.",
"b154":"In school systems where success is measured almost entirely by examination results, children can spend most of their waking hours at a desk. I agree that parents in such settings should shift some of that time towards exercise, although the right balance varies from child to child.\n\nThe main argument for more physical activity is that long hours of study bring diminishing returns. A pupil in Seoul or Hanoi, for instance, who has already sat through a full school day and two hours of homework absorbs little from a third hour of evening classes. An hour of football or swimming, by contrast, improves sleep, mood and the ability to concentrate the following day, so the remaining study time is used better. Health matters too. Children who sit for most of the day are more likely to gain weight, and exercise is a habit best formed early. Parents are well placed to make this change because they control the family timetable and set an example through their own behaviour.\n\nThis advice should not be applied to every child, however. Some young people already do plenty of sport and too little reading, and encouraging them to study less would only widen the gap between them and their classmates. Others are approaching examinations that will decide their university place, and a temporary increase in revision is reasonable. Academic knowledge remains the basis of most careers, so physical activity ought to support learning rather than compete with it. The aim is a sensible daily routine, one in which neither books nor sport crowds out the other.\n\nChildren who are overloaded with schoolwork would gain in health, happiness and even in results if some of that time were spent moving. For them I believe the statement is sound, but it is a correction for excess, not a rule for all families.",
"b155":"It is tempting to imagine the past as a simpler and happier time. Judged by the things that most affect daily existence, however, I agree that people alive today enjoy a far better quality of life than their ancestors did. This is true even though progress has not reached all parts of the world equally.\n\nThe most persuasive evidence is in health. For most of history, parents expected to bury some of their children, and an infected wound or a difficult birth could kill a healthy adult within days. Vaccines, antibiotics, clean water and anaesthetics have removed much of that fear. A child born with a heart defect, for example, can now be operated on and grow up normally, whereas a century ago nothing could have been done. People not only live decades longer but spend more of those years free from pain, which is surely the foundation of any good life.\n\nFreedom of choice has expanded alongside health. In earlier centuries a person's occupation, religion and marriage were largely decided by birth, and women in particular had little say over their own futures. Education is now widely available, and many more people can choose their work, their partner and where they live. These gains are admittedly uneven. In regions affected by war or extreme poverty, daily life may differ little from that of the distant past, and wealthy societies face problems of their own, such as loneliness and long working hours. Such difficulties are real, yet few of those who complain of them would exchange places with a medieval farmer.\n\nLife in this century is longer, healthier and freer than at any earlier time for which we have records. For that reason I agree with the statement, while recognising that the task of extending these benefits to the poorest countries remains unfinished.",
"b156":"Retailers frequently divide their customers by generation, on the assumption that the young and the old shop in different ways. I disagree that age is the strongest influence, since what people buy, and where, is determined above all by how much money they have.\n\nIncome sets the boundaries within which every other preference operates. A person on a low wage, whether twenty-five or sixty-five, compares prices carefully, waits for discounts and buys supermarket own brands, while a wealthy shopper of either age can choose on quality or convenience. Two students illustrate the point. One whose parents pay her rent may order clothes online each month, whereas a classmate supporting himself through part-time work buys second-hand and cooks at home. They belong to the same generation and share the same tastes, yet their shopping has little in common. Age can tell a retailer what someone would like to buy, but income decides what actually reaches the basket.\n\nAge does have a visible effect on certain habits. Younger consumers are more comfortable buying through phone applications and are more likely to follow fashion, for example. Many older people, by contrast, prefer to examine goods in a shop and remain loyal to brands they have trusted for years. These differences are real, although they are narrowing as people of all ages become used to shopping online. They also concern the method of buying more than its substance. A pensioner and a teenager may use different channels, but if their budgets are similar, the value of what they purchase, and the care they take over it, will be much the same.\n\nGenerational differences in shopping are easy to observe, which is perhaps why they are overestimated. In my view, the size of a person's income shapes their habits far more deeply than the year in which they were born.",
"b157":"It is common to speak of teenage shoppers or elderly shoppers as if each group behaved alike. I only partly agree that age is the main influence on shopping, because in my view it is a person's household circumstances, which age merely hints at, that matter most.\n\nThere is no doubt that shopping changes over a lifetime. Teenagers spend on clothes, games and snacks, adults in middle age on housing, cars and insurance, and retired people on health and comfort. Marketing departments rely on these patterns, and they are accurate as broad averages. They are accurate, however, because people of a similar age tend to be in similar situations, not because age itself produces a taste for particular goods. The typical forty-year-old buys school uniforms because many forty-year-olds have school-age children. Remove the children, and the purchases disappear with them.\n\nCircumstances explain habits that age cannot. Consider two women of thirty-five, one living alone in a city flat and the other raising three children in a suburb. The first may shop little and often, eat out and spend freely on travel, while the second plans a weekly trip to a large supermarket, buys in bulk and watches for offers. Their age is identical, yet a retailer would learn almost nothing from it. Where people live has a similar effect. Someone in a rural area with few shops, for example, is likely to order online at seventy as readily as a student would. Age remains a useful first guess when nothing else is known about a customer, although it is no more than that.\n\nAge groups differ in their shopping mainly because they differ in their responsibilities and surroundings. I believe these underlying circumstances are the stronger influence, with age serving as a convenient but imprecise guide to them.",
"b158":"Countries that have industrialised, from Britain in the nineteenth century to China in recent decades, have tended to pay for their prosperity with dirty air and poisoned rivers. I agree that some environmental damage accompanies economic growth, but I disagree that it is unavoidable, because its scale depends largely on the choices a country makes.\n\nThe early stages of development are certainly hard on the environment. A poor country that wants to raise living standards quickly needs factories, power stations, roads and housing, and the cheapest way to provide them is usually the dirtiest. Coal is often the quickest source of power, and a government whose citizens lack electricity will put supply before clean air. Rising incomes add to the pressure, as families buy cars, eat more meat and throw more away. Repeated across millions of households, these habits mean more exhaust fumes, cleared land and landfill. To this extent, a developing economy is bound to leave some mark.\n\nThe mark need not be as deep as it was in the past, however. Countries developing today can adopt technologies that did not exist when Europe industrialised. Costa Rica, for example, generates almost all of its electricity from rivers, volcanic heat and wind, and has expanded its forests while its economy has grown. Regulation matters as much as technology: where factories must treat their waste water and vehicles must meet emission standards, the same output is produced with far less harm. Such measures raise costs in the short term, which is why they require political will rather than merely wealth.\n\nEnvironmental damage is a common companion of development, and a degree of it may be the price of escaping poverty. How much is suffered is a matter of policy, though, and I believe that countries which plan carefully can become richer without ruining their surroundings.",
"b159":"Sending offenders to jail remains the standard answer to crime almost everywhere, and it is often contrasted with investment in education. I agree that education prevents more crime than prison does, but I do not accept that one can simply be substituted for the other, because they do different jobs.\n\nPrison has one clear strength, which is that it stops a particular person from offending for as long as he is held. For the small number of people who commit violent or repeated crimes, this protection is worth its considerable cost, and no classroom can provide it. As a general solution, however, imprisonment performs poorly. The threat of a sentence deters few people who act in anger, under the influence of drugs or in the belief that they will not be caught. Those who serve time often come out with fewer prospects than before, and many return to crime.\n\nEducation succeeds where prison fails because it changes what people are able to do with their lives. This applies not only to schools but also to adults who have already offended. An inmate who learns to read fluently or gains a recognised qualification, such as a certificate in bricklaying or catering, leaves with a realistic alternative to crime. Outside prison, good vocational colleges and second-chance courses give young adults without qualifications a way into steady work, and people with steady work have strong reasons to stay within the law. The benefit is slow to arrive and depends on jobs being available afterwards, yet it lasts, whereas the effect of a sentence ends at the prison gate.\n\nEducation is the better long-term investment, and on that point I agree with the statement. It is most effective, though, when it is provided alongside imprisonment and within it, not as a replacement for the protection that prisons give.",
"b160":"A leading footballer or film star can earn more in a week than a nurse or teacher earns in several years, which strikes many people as unjust. I agree that such professionals deserve considerably better pay, but I do not think their salaries can sensibly be made to exceed those of celebrities.\n\nThe moral argument is easy to accept. Doctors and nurses keep people alive, and teachers shape the knowledge and character of each new generation. Their work requires years of training and carries heavy responsibility, yet in many countries their wages are low enough to drive capable people into other careers. When a hospital cannot recruit staff or a school loses its best teachers, society as a whole pays the price. Higher salaries for these professions would be money well spent, although the amount any government can offer is limited by what taxpayers are willing to fund.\n\nThe comparison with entertainers, however, misunderstands how incomes are decided. Pay reflects not the social value of a job but the revenue that a particular person generates. A footballer at a club such as Real Madrid is watched by a worldwide audience, and broadcasters and sponsors compete to be associated with him. A teacher, however gifted, serves one classroom at a time. The stars' earnings come from private companies and willing fans rather than from the public purse, so reducing them would not release any money for hospitals or schools. It should also be remembered that such fortunes are rare. Most athletes and actors earn modest sums over short careers.\n\nTeachers and medical staff should be paid enough to reflect the importance of what they do and to keep talented people in those professions. On balance, though, I think this is a matter of public funding, and it has little to do with what entertainers earn.",
"b161":"Being released from prison is, for a great many offenders, only a pause before the next conviction. This pattern arises mainly because of the way sentences end, and I believe it can be reduced by preparing prisoners for freedom well before the gate opens.\n\nThe central problem is that release is abrupt. After months or years in which every decision was made for them, people walk out with a small sum of money, a criminal record and, in many cases, nowhere to live. Relationships have often broken down during the sentence, so there is no family to offer a bed or advice. A man who cannot rent a room or find an employer willing to take him may turn to old friends, who are frequently the people he offended with. Short sentences make matters worse, since they are long enough to cost someone a job and a tenancy but too short for any training to be completed.\n\nReducing reoffending therefore means making the return to society gradual. In the open prisons of Norway, for example, inmates near the end of their sentences go out to ordinary jobs during the day, so that they leave with a wage, a reference and a routine. Similar schemes could be combined with help in arranging housing and identity documents before release, and with a mentor who stays in contact for the first year. For minor offences, courts could impose supervised community sentences rather than short prison terms. Such measures will not work for every offender, and they require money and public patience, although repeated imprisonment is costly too.\n\nMost reoffending can be traced to the moment of release, when people face freedom with no home, work or support. If that transition is planned and supervised, I think far fewer former prisoners will come back.",
"b162":"Faced with melting ice caps and polluted oceans, many people doubt that their own behaviour makes any difference, while others insist that nothing will change until ordinary citizens act. I believe both groups are partly right: individual action is necessary, but it matters most when it pushes governments and companies to change.\n\nThe view that these problems exceed the reach of individuals is based on scale. Most emissions come from power generation, heavy industry and transport systems that no household designed or controls. A family may switch off lights and sort its rubbish with great care, yet if its electricity still comes from a coal-fired plant, the effect on the climate is slight. People can also choose only among the options available to them. A commuter in a town without a bus service will drive, however concerned she is about the air. Seen from this angle, asking individuals to solve the problem shifts responsibility away from those with real power.\n\nThose who stress personal action reply that governments and businesses do not act in isolation. Politicians pass environmental laws when they believe voters want them, and manufacturers redesign products when customers stop buying the old ones. The decline of plastic straws and the growth of the market for electric cars, for instance, both began with changes in what consumers demanded. Although a single person's choice is negligible, millions of similar choices create the pressure that makes larger reforms possible. This argument holds only if people go beyond private gestures, though, and are prepared to vote and pay for the policies they say they support.\n\nNeither view is sufficient alone. Large institutions hold the tools that can cut pollution on a meaningful scale, but in my view they will use them only when individuals, as voters and consumers, make it clear that they expect it.",
"b163":"The lorries, ships, aircraft and private cars that move goods and passengers around the world are widely regarded as a leading cause of dirty air and rising emissions. Some hold the state responsible for controlling this, whereas others blame the people who travel and consume. I believe that individuals share the blame, but that only governments are in a position to take charge.\n\nThose who fault individuals have a point, because transport exists to serve their wishes. No law obliges people to fly abroad for a weekend, to drive to a shop within walking distance or to order goods for next-day delivery. Each of these choices adds to traffic and to the fuel burned, and collectively they explain why the problem keeps growing despite cleaner engines. Blame is not the same as control, however. A shopper who orders a parcel cannot decide whether it arrives in a diesel van or an electric one, and a worker living far from the office may have no alternative to the motorway.\n\nThis is why others argue that government must be in charge. The state decides whether a city has a reliable underground network or merely wider roads, sets the emission standards that manufacturers must meet and can tax the most polluting fuels. In the Netherlands, for example, decades of public investment in cycle lanes and railways have made it normal to travel without a car. Such decisions shape the behaviour of millions of people at once, which no appeal to conscience can do. Public policy works only with public consent, though, and measures that voters resent tend to be reversed.\n\nIndividuals create the demand that makes transport so polluting. Even so, I think the task of reducing that pollution belongs mainly to governments, since they alone can change the options from which people choose.",
"b164":"There are signs that a section of the public has grown resistant to advertising that would once have persuaded it. This change stems from greater access to independent information and from sheer overexposure. I believe it is a positive development for consumers and for honest businesses alike.\n\nThe main reason is that shoppers no longer depend on the seller for information. Before the internet, an advertisement was often the only description of a product a buyer would see before entering the shop. Today the same buyer can read hundreds of customer reviews, watch an independent demonstration and compare prices in a few minutes. Someone choosing a vacuum cleaner, for instance, is likely to trust the complaints of previous owners more than the manufacturer's slogan. Familiarity has also bred scepticism. People who have grown up surrounded by commercials recognise the techniques, and many now skip, block or simply ignore them. Not all consumers have changed in this way, but the better informed among them clearly have.\n\nThis shift benefits buyers because decisions based on evidence tend to be better ones. Money goes to products that perform well in use rather than to those with the largest marketing budgets, and this in turn rewards companies that invest in quality. There are some costs. Advertisers have responded by disguising promotion as personal recommendation, paying online personalities to praise goods, and reviews themselves can be faked. Free services funded by advertising may also suffer if it loses its effect. These concerns call for vigilance, though they do not outweigh the advantage of a public that is harder to mislead.\n\nConsumers are less easily swayed because they can check claims for themselves and have learned to doubt them. In my view this is a healthy change, since it shifts power from those who sell to those who buy.",
"b165":"Moving freight and passengers by road, sea and air now accounts for a large share of the pollution produced across the globe. Opinion is divided over whether the state or the private citizen should put this right. In my view the responsibility is shared, but it falls on each side for a different part of the problem.\n\nGovernments are the obvious party to deal with the transport of goods. The container ships and heavy lorries that carry products between continents are owned by companies, and companies respond to rules and prices rather than to appeals. Only public authorities can require cleaner marine fuel, move freight from road to rail or agree international standards for aviation. They also control the largest investments. Electrifying a railway or building charging points along a motorway, for instance, is far beyond the means of any private citizen. Where the state fails to provide such alternatives, it can hardly complain that people continue to pollute.\n\nIndividuals, on the other hand, decide how they themselves travel, and here the argument for personal responsibility is stronger. In a city with good buses and trains, driving alone to work is a choice rather than a necessity. So is taking a short flight when a train covers the same route, or buying fruit flown in from another hemisphere when local produce is in season. Laws cannot easily reach decisions of this kind without becoming intrusive, so they depend on people's willingness to accept a little inconvenience. This applies only where a real alternative exists, although in most large cities it does.\n\nCleaner transport therefore requires effort from both sides. I believe governments must regulate industry and build the infrastructure, while individuals have a duty to use it, and neither can reasonably wait for the other to act first.",
"b166":"Urban areas with populations larger than those of many countries, such as Tokyo, Delhi and São Paulo, are becoming home to a growing share of humanity. Although cities of this scale generate great wealth, I believe their growth is a negative development, because beyond a certain size the costs of concentration exceed its rewards.\n\nThe attraction of a mega-city is economic. Bringing millions of workers, firms and customers together allows specialised industries to develop, and a talented person has a better chance of finding the right job there than anywhere else. Universities, hospitals and cultural institutions of the highest standard can be supported by so large a population. While these advantages are real, they are available in cities of a more modest size as well, and they do not keep increasing as the population grows ever larger.\n\nWhat does keep increasing is the difficulty of everyday life. Homes within reach of the centre become unaffordable, so workers are pushed to distant suburbs and may spend a large part of each day travelling. Those hours are taken from sleep, family and leisure, and the crowded roads and trains make the city less pleasant for all. A city of this size is also fragile. A flood, an earthquake or an epidemic in a single place can disrupt a large part of a nation's economy at once. Good management can soften these effects, as Tokyo's rail network demonstrates, although few governments have the resources to match it. In poorer countries, rapid growth tends to outrun planning, leaving millions in informal settlements without proper sanitation.\n\nFor these reasons I believe that the rise of mega-cities does more harm than good to the people who live in them. A country is better served by several large, well-connected cities than by a single giant that absorbs its talent and strains its resources.",
"b167":"Pollution does not stop at borders, which is why some people would like a single global body to take charge of protecting the planet. I disagree that responsibility should pass from national governments to such an organisation, although I accept that international coordination is essential for problems no country can solve alone.\n\nSome environmental threats are plainly global. Greenhouse gases released in one continent warm the climate of every other, and fish stocks in the open ocean belong to no state in particular. If each government acts only in its own interest, it is tempted to let others bear the cost of cutting emissions while it enjoys the benefit. An international body can set common targets and monitor whether they are met, as the agreement to phase out ozone-damaging chemicals showed. Such bodies succeed, however, only when national governments choose to enforce what has been agreed.\n\nThat dependence is the reason responsibility cannot be handed over. An organisation based in a distant city has no police, no tax revenue and no power to close a factory, whereas a national government has all three. If a company dumps waste illegally, it is a national court that can fine it and a national inspector who can check that the dumping has stopped. Most environmental damage is also local in character. A polluted river, illegal logging in a particular forest or smog in a capital city, for example, calls for decisions about land, transport and industry that only local authorities understand well enough to make. While global rules give direction, the work itself is done at home.\n\nEnvironmental protection therefore needs both levels, with different tasks. In my view, international organisations should coordinate and set shared goals, and national governments should remain responsible for meeting them.",
"b168":"Few teenagers follow events beyond their own borders with any regularity, which has led to calls for international news to be taught as a school subject. Others regard such lessons as time taken from more important work. I believe that current affairs deserve a place in secondary education, though as a regular part of existing subjects rather than a separate one.\n\nSupporters of the idea argue that school is where young people should learn to make sense of the world they will inherit. Events abroad affect their lives directly, since a conflict or a trade dispute in one region can raise prices and cost jobs in another. A family that pays more for fuel because of a distant war has good reason to ask why. Pupils who discuss such events in class also learn to compare sources and to notice when a report is one-sided. A class that follows an election in another country, for example, sees how the same event is presented differently by different newspapers.\n\nOpponents reply that the timetable is already full and that news is a poor basis for a subject. Stories change from week to week, so there is no stable body of knowledge to teach or to examine, and teachers would need to prepare new material constantly. These objections are reasonable, although they apply to a separate examined subject rather than to the study of news as such. A geography lesson on migration or a history lesson on a border dispute gains a great deal when it is linked to what is happening this month.\n\nTreating international news as a waste of time underestimates how much pupils need to understand the wider world. On balance, I think it should be taught, but within subjects such as history and geography instead of being given a timetable slot of its own.",
"b169":"A secure home is the basis of almost everything else in a person's life, from holding a job to raising children. I agree that the state should make sure the poorest citizens are housed, although this need not mean handing over a house free of charge.\n\nPublic provision is needed because the housing market does not serve people with very low incomes. Builders and landlords supply what is profitable, and in most large cities rents have risen well beyond what a cleaner or a pensioner can pay. Those who are priced out end up in overcrowded rooms or on the street, where it becomes far harder to stay healthy, keep children in school or find work. A man with no address, for instance, will struggle even to apply for a job. Housing people is therefore less an act of generosity than a way of preventing costs that the state would otherwise meet through hospitals, shelters and the courts.\n\nHow this help is given matters as much as whether it is given. Giving houses away would be extremely expensive and would seem unfair to families who are only slightly better off and are struggling with a mortgage. It might also weaken the incentive to earn more, since a higher wage could mean losing the home. A more sustainable approach is publicly owned housing let at low rents, as in Vienna or Hong Kong, combined with rent support that falls gradually as income rises. Such schemes work only if governments keep building, however, because long waiting lists leave the poorest no better off.\n\nFor these reasons I agree that governments have a duty to house those who cannot house themselves. I believe that duty is best met through affordable rented homes rather than free ownership.",
"b170":"The idea of a purpose-built language belonging to no single nation has attracted idealists for well over a century. While such a language has some appeal in principle, I believe its disadvantages clearly outweigh its advantages, chiefly because the world already has a working common language.\n\nThe main attraction of an invented language is fairness. At present, people who grow up speaking English enjoy an advantage in business, science and diplomacy that they have done nothing to earn, while others spend years acquiring what their competitors received at home. A new language would place all learners on equal terms. It could also be designed to be easy, with regular spelling and grammar and none of the exceptions that make natural languages slow to master. Esperanto, for example, was created with exactly these aims. These benefits would only be felt, however, if most of the world agreed to adopt the language.\n\nThat condition is where the proposal fails. Introducing a new language would mean retraining teachers, rewriting textbooks and translating an enormous body of scientific and legal writing, at a cost few governments would accept. The hundreds of millions of people who have already invested in English would also see much of that effort lose its value, so resistance would be strong. Lack of motivation is the second obstacle. A constructed language has none of the films, songs and native speakers that draw people to a living one. Learners of English or Korean are often led by music or television, whereas Esperanto has remained a minority interest despite its simplicity.\n\nA neutral, simple world language is an attractive idea, but the expense and disruption of introducing one would be far greater than any gain in fairness. In my view, the effort would be better spent on teaching existing languages well.",
"b171":"Sport occupies very different places in children's lives. For most it remains a casual game after school, but a growing number now treat it as a serious commitment involving coaches, training schedules and competitions. I believe this development is positive, as long as the serious path is chosen by the child and not imposed by adults.\n\nChildren who train seriously gain qualities that are hard to teach in a classroom. A young swimmer who gets up early for practice learns to manage time, to accept criticism from a coach and to recover from defeat. These habits tend to carry over into schoolwork. Such gains depend on the commitment being kept in proportion. When training takes up most evenings and weekends, schoolwork and friendships may suffer, and growing bodies can be injured by too much repetition. Some parents also push their children towards a professional career that only a tiny minority will achieve, which turns enjoyment into pressure.\n\nPlaying purely for enjoyment has its own value, and it suits the majority. A group of friends kicking a ball in the park, for example, get exercise, learn to settle their own disputes and return home in a better mood. They do so without the fear of being dropped from a team. Because there is nothing at stake, children of any ability can take part. Casual play is, however, less likely to build real skill or persistence, since a child can simply stop when the game becomes difficult. This is why it helps that both routes exist, and that a child can move between them as interests change.\n\nNeither approach is better for every child. In my view, it is healthy that more young people now treat sport as a discipline and not only as a pastime, provided that the child's own enthusiasm decides which.",
"b172":"A number of cities now close their centres to private vehicles on certain days and ask residents to travel by bus, taxi or metro instead. Although these days cause genuine inconvenience, I believe their benefits are considerably greater, provided that public transport can carry the extra passengers.\n\nThe immediate gain is a city centre that is quieter, cleaner and safer for a day. With cars, trucks and motorcycles absent, the air improves noticeably and streets normally given over to traffic can be used for walking, cycling and markets. The more lasting benefit is that people are pushed to try an alternative they would otherwise ignore. A commuter who has driven to work for years may discover, for instance, that the metro is quicker than he assumed, and some of these people go on using it. Cities such as Bogotá and Jakarta have held car-free days for many years, and residents now treat them as popular events. A single day does little to reduce annual emissions, however, so the real value lies in changing habits and attitudes.\n\nThe disadvantages fall first on businesses, for which a vehicle is a tool rather than a convenience. Shops and restaurants cannot receive deliveries if trucks are banned, and a plumber cannot carry heavy equipment on a bus, so some lose a day's income. The second group is people who find public transport hard to use. Elderly or disabled residents may struggle with crowded carriages, and those who live far from a station face longer journeys. Most of these problems can be managed, however. Delivery vehicles and disabled drivers can be exempted, and dates announced in advance allow businesses to plan.\n\nVehicle-free days therefore impose costs that are temporary and largely avoidable, while offering a glimpse of a cleaner and calmer city. On balance, I think the advantages clearly outweigh the drawbacks.",
"b173":"In a growing number of countries, the law now sets a ceiling on the hours a company may require of its staff. These limits exist because employees are rarely in a position to refuse, and I believe they are a positive trend, provided that they leave room for people who choose to work longer.\n\nThe main reason for such laws is the unequal relationship between employer and employee. A worker who depends on a wage to pay the rent cannot easily say no when asked to stay late, particularly where jobs are scarce. Without a legal limit, the length of the working day is set by whoever has more power, and that is seldom the employee. Safety is a second concern. Exhausted people make mistakes, and in some occupations, such as lorry driving or nursing, those mistakes can cost lives. A legal maximum takes the decision out of a negotiation that one side was likely to lose.\n\nI regard this as a welcome trend because its benefits reach beyond the individual worker. People with predictable hours can rest, look after their children and take part in community life, and they tend to be more productive while they are at work. Children see more of their parents, and local clubs and voluntary groups find it easier to attract members. The rules can be too rigid, however. A young professional who wants to earn extra, or a small firm facing a sudden rush of orders, may be badly served by a strict cap. Sensible laws therefore restrict what an employer can demand while permitting paid overtime that is truly voluntary.\n\nLimits on working hours correct an imbalance that the market leaves untouched. In my view they are a positive development, as long as they protect workers from pressure without denying them choice.",
"b174":"Legislation capping the working week, once associated mainly with European countries, has spread widely. Governments pass these laws to protect health and family life from a culture of overwork. I think they are a positive development on the whole, even though they do not reach the people who need them most.\n\nOne reason for these laws is the damage that very long hours do to health. Persistent overwork is linked to heart problems, depression and even early death, a danger so familiar in Japan that the language has a word for it. Another reason is social. Parents who leave home before their children wake and return after they are asleep have little part in bringing them up, and couples in this position may decide not to have children at all. Because individual employees fear being seen as less committed than their colleagues, few will leave early unless the law gives them all the same right.\n\nThe effect of a legal limit has been beneficial where it is enforced. In France, for instance, a shorter working week has not prevented workers from being among the most productive in the world, which suggests that output depends on more than time spent at a desk. Employees who are rested tend to concentrate better and make fewer mistakes, so the hours they do work count for more. The weakness of such laws is their coverage rather than their purpose. Delivery riders, the self-employed and workers in the informal economy usually fall outside them, and managers can still expect emails to be answered late at night.\n\nLaws limiting working hours are a response to real harm, and they have improved life for the employees they cover. The trend is, in my view, a positive one, though it will remain incomplete until the rules extend to less secure forms of work.",
"b175":"Cities from Copenhagen to Paris have invested heavily in cycle lanes, bike-hire schemes and secure parking. They do so because the bicycle solves several urban problems at once. In my view, though, it is not the best single answer to congestion, which depends more on good public transport.\n\nGovernments promote cycling largely because it is cheap and its benefits are broad. A cycle lane costs a fraction of what a new road or railway line does, and a bicycle takes up far less space than a car carrying one person. Each commuter who switches also stops producing exhaust fumes and gets daily exercise, which reduces the burden on health services. In Amsterdam, for example, protected lanes have made cycling the normal way to travel short distances. For a city authority with a limited budget, few other measures offer cleaner air, healthier residents and quieter streets together.\n\nAs a remedy for traffic jams, however, cycling has clear limits. It suits short journeys in flat, compact cities with a mild climate, and it is of little use to someone who lives far from work, is elderly, or must carry children or goods. In a hot or mountainous city, or one that sprawls over a wide area, most people will keep driving however many lanes are painted. What moves large numbers of people over long distances is frequent and affordable public transport, such as underground trains and bus lanes, supported where necessary by charges for driving into the centre. Cycling works best as a link in this system rather than a replacement for it.\n\nSpending on bicycles is justified by its low cost and its benefits to health and air quality. It can only ease congestion, though, as one part of a wider transport policy, and I believe that reliable public transport remains the better solution.",
"b176":"Employers who once looked mainly at certificates now pay close attention to how candidates deal with other people. I agree that social skills matter as much as qualifications for success in most jobs, although the balance between the two shifts with the nature of the work.\n\nQualifications remain the foundation of competence. A hospital would not hire a surgeon, however charming, who had not completed medical training, and the same applies to pilots, engineers and accountants. Formal study gives people the knowledge without which the job cannot be done at all, and it assures an employer that a stranger has reached a known standard. Qualifications are a starting point, though, rather than a measure of how well someone will perform. Two graduates with identical degrees can have very different careers, and the difference is seldom explained by their marks.\n\nWhat usually explains it is the ability to work with others. Very little modern work is done alone: projects are shared between departments, clients have to be persuaded, and disagreements need to be settled without lasting damage. A software developer who writes excellent code but cannot explain it to colleagues, for example, will slow the whole team down. A slightly less gifted one who listens and communicates clearly will often be promoted first. These skills become more valuable with seniority, since managing people is largely a matter of understanding them. There are exceptions, such as laboratory research or long-distance driving, where contact with others is limited and technical ability counts for more.\n\nKnowledge and the capacity to get on with people are therefore partners rather than rivals. I believe that employers are right to look for both, because a qualification shows what a person knows, while social skills decide how much use that knowledge will be to everyone else.",
"b177":"Choosing between work that is enjoyable and work that is safe is a decision most people face at some point. Both matter, but in my view satisfaction deserves more weight, because a secure job that a person dreads brings little real security in the end.\n\nThose who favour a permanent post are thinking about risk. A stable contract allows people to plan: they can obtain a mortgage, start a family and save for old age without fearing that their income will stop next month. A parent with two young children, for instance, may reasonably prefer a dull position in the civil service to an exciting one at a new company that could close within a year. Such caution is sensible, although it rests on the assumption that permanent jobs stay permanent, and whole industries have shown that they do not.\n\nSupporters of job satisfaction point to the sheer amount of life that work occupies. Someone who finds the work interesting tends to put more effort into it, learns faster and becomes better at it, and these are the qualities that keep a person employable when circumstances change. An unhappy employee, by contrast, often does the minimum, and years of this can leave him or her with a safe contract but outdated skills. Enjoyment also protects health, since constant boredom or frustration is tiring. Someone who comes home drained has little patience for family. This argument has a limit, however. Satisfaction cannot pay the rent, and a job that is loved but pays too little soon stops being enjoyable.\n\nSecurity is therefore a need that has to be met, yet it is not a goal in itself. Once a job pays enough to live on, I believe people should choose the work that engages them, since that is what keeps them both content and employable.",
"b178":"Language schools often advertise small groups as their main selling point, yet there are people who doubt that class size makes any real difference. I believe that it does matter in language teaching, more than in most subjects, because a language is learned largely by using it.\n\nThe case for small classes begins with speaking time. In a lesson shared by thirty students, each may say only a sentence or two aloud, and the teacher has little chance of hearing them all. In a group of eight, every learner can take part in a conversation, be corrected on the spot and try again. Pronunciation shows why this matters, since errors that go unnoticed in a crowded room quickly become habits. The teacher can also draw in learners who stay silent, whereas in a large class the confident few do most of the talking.\n\nThose who think numbers are unimportant argue that the teacher and the method count for more. A skilled teacher can organise a large class into pairs so that the whole room is talking at once, while a dull one can waste the advantages of a small group. They also point out that some parts of a language, such as reading, listening and grammar, can be taught to a hundred people as easily as to ten. An explanation of a tense, for instance, loses nothing in a full lecture hall. This does not show that size is irrelevant, however. Pair work without supervision allows mistakes to pass uncorrected, and it is in speaking and writing, where individual feedback is needed, that most learners struggle.\n\nGood teaching matters most, but good teachers achieve more when they can hear and respond to each student. This is why, in my view, small classes are a real benefit for language learners and not merely a marketing claim.",
"b179":"As people live longer, the question of who should pay for the years after work has become pressing. Some look to the state and others to the individual. I believe a basic public pension is necessary, but that it should be a floor on which people build their own savings.\n\nThe case for state support begins with the fact that not all workers are able to save. People in low-paid jobs spend most of what they earn on rent and food, and those who have raised children or cared for relatives may have gone years unpaid. A woman who has worked as a cleaner all her life, for example, cannot be blamed for reaching sixty-five with little in the bank. Pensioners have also paid taxes throughout their careers, so public support is something they have contributed to rather than charity. It would be unjust to withdraw that support once they are too old to make other plans. The difficulty is cost. Where the number of retired people is rising faster than the number of taxpayers, a generous pension for all becomes hard to sustain.\n\nThose who emphasise personal responsibility argue that individuals know their own needs best. Someone who wants to travel in retirement, or to stop work early, should fund that choice instead of expecting younger taxpayers to do so. This reasoning is fair for people on comfortable incomes, although it assumes a degree of foresight that many lack. Retirement feels distant at thirty, and voluntary schemes tend to attract those who need them least. This is why countries such as Australia require employers to pay part of every salary into a retirement fund.\n\nA sensible system combines the two approaches. In my view, governments should guarantee every older citizen enough to live decently, while requiring and encouraging workers to save for anything beyond that.",
"b180":"Explanations of crime tend to fall into two camps: those that blame the conditions in which offenders live and those that blame the offenders themselves. I think both contain some truth, but in my view circumstances explain far more of the crime we see than any fixed flaw of character.\n\nThe link between hardship and crime is difficult to ignore. Theft, drug dealing and gang violence are concentrated in districts where jobs are scarce, housing is overcrowded and schools are poor. For a young man in such a place, the lawful route to a decent income may look long and uncertain, while the unlawful one is on offer at the end of his street. Addiction and family breakdown, which are more common where money is short, add further pressure. When the same neighbourhood receives investment and employment returns, offending usually declines, which suggests that the people living there were responding to their situation rather than expressing a criminal nature.\n\nSupporters of the opposing view reply that most poor people are honest. This is true, and it shows that poverty does not force anyone to offend. They also point out that some crimes have nothing to do with need, such as fraud committed by wealthy executives or cruelty with no financial motive. Cases like these indicate that individual character matters, and that people remain responsible for what they choose. Even so, describing offenders as simply bad explains very little. It cannot account for why crime is so unevenly spread across a country, unless one believes that bad people happen to be born mainly in poor areas.\n\nCharacter may help to explain which individuals offend, but social conditions largely determine how many do. I believe, therefore, that crime is better understood, and better reduced, by addressing deprivation than by condemning human nature.",
"b181":"Screens around the world are filled with films and series made elsewhere, and governments differ over whether to welcome this or to resist it. I believe the two options are not rivals: a country should remain open to foreign productions while making sure that its own industry survives beside them.\n\nSupporters of imports argue that audiences deserve the best of what the world produces. Foreign films and programmes let viewers see how other societies live, think and joke, which is a form of education few would otherwise receive. A teenager who follows a Japanese series, for instance, learns something of a country he may never visit. Cost matters too. Buying the rights to an existing series costs a broadcaster far less than filming one, so a small country can offer a wide schedule that it could not afford to create. This argument has a limit, though, because a schedule built only on what is cheapest will soon contain little that is local.\n\nThe case for domestic production begins there. Stories set in familiar streets, and told in the national language give people something imported drama cannot, namely a picture of themselves. Without them, a nation's own history and humour would slowly fade from its screens. Home-grown production also provides work for actors, writers and technicians, and success can travel abroad. South Korean dramas, for example, began as programmes for a domestic audience and now attract viewers on several continents. Protection can be taken too far, however. If foreign material is shut out, local producers face no competition, and quality tends to fall when viewers have no alternative.\n\nNeither approach is sufficient alone. In my view, the sensible policy is to let foreign films and programmes in freely, and to use public funding or modest quotas so that local stories continue to be told.",
"b182":"Access to higher education is increasingly described as a right, and from this it is often concluded that the state should abolish tuition fees for rich and poor alike. I agree that no capable applicant should be excluded by cost, but I disagree that the answer is to make university free for every student.\n\nThe principle behind the claim is sound. A place at university ought to depend on ability and effort, and a country wastes talent whenever a gifted school-leaver decides that a degree is beyond the family's means. Public funding is the most reliable way of preventing this, because loans, however generous, deter those who have grown up in households where debt is feared. A student whose parents work in low-paid jobs, for instance, may choose immediate employment over a course that would have transformed her prospects. For such applicants, free tuition and help with living costs are fully justified.\n\nExtending the same subsidy to wealthy families is harder to defend. Public money is limited, and each sum spent on the fees of students who could easily pay is unavailable for grants, for schools or for young people who do not attend university. Since students from prosperous homes are more likely to win places, universal free tuition tends to transfer resources towards those who need them least. A system that charges according to family income avoids this, although it must be simple enough that poorer applicants are not discouraged by the paperwork. The right to education is served by removing barriers, not by treating unequal circumstances as if they were equal.\n\nUniversity should be open to anyone able to benefit from it, whatever their background. I believe this is best achieved by covering the costs of those who cannot pay, while asking families who can afford fees to contribute.",
"b183":"Because health matters to every person, many feel that hospitals and clinics should not be run as businesses. Private medicine does bring genuine benefits, but I believe its drawbacks are greater wherever it becomes the main way in which people obtain care.\n\nThe chief advantage of private providers is that competition forces them to be efficient and responsive. A clinic that depends on paying patients has every reason to shorten waiting times, invest in modern equipment and treat people courteously, and patients gain a choice over when and by whom they are treated. In countries such as Germany and Singapore, private hospitals work alongside public ones and standards are high. Profit can drive innovation as well. Companies that develop a better drug or scanner are rewarded for it, and this prospect pays for research that governments alone might not fund. These benefits, however, are enjoyed mainly by people with money or good insurance.\n\nThe central weakness of a profit-driven system is that it distributes care according to wealth rather than need. A serious diagnosis can arrive without warning, and in the United States, for instance, medical bills are a well-known cause of personal bankruptcy. Insurers also have an incentive to avoid the old and the chronically ill, who are the very people requiring most treatment. The second weakness is a conflict of interest. When a doctor's income rises with every test and operation, there is a temptation to recommend more than is needed, and patients cannot easily judge what is necessary. Regulation can limit these problems, although it rarely removes them.\n\nPrivate healthcare offers speed, choice and innovation, and it is valuable as a supplement to public provision. As the foundation of a health system, though, its disadvantages outweigh its advantages in my view, because access to treatment should not depend on the ability to pay.",
"b184":"Tuition fees have become one of the most disputed questions in education policy, with some arguing that the state should meet the entire cost of every degree course. Although I accept that public money should cover the greater part of that cost, I disagree that governments should pay all of it.\n\nThere are sound reasons for generous public funding. Graduates do not keep the benefits of their education to themselves, since a country relies on trained nurses, teachers and engineers. It is reasonable for the public to pay for training from which it gains. Full payment of fees also removes a distortion in the choice of course. Where students must borrow heavily, many are drawn towards degrees that lead to high salaries, such as law or finance, and away from useful but modestly paid professions. A state that wants enough social workers and primary teachers has good reason to make those courses cost nothing.\n\nPaying the whole bill for every student, however, creates difficulties of its own. Universities that depend on a single government budget are exposed whenever that budget is cut, and the usual results are fewer places or larger classes. A system that is free in principle may thus become harder to enter in practice. People also tend to value what costs them something, and a modest contribution, repaid only when a graduate is earning well, encourages applicants to choose carefully without shutting anyone out. This arrangement is fair only if the contribution stays small, because large debts would bring back the very problems that public funding is meant to solve.\n\nGovernments should remain the main source of funding for higher education, and should pay in full for courses the country badly needs. For other degrees, I think a small deferred contribution from graduates is both fairer and more sustainable than full state payment.",
"b185":"Hardly anyone disputes that children should attend school at public expense, but opinion divides sharply once education becomes voluntary at eighteen. Some would extend free provision to degree level, whereas others hold that undergraduates should fund themselves. In my view, the state should pay for schooling in full and for most, though not all, of the cost of university.\n\nThose who want education to be free at every stage see it as a single ladder. It makes little sense, they argue, to carry a child through twelve years of schooling and then let the final step depend on what the parents earn. Where university is free, as in Germany and the Nordic countries, students can choose a course on the basis of interest and ability. The weakness of this position is cost. Higher education is far more expensive per student than school, and the money must come from taxpayers, many of whom did not go to university themselves.\n\nThis is the point on which the opposing side relies. Primary and secondary education are compulsory and benefit every citizen, while a degree is a personal choice that usually leads to a better-paid career. On this view, it is reasonable for the individual who gains to bear the expense. The argument is logical, yet it overlooks how much a society depends on its graduates. It would also, in practice, reserve the professions for the children of the well-off unless loans or grants were widely available. A charge that covered the whole cost of a medical degree, for example, would deter all but the richest applicants.\n\nDegrees serve the public, and they also reward the people who hold them. I believe the cost of university should be shared accordingly, with the state paying the larger part and students a moderate fee from which the poorest are exempt.",
"b186":"Diet-related illness is now among the leading causes of early death in wealthy and developing countries alike. Opinion is divided over whether eating well is a private matter or something the state should secure. I believe adults must make their own choices, but that governments are responsible for making good choices possible.\n\nPersonal responsibility begins with freedom. What a person eats is among the most private of decisions, and adults are entitled to weigh pleasure against risk for themselves. It is also the individual who is best placed to act, because only he or she knows the daily circumstances in which meals are bought and prepared. A person who decides to cook at home and walk to work will benefit more than any official campaign could achieve. This argument assumes, however, that people have the information and the means to choose well, which is not the case for all.\n\nSupporters of state involvement argue that choices are shaped by forces no individual controls. Food companies spend heavily on advertising products high in sugar, salt and fat, and in poorer districts fresh produce may be harder to find and more expensive than fast food. Governments can correct this imbalance. Clear labelling, such as the traffic-light colours printed on packaging in Britain, lets shoppers judge a product, and school meals can be held to nutritional standards. Measures like these inform and enable rather than compel. Once the state goes further and dictates what adults may eat, it is likely to meet resentment and to be ignored.\n\nHealth cannot be handed to people by law, yet nor can it be left wholly to willpower in a market designed to tempt. The sensible division, in my view, is that governments provide honest information and affordable options, while individuals take responsibility for using them.",
"b187":"It is clear to any teacher that some pupils pick up a foreign language far more quickly than others. Some conclude from this that language lessons should not be imposed on those who struggle. I disagree with this conclusion, because differences in aptitude are a reason to teach differently, not a reason to stop teaching.\n\nNatural ability varies in every school subject, yet it is rarely proposed that pupils who find mathematics hard should give it up at eleven. Schools insist on certain subjects because the benefit does not depend on being talented. A pupil who will not become fluent can still learn enough to order a meal, read a sign or greet a colleague, and will have discovered that other people organise their thoughts in other ways. Early difficulty is also a poor guide to later success. Many adults who speak a second language well, such as migrants who learned it through work, showed no special gift for it at school.\n\nThe view I am opposing does contain a fair point about how long compulsion should last. A fifteen-year-old who has studied a language for several years, made a real effort and still finds it a source of anxiety gains little from two more years of it. What should change before then is the method. Weaker learners often fail because lessons are built around written grammar tests, whereas they cope much better when the emphasis is on listening and simple conversation. A change of approach, rather than an exemption, is what most of them need.\n\nAptitude affects how far a student will go in a language, but it does not make the first steps worthless. For that reason I believe schools should require all pupils to study one for some years, while allowing those who struggle to stop at the later stages.",
"b188":"Whether life has improved over the centuries depends on what is meant by a good life. In material terms the answer is plainly yes, and for that reason I agree with the claim to a large extent, though some things that once made life satisfying have become harder to find.\n\nComfort and knowledge are the areas in which the present most clearly surpasses the past. An ordinary family in a developed country has heating, electric light, running water and more varied food than a king could command three hundred years ago. Physical labour has been lightened by machines, leaving time for leisure that earlier generations hardly knew. Access to information has changed just as much. A teenager with a phone can read almost any book, follow a university lecture or speak to a relative overseas, which were once privileges of the rich or simply impossible.\n\nQuality of life is not only a matter of comfort, however, and here the comparison is less one-sided. People in earlier centuries usually lived among relatives and lifelong neighbours, and they seldom doubted where they belonged. Many modern city-dwellers, by contrast, live alone, change jobs frequently and feel pressure to be reachable at every hour. The natural world has suffered too. Residents of a large industrial city such as Delhi breathe air that their great-grandparents would have found intolerable. These losses should be taken seriously, although they do not cancel the gains, since a close community was little consolation to a family facing hunger after a failed harvest.\n\nPeople today are better fed, better housed and better informed than those of any previous era. On the whole I think their lives are better, but the claim holds less firmly for community and environment than it does for material comfort.",
"b189":"An entire library can now be carried on a device that weighs less than a single paperback. This has persuaded some people that paper books are obsolete, while others insist that they remain valuable. I believe printed books still have an important place, although it is a narrower one than before.\n\nThe argument against print is mainly one of convenience. Electronic texts cost little to distribute, take up no shelf space and can be searched in seconds, so a student can carry every textbook for a course in one bag. Readers with poor eyesight can enlarge the type, and people far from a bookshop can download a title immediately. For reference works such as dictionaries and encyclopaedias, the digital version is plainly superior, and few people miss the heavy volumes. What this shows, however, is that some kinds of book have been replaced, not that all of them can be.\n\nDefenders of print stress what happens during long, careful reading. A paper book has no notifications and no links to follow, and many readers find that they concentrate better and remember more when they can see and feel how far they have progressed. This matters most for novels and serious non-fiction, which demand hours of unbroken attention rather than a quick search. Durability is the other consideration. A volume printed two centuries ago can still be opened and read, whereas an electronic file may become unusable when a format changes or a company withdraws it. Paper is therefore the safer medium for works of lasting value.\n\nOn balance, I think the two formats will continue side by side. Screens suit quick consultation and travel, but they have not matched paper for sustained reading or for anything meant to last.",
"b190":"Competitive sport places young athletes under pressure, and a few people conclude that they should be kept out of competitions altogether. I disagree with this view, because the stress involved is usually manageable and the experience teaches lessons that are hard to learn elsewhere.\n\nIt is true that competition can be distressing. A fourteen-year-old gymnast who trains every day and is judged in front of a crowd may dread failure, particularly if a coach or parent treats each result as a verdict on her worth. In extreme cases this leads to anxiety, injury from overtraining or a lasting dislike of sport. Such harm deserves to be taken seriously. It stems, however, from the way adults manage competition rather than from competition itself, and it affects a small number of children in highly demanding programmes.\n\nFor the great majority, taking part in matches and races is beneficial. A school football tournament, for instance, gives players a reason to practise, to cooperate and to keep going when they are losing. Learning to cope with nerves before a game is useful preparation for examinations and job interviews, where pressure cannot be avoided. Defeat teaches something as well, namely that disappointment passes and effort can be renewed. A ban would remove these benefits and would probably reduce participation in sport generally, since many young people exercise precisely because they enjoy competing. A better response is to control the pressure. Coaches can be trained to emphasise improvement over winning, training hours for young children can be limited, and parents can be reminded that sport is meant to be enjoyed.\n\nShielding young people from every source of stress would leave them poorly prepared for adult life. I believe competitions should remain open to them, with adults taking responsibility for keeping the pressure within healthy limits.",
"b191":"Raising children is a task for which few adults receive any preparation, so the idea of compulsory yearly parenting classes has some appeal. I disagree, however, that this would be the best way to secure a happy family life, since what happens in a home depends on much more than what parents know.\n\nParenting courses do have value. A first-time mother or father can learn how to settle a baby, how to respond to tantrums without shouting, and what behaviour to expect at each age. Advice of this kind prevents a good deal of anxiety and conflict, and voluntary programmes run by health visitors in countries such as Britain are popular for that reason. Making attendance compulsory every year, however, is another matter. Parents of a confident sixteen-year-old have little to gain from a course they have already sat through many times, and those who most need help are the least likely to respond well to being forced.\n\nA happy family life also rests on conditions that no classroom can provide. Children thrive when their parents have time for them, when money worries are not constant, and when the adults in the home treat each other with respect. A couple working long shifts to pay the rent, for example, may know perfectly well that they should read to their children and still be too exhausted to do so. For such families, shorter working hours, affordable childcare and decent housing would achieve more than any lecture. Good advice still has a part to play, although it helps only when parents have the energy and peace of mind to act on it.\n\nGuidance for parents is worth offering, especially around the birth of a first child. Annual compulsory courses would add little, and in my view time, security and respect at home do far more to make a childhood happy.",
"b192":"Abolishing fares and running buses and trains around the clock is sometimes presented as the surest cure for crowded roads. I disagree that it is the best one, because the price of a ticket is rarely the reason people choose to drive.\n\nMost motorists stay in their cars for the sake of time and convenience, not money. A car leaves when its owner is ready, goes door to door and offers a seat, whereas a bus may come only a few times an hour and stop two streets from the destination. Someone who already pays for fuel, parking and insurance is unlikely to give all that up to save a small fare. Luxembourg, for example, made its public transport free nationwide, yet its roads remain crowded with commuters. Free travel does attract new passengers, but many of them are people who previously walked or cycled, and they leave the traffic much as it was.\n\nWhat persuades drivers to switch is a service that competes with the car on speed and reliability. Buses with their own lanes, trains that run every few minutes and stations within walking distance of home all shorten the journey, which is the thing commuters care about. These improvements are expensive, and fare income is one of the main ways of paying for them. A network that gives up this income may have to cut the very services that make it attractive. This is not to say that price is irrelevant, since cheaper tickets help low-income households considerably, although that is an argument about fairness rather than congestion.\n\nFree transport at all hours would be popular and would help those who struggle to afford travel, but I do not believe it would do much to clear the roads. Faster and more frequent services, in my view, would achieve far more.",
"b193":"The claim that a degree is a purely private advantage, and that undergraduates should therefore meet its entire cost, has an appealing simplicity. I disagree with it, since the premise is mistaken: the gains from higher education are shared widely, and the bill should be shared as well.\n\nGraduates clearly profit from their studies, and this is the grain of truth in the argument. They typically earn more, have more secure employment and enjoy a wider choice of careers than those who left education at eighteen. It is reasonable to expect them to pay something towards an experience that rewards them so well. This does not, however, establish that they are the only ones who benefit. A patient treated by a well-trained surgeon, a town whose bridge was designed by a competent engineer and a child taught by a qualified teacher have all gained from someone else's degree.\n\nOnce these public benefits are recognised, charging students the full cost looks both unfair and unwise. Courses such as medicine and engineering are extremely expensive to run, and if students had to fund them alone, only those from wealthy families could enter the professions on which the rest of society depends. Shortages would follow, and the public would bear the consequences. Graduates also repay the state indirectly, because higher salaries mean higher tax payments over a working life. While this does not justify making university free, it does suggest that a substantial public contribution is a sensible investment rather than a favour.\n\nUniversity education benefits the individual and the community together. I believe, therefore, that its cost should be divided between them, and that asking students to pay everything would harm society far more than it would save.",
"b194":"Parents and teachers have long worried about the effect of television on the young, but they disagree about where the danger lies. Some point to the programmes children see, others to the hours they spend in front of the screen. I believe content has the stronger influence on behaviour, although quantity becomes important once viewing begins to replace other activities.\n\nThe view that content shapes behaviour rests on the way children learn. Young children imitate what they see, and they do not yet separate fiction from real life as adults do. A boy who regularly watches cartoons in which fights are funny and painless, for example, may try the same moves on a younger sister. The reverse is also true, since programmes that show characters sharing, apologising or solving problems give children models worth copying. What matters, on this account, is what a child is shown rather than for how long, and an hour of one kind of programme is not equivalent to an hour of another.\n\nPeople who stress viewing time make a different point. Whatever is on the screen, a child who is watching is not running about, reading, playing with friends or talking to adults. These are the activities through which patience, physical confidence and social skills develop, and a child who misses them may become restless, withdrawn or easily bored. Although this concern is justified when television fills most of a child's free time, it is less convincing for moderate viewing, because a short period each day leaves plenty of room for everything else.\n\nBoth factors therefore deserve attention, but they are not equal. Parents should, in my view, first consider what their children watch, and should limit the hours mainly to protect the time needed for play, sleep and conversation.",
"b195":"Police and armed forces were for a long time almost entirely male, and the place of women in them is still debated. While some people doubt that women are suited to such work, I believe they should serve on equal terms, with every recruit judged against the demands of the role.\n\nThose who object usually point to physical strength. Front-line soldiers carry heavy equipment over long distances, and police officers sometimes have to restrain violent suspects, tasks at which the average man has an advantage over the average woman. In an emergency, colleagues may depend on that strength. These concerns deserve to be taken seriously, but they describe averages rather than individuals. Some women pass the hardest fitness tests and many men fail them, so the argument supports a strict standard, not a ban on half the population.\n\nThe argument for equality starts from what these services need. Much modern policing and military work depends less on strength than on judgement, communication and technical skill. Flying an aircraft, analysing intelligence or calming a dispute between neighbours requires abilities that are spread evenly between the sexes. Women also bring practical advantages. Victims of domestic violence, for example, are often more willing to speak to a female officer, and in some societies only women soldiers can search or question local women. A force that recruits from the whole population has more talent to choose from. Equality should not mean lowering requirements, however, since a weaker standard would endanger colleagues and undermine respect for the women who serve.\n\nSuitability, then, is a matter of the individual and the task, not of sex. In my opinion, women should have access to every role in the police and the military, as long as they meet the same tests as the men beside them.",
"b196":"Deciding what to do for a living is among the first serious choices a young person makes, and families often disagree about how it should be made. Some favour following personal interest, while others prefer careers that promise stability. I believe interest should lead, as long as the choice is made with open eyes.\n\nPeople who urge practicality are concerned with security. Fields such as nursing, accounting and engineering offer steady demand and a reliable income, which makes it possible to rent a home and support a family. A degree in a subject with few openings can leave a graduate in work unrelated to it, with years of study wasted. This view is weaker than it looks, however, because it treats the job market as fixed. Occupations that look safe today may shrink within a decade, so a practical choice offers less certainty than it seems to.\n\nThe argument for freedom is that people do their best work in fields they care about. Someone who enjoys the subject will practise more, persist through setbacks and gradually become good enough to earn a living from it. A young woman who loves cooking, for instance, may begin on low wages in a restaurant kitchen and end up running her own business. Had she trained as an accountant to please her parents, she might have been competent but unhappy. Freedom of this kind works best, though, when it is combined with realism about what the path involves. A desired career still has to be planned, with the necessary training and some idea of how to manage the early years.\n\nPractical considerations should therefore inform the decision without making it. In my view, young people are more likely to build a secure future in work they have chosen themselves than in a sensible job they resent.",
"b197":"A murder or a violent robbery is no less terrible for its victims because the offender was sixteen. Even so, I disagree that young people who commit serious crimes should receive the same punishment as adults, although the gravity of the offence must still be reflected in the sentence.\n\nYoung offenders deserve different treatment chiefly because they are less fully responsible. Adolescents are more impulsive than adults, more easily led by friends and less able to picture the long-term results of what they do. A boy who joins older gang members in an attack, for example, has done something very wrong, although his choice was not made with an adult's judgement or independence. The law already accepts this reasoning in other areas, since people under eighteen may not vote, sign contracts or buy alcohol. It would be inconsistent to regard them as children for every purpose except punishment.\n\nTeenagers are also more capable of change. Character is not settled at fifteen, and many violent teenagers become quite different people in their twenties if they are educated and supervised in the meantime. A sentence of several decades in an adult prison removes that possibility and places a young person among experienced criminals for the years in which he is most easily influenced. Serious crimes should not be met with leniency, however. Long periods in secure youth custody are sometimes necessary to protect the public and to recognise the harm done, and release should depend on evidence of real progress.\n\nSerious offences by the young demand a serious response, but not an identical one. Because adolescents are less mature and more open to reform, I believe their sentences should be shorter than an adult's, served separately and directed towards their eventual return to society.",
"b198":"Sports such as motor racing, boxing and mountaineering carry a real chance of serious injury, and each fatal accident renews the demand that the state should forbid them. Opponents of a ban insist that the choice belongs to the individual. I believe the second position is stronger, provided that governments regulate how such sports are run.\n\nThe argument for prohibition begins with the duty of a government to protect its citizens. Seat belts and motorcycle helmets are compulsory because people tend to underestimate danger, and the same reasoning could be applied to sport. Supporters of a ban add that the costs do not fall on the participant alone. When a climber is stranded on a mountain, rescue teams risk their own lives, and a boxer with brain damage may need public healthcare for decades. This reasoning has some force, although it would apply equally to smoking, drinking and driving, which no democratic government proposes to outlaw.\n\nThose who defend free choice argue that adults are entitled to decide which risks make their lives worth living. For a racing driver or a mountaineer, the danger is part of the attraction, and the sense of achievement cannot be had in any safer way. A ban would also be hard to enforce. People would continue to climb or race in secret, without marshals, medical staff or inspected equipment, and the number of injuries might well rise. Freedom of this kind is not unlimited, however. It assumes that participants understand what they are doing, which is why training and licensing matter.\n\nBanning risky sports would restrict personal liberty without making anyone much safer. In my view, the state should instead set standards for equipment, instruction and medical cover, and then leave adults to make their own decision.",
"b199":"High-resolution images and virtual tours now allow anyone with a laptop to wander through famous collections without leaving home. Some people predict that physical museums and galleries will become redundant as a result. I disagree, as viewing a reproduction is a different experience from standing before the original.\n\nAn original work has qualities that a screen flattens. Size is the most obvious. Monet's water lily paintings in Paris, for example, stretch along curved walls and surround the viewer, an effect that disappears on a small display. Texture, the thickness of paint and the way colours shift in natural light are lost in the same way. There is also the knowledge that the object in front of us is the one a person made by hand centuries ago, which gives a visit an emotional weight no copy carries. Visitors still queue for hours to stand near a famous painting they have seen many times in print, which suggests the real object matters to them.\n\nComputers do offer real advantages, and I accept that they have changed how collections are used. Someone living far from Florence or Cairo can now see masterpieces that would otherwise be out of reach, and can zoom in on details invisible from behind a barrier. Although this is a genuine gain, it adds to the museum rather than replacing it. Recorded music has not put an end to concerts, and in a similar way a digital image seems as likely to prompt a visit as to make one unnecessary.\n\nDigital access is a valuable addition for people who cannot travel, yet it remains a substitute of last resort. I believe public museums and galleries will be needed for as long as people care about seeing the real thing.",
"b200":"Much of what fills a household bin is not the product that was bought but the plastic, card and foil it came in. There is disagreement over who should be responsible for reducing this waste. I believe both producers and shoppers have a part to play, but that the greater responsibility lies with the companies that design and sell the goods.\n\nThe argument for holding manufacturers and supermarkets responsible is that they make the decisions. A shopper does not choose to have four apples sealed on a plastic tray, and much packaging exists to attract attention on the shelf rather than to protect what is inside. Because large firms sell in enormous quantities, one change in design has an immediate effect. A supermarket chain that sells fruit and vegetables loose, for instance, removes a great deal of plastic in a single step. Some packaging is necessary, however, since it keeps food fresh and prevents it from being thrown away, so the aim should be to remove the excess.\n\nOthers maintain that companies simply supply what customers are willing to buy. If enough people left heavily wrapped products on the shelf, brought their own bags and chose refillable containers, businesses would have a commercial reason to change. The weakness of this view is that the choice is often missing. When every brand of biscuits is wrapped in the same way, or when goods sold without packaging cost more and are stocked only in specialist shops, a shopper on a tight budget has little power. Consumer pressure works only where an alternative already exists, and it is producers who decide whether to offer one.\n\nShoppers can help by favouring goods with less wrapping when they are available. The main duty, in my view, belongs to manufacturers and retailers, because they alone control how products are packaged.",
"b201":"Young people in many countries spend most of their waking hours in classrooms or preparing for examinations, and public spending reflects the same priority. I agree that governments should also fund leisure activities for the young, although I would not say that education itself receives too much attention.\n\nPublic money for leisure is justified because much of what young people need to learn cannot be taught at a desk. Team sports teach cooperation and how to lose, a youth orchestra demands patience, and a drama club builds confidence in speaking. These are qualities that employers and communities later depend on, yet a timetable built around examinations leaves little room for them. Where nothing is provided, the difference between families becomes obvious. A teenager whose parents can pay for swimming lessons or music tuition has such opportunities anyway, while one from a poorer home may have only a phone screen or the street. Sports grounds, youth centres and subsidised clubs, for example, give the second teenager what the first already has.\n\nIt does not follow, however, that education is overvalued. Qualifications remain the surest route to stable work, and schools in many areas still lack teachers and basic equipment, so money for leisure should not be taken from them. The real problem is that education has come to mean examination results alone. A system that judged schools partly on the sport, art and voluntary work they offer would bring the two aims together and would not force them to compete for funds. Seen in this way, leisure spending completes an education and is not a distraction from it.\n\nMy agreement is therefore with the proposal more than with the complaint behind it. Governments should invest in leisure for young people, but I believe they should do so in addition to strong schooling, not at its expense.",
"b202":"Many shoppers regard advertisements as a form of entertainment or as an irritation, and insist that neither reaction affects what ends up in their basket. I disagree with this claim for the most part, because advertising rarely works by convincing people on the spot. Its influence is quieter than that, which is why it is so easy to deny.\n\nThe effect of an advertisement does not depend on whether the viewer likes it. A jingle that people find irritating is often the one they cannot forget, and a funny commercial is shared and discussed long after it is broadcast. In both cases the brand name becomes familiar, and familiarity is what counts in a shop. Faced with two similar bottles of shampoo, for instance, a customer in a hurry will usually pick the name she recognises, without recalling where she heard it. She would say, quite sincerely, that no advertisement influenced her.\n\nThis is not to say that shoppers are helpless. When a purchase is expensive, such as a car or a laptop, most people read reviews, ask friends and compare prices, and an advertisement is only one voice among many. A person with a fixed budget and a shopping list is also harder to sway than someone browsing for pleasure. Even here, though, advertising has often decided which brands are considered in the first place. The buyer chooses freely, but from a shortlist that marketing helped to write.\n\nPeople are right to say that they do not rush out to buy whatever a commercial tells them to. I believe they are mistaken, however, in concluding that advertising leaves them untouched, since its real work is done long before they reach the till.",
"b203":"A child grows up under many influences, from parents and siblings to classmates, screens and popular culture. While the outside world now reaches children earlier than it used to, I believe the family remains the strongest force, because it shapes how all the other influences are received.\n\nThose who stress outside factors have changing habits on their side. Children today may spend more waking hours with a phone or television than in conversation with their parents, and what they see there affects how they speak, dress and spend their money. Friends matter even more as children approach adolescence. A thirteen-year-old is often more anxious to be accepted by classmates than to please a parent, and will adopt the group's taste in music, its slang and sometimes its attitude to school. Such effects tend, though, to operate on the surface of behaviour, on fashion and preference rather than on character.\n\nThe family's influence runs deeper because it comes first and lasts longest. In the early years, when language, emotional security and basic habits are formed, parents are almost the only source of example. A child who is read to, listened to and treated fairly acquires a confidence and a sense of right and wrong that later pressures rarely erase. Parents also decide, to a large degree, which outside influences a child meets. They choose the neighbourhood and school, and they set limits, for instance on how long a child may play online and with whom. Where parents are absent or indifferent, friends and media fill the space, which shows that their power depends largely on what the family leaves undone.\n\nMedia and friends have gained ground, particularly during the teenage years. On the deeper matters of values and character, however, in my view the home still counts for more than anything a child meets outside it.",
"b204":"Getting pupils to school each morning involves cost and effort, and opinions differ on whether the state or the family should bear them. I believe the main responsibility lies with parents, but that governments should step in wherever distance or poverty would otherwise keep a child out of the classroom.\n\nThe argument for public provision is one of equal access. Education is compulsory in most countries, so it seems reasonable that the authority which requires attendance should make it possible. In rural areas the nearest school may be many kilometres away, and a family without a car has no realistic means of covering that distance twice a day. School buses, such as the yellow buses used across the United States, solve this problem and bring wider benefits too. One bus replaces dozens of cars, which eases congestion and pollution around school gates.\n\nSupporters of parental responsibility reply that children are, in the end, their parents' concern. Mothers and fathers choose where to live and often which school to use, and taxpayers without children may object to funding the consequences of those choices. A walk or cycle ride with a parent also provides exercise and a chance to talk, something a bus cannot offer. Over time, children who travel this way learn to cross roads safely. Although this view is persuasive for families who live close to a school and have time to spare, it works less well for others. Parents whose shifts start before the school opens, for instance, might have to give up paid work to make the journey.\n\nA universal free service would be expensive and, in towns where most pupils live within walking distance, unnecessary. In my view, parents should normally make their own arrangements, while the state guarantees transport for children who live far away or whose families cannot afford it.",
"b205":"Every growing town faces competing demands on the little open land it has left, and tree planting and house building are among the strongest. I agree to a large extent that greenery deserves priority on these sites, not because homes matter less, but because they can usually be built elsewhere.\n\nOpen space in a built-up area is scarce and, once lost, is almost impossible to recover. Trees planted there do work that nothing else in a city can do: they cool the streets in summer, absorb rainwater that would otherwise flood drains, and filter traffic pollution. A wooded park also gives residents of small flats somewhere to walk, play and rest, which matters most to those who cannot afford a garden of their own. Central Park in New York, for example, would be worth a fortune as building land, yet few residents would exchange it for more apartments. These benefits depend, however, on the space being open to the public and properly looked after.\n\nThe need for housing is just as real, and in many cities it is more urgent. What this argument overlooks is that housing does not have to be built on parks and playing fields. Most cities contain abandoned factories, empty offices and car parks that could be redeveloped, and existing districts can often accommodate taller buildings. Using this land first provides homes while keeping the green areas that make dense living bearable. Only where such sites have run out is there a case for building on part of an open space, and even then the loss should be kept small.\n\nHousing and trees are both necessary, but they do not compete for the same land as often as is claimed. For that reason, I believe open spaces should be planted and homes built on land that is already developed.",
"b206":"Public libraries were built for an age in which books were expensive and information was scarce, and neither is true today. For this reason some regard them as an unnecessary expense, while others see them as essential. I believe libraries remain a worthwhile public investment, though their role has changed.\n\nThe case against libraries is easy to state. Most questions can be answered more quickly by a search engine than by a walk to the reference shelves, and novels can be downloaded in seconds. Buildings, staff and stock are costly to maintain, and when local budgets are tight it seems reasonable to ask whether the money would do more good elsewhere. This reasoning assumes, however, that every household has a computer, a reliable connection and a quiet room in which to use them. For a large number of families that is not the case.\n\nIt is these people whom libraries serve best. A teenager who shares a small flat with younger siblings, for instance, may have nowhere to revise except the local reading room. Older residents who must complete official forms online often rely on library computers and helpful staff. Librarians also guide readers towards reliable sources, a service that matters more as poor information spreads. A search engine returns thousands of results without showing which deserve trust, whereas a librarian can direct a student to a sound textbook. While it is true that fewer people now come simply to borrow a book, libraries have responded by offering internet access, study space and children's reading sessions. Technology has altered what they do without making them redundant.\n\nLibraries cost money, but they give people without resources at home a fair chance to learn and to take part in a digital society. That is why, in my opinion, closing them would be a false economy.",
"b207":"Laboratories and universities absorb large amounts of public money, and it is easy to find projects whose purpose seems obscure to an outsider. Some conclude that much of this work is wasteful. I disagree for the most part, because the value of research can rarely be judged at the time it is carried out.\n\nThe main reason for caution is that useful discoveries often come from studies that appeared to have no practical purpose. Scientists who investigated how bacteria defend themselves against viruses, for example, were not trying to cure human disease, yet their findings led to the gene-editing techniques now used in medicine. The physics behind satellite navigation and the mathematics behind secure online payments have similar histories. If funding had been restricted to projects with an obvious application, these advances might not have been made. Dead ends are the unavoidable cost of exploring what is not yet understood, and a failed experiment still tells other researchers where not to look.\n\nThis does not mean that every project deserves its grant. Academic careers depend on publishing frequently, which encourages some researchers to produce minor variations on existing work rather than anything new. Studies are sometimes too small or too poorly designed for their results to be trusted, and others repeat what is already known because the earlier work was overlooked. Money spent in these ways is wasted. The fault lies in how research is rewarded and reviewed, however, and it can be reduced by funding fewer, more rigorous projects and by valuing quality above quantity.\n\nScience is inefficient by nature, since it cannot know in advance which questions will prove fruitful. While some waste could be eliminated through better management, I believe that the great majority of research is a sound investment whose returns arrive later than its critics are willing to wait.",
"b208":"Rising output has lifted enormous numbers of people out of poverty, yet it has also filled the atmosphere with carbon and stripped forests. Some conclude that growth is the only cure for hunger, others that it must be halted to save the environment. I believe neither claim is right as it stands: poorer countries still need growth, but of a cleaner kind.\n\nThe argument for growth rests on the record of countries that have achieved it. In China and Vietnam, for example, decades of expansion in manufacturing and trade turned widespread hunger into a memory for most families. A growing economy creates jobs and generates the tax revenue that pays for clinics, schools and roads. It is too much to call growth the only solution, though. Where the gains are captured by a small elite, national income can rise while the poorest remain hungry, so fair distribution matters as much as the total.\n\nPeople who want growth stopped point to its physical limits. More production has generally meant more fossil fuel burned and more land cleared, and a damaged climate will hurt poor farmers before anyone else. The warning is serious, but the remedy does not follow from it. Freezing the world economy at its present size would leave hundreds of millions without electricity or enough to eat, and would ask those who have polluted least to pay the most. Growth and pollution are also less tightly linked than they were, since solar power and more efficient machinery allow output to rise while emissions fall. The pace of that change depends on governments pricing pollution and investing in clean energy.\n\nOn balance, I think the choice between prosperity and the planet is a false one. Poor nations should continue to grow, and rich ones should bear most of the cost of making that growth clean.",
"b209":"Whether creativity is a universal human capacity or a gift reserved for a few has long been debated. The answer depends largely on what is meant by art. I believe that anyone can create it in a meaningful sense, although work of lasting greatness does seem to require unusual talent.\n\nThe view that all people are capable of art is supported by ordinary experience. Small children draw, sing and invent stories without being taught, which suggests that the impulse to express oneself is part of human nature. Many adults lose this confidence only because they come to compare their efforts with professional standards. Yet drawing or playing an instrument can be learned by most people with patient instruction, much as writing can. A retired office worker who takes up watercolour painting, for instance, may produce pictures that give real pleasure to herself and others. If art is the sincere expression of feeling through a crafted form, it is open to all.\n\nThose who believe in inborn ability point to the rare individuals whose gifts appear early and cannot be explained by teaching. Mozart was composing as a young child, and some people seem to possess an ear for melody or an eye for colour that others acquire slowly, if at all. Such differences do not show, however, that the less gifted cannot create art, only that they are unlikely to create masterpieces. Even natural talent achieves little unless it is developed through years of practice, as the long apprenticeships of most great painters demonstrate.\n\nTalent is unevenly distributed, and it helps to explain why a handful of artists are remembered for centuries. Creating art, however, is not the same as creating great art, and in my view the former is within the reach of any person willing to learn and practise.",
"b210":"Old age has long been associated with decline, and some argue that modern society has made it bleaker still. Others maintain that the elderly are better off than at any time before. I believe the second view is closer to the truth, though the gains have been physical and financial more than social.\n\nThe pessimistic view focuses on what older people have lost. In traditional communities the old were consulted for their knowledge, whereas today their experience counts for less because skills change quickly. A retired engineer may struggle with the devices his grandchildren use without thinking, and societies that prize youth and productivity can make him feel like a burden. The other loss is company. Families are scattered, so many pensioners live alone and see relatives only on holidays, and a person once surrounded by grandchildren may now spend days without conversation. These are real hardships, although describing old age as entirely bad ignores how much else has changed.\n\nThe optimistic view rests on health and security. People now live longer than their grandparents did, and thanks to treatments such as hip replacements and cataract surgery, many of those extra years are active ones. Someone of seventy can often walk, read and drive much as they did at fifty, which was once unusual. Money matters too. State pensions mean that old age no longer leads to poverty or to dependence on one's children, so retirement has become a stage of life in which people travel, study or volunteer. These benefits are unevenly spread, admittedly, and in poorer countries without pensions or public healthcare much less has improved.\n\nGrowing old still brings loss, and loneliness in particular has become more common. On balance, however, I think that longer, healthier and more secure lives make old age today a better experience than it was in the past.",
"b211":"Because efforts to halt global warming have been slow and costly, it is sometimes suggested that humanity should stop trying and simply adjust to a hotter world. I disagree with this proposal. Adaptation is needed, but it works only if warming itself is kept within limits.\n\nThe suggestion has some appeal, and it is not entirely mistaken. Preventing climate change requires nearly every country to transform the way it produces energy, which is expensive and politically difficult, whereas adapting can be done by one city or one farmer at a time. London, for instance, is protected from tidal surges by the Thames Barrier, a project that needed no international treaty. Since the climate has already begun to shift, measures of this kind are plainly necessary. The mistake lies in treating them as an alternative to prevention rather than as a complement to it.\n\nWithout limits on emissions, the conditions to which we must adapt keep moving. A barrier designed for today's sea level will be inadequate in fifty years if the ice sheets continue to melt, and it will have to be rebuilt at greater cost. The same is true of drains and crops chosen for a climate that will not stay still. Some losses cannot be managed at all. Low-lying island states in the Pacific may disappear beneath the water, and regions that become too hot for outdoor work cannot be made habitable by better planning. For the people who live there, adaptation would mean leaving. Abandoning prevention would therefore mean accepting open-ended costs and, in some places, the loss of the land itself.\n\nCoping with the effects of a changing climate is unavoidable, but it cannot replace the effort to slow that change. To give up on prevention would, in my view, commit future generations to a struggle that grows harder every year.",
"b212":"Anyone choosing a language course soon has to decide whether a small group is worth the higher price, or whether class size is beside the point. My own view is that small classes are clearly better for beginners and for speaking practice, though the advantage narrows at higher levels.\n\nSmaller groups help mainly because speaking a new language in public is uncomfortable. Beginners are afraid of making mistakes, and in a class of forty the easiest response is to stay silent and let more confident students answer. In a small group there is nowhere to hide, but there is also less to fear, because the learners come to know one another and the teacher can encourage each of them personally. The teacher also learns their individual weaknesses. One student may confuse tenses, for instance, while another reads well but cannot follow natural speech, and only a teacher with few students can respond to both.\n\nThe opposing argument is that a language is learned mostly outside the classroom. However small the group, a few hours of lessons a week cannot replace regular reading, listening and conversation in the learner's own time. A motivated student in a large class will overtake a lazy one in a small class. This is a fair point, and it applies with particular force to advanced learners, who can study independently. It is less true of beginners. They do not yet know how to practise alone, and depend on the lesson itself for almost all their contact with the language.\n\nClass size is not the only factor in learning a language, and effort outside lessons counts for a great deal. Even so, I think a small class gives learners, especially new ones, the attention and confidence that a large class cannot.",
"b213":"It has become common to argue that the gravest difficulties facing the world are beyond the reach of any single government. I agree with this to a large extent, although the claim that such problems can only be solved jointly overlooks how much still depends on action at home.\n\nThe case for cooperation is strongest where a problem ignores borders. Greenhouse gases released in one country warm the climate of every other, so a state that cuts its emissions alone bears the cost while its neighbours share the benefit. The same logic applies to infectious disease, since a virus left to spread in one region will eventually reach the rest. Agreements such as the Montreal Protocol, under which governments phased out the chemicals destroying the ozone layer, show what coordinated action can achieve. Without a shared commitment of this kind, each country has a reason to wait for the others to act first.\n\nInternational agreements are, however, only as effective as the national measures that follow them. A treaty on emissions changes nothing until individual governments close coal plants, tax fuel or invest in public transport, and these are domestic decisions that voters must accept. Many urgent problems are also local in origin. A shortage of affordable housing, for instance, or a failing school system can be addressed by a single country with its own laws and its own budget. Waiting for global consensus would merely delay the remedy. Cooperation is therefore necessary for some problems rather than for most.\n\nProblems that cross frontiers, such as climate change and pandemics, cannot be solved by one nation acting alone, and on this point I agree with the popular view. Many other pressing issues remain the responsibility of national governments, which should not treat the absence of a global agreement as a reason for inaction.",
"b214":"Statues, fountains and large modern sculptures stand in the squares and parks of most cities, many of them paid for by taxpayers. I agree that this is a reasonable use of public money, as long as the sums are modest and local people have a say in what is chosen.\n\nPublic art deserves support because it is one of the few forms of culture that costs nothing to enjoy. A gallery charges for entry or at least requires a deliberate visit, whereas a sculpture in a square is seen by commuters, children and tourists alike. A successful work can also give a place its identity. The Angel of the North, for instance, a huge steel figure in northern England, was criticised when it was commissioned and is now a symbol of which the region is proud. Works of this kind attract visitors and give residents a sense that their town is worth caring about. Since no private buyer profits from such a piece, only a public body is likely to pay for it.\n\nThe usual objection is that the money would be better spent on hospitals or housing. Although this would be persuasive if art took a large share of the budget, a sculpture in practice costs very little compared with a single stretch of motorway. A more serious concern is that officials may commission expensive pieces that the public dislikes. That risk can be reduced by holding open competitions, displaying the shortlisted designs and letting residents vote, so that the result belongs to the community rather than to a committee. Even so, in a town that lacks clean water or safe roads, art should wait.\n\nPublic sculpture enriches daily life for people who may rarely enter a museum. Governments are right to fund it, I believe, within sensible limits and with public involvement.",
"b215":"Advertisers describe their work as informing the public, while their critics accuse them of selling people things they could happily live without. I agree with the critics for the most part. The central aim of advertising is to raise sales, and most of what it promotes lies well beyond necessity, although 'need' is a less simple idea than the statement assumes.\n\nA glance at what is advertised most heavily supports the claim. The goods that fill commercial breaks and social media feeds are rarely rice, electricity or medicine, which people buy without encouragement. They are fizzy drinks, perfume, fashion and the newest version of a phone that still works perfectly well. To sell these, advertising has to supply a reason that the product itself does not, and so it attaches the item to youth, success or belonging. A campaign suggesting that last year's phone is embarrassing, for example, exists to create a want where none was felt.\n\nThe statement is too sweeping in two respects, however. Some advertising concerns things people do need, such as insurance, housing and food. A supermarket that advertises cheaper bread aims to win customers from a competitor rather than to invent demand, and shoppers gain from knowing its prices. The line between needs and wants also moves. A washing machine was once a luxury, and few households would now call it one. What counts as a need therefore depends on the period, and goods first sold as treats often become ordinary expectations. Advertising becomes a problem only when it pushes people to spend money they do not have.\n\nAdvertising does exist chiefly to sell, and much of what it sells is far from essential. On that basis I agree with the statement, while recognising that wanting more than we need is a human habit that advertisers exploit but did not create.",
"b216":"Plants and animals are disappearing at a rate that alarms biologists, and for some observers this is the gravest environmental threat we face. Others give that place to different dangers, above all the warming of the climate. I believe the second group is right, though the two problems are more closely linked than the debate suggests.\n\nThose who put the loss of species first stress that it cannot be reversed. A polluted river can be cleaned within a generation, but a frog or an orchid that has died out is gone for good, along with whatever it might have offered medicine or agriculture. Each species also supports others. When bees decline, for instance, the fruit trees and crops that depend on them for pollination produce less. The disappearance of particular species can therefore weaken a whole ecosystem. This is a strong argument, although it describes a consequence more often than a cause.\n\nPeople who regard other problems as more serious tend to look for those causes. Climate change, the clearing of forests and the pollution of air and water threaten human life directly, through floods, failed harvests and disease. They are also the main reasons why species are vanishing in the first place. Coral reefs die because the sea is growing warmer and more acidic, and orangutans decline because their forests are cut down for plantations. Efforts to save individual species while these pressures continue may succeed for a time, but they do not address what is killing them. Tackling the underlying damage protects both people and wildlife.\n\nThe extinction of species is a tragedy and a warning of deeper trouble. It is that deeper trouble, particularly a changing climate and the destruction of habitats, which in my view deserves to be called the principal environmental problem of our time.",
"b217":"Wealth means different things to different people: comfort, status, freedom, or the power to do good. I agree with the statement only in part. For those who have far more than they need, helping others is indeed the most valuable use of money, but the first importance of wealth lies in the security it gives its owner.\n\nFor most people, the chief benefit of money is freedom from anxiety. Someone with savings can leave a job that is making them ill, pay for a parent's operation without borrowing, and give their children a good education. These are not selfish luxuries. They are the conditions for a settled life, and a person who has not secured them is in no position to support anyone else. To claim that generosity matters more than this seems to overlook how most families actually experience money.\n\nBeyond a certain point, though, additional wealth adds very little to its owner's wellbeing. A third house or a faster car makes no difference to a person's health or happiness, whereas the same sum could pay for vaccines, scholarships or clean water for thousands. It is here that the statement becomes convincing. Wealthy donors such as Bill Gates have funded campaigns against malaria and polio on a scale that few governments could match, and this is surely a better use of a fortune than further consumption. Giving is not without problems, since large donors can decide which causes deserve attention without being accountable to the public. Even so, the opportunity to change other people's lives is one that only wealth provides.\n\nThe importance of money therefore depends on how much of it a person has. I believe its first value is security for oneself and one's family, and that the chance to help others matters most only once that security is assured.",
"b218":"Behind most arguments about what schools should teach lies a deeper disagreement about what education is for. One view holds that it exists to produce useful citizens, and another that it exists to help each person pursue his or her own goals. I believe these aims conflict far less than is usually assumed, and that where they do, the individual should come first.\n\nThe social view starts from who pays. Schools and universities are funded largely by taxpayers, and it seems reasonable that the public should receive something in return. That return is a workforce with the skills the economy needs and citizens who take part in public life. Taken too far, though, this view treats students as resources to be allocated. A young person steered into a career only because the country needs it is unlikely to do the work well or to stay in it for long.\n\nThe individual view holds that education should allow people to discover what they are good at and to go as far as their abilities permit. A pupil with a gift for music or a passion for marine biology should be helped to follow it, whether or not it meets an obvious national need. Although this position can sound self-centred, personal ambition is usually how society is served. People who choose their own path tend to work harder, and their achievements, such as new businesses, medical advances and works of art, benefit others as well. Ambition does need a foundation, which is why literacy, numeracy and an understanding of civic duties should be common to all.\n\nA society made up of capable, fulfilled individuals is a well-served society. Education should therefore equip students to pursue their own ambitions, which is, in my view, also the most reliable way of making them useful to others.",
"b219":"Appeals to save endangered creatures are a familiar feature of television and school lessons, and there are people who think this concern is out of proportion when so many humans are in need. I disagree with them, because protecting wildlife is in most cases a way of protecting people as well.\n\nThe opinion in question assumes that money spent on animals is money lost to humans, and this is rarely so. Healthy populations of wild species perform services that would be very expensive to replace. When vultures almost vanished from India, for instance, animal carcasses were left to rot and the number of stray dogs rose, bringing a greater risk of rabies to nearby villages. Wildlife is also a source of income. In Kenya and Tanzania, visitors who come to see lions and elephants support hotels, guides and drivers, and the revenue from national parks helps to pay for public services. Conservation in such cases is an investment with a human return.\n\nThere are situations in which the criticism deserves a hearing. Families who farm beside a protected area may lose crops to elephants or livestock to big cats, and they sometimes receive little compensation while outsiders praise the park. If conservation ignores these neighbours, it will seem to value animals above people, and it will also fail, since resentful communities have no reason to discourage poaching. The remedy is to share the benefits with those who live closest to the wildlife, through jobs and a portion of the entrance fees. That is an argument about how the resources are used rather than about whether they are excessive.\n\nWild animals and birds are sometimes protected in ways that are insensitive to local people, but their protection is not overfunded. The attention it receives is, in my opinion, justified and ought if anything to increase.",
"b220":"Human beings have relied on other species for food, clothing, labour and companionship for as long as records exist. Whether this reliance is legitimate or a form of exploitation is now widely debated. In my view the use of animals is defensible when it meets a real human need and avoids unnecessary suffering, but not otherwise.\n\nPeople who accept the use of animals argue that it is part of how human societies survive. In much of the world, livestock provides protein that poor soils could not supply through crops, and oxen or horses still plough fields where tractors are unaffordable. Guide dogs give blind people independence, and herding communities such as the Maasai depend on their cattle for almost everything. From this perspective, using animals is no more immoral than a wolf hunting a deer, provided that the animals are treated decently while they are alive. The weakness of the comparison is that humans, unlike wolves, usually have a choice.\n\nThat choice is the starting point for the opposing view. Its supporters hold that animals are capable of pain and distress and therefore have interests of their own, which should not be sacrificed for human convenience. They point to practices in which the benefit is trivial and the suffering considerable, such as keeping hens in cages too small to turn round in, or breeding mink for fur coats. In wealthy countries, where alternatives to most animal products are readily available, this objection carries real force. It is less convincing when applied to a subsistence farmer, for whom giving up animals would mean giving up a livelihood.\n\nBoth positions contain something true. I think the sensible conclusion is that necessity and humane treatment mark the boundary: uses that sustain life or health can be justified, whereas those that merely provide luxury or amusement cannot.",
"b221":"Adults today can teach themselves almost anything from online videos, from cooking to car repair, and many do so with success. Whether this is preferable to attending a class with an instructor depends partly on the skill in question. On the whole, I believe a teacher is the better choice for skills that are complex or carry some risk, while self-teaching suits simpler ones.\n\nLearning alone has obvious attractions for busy adults. It costs little, it can be fitted around work and family, and the learner can repeat a step as often as necessary without holding anyone back. Someone who wants to bake bread, edit photographs or put up shelves can reach a good standard this way. The limitation is that a video cannot watch the learner. Mistakes in technique go uncorrected, and a person may practise a bad habit for months without realising it.\n\nThis is where a teacher makes the difference. An instructor observes what the learner is actually doing, identifies the fault and demonstrates the correction, which can save a great deal of wasted effort. Feedback of this kind matters most where errors are dangerous or expensive, such as in driving, first aid or electrical work. A class also imposes a timetable. Many adults who would abandon a course of self-study after a few weeks keep attending because they have paid a fee and others expect them. Classroom learning is admittedly slower and less convenient, although these costs are small compared with the benefit of being taught correctly from the start.\n\nIndependent learning is a valuable option that technology has made far easier. For any skill in which accuracy or safety matters, however, I think adults learn more quickly and more reliably from a teacher than they do alone.",
"b222":"Nations such as South Korea and Japan are often cited as proof that long hours build prosperous economies. I agree that this way of working carries a heavy social price, and I would add that its contribution to economic success is smaller than is commonly assumed.\n\nThe social costs are most visible in family life. When both parents return home late, children are raised largely by grandparents, childminders or screens, and couples have little time for each other. Many young adults in such societies conclude that a career and a family cannot be combined, which helps to explain why birth rates in East Asia have fallen so low. Health suffers too. People who work late sleep less, exercise less and are more prone to stress-related illness, which adds to the burden on hospitals. These effects fall on the whole society, because an ageing population with few children eventually has too few workers to support it.\n\nIt is true that long hours have accompanied rapid growth in several countries, and during the early stages of industrial development extra labour does raise output. The relationship weakens, however, as an economy matures. Germany and the Netherlands, for instance, have some of the shortest working years in the developed world and remain highly productive, because output per hour matters more than the number of hours. Tired employees make more mistakes and have fewer good ideas, so the final hours of a long day add little. Although hard work contributed to these countries' rise, their present success owes more to education, technology and investment than to time spent in the office.\n\nLong working hours damage families, health and, in the end, the population on which an economy depends. I therefore agree with the view expressed, and I believe that wealthy countries could shorten the working day without becoming poorer.",
"b223":"Skydiving, rock climbing and similar pursuits attract growing numbers of people who enjoy testing themselves against height, speed or the natural world. I disagree with the suggestion that they should be prohibited, because the risk involved is smaller and more manageable than it appears.\n\nExtreme sports look more dangerous than they usually are. A first-time skydiver jumps attached to an instructor, with a reserve parachute and a device that opens it automatically. Climbers on a rock face are secured by ropes and anchors that are checked before every ascent. Accidents do happen, and they are widely reported because they are dramatic. Yet ordinary activities such as cycling in city traffic or riding a horse also injure many people without prompting calls for a ban. What separates a safe climb from a fatal one is generally preparation, which suggests that the sensible response is to insist on training and certified equipment rather than to forbid the activity.\n\nThese sports also give participants something of real worth. Standing at the door of an aircraft or halfway up a cliff requires a person to control fear, to concentrate and to trust a partner. Many say that this experience gives them confidence in the rest of their lives. There is an economic benefit as well. Tourism built on such activities supports mountain and coastal communities that have few other sources of income. In Queenstown, New Zealand, for example, many jobs depend on such visitors. None of this means that any risk is acceptable, however. Operators who ignore weather warnings or employ unqualified instructors should lose their licences, since customers cannot judge these matters for themselves.\n\nExtreme sports are dangerous mainly when they are badly organised. Since that can be dealt with through regulation, I believe a ban would be both unnecessary and unfair to those who take part responsibly.",
"b224":"Vegetarianism has moved from the margins to the mainstream, and its supporters argue that meat is simply unnecessary. I accept that most adults can live well without it. Even so, I disagree that all people should give it up, because what is possible for some is not practical or desirable for the whole world.\n\nThe premise of the argument is largely sound. Protein can be obtained from beans, lentils, nuts and dairy products, and millions of people in India have followed vegetarian diets for generations without ill effects. The environment gives a further reason, since raising livestock uses far more land and water than growing crops. Land freed in this way could feed people directly. For a city dweller with a supermarket nearby, vegetarianism is a reasonable and healthy choice, provided that nutrients such as iron and vitamin B12 are replaced.\n\nIt does not follow, however, that the same choice should be required of all. Much of the world's population lives where a varied plant diet is not available. Herding communities on dry grassland, for example, or fishing villages in the Arctic, depend on animals because few crops will grow there. Poor families in developing countries often rely on small amounts of meat, eggs or fish to prevent malnutrition in their children. Food is also bound up with culture. Festival and wedding dishes often centre on meat, so a universal rule would be resented and widely ignored. A more realistic aim is moderation. Eating meat less often and in smaller portions would bring most of the health and environmental gains.\n\nMeat may not be essential to a healthy diet, but that fact alone does not justify asking the whole of humanity to abandon it. On balance, I think reducing consumption is a wiser goal than universal vegetarianism.",
"b225":"Health policy is usually discussed in terms of hospitals, doctors and medicines, all of which deal with people once they are already ill. I agree to a large extent that governments would prevent more disease by paying greater attention to the air citizens breathe and the homes they live in. These are not, however, the only causes that need addressing.\n\nPolluted surroundings and poor housing make people ill in ways that no clinic can undo. Fumes from traffic and factories damage the lungs and the heart over many years, and children who grow up beside busy roads are more likely to develop asthma. Housing has a similar effect. A damp, overcrowded flat spreads infections such as tuberculosis from one family member to the next, and a home that cannot be kept warm in winter puts elderly residents at particular risk. Because these conditions affect whole neighbourhoods, improving them protects large numbers of people at once. London's clean air laws of the 1950s, for example, ended the smogs that had killed thousands.\n\nIt would be a mistake, however, to treat the environment as the answer to every illness. Many of the most common diseases in richer countries, including diabetes and several cancers, are linked more closely to diet, smoking and lack of exercise than to pollution or housing. Vaccination and screening programmes also prevent a great deal of disease at low cost. A child immunised against measles is protected wherever the family lives, so this funding must continue. A sensible government would therefore shift its priorities towards environmental causes rather than devote itself to them alone.\n\nCleaner air and decent housing would prevent much of the ill health that medicine currently struggles to treat. For this reason I believe they deserve a larger share of public effort, alongside vaccination and measures that encourage healthier habits.",
"b226":"Almost any fact can now be found on a phone within seconds, and this has led some to question whether public libraries still deserve taxpayers' money. I believe they do. The internet has changed what libraries are for, but it has not made them unnecessary.\n\nThose who regard libraries as wasteful have a simple argument. Buildings, staff and stock are expensive, while a search engine answers most questions instantly and at no charge to the state. Reference books that once filled whole rooms have been replaced by websites, and fewer people borrow printed novels than a generation ago. If a service is used less each year, critics say, the funds would do more good in schools or hospitals. This view is reasonable as far as simple information is concerned, but it treats a library as nothing more than a store of facts.\n\nSupporters see a wider purpose. Much of what is valuable to read is not free online. Recent books, academic journals and newspaper archives usually sit behind a paywall, and a library pays the subscription once for the whole community. A student or a small business owner who needs a single article would otherwise pay a fee that few can justify. Free borrowing matters most to families who cannot afford to buy books. A child whose parents have little spare income, for example, can still take home a new pile of stories every week and develop the habit of reading. No website offers that experience in the same way, although libraries will keep their place only if they continue to adapt, lending e-books and teaching digital skills.\n\nAccess to the internet is not the same as access to knowledge. Free public libraries remain, in my view, a sound investment, because they give every citizen the books and resources that the web alone does not supply.",
"b227":"History is sometimes dismissed as a record of dead rulers and distant battles with no bearing on modern concerns. Others regard it as essential to making sense of the world we live in. I believe the second view is far closer to the truth, although the lessons of the past are seldom simple.\n\nSceptics argue that the modern world is too different from earlier ages for comparisons to be useful. Societies without electricity, let alone the internet, faced problems unlike those of climate change or artificial intelligence, and time spent on them might be better given to science or technical skills. They also point out that the past is easily misused, since politicians often choose whichever historical parallel suits their purpose. A leader seeking support for a war can usually find an earlier conflict that seems to justify it. These warnings are fair. They show that history should be studied carefully, however, not that it should be ignored.\n\nThe stronger argument is that the present cannot be explained without the past. Current borders, laws, languages and conflicts are the outcome of earlier events. It is impossible to understand why English is widely spoken in India, for example, or why so many African frontiers run in straight lines, without some knowledge of colonial rule. A citizen who knows this background is better placed to judge the news and less likely to be persuaded by simple slogans. Disputes within a country over land or language usually have equally long roots. It is an explanation of how we arrived where we are rather than a set of instructions for what to do next.\n\nThe past rarely repeats itself exactly, and those who expect ready-made answers from it will be disappointed. As a means of understanding why the present looks as it does, though, history is in my opinion indispensable.",
"b228":"The right to stage an Olympic Games or a football World Cup is fiercely contested, yet the cities that win it do not always look back on the experience with pleasure. Both the enthusiasm and the doubts are understandable. I believe the drawbacks are usually greater, unless the host already has most of the facilities it needs.\n\nSupporters of hosting point to the attention and investment it brings. For several weeks the country is watched by a global audience, which can raise its profile as a place to visit and do business. A city that was little known abroad may find itself on travellers' lists for years afterwards. The deadline also forces governments to complete projects that might otherwise be delayed for years. Barcelona, for example, used the 1992 Olympics to rebuild its neglected waterfront and has attracted far more tourists ever since.\n\nOpponents reply that these gains seldom match the bill. Stadiums, athletes' villages and security cost enormous sums, budgets are routinely exceeded, and the debt is repaid by taxpayers long after the visitors have left. Many venues have little use afterwards. Several arenas built for the Athens Olympics and for the World Cup in Brazil now stand largely empty, while local residents were moved to make room for them. Maintaining a stadium that is seldom used continues to drain public funds each year. Although a well-planned event can avoid some of these problems, such planning has been the exception.\n\nHosting is worthwhile for a country that can reuse existing stadiums and transport, as Los Angeles did in 1984. For most others, in my view, the money would do more lasting good if it were spent directly on the housing, railways and local sports grounds that the event is supposed to deliver.",
"b229":"Rockets and space probes are among the most expensive machines ever built, and critics argue that the funds would be better used on Earth. I disagree with this view. Money invested in space technology has repaid itself many times over in practical benefits, and it is a much smaller share of public spending than its critics suppose.\n\nThe strongest justification is that technology developed for space is now part of ordinary life. Satellites carry telephone calls and television signals, guide ships, aircraft and delivery drivers, and allow forecasters to warn coastal towns days before a storm arrives. Farmers use satellite images to decide when to irrigate, and scientists use them to measure melting ice and shrinking forests. None of this was the original purpose of the early space programmes, which shows how difficult it is to predict where research will lead. A government that had cancelled its space budget in the 1960s as unjustifiable would have deprived its citizens of all these services.\n\nThe objection that the money should go to other needs, such as hospitals or poverty relief, assumes that one must be chosen over the other. In most countries, however, space agencies receive a tiny part of the national budget compared with health, pensions or defence, so closing them would make little difference to those services. The funds are also spent on Earth, on the salaries of engineers and the contracts of manufacturers. Those trained this way take their skills to industries such as aviation or computing. Not every project deserves support, admittedly. Missions designed mainly for national prestige, with little scientific purpose, are harder to defend when public finances are under strain.\n\nSpace exploration does not compete with human welfare so much as contribute to it. I believe the investment remains justified, provided that funding favours projects with clear scientific or practical value.",
"b230":"The gap between what executives and ordinary employees earn has widened in many companies, and opinions differ on whether this is healthy. I agree that senior managers should be paid more than other staff, but I disagree that a very large difference is good for a company.\n\nHigher pay at the top reflects responsibility. A chief executive makes decisions that affect thousands of jobs, answers to shareholders and is held accountable when things go wrong. Few people have the experience and judgement to do this well, so firms must offer attractive salaries to recruit them and to keep them from moving to competitors. The prospect of such rewards also gives ambitious employees a reason to seek promotion. A capable engineer or sales manager is more willing to accept the pressure of leadership if it is well paid. These arguments justify a substantial premium. They do not, however, explain why a manager should earn vastly more than the people who make or sell the company's products.\n\nWhen the gap becomes extreme, it begins to do harm. Employees who see their own wages held down while directors receive large bonuses are likely to feel that their contribution is not valued, and this resentment reduces effort and loyalty. The damage is greatest when the rewards bear no relation to results. A bank whose executives collect bonuses in a year of heavy losses, for example, tells its staff that pay at the top depends on position rather than performance. Although a company cannot pay all its staff the same, it succeeds through cooperation, and cooperation is hard to sustain when rewards seem unfair.\n\nA difference in pay is reasonable, and a generous one may be necessary. It serves a company well, I believe, only when it stays within limits that the rest of the workforce can accept as deserved.",
"b231":"Laws differ so widely between countries that an act permitted in one may lead to prison in another. Some people would replace this variety with one legal code for the whole world, while others defend each nation's right to make its own. I believe countries should keep their own laws, within a framework of shared rules on matters that cross borders.\n\nA single global system has obvious attractions. Businesses trading internationally would no longer need to study dozens of regulations, and travellers would know what was expected of them wherever they went. A small exporter could sell in many markets without paying lawyers in each. The other attraction is that criminals could not escape justice by moving to a country with weaker laws. Fraud and tax evasion, which now exploit the gaps between national systems, would be far harder to hide. Although these are real advantages, they assume that the world could agree on what the common law should say.\n\nThat assumption is the weakness of the proposal. Law grows out of a society's history, religion and values, and these differ profoundly. Countries disagree, for example, about the death penalty, the drinking age and the rules of inheritance, and no single answer would be accepted by all of them. Unless citizens regard a law as their own, they are unlikely to respect it, and enforcing it would require a world government that hardly anyone wants. Where common rules are needed, as with war crimes, aviation or trade, treaties already provide them without abolishing national systems.\n\nUniform law would bring convenience at the cost of consent. The present arrangement seems to me the better one, and I believe each country should make its own laws while international agreements cover the limited areas in which nations must act together.",
"b232":"Transport budgets are finite, and governments must decide how much to devote to roads for private vehicles and how much to shared systems like trains and trams. I disagree that roads should come first, at least in and around cities, where most journeys are made.\n\nThe case for roads is strongest outside urban areas. Goods travel largely by lorry, emergency services need direct routes, and villages are too scattered for a railway to serve them economically. A farmer taking produce to market, for example, has no realistic alternative to a road, and a country whose rural highways are poor will pay for it in higher prices and isolated communities. Spending of this kind is clearly justified. Within cities the argument weakens, because new road space tends to attract additional traffic until it is as congested as before.\n\nRailways and trams, by contrast, move far more people in far less space. A single metro line can carry as many passengers as a motorway many lanes wide, without requiring a parking space for each of them at the other end. Tokyo, for instance, functions as one of the largest cities in the world largely because most of its commuters arrive by train. Public transport also serves those who cannot drive, including teenagers, older residents and people who cannot afford a car. Without it, they depend on lifts from others or are cut off from jobs and hospitals. Although rail projects are costly and take years to complete, they go on carrying passengers for generations, whereas a widened road may be full again within a few years.\n\nRoads remain essential, and a sensible government will maintain them and extend them where the countryside needs them. As far as cities are concerned, though, I believe public transport offers a better return on public money and should receive the larger share.",
"b233":"A common complaint about the news is that it describes distant events which make no difference to an ordinary person's day. From this, some conclude that reading newspapers and watching bulletins is time thrown away. I disagree, because a great deal of news bears directly on the decisions people have to make, even if not all of it does.\n\nThe belief that news is irrelevant overlooks how often it has practical consequences. Reports about a rise in interest rates, a change in tax rules or a new housing law tell people something they can use when they borrow, save or rent. Local news is more immediate still. A resident who learns that the council plans to close a nearby school, for instance, has time to object before the decision is final. Beyond these personal uses, news is what allows citizens to judge those who govern them. Voters who follow no news can only choose between candidates on the basis of rumour or habit, which weakens the whole system.\n\nThe criticism is fair, however, when it is directed at a certain kind of coverage. Stories about celebrity divorces, or endless speculation about events that have not yet happened, add little to anyone's understanding, and an hour spent on them probably is wasted. Rolling news channels repeat the same few facts all day, which can leave viewers anxious without leaving them better informed. The sensible response is to be selective rather than to give up. A short time each day with a reliable source will usually cover what a person needs to know.\n\nNot every headline deserves attention, but that is a reason to choose carefully. I believe following the news remains worthwhile, since it is the main way people learn about decisions that will affect them before it is too late to respond.",
"b234":"Public money for science is limited, and deciding where to direct it is a difficult choice. Some would concentrate research on disease and other global health problems, whereas others regard different challenges as more urgent. My view is that health deserves a leading share of funding, but not an exclusive one.\n\nHealth has an obvious claim to come first. Illness causes suffering in every society, and medical research has a clear record of reducing it. Vaccines have removed smallpox from the world and brought polio close to elimination, and the rapid development of vaccines against Covid-19 showed what concentrated effort can achieve. Diseases such as malaria and tuberculosis still kill large numbers of people in poorer countries, largely because they attract less commercial investment. Few would dispute that solving these problems is a worthy aim. Medicine alone cannot secure human wellbeing, however.\n\nThose who see other priorities argue that some threats endanger health and much else besides. Climate change is the clearest case. Rising temperatures threaten harvests, water supplies and coastal cities, and research into clean energy and resilient crops may prevent more deaths than any single drug. Basic science also deserves support, although its benefits cannot be predicted. The physicists who first studied how atoms behave in magnetic fields had no medical purpose in mind, yet their work made the hospital scanner possible. A policy that funded only health research would weaken the very fields on which future medical advances depend.\n\nHealth research should remain among the highest priorities, since its results relieve suffering directly. I believe, however, that science serves humanity best when funding is spread across several fields, including energy, agriculture and fundamental inquiry.",
"b235":"Most people know of someone who smoked, ate badly and still lived to ninety, and such cases lead some to decide that healthy habits make little difference. I disagree. Although genes and luck influence how long we live, I believe diet and exercise remain the most reliable means of staying well.\n\nSceptics about healthy living are right about one thing: it offers no guarantee. Some people inherit a strong constitution and reach old age despite poor habits, while others who eat carefully and exercise daily develop cancer in middle age. These facts show that lifestyle is not the only influence on lifespan. They do not show that it is unimportant, any more than a careful driver's accident proves that caution on the road is pointless.\n\nFor the great majority, what they eat and how much they move strongly affect both the length and the quality of life. Conditions such as heart disease, type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure are closely tied to excess weight and inactivity, and they are far more common where diets are rich in sugar and processed food. The Japanese island of Okinawa, for example, became known for its many healthy elderly residents, who traditionally ate vegetables, fish and modest portions and remained active into old age. The quality of those later years matters as much as their number. A person who has stayed fit is more likely to remain independent and mobile, whereas medicine can prolong life without restoring vigour.\n\nExceptions exist, and no way of living can remove the influence of heredity or chance. They remain exceptions, though, and I believe that for nearly all of us a balanced diet and regular exercise are necessary for a long life that is also a healthy one.",
"b236":"Adults spend so much of their week at work that colleagues easily become their main company. Some welcome this, while others prefer to keep friendship separate from employment. In my view, the workplace is a natural source of friends, but it is unwise to depend on it alone.\n\nWork brings people together in a way that few other settings do. Colleagues see each other daily, share the same pressures and often have similar education and interests, so friendships form with little effort. For someone who has moved to a new city for a job, for example, the office may be the only place to meet people at first. Such friends also understand the demands of the job in a way that outsiders rarely do, which makes their support especially useful. The limitation is that these friendships are tied to the job. When a person resigns, retires or is made redundant, many of them fade quickly.\n\nThis fragility is the main reason why some prefer to build their social lives elsewhere. Friends made through a sports club, a choir or a neighbourhood are not affected by a change of employer, and they offer a break from talk about deadlines and managers. There is a question of freedom too. With colleagues, people are often careful about what they say, because a careless remark or a quarrel can have consequences at work, whereas old friends can be spoken to frankly. Keeping the two worlds apart is not easy for all workers, however. Those who work long hours or irregular shifts may have little time left to meet anyone else.\n\nFriendship at work is valuable and should not be avoided. I think the healthiest arrangement, though, is to have a circle outside it as well, so that a person's social life does not end when the job does.",
"b237":"Prison is the punishment most people think of first, and some would apply it to every offence, however small. Others favour penalties such as unpaid community work for minor crimes. I think the second view is the more sensible, as custody for petty offenders tends to do more harm than good.\n\nThose who want all lawbreakers jailed argue from deterrence and equality. If shoplifters and vandals knew that a cell awaited them, fewer would take the risk, and the public would see that the law treats every offence seriously. There is something in this, since a penalty that is regarded as trivial earns little respect. In practice, though, the policy would be hard to defend. Prisons are costly and in many countries already overcrowded, and filling them with people who have dropped litter would leave less room and less money for dangerous offenders.\n\nCommunity penalties avoid these costs and often achieve more. Someone ordered to spend his weekends cleaning graffiti or maintaining a public park repays the neighbourhood he has harmed, and he does so in full view of it. Residents see that the offence has had a consequence. He also keeps his job, his home and his family ties, which are the things most likely to prevent a second offence. A short prison term, by contrast, can cost a person all three, and it introduces first-time offenders to experienced ones. Such alternatives work only if they are properly enforced. An offender who fails to turn up for work, for example, should know that a stricter penalty will follow.\n\nImprisonment should be kept for those who are a real danger to others or who repeatedly ignore lighter penalties. For minor crimes, in my view, supervised work in the community punishes adequately, costs less and gives the offender a better chance of staying out of trouble.",
"b238":"Charities have limited funds, and deciding whose suffering to relieve first is among the hardest choices they face. One view holds that they should look after their fellow citizens only, another that need alone should guide them. I believe the second principle is the right one, although it does not require every charity to work abroad.\n\nSupporters of charity at home start from the idea that obligations are strongest towards those closest to us. Donors can see local problems for themselves, and a charity running a shelter in its own city understands the people it serves and can check that its money is well used. Aid sent overseas, by contrast, passes through several organisations and may be lost to waste or corruption. It also seems odd to some donors to fund projects on another continent while people are sleeping on the streets nearby. These points explain why local charities deserve support, but they do not show that foreign need should be ignored.\n\nThe argument for giving wherever need is greatest rests on the equal worth of human lives. A child dying of malaria or hunger is in no less urgent a position because of the passport he or she holds. Money also goes much further in poor countries. The cost of one night in a shelter in a wealthy city could, for instance, buy mosquito nets for several families in rural Africa. A donation that changes little at home may therefore protect many lives abroad. Such work succeeds only if charities cooperate with local partners rather than imposing their own plans.\n\nBorders are a poor guide to who deserves help. Some charities will sensibly remain local, yet in my view the sector as a whole should direct its resources to where suffering is most severe.",
"b239":"Sorting rubbish and avoiding waste are habits, and habits are easiest to form in childhood. People disagree about who should form them: teachers in the classroom or parents in the kitchen. I believe both are needed, but that what happens at home decides whether the lesson lasts.\n\nSchools have clear advantages as a place to teach these things. A teacher reaches every child in the class, including those whose parents have little interest in the subject, and can explain why waste matters and not merely what to do with it. Pupils who learn where a plastic bottle ends up, or how much energy goes into making an aluminium can, understand the purpose behind the coloured bins. Many schools reinforce this with practical schemes, such as composting leftover food from the canteen. Knowledge of this kind is valuable, although it does not by itself change behaviour, since a child may pass a test on recycling and still drop litter.\n\nBehaviour is shaped mostly at home, which is the basis of the opposing view. Children copy what they see adults do every day, far more than what they are told once a week. A girl who watches her parents repair a broken chair, take their own bags to the market and finish the food they cook absorbs the idea that things should not be thrown away lightly. No lesson is required. The limit of this approach is that it depends on the parents, and those who are wasteful themselves will pass on the opposite habit.\n\nSchools can give every child the reasons for recycling, and they should. Whether those reasons become routine, however, depends on daily practice in the family, which is why I think the home has the greater influence.",
"b240":"Every year people are seriously hurt in boxing rings, on rugby pitches and on ski slopes, which leads some to argue that dangerous sports ought to be outlawed. Others maintain that people should be free to pursue whatever sport they enjoy. I think adults should keep that freedom, although children deserve greater protection.\n\nPeople who favour a ban are chiefly concerned with harm that cannot be undone. A broken leg heals, but repeated blows to the head, which are common in boxing and in contact sports such as rugby, may lead to memory loss and other brain disorders later in life. It is argued that a sport whose aim, or regular result, is injury to the brain has no place in a civilised society. This concern is especially strong in the case of the young, who start such sports before they are able to weigh the long-term consequences.\n\nThe contrary view is that a competent adult is the best judge of his or her own interests. Many activities that give life meaning involve danger, and a society that removed all of them would be safer but much narrower. Boxing clubs, for instance, have given discipline and purpose to young people in poor districts who might otherwise have drifted into crime. The right to choose, however, depends on knowing what is being chosen. Sports bodies therefore have a duty to tell players honestly about the risks and to change the rules when evidence of harm emerges, as rugby has done by limiting high tackles.\n\nBoth sides are responding to something real, namely serious injury on the one hand and personal liberty on the other. On balance, I believe informed adults should decide for themselves, while contact in children's sport should be restricted until they are old enough to do the same.",
"b241":"Dress codes have relaxed in many workplaces, and some argue that employers should abandon them and judge staff by results. I agree with this only in part. For work done out of public view the argument is sound, but in many roles appearance is part of the job.\n\nWhere employees have little contact with outsiders, clothing has no real bearing on what they produce. A programmer writes the same code in a T-shirt as in a suit, and probably writes it more comfortably. Insisting on formal dress in such settings costs staff money and suggests that the company values appearances over ability. Many technology firms have dropped their dress codes for this reason, without any visible loss of quality. Even here, though, some basic expectations remain, since clothing that offends or distracts colleagues affects the working atmosphere.\n\nThe situation changes once staff deal with the public. Customers and clients form an impression of an organisation within moments of meeting its representatives, and they do so largely on the basis of how those people look. A lawyer who arrives in court in beach clothes, for example, may be highly capable, yet the client will have reason to doubt how seriously the case is being taken. In such professions, dressing appropriately is one of the ways in which good work is delivered. Other rules exist for practical reasons. Uniforms allow passengers to identify airline staff, and protective clothing in kitchens, hospitals and factories prevents injury and infection. An employer who ignored these matters would be failing in a duty rather than showing respect for individual freedom.\n\nQuality of work should be the main test of an employee, and rigid rules about clothing are often unnecessary. Employers are nevertheless entitled, in my view, to set standards wherever dress affects safety or the confidence of the people they serve.",
"b242":"Whether the state has any business telling people how to spend their leisure time is an old dispute, and dangerous sports bring it into sharp focus. One side wants such activities banned, while the other claims a right to do anything at all. In my view, the correct line lies where the danger begins to fall on other people.\n\nThe demand for a ban usually follows a tragedy. When a young person dies in a motorcycle race or a diving accident, it seems reasonable to ask why the activity was permitted. Families are left to grieve, and children may lose a parent. Advocates of prohibition also note that the thrill of danger can be addictive, so that participants take ever greater risks until something goes wrong. Their choice is then less free than it appears. These are heavy costs. They do not, however, distinguish sport from other lawful choices, such as riding a motorbike to work, that adults are trusted to make.\n\nSupporters of freedom reply that a life without risk is not what most people want. They are right that competent adults should be allowed to ski off marked runs, dive in deep caves or fight in a ring, since the person who may be hurt is the person who chose. The principle becomes weaker once the word 'any' is taken literally. Racing cars on public roads, for example, or jumping with a parachute from a city tower, endangers passers-by who agreed to nothing. Activities of this kind are rightly illegal because the harm falls on strangers rather than on the participants alone.\n\nA blanket ban on dangerous sports would treat adults as children, yet unlimited freedom would ignore the safety of bystanders. I believe, therefore, that governments should permit sports whose risks are borne by willing participants and prohibit only those that threaten others.",
"b243":"Public budgets for sport are small, and every pound spent on an Olympic training centre is a pound not spent on a neighbourhood swimming pool. Both uses have their defenders. I believe the larger share should go to facilities for the general public, with a smaller amount reserved for those who compete at the highest level.\n\nPeople who favour elite funding argue that champions benefit the whole nation. International competition is now so intense that talent is not enough: athletes need specialised tracks, pools, coaches and medical support, which few could pay for themselves. When they win, the country gains prestige and a moment of shared pride. Their success is also said to inspire children to take up sport. A girl who watches a compatriot win a gold medal in gymnastics, for example, may ask to join a club the following week. This effect is real but short-lived unless there is an affordable club for her to join.\n\nThat condition is the heart of the opposing view. Facilities for ordinary people, such as local pitches, public pools and cycle paths, are used by far greater numbers, and their main benefit is health. Regular exercise reduces obesity, heart disease and depression, and so lowers the cost of medical care for all taxpayers. There is a link between the two levels as well, since future champions are discovered in local clubs and school teams, not in national academies. A country with few public pools will produce few swimmers. While this does not make elite centres unnecessary, it suggests that they depend on a broad base.\n\nMedals bring a country brief glory, whereas public facilities improve health year after year. Governments should, in my view, treat sport for ordinary citizens as the priority and fund elite training as a modest addition to it.",
"b244":"Cities compete fiercely for the right to stage the Olympic Games or a continental championship, yet residents are often less enthusiastic than their leaders. Hosting brings a country both opportunities and burdens. I believe it is beneficial on the whole, but only when the event is planned around what the host will need afterwards.\n\nSupporters see such an event as a rare chance to transform a city and its reputation. Preparations give governments a deadline for projects that might otherwise be postponed for decades, such as new metro lines, airports and housing. Barcelona, for example, used the 1992 Olympics to rebuild its neglected seafront, and has attracted visitors in large numbers since. There are less tangible gains as well. A successful tournament shows the country to a global audience and gives citizens a shared source of pride. Tourists and investors may consider the city for the first time. These effects are hard to measure, although few who have lived through such a summer doubt them.\n\nOpponents reply that residents bear the disruption and see little in return. Years of construction bring noise, traffic and rising rents, and in some host cities poorer communities have been moved to make room for venues. During the event itself, security restrictions and crowds make daily life difficult. Afterwards, stadiums built for a few weeks of competition may stand empty, as several did in Athens after 2004. This criticism is fair where organisers build for prestige rather than for use. It carries less weight where facilities are designed to be converted into homes, parks or community sports centres.\n\nA sports event is neither a blessing nor a curse in itself. Its value depends on preparation, and in my view a country that invests in lasting improvements, and resists building monuments, will find that hosting does more good than harm.",
"b245":"Modern life presents an apparent contradiction. People rely on vast networks of strangers for almost everything they use, and at the same time they need their relatives and neighbours less than their grandparents did. Both observations are accurate, but I believe that our dependence has grown rather than diminished.\n\nThe case for greater independence concerns personal relationships. In the past, a young adult could rarely survive without the support of family, and an elderly person without children faced real hardship. Salaries, pensions, insurance and public services now allow individuals to live alone, move to another city or leave an unhappy marriage without being ruined. Technology has added to this self-reliance. Someone who needs directions, a recipe or a simple repair can find instructions online instead of asking a neighbour. In this sense, people are freer of one another than they have been before.\n\nThis freedom rests, however, on a much deeper reliance on people we do not see. A person living alone in a city flat grows no food, generates no power and could not repair the phone on which daily life is organised. Even a simple breakfast, for instance, may involve farmers, shippers and factory workers on several continents. The same is true of nations. When a single trade route is blocked, as happened in the Suez Canal in 2021, shops thousands of miles away can run short of goods. Although such dependence is impersonal and therefore easy to overlook, it is far more extensive than the dependence on family that it replaced.\n\nWhat has changed is not the amount we need others but who those others are. In my opinion, we have exchanged reliance on a few familiar people for reliance on countless unseen ones, which makes us more interdependent than any previous generation.",
"b246":"Cities can grow outwards, with low houses spread over a wide area, or upwards, with residents concentrated in tall blocks. Each form offers a distinct way of life, and while I understand the attraction of the first, I believe that the vertical city is the better place to live for most people.\n\nSupporters of the horizontal city value space above all. A detached house with a garden gives a family privacy, room for children to play and freedom from the noise of neighbours above and below. Streets are quieter, and many people feel more attached to a home that stands on its own land. Although these are real pleasures, they come at a price that is paid daily. In a sprawling city such as Los Angeles, workplaces, shops and friends may be many kilometres apart, so that residents spend much of their lives in traffic. Those who cannot drive, including teenagers and many elderly people, are left dependent on others.\n\nThe appeal of the vertical city is that everything is close. When homes are stacked, a large population lives within a short walk or metro ride of offices, schools, restaurants and cinemas, and the hours saved on travel can be put to better use. In Hong Kong, for instance, it is common to reach work, a doctor and a hiking trail without owning a car. High-rise living has clear drawbacks, since flats are smaller and private outdoor space is rare. These can be eased, though, by good sound insulation, generous balconies and public parks between the towers.\n\nThe choice depends partly on what a person values, and a family that prizes a garden above convenience may reasonably prefer to spread out. For the majority of residents, however, I think the time and opportunities that a compact, vertical city provides make it the better home.",
"b247":"Culture is carried largely by stories, and today most stories reach people through a screen. It is therefore reasonable to ask whether a nation's culture gains more from films and programmes brought in from abroad or from those made at home. While imports have something to offer, I think local production does more for a culture and deserves priority.\n\nPeople who favour imports see culture as something that grows through contact. A director who has watched Japanese animation or French cinema brings new techniques and themes to her own work, and audiences exposed to different ways of life become more curious and less suspicious of outsiders. From this point of view, a culture closed to foreign influence does not stay pure; it simply stops developing. The argument is sound provided that imports arrive alongside local work and do not replace it, which is often what happens in smaller markets.\n\nThose who prefer local production are concerned with exactly that risk. A language, a sense of humour and a shared memory of the past survive only if they are used, and film and television are where most people now meet them. When children grow up watching cartoons dubbed from another language, for instance, the heroes, festivals and landscapes they come to know best belong to another country. Locally made programmes reverse this by giving traditional music, regional accents and national history a regular place in daily life. They may be less polished than expensive foreign productions, but polish is not what keeps a culture alive.\n\nForeign films can refresh a culture, yet only domestic ones can pass it on. This is why I believe governments should support local production first, while leaving room for the best of what is made elsewhere.",
"b248":"Any regular exercise improves health, so the choice between joining a team and training alone is really about what else sport can give. Each kind has its strengths, but I believe team sports offer more, particularly to children and teenagers.\n\nPeople who prefer team games emphasise the social skills they develop. A footballer has to pass to a better-placed teammate, cover for another player's mistake and accept a coach's decision, and has to share defeat as well as victory. These habits of cooperation are the ones that employers later look for. Commitment is another benefit, because it is harder to miss training when others are relying on you. A teenager who would give up running alone after a few weeks will often keep attending for the sake of the squad. The experience is less positive for weaker players who spend matches on the bench, so much depends on how a team is coached.\n\nIndividual sports, their supporters argue, build a different kind of strength. A swimmer or runner measures progress against his or her own previous times, and success or failure cannot be attributed to somebody else. This encourages self-discipline and honest self-assessment. Such activities are also more flexible. Swimming, for instance, can be done at almost any hour and at any age without the need to gather a full side, which makes it easier to continue throughout adult life. While these are real advantages, training alone demands a level of motivation that many people lack, and it offers little practice in dealing with others.\n\nIndividual sports reward self-reliance and are easier to keep up in later life, yet they cannot teach people to work together. That is the main reason why, to my mind, team games are the more beneficial of the two, above all for the young.",
"b249":"More than half of humanity now lives in urban areas, so the effect of city life on health concerns most of us. I agree that large cities expose their residents to particular risks, but I believe the claim is too sweeping, since cities also offer advantages that rural areas cannot match.\n\nThe harm that cities can do is well documented. Heavy traffic and industry fill the air with fine particles, and residents of places such as Delhi or Beijing breathe air that damages the lungs and heart over time. Those who live in cramped housing beside a busy road cannot avoid it by their own efforts, and children and the elderly suffer most. Urban routines also tend to be sedentary. Office workers sit for most of the day and, after a long commute, have little time or energy to exercise or cook. Many rely on takeaway food instead, which makes weight gain and related illness more likely.\n\nCity dwellers, however, enjoy forms of protection that are easily overlooked. Major hospitals, specialist doctors and emergency services are concentrated in urban centres, so a heart attack or a difficult birth is far more likely to be treated in time than in a remote village. Clean piped water, sanitation and health education are generally better as well. Nor is urban life necessarily inactive. In compact cities such as Amsterdam, for example, many residents walk or cycle daily, while people in the countryside often depend on cars for every journey. Whether a city damages health therefore depends largely on how it is designed and on what its inhabitants can afford.\n\nBig cities are unhealthy where the air is dirty, housing is poor and green space is scarce. These are failures of planning, not inevitable features of urban life, which is why I agree with the statement only in part.",
"b250":"Museums today compete for visitors with cinemas, theme parks and screens of every kind, and many have responded with interactive displays and family events. Some approve of this shift towards enjoyment, whereas others hold that a museum exists to teach. I believe education is the purpose and enjoyment the best means of achieving it.\n\nThe argument for making museums entertaining starts from the fact that people visit them by choice, in their free time. Rows of glass cases with long printed labels may satisfy a specialist, but they bore most children and many adults, who then have no wish to return. The Science Museum in London, for example, lets young visitors carry out simple experiments themselves, and its galleries are crowded as a result. Visitors who enjoy themselves tend to return and bring others, so the collection reaches a wider public. The danger comes when amusement becomes the goal, because a museum that offers only spectacle is hard to distinguish from a theme park.\n\nThose who stress education point to what makes a museum distinctive. It holds authentic objects and employs people who understand them, and it is usually supported by public money so that this knowledge can be passed on. Schools depend on it to show pupils what textbooks can only describe. A museum also has a duty to present the past truthfully, including episodes that are painful. An exhibition about slavery or war should not be fun, yet it may be among the most valuable a visitor sees. Teaching need not be solemn, however, and people generally learn more when their curiosity has been aroused.\n\nThe two aims conflict far less than the debate suggests. A museum should be judged, I think, by what its visitors understand when they leave, and making the visit enjoyable is the surest way of ensuring that they learn.",
"b251":"Current events can now be followed on television, radio, websites and social networks as well as in the press. Supporters of these newer channels consider them superior, while others maintain that newspapers have yet to be bettered. I think that, for anyone who wants to understand events and not merely hear about them, the newspaper is still the best guide.\n\nThe appeal of other media is mainly a matter of convenience. A radio bulletin can be heard while driving, and a phone delivers headlines the moment something happens, without any effort from the user. Social media go further by letting people see events through the eyes of those present, as when residents film a protest in their own street. For someone who wants to know quickly what is going on, these sources are hard to beat. They tend to deliver fragments, however, and a person can scroll through dozens of headlines without grasping the background to any of them.\n\nNewspapers are valued for the opposite quality. Because a paper appears once a day, its journalists have time to check facts, speak to several sides and set an event in context. A long article on a pension reform, for example, can explain who gains, who loses and how other countries have handled the same problem, which a brief broadcast item cannot do. Reading also allows people to go at their own pace and to return to a difficult paragraph. A broadcast, by contrast, moves on whether or not the listener has followed it. A newspaper is admittedly slower and requires more effort, but that effort is largely what turns information into knowledge.\n\nOther media are excellent at telling us that something has happened. Newspapers, I think, remain the better way to learn why it happened and what it means, which is the more valuable kind of knowledge.",
"b252":"In the final years of school, timetables can be designed in two quite different ways: many subjects studied lightly, or a few studied thoroughly. Each design reflects a view of what knowledge is for. I believe the most sensible arrangement combines them, with pupils taking a small number of subjects in depth alongside a lighter programme in several others.\n\nBreadth is defended on the grounds that the problems adults face do not respect subject boundaries. Understanding climate change, for instance, calls for some science, some economics and some geography, and a voter who dropped all but one of these at sixteen is poorly placed to judge competing claims. Breadth has value, though, only while each subject receives enough attention. When a week is divided among ten or twelve subjects, pupils rarely go beyond the basics, and knowledge acquired in this way is quickly forgotten.\n\nDepth offers what breadth lacks. Studying a subject in detail teaches pupils what it means to know something properly: to read beyond the textbook, to deal with complications and to produce extended work of their own. This experience of mastery is satisfying, and it is the best preparation for university, where a single discipline is studied for years. Concentrating on very few subjects has a price, however, since gaps appear that are hard to fill later. A future doctor may leave school having written no essays since sixteen, and a future lawyer with no understanding of basic science. The International Baccalaureate avoids this by pairing three advanced courses with three taken at a lower level.\n\nNeither a long list of subjects nor a very short one serves older pupils well on its own. A few subjects studied in depth, supported by a wider range at a lower level, offers in my view the best balance between mastery and general knowledge.",
"b253":"Raising the price of petrol and diesel is often proposed as a simple way of making people drive and pollute less. I agree that higher fuel costs can contribute, but I disagree that they are among the best solutions, because they address only one part of the damage and work poorly unless alternatives are in place.\n\nThe appeal of the idea is that price influences behaviour more reliably than persuasion. When filling the tank becomes expensive, drivers combine errands, share lifts and, when they next buy a car, choose a smaller or electric model. In much of Europe, where fuel is heavily taxed, cars tend to be more economical than in countries where it is cheap. The policy fails, however, when people have no other way of travelling. A nurse in a rural area with no bus service, for instance, must drive to work whatever petrol costs, so the tax reduces her income rather than her emissions.\n\nA further difficulty is that vehicle fuel accounts for only part of the harm done to the environment. Dearer petrol does nothing about the coal burned in power stations, the clearing of forests for farmland or the plastic accumulating in the oceans. Even within transport, it leaves aviation and shipping largely untouched. Measures aimed at these sources, such as replacing coal with renewable energy and protecting forests by law, are likely to achieve more. Fuel taxes are therefore best seen as one instrument among several, useful in cities with good public transport but not a remedy in themselves.\n\nMore expensive fuel can discourage unnecessary driving, and it has a place in environmental policy. In my view, though, it is too narrow and too hard on those without alternatives to be counted among the most effective answers to environmental problems.",
"b254":"Some people settle on an occupation in their early twenties and stay in it until they retire, while others move repeatedly between employers or even professions. I partly agree that the first group is more likely to find its working life satisfying, although I think the benefit comes from commitment and not from choosing early.\n\nStaying in one field allows a person to become good at it. Skill takes years to build, and much of the pleasure of work comes from doing difficult things well and being recognised for it. A carpenter who has practised the trade for two decades, for instance, can take on projects that would defeat a beginner. With that skill comes a reputation, and with reputation the more interesting work. Someone who changes direction every couple of years starts again near the bottom each time, and may reach middle age without real expertise in anything.\n\nThis advantage depends, however, on the first choice being a good one. Most people choose a career at an age when they know little about the working world or about themselves, and some discover within a few years that the job does not suit them. For them, staying out of loyalty or fear leads to long-term frustration rather than fulfilment. A few changes early in a career are often the means by which people find work they can commit to. A graduate who tries sales and then administration before settling into teaching has not wasted that time. The pattern becomes harmful only when moving on turns into a habit, used to escape every difficulty.\n\nSatisfaction at work seems to me to grow from depth, which requires staying somewhere long enough to master it. I believe, though, that people reach that point more reliably by finding the right job than by clinging to the first.",
"b255":"Now that books, newspapers and reference works can be read on a screen, public libraries are sometimes described as buildings that have outlived their purpose. I disagree, because a library does several things that a computer at home cannot do, although its role has clearly changed.\n\nThe argument for closure assumes that a library is simply a store of information. If that were so, the case would be strong, since a search engine finds facts faster than any catalogue. The assumption also takes for granted that every household has a computer, a fast connection and the money for subscriptions. Many do not. For a family on a low income, for example, the library may be the only place where children can use the internet for homework or borrow as many books as they can read. Rather than replacing libraries, computer technology has given them a new function as a point of free access.\n\nLibraries also provide a kind of space that is becoming rare. They are quiet, warm and open to anyone without charge, which makes them valuable to students from crowded homes, to jobseekers writing applications and to elderly people who would otherwise spend the day alone. Trained librarians matter as much as the building. They help visitors to judge which online sources can be trusted and to fill in official forms, a service no search engine offers. It is true that fewer people borrow printed books than in the past, and a library that only lends novels will struggle to justify its budget. Those that adapt, however, by offering computers, classes and study rooms, remain busy.\n\nComputers have taken over one of the library's tasks and left the others largely untouched. Libraries are still needed, in my view, particularly by those who cannot afford the alternatives, and they deserve continued public funding.",
"b256":"Robots are expected to become far more capable in the coming decades, and opinion is divided over whether this should be welcomed. Some regard them as essential to human progress, while others see a threat to jobs and safety. I believe the first view is closer to the truth, although the dangers call for firm regulation.\n\nThose who consider robots important to our future point to problems that human labour alone cannot solve. Many countries have ageing populations and too few workers to care for the elderly, staff factories and harvest crops. Machines can fill part of this gap. They can also go where people cannot safely go, such as the deep ocean, a damaged nuclear plant or the surface of another planet. Such tasks would otherwise be left undone or would cost lives. Progress of this kind depends on robots remaining tools under human direction, which is where the opposing concerns begin.\n\nPeople who regard robots as dangerous have two main fears. One is economic. If machines can do most routine work more cheaply than people, large numbers of drivers, cleaners and factory workers may lose their livelihoods, and the profits will go to the owners of the machines. The other fear concerns control, particularly the use of autonomous weapons that select targets without a human decision. A mistake could then kill civilians with no person clearly responsible. Neither worry is unreasonable. Both, however, describe choices that societies make rather than something inherent in the machines. Tax and training policies can spread the gains from automation, and international agreements can restrict military uses.\n\nBoth sides are describing real possibilities, but the harm robots might do is a matter of how they are governed. For that reason I think they will be of great value to humanity, provided that governments act early to set limits.",
"b257":"How pupils should be grouped is one of the oldest disputes in education. Some favour separate schools for the most able, whereas others want children of every level in the same classroom. I believe mixed schools are preferable, as long as they group pupils by level in certain subjects.\n\nSelection by ability has an obvious appeal. In a class where all pupils work at a similar pace, the teacher can move quickly without leaving anyone behind, and gifted children are stretched instead of waiting for others to catch up. Grammar schools in England, for instance, have long been defended on the ground that they give bright children from modest homes an academic education. The weakness of this system lies in what happens to those who are not selected. A child labelled as less able at eleven tends to be taught with lower expectations, and children develop at such different rates that an early test is a poor guide to later achievement.\n\nMixed-ability schooling avoids this early sorting. Pupils who struggle benefit from working alongside stronger classmates, whose questions and methods show them what is possible. The stronger pupils gain as well, since those who explain an idea to a friend generally understand it better themselves. One lesson cannot, however, suit every level in subjects where knowledge builds step by step. For mathematics or foreign languages, therefore, it makes sense to teach pupils in sets, while keeping them together for the rest of the timetable. Movement between sets should remain possible, so that a late developer is not held back by an old result.\n\nSelective schools serve the chosen few well but at a cost to the majority. On balance, I think children are better educated together in one school, with flexible grouping in the subjects that require it.",
"b258":"Parents and employers often complain that young people leave school knowing algebra and poetry but not how to hold down a job. While I accept that schools could do more to prepare pupils for work, I disagree that this should come at the expense of traditional subjects.\n\nThe case for a more vocational timetable is easy to understand. A sixteen-year-old who can write a clear email, manage a budget or use a spreadsheet has an immediate advantage when applying for a first job. For pupils who have already chosen a trade, such as catering or car mechanics, practical courses can also make school feel relevant at an age when many lose interest. Pupils who see the point of lessons attend more willingly. This argument holds, however, only for skills that stay in demand. Training tied to a particular job or piece of software can be out of date before the pupil has had much chance to use it.\n\nTraditional subjects, by contrast, give pupils abilities that do not expire. Mathematics trains them to reason step by step, science to test a claim against evidence, and history or literature to follow an argument and express their own. These are the capacities that allow an adult to retrain when an industry changes, which most workers will now have to do. They are also much harder to acquire later, whereas a specific workplace skill can usually be learnt in the first months of a job. Although not every pupil enjoys academic study, narrowing the curriculum early would close doors for children too young to know which ones they will need.\n\nSchools should certainly make room for some practical preparation, particularly in the final years. I believe, however, that it should supplement the traditional subjects rather than replace them, because those subjects remain the most lasting preparation for working life.",
"b259":"Screens now compete with the printed page in almost every classroom, and some argue that books, which many pupils find dull, should give way to films, games and computers. I disagree. Visual media can enrich lessons, but they cannot do the work that reading does.\n\nIt is fair to admit that screen-based material has real strengths. A documentary can show a volcano erupting or a cell dividing in a way that no paragraph can match, and a well-designed game gives a pupil instant feedback on every answer. Children who are reluctant readers may engage with a topic through film when a textbook would have lost them. Such material works best, however, as an introduction. It presents information at its own speed, leaving little opportunity to stop, reread a difficult step or check whether an argument holds together.\n\nReading develops precisely these habits. A pupil working through a chapter must hold a line of reasoning in mind, meet unfamiliar vocabulary and build a picture from words alone, which demands more effort than watching and therefore leaves more behind. This skill is not optional, since examinations, university study and most professional work still depend on the ability to understand long written texts. The complaint that books are boring is also less a fact about books than about the ones chosen. A class that finds a dated textbook tedious may read eagerly when given a novel or an article that speaks to its interests, such as a story about teenagers of its own age. The format matters less than the activity, and a text read on a tablet is still a text.\n\nFilms and computer programs deserve a place in teaching as a supplement to books. Removing books from schools, though, would in my view deprive children of the sustained reading on which most later learning rests.",
"b260":"Sugary drinks, salty snacks and fatty ready meals fill the shelves of most supermarkets, and some argue that the law should remove whatever science has shown to be harmful. I disagree with a ban of this breadth, because harm in food is almost entirely a question of quantity.\n\nThe proposal springs from a reasonable concern. Diet-related conditions place a heavy burden on families and health services, and manufacturers design many products to be eaten in excess. If the most damaging items disappeared from shops, people would eat less of them. The difficulty is the word 'any'. Science has shown that sugar, salt, butter, red meat, coffee and alcohol can damage health when consumed in large amounts, yet each is harmless or even beneficial in small ones. A law that took the statement literally would empty the shelves of chocolate, cheese and white bread, while a looser version would require officials to draw lines that the evidence does not supply.\n\nBanning products would also treat adults as incapable of judging risk. Someone who eats a slice of cake at a birthday party is not endangering anyone, and removing that choice by law would be widely resented. A better approach is to make excess less likely without forbidding moderation. Clear warnings on packaging and higher taxes on the least healthy items, such as the levy on sugary drinks in Mexico, push consumption down while leaving the decision with the buyer. Stricter rules are appropriate for children, however, who cannot be expected to weigh long-term consequences. Schools can refuse to sell sweets and fizzy drinks.\n\nSince nearly every food is harmful in sufficient quantity, prohibiting whatever has been proved unhealthy is neither practical nor fair to responsible consumers. I believe governments should instead inform, tax and protect the young, and leave adults to decide what they eat.",
"b261":"Stories of success are usually told as stories of effort, yet many people suspect that wealth and good looks count for more. Both views contain some truth. I believe that advantages such as money make the start easier, but that effort and persistence decide how far a person goes.\n\nThe sceptics are right that people do not begin from the same position. A child of wealthy parents attends better schools, can afford to study abroad and may take an unpaid internship that leads to a well-paid career. If a business fails, family money allows a second attempt. Appearance has its influence as well, particularly in fields such as television, sales and modelling, where an attractive face opens doors. A candidate who looks the part is more easily remembered after an interview. It would be naive to deny these factors. What they provide, though, is opportunity. A fortune can be wasted, and a good first impression fades quickly if nothing lies behind it.\n\nHard work and determination are what turn opportunity into achievement. Mastery of any skill, whether surgery, music or running a company, requires years of practice that cannot be bought or inherited. Determination matters most when things go wrong, because almost every career includes failures, and those who succeed are generally those who continue after them. The footballer Cristiano Ronaldo, for example, came from a poor family in Madeira and is known for training longer than his teammates. Effort does not guarantee success, however. Someone who works hard without access to education or capital may still struggle, which is why societies should try to widen opportunity.\n\nMoney and appearance, then, affect where the race begins, not how it is run. In my opinion, sustained effort remains the most reliable route to success, and the only one that lies within a person's own control.",
"b262":"Young drivers and riders are involved in a disproportionate share of serious accidents, which has led to calls for the legal driving age to be raised. I accept that such a change might prevent some crashes, but I disagree that it is the most effective way of making roads safer.\n\nThe argument for a higher age limit is easy to understand. Teenagers tend to underestimate danger, are more easily influenced by passengers, and are more inclined to treat speed as entertainment. Keeping them off the road a little longer would spare some lives. The flaw in this reasoning is that it confuses youth with inexperience. A person who first drives at twenty-one is still a beginner, and beginners of any age misjudge distances, react slowly to hazards and panic in situations they have not met before. Raising the age may therefore postpone the dangerous first years of driving rather than remove them.\n\nA more promising approach is to change how people learn rather than when they start. Many accidents involving new drivers happen at night, at high speed or with a car full of friends, and these are conditions that a licence can restrict. Under the graduated schemes used in countries such as Australia and New Zealand, for example, a new driver gains full privileges only in stages. Night driving and young passengers are permitted only after a period without offences, so experience is gained in safer conditions first. Although such measures need to be enforced by the police to have any effect, they target the actual cause of the risk, which is lack of experience.\n\nRather than making young people wait, governments should, in my view, introduce a gradual route to a full licence. An age limit has its place, particularly for powerful motorbikes, but it is not the best tool available.",
"b263":"It is often suggested that fewer people would die on the roads if teenagers had to wait longer before driving a car or riding a motorbike. I disagree that this is the best way to increase safety, since it addresses only one small group of road users and ignores the main causes of accidents.\n\nMost collisions are not caused by the youngest drivers. They result from speeding, drinking, tiredness and the use of mobile phones, and these are habits found among motorists of every age. A higher minimum age would do nothing about the experienced adult who drives home after an evening in a bar. What does reduce such behaviour is the likelihood of being caught. Speed cameras, random breath tests and heavy penalties for using a phone at the wheel affect all drivers, including young ones. Design matters as well. Features such as roundabouts, barriers between opposing lanes and separate paths for cyclists make mistakes less likely and less deadly when they do occur.\n\nThere is also a practical objection to making young people wait. In rural areas and in countries where the motorbike is the main form of transport, a vehicle is often the only means of reaching a college or a first job. A seventeen-year-old apprentice with no bus service would either lose that opportunity or, more probably, ride without a licence and without any training. The law would then have made the roads more dangerous rather than less. Although a slightly higher age might be reasonable for the most powerful motorcycles, the benefit would be modest.\n\nRoad deaths fall when enforcement is consistent and roads are built to forgive human error, and these measures protect drivers of all ages. Raising the age limit, I believe, would achieve much less and would penalise the young people who most need to travel.",
"b264":"Lengthening prison terms is a popular response to public concern about crime, largely because it appears tough and is simple to announce. I accept that long sentences are appropriate for the worst offenders, but I believe other measures reduce crime more effectively.\n\nAdvocates of longer sentences make two claims. One is that criminals who are locked up cannot commit further offences, which is plainly true for as long as they are inside. Removing a few persistent burglars can make a whole neighbourhood safer. The other is that the prospect of many years in prison will discourage people from offending in the first place. This second claim is weaker than it sounds. It assumes that offenders calculate the risks beforehand, whereas a great deal of crime is committed on impulse, under the influence of alcohol or by people who are confident of escaping detection. A burglar who does not expect to be caught is unlikely to care whether the penalty is three years or six.\n\nFor this reason, those who prefer alternatives argue that the likelihood of punishment matters more than its length. Raising the chance of arrest, for example through quicker investigation of burglaries and better use of security cameras, changes the calculation in a way that a distant penalty cannot. Other measures aim at the reasons for offending. Treatment for drug users removes the motive behind much theft, and training for prisoners improves their chances of finding work on release. These approaches are slower and less visible than a new sentencing law, but they do reduce the number of crimes committed.\n\nLong sentences protect the public from a small group of serious offenders, and for them they should remain. As a general policy, however, I think crime falls further when offenders expect to be caught and when the causes of their behaviour are treated.",
"b265":"Rates of obesity and diabetes have climbed in rich and poor nations alike, and the cost is felt by whole societies. Some hold the state responsible for what its citizens eat, while others consider diet a personal matter. I think individuals carry the final responsibility, yet governments have a larger duty than is often admitted.\n\nPlacing the duty on individuals is, for many, a matter of common sense. No official stands in the kitchen or at the restaurant table, and a person who wishes to eat badly will find a way to do so whatever the law says. Basic nutritional knowledge is widely available, and most adults know that vegetables are better for them than fried food. This reasoning holds for people with adequate income, time and education, although it is less convincing for a family that can afford only the cheapest calories.\n\nAdvocates of government action point out that the state already pays for poor diets through its hospitals, and so has both an interest and a right to act. Treating illnesses linked to diet uses beds and funds that other patients need. More importantly, only governments can alter the conditions under which people choose. They can press manufacturers to reduce salt and sugar in everyday products such as bread and breakfast cereals, as Britain has done. They can subsidise fruit and vegetables and limit fast-food outlets near schools. Such policies improve the diet of the whole population without demanding willpower from each person. They stop short of coercion, however, since no adult is prevented from buying what he or she wants.\n\nEach adult decides what to put on the plate, and no law can make that decision for them. What ends up within reach and within budget is shaped by policy, though, which is why I believe the state shares this duty in full.",
"b266":"Almost every country accepts that primary and secondary schooling should cost families nothing. Whether that principle should extend to university is far more contested. I believe higher education ought to be paid for jointly by the state and the student, with generous help for those on low incomes.\n\nThose who want education to be free at all stages see it as a right that should not depend on wealth. In countries such as Germany, where public universities charge little or no tuition, a young person can choose a course on the basis of interest and ability alone. Advocates add that the public gains from every additional nurse or engineer, so the public should pay. Patients, pupils and employers all rely on skills that graduates acquired at university. This is a strong argument in principle, although it overlooks a practical limit. Budgets are finite, and money spent on free degrees for all is money not spent on nurseries and primary schools, where disadvantage begins.\n\nThe opposing view holds that a degree is largely a private investment. Graduates generally enjoy higher salaries and more secure careers, and many of them come from families who could afford to contribute. Asking them to pay part of the cost releases public funds for the earlier stages of education, which benefit the whole population rather than a section of it. Fees become unjust only when they deter capable applicants from poorer homes. That risk can be met through grants and reduced fees for such students, without making university free for the children of the wealthy.\n\nFree schooling for all is beyond dispute, but at university level the individual gains enough to bear some of the cost. In my view, a system of moderate fees combined with targeted support is fairer than one with no charges at all.",
"b267":"A town library used to be the only place where an ordinary family could look something up. Now that most households are online, some people see no reason for the state to open one in every town, while others consider it as necessary as ever. I side with the second group, although I think a modern library should look different from a traditional one.\n\nThe objection to universal provision is about value for money. Running a building with staff and thousands of books is a permanent cost, and in a small town it may serve only a handful of visitors a day. Meanwhile, a home internet connection gives access to more information than any local branch could hold. For residents who are comfortable online and have a quiet house to work in, the case is hard to answer. It assumes, however, that such conditions apply to the whole population.\n\nIn practice they do not. Many elderly people have never learned to use a computer, and poorer households may share one phone with limited data among several members. A library gives these residents free computers, a reliable connection and someone to show them how to complete a form or apply for a job. It is also a place, which the internet is not. A student living in a crowded flat, for instance, needs a silent desk rather than another website. Where a town is too small to justify a full branch, a mobile library or a room shared with the local school can provide the same service more cheaply.\n\nInformation may be available at home, but not all residents have the home, the equipment or the skills to use it. That is why I believe free libraries in every town are worth their cost, provided that they offer space and support as well as books.",
"b268":"Public money for the arts is easy to attack, because a poem or a painting has no obvious price and hospitals are short of funds. Even so, I believe modest state support for artists is justified, provided that it is directed at work the market would not otherwise sustain.\n\nThose who regard such spending as wasteful have a serious point about priorities. A grant given to a composer is money not spent on a nurse or a school roof, and taxpayers who have no interest in contemporary poetry may reasonably resent paying for it. They also argue that art which people value will find buyers, as popular music and commercial cinema do. This reasoning holds for entertainment with a mass audience, although it says little about forms that are costly to produce and slow to find a public.\n\nIt is those forms that supporters of funding have in mind. An orchestra, for example, cannot cover the wages of dozens of musicians from ticket sales unless seats are priced beyond the reach of ordinary families. Without a subsidy, such institutions either close or become a pastime for the wealthy, and a country loses part of what distinguishes it from its neighbours. Individual artists face a similar problem at the start of their careers. Few earn a living from early work, and many take other jobs before their talent has matured. A small grant can give a young painter the year she needs to develop, and the work produced in that time may be enjoyed long after the money is forgotten.\n\nGovernments should not pay for whatever calls itself art, and funding bodies should show the public what it has received. Within those limits, I think supporting artists is a sensible use of a small share of public money rather than a waste of it.",
"b269":"Teenagers today produce and consume more written words in a day than their grandparents did in a week, yet much of it appears on a screen in fragments. I agree that this habit has weakened certain abilities, but I think the claim that it has damaged literacy as a whole goes too far.\n\nThe real loss is in sustained reading. Messages, captions and comments are short by design, and a young person who reads little else becomes used to text that makes its point within a few lines. A novel or a textbook chapter asks for something different: holding an argument in mind over many pages. Teachers often notice that pupils who are quick and witty in a group chat struggle to finish a set book, and this weakness matters because examinations and most professions still depend on long documents. The effect is not universal, however. Children who are encouraged to read books seem to cope with both kinds of text.\n\nWriting is a more mixed picture. Abbreviations and missing punctuation, such as those common in text messages, do sometimes appear in school essays, and automatic spelling correction removes one reason to learn how words are spelt. Yet these same young people write far more often than earlier generations did, and they do so for a real audience whose reaction they care about. Many learn to adjust their tone for different readers, which is a genuine writing skill. What digital communication fails to teach is formal structure, so the problem lies less in the devices than in the absence of practice in longer, planned writing.\n\nPhones and computers have therefore changed young people's literacy rather than reduced it. I believe the harm is limited to extended reading and formal writing, both of which schools and parents can protect by making room for them.",
"b270":"Countries work together for many reasons, from security to science, but two purposes are often singled out as the most valuable. Some see the greatest gain in protecting the planet, while others point to the growth of global trade. I believe the environmental benefit matters more, because it is the one that no country can obtain by acting alone.\n\nThose who put commerce first have solid grounds. Agreements that lower tariffs and harmonise standards allow a manufacturer in one country to sell to customers in dozens of others, and the resulting competition tends to reduce prices and raise quality. Whole economies have been transformed in this way. South Korea and Vietnam, for instance, grew rapidly once they were able to export freely to wealthier markets. Trade of this kind lifts incomes on both sides, which explains why business is frequently regarded as the main reward of cooperation. Its benefits are unevenly shared, however, and a good deal of commerce would take place even if governments did little to encourage it.\n\nEnvironmental protection is different, since here cooperation is not merely helpful but indispensable. Polluted air and warming oceans do not stop at borders, so a nation that cuts its emissions gains little if its neighbours carry on as before. Each government is therefore tempted to wait for others to move first. International treaties remove that temptation by binding countries to common targets, as the agreement to phase out ozone-damaging chemicals showed. Although such treaties are slow to negotiate and difficult to enforce, there is no alternative route to the same result.\n\nTrade brings prosperity, and the two aims need not conflict. Even so, in my view the protection of the environment is the more important benefit of international cooperation, as it depends on joint action in a way that business does not.",
"b271":"Each generation inherits customs from the one before and has to decide how far to keep them. Tradition has real value, but I believe young people should be free to live as individuals, provided that they understand what they are setting aside and respect those who choose differently.\n\nThose who want the young to follow tradition see it as the thread that holds a society together. Festivals, forms of courtesy and family obligations give people a shared identity and a sense that they belong to something older than themselves. Customs often contain practical wisdom too. The expectation in many Asian cultures that children care for their ageing parents, for example, provides security that no government scheme fully replaces. When young people abandon such practices without thought, a community can lose in a few years what took centuries to build. This is a strong argument for teaching traditions, although not for imposing them.\n\nThe opposing view holds that a life is one's own to shape. Young adults today face choices their grandparents did not, about careers, partners and where to live, and rules designed for a farming village may fit them poorly. Some traditions are also unjust. Customs that require a daughter to leave school early or to accept a husband chosen for her limit her prospects for the sake of habit. Such practices usually end only when the young people affected begin to question them. Freedom of this kind is not a licence to disregard others, however, and individual choice works best when it is informed by an understanding of why a custom existed.\n\nTraditions deserve to be known and, where they still serve people well, kept. The decision should nonetheless rest with the young themselves, and in my view a society is healthier when its customs survive by consent rather than by pressure.",
"b272":"Engineers and programmers are in short supply in many economies, and it is tempting to assume that schools should concentrate their resources on science. I disagree that science teaching should receive more money than other subjects as a matter of policy, although I accept that it is an expensive subject to teach well.\n\nFavouring science has an obvious appeal. Modern industries, from medicine to renewable energy, depend on people with technical training, and countries such as South Korea and Singapore have grown rich partly by producing them. Science is also costly to teach properly, because laboratories, equipment and qualified teachers do not come cheap. A school that cannot afford microscopes or chemicals will teach the subject from a textbook, and its pupils will learn very little. To that extent, spending more per pupil on science is simply paying what the subject costs.\n\nDevelopment, however, requires more than technical knowledge. A new invention has to be financed, marketed, regulated and explained, and these tasks call for people trained in economics, law, languages and design. A software company, for instance, needs managers who can negotiate with foreign clients and writers who can make a product understandable, as well as programmers. Subjects such as history and literature also teach people to weigh evidence and argue clearly, abilities on which sound public decisions depend. If these areas are starved of funds, a country may produce skilled technicians while lacking the judgement to use their work wisely.\n\nA country progresses when its education is strong across the board. In my view, science should be funded generously enough to be taught through real experiments, but not given priority over the subjects that make scientific knowledge useful to society.",
"b273":"Trade, travel and the media now connect countries more closely than at any earlier time. Many welcome the prosperity and understanding that this brings, while others fear that nations are becoming alike. I believe the gains are considerable and that national identity, though it changes, is more resilient than the pessimists suggest.\n\nThe benefits of closer contact are easy to see. International trade gives companies larger markets and consumers a wider choice at lower prices, and foreign investment creates jobs in countries that lack capital of their own. Vietnam's growth as a manufacturing centre, for example, has depended heavily on firms from abroad. Cultural exchange has a quieter but equally real value. People who study overseas, work with foreign colleagues or simply watch films from other countries tend to become less suspicious of unfamiliar customs. These advantages are not shared evenly, however, and small local producers can struggle when large foreign brands arrive.\n\nCritics are concerned about what is displaced. The same coffee chains, clothing shops and films now appear in cities across the world, and young people in particular may prefer them to local alternatives. Traditional crafts, festivals and even languages can decline when they seem to offer no economic advantage, and with them goes part of what makes a nation distinctive. This loss should not be dismissed. It is, though, only one side of the picture. Cultures have long borrowed from each other without disappearing, and contact can strengthen identity as well as weaken it. Korean music and Japanese cooking have reached a global audience precisely because the world has become more connected.\n\nInternational contact, on balance, enriches countries more than it erodes them. The sensible response to the risk, in my view, is to support local languages and arts actively, rather than to limit exchange with the outside world.",
"b274":"Sending a crew to the Moon or a robot to Mars costs sums that could build many schools and clinics, and it is fair to ask whether such spending can be defended. I agree with the criticism to some extent. Prestige missions are difficult to justify, but the scientific side of space research deserves its funding.\n\nThe complaint is most convincing when it is directed at projects driven by rivalry between nations. Much of the money spent during the race to the Moon served to demonstrate one country's superiority over another, and some present plans to send people to Mars have a similar character. Keeping humans alive in space is far more expensive than sending instruments, and it adds little to what is learned. When a government has citizens without safe drinking water or basic healthcare, it is hard to argue that planting a flag on another planet should come first. In such cases the critics are right that there are more beneficial uses for the money.\n\nIt would be a mistake, however, to extend this judgement to space research as a whole. Unmanned missions and orbiting telescopes cost far less and have transformed our understanding of the universe. Although the returns from pure science are uncertain and slow, they have repeatedly proved larger than anyone expected. The practical benefits are easier to measure. Satellites built with the same technology monitor the climate, track hurricanes and detect crop failures before they turn into famine, for instance. An early warning gives a poor country time to move people to safety or bring in food.\n\nWhat matters is therefore the kind of space spending, not the principle. Governments should cut back on costly displays of national pride, in my view, and direct their budgets towards research and satellites whose value to people on Earth is clear.",
"b275":"Turning on a tap feels like a private act, which is perhaps why many people resent being told how long they may water a garden. They regard water they have paid for as theirs to use, whereas others insist that the authorities should ration a resource that is running short. I believe people have a right to enough water, but not to an unlimited amount.\n\nSupporters of free use make two reasonable points. Water is a basic need, and a government that restricts it interferes in the most ordinary parts of daily life, from washing to cooking. Being told how often to shower feels intrusive to most people. They also argue that households are a minor part of the problem, since agriculture and industry consume far more. A family that pays its bill has done all that can fairly be asked. This reasoning breaks down in a drought, when one street filling swimming pools can leave another with low pressure or none.\n\nThe opposing view starts from that shared dependence. Because a city draws on the same reservoirs, each person's consumption affects the others, and only a public body can balance their claims. Cape Town, for example, came close to running dry some years ago and avoided it largely through strict daily limits per resident. Controls need not be so severe in normal times. A pricing system in which the first, essential litres are cheap and heavy use becomes steadily more expensive protects basic needs while discouraging waste. Rules of this kind work, provided that they apply to farms and factories as firmly as to homes.\n\nThe freedom to use water therefore ends where it begins to reduce someone else's supply. In my view, governments are right to control consumption firmly, as long as they guarantee each household what it needs to live decently.",
"b276":"Hide-and-seek, skipping and board games have largely given way to consoles and phone apps in many children's lives, and some adults regret the change. I agree that traditional games are better at developing physical and social abilities, but I do not accept that they are superior in every respect.\n\nThe strength of older games is that they involve the whole child. Chasing games and hopscotch build balance, strength and stamina in a way that sitting with a screen cannot. Children get this exercise without thinking of it as exercise. They are also played face to face, usually without an adult in charge. Children must agree on the rules, decide what counts as cheating and deal with a friend who is upset at losing, and through this they learn to negotiate and to control their tempers. Much of this is lost when a child plays alone against a computer that enforces the rules automatically.\n\nModern games, however, develop abilities that playground games barely touch. Building games such as Minecraft ask players to design structures, manage resources and solve problems that have no single answer. To build a bridge there, a child must work out which materials are needed and in what order. Games played online in teams demand cooperation. Players must divide tasks, share information quickly and change their plan when it fails. The weakness is that such games are designed to be difficult to put down, whereas a game of tag ends when the players are tired.\n\nTraditional games remain the better way for children to grow stronger and to learn to get along with one another. Even so, I think digital games teach useful skills of their own, so a childhood that includes both is preferable to one that relies on either.",
"b277":"In many countries the school-leaving age has risen steadily over the past century, and some now propose that all young people should remain in full-time education until eighteen. I agree that sixteen is too early to stop learning, but I do not think full-time study should be the only route permitted.\n\nThe case for keeping teenagers in education longer is strong. Jobs that require no qualifications are disappearing, and a sixteen-year-old who leaves school with basic certificates is likely to move between insecure, low-paid positions. Two further years allow young people to reach a level of literacy, numeracy and technical knowledge that employers increasingly treat as a minimum. They also postpone a decision that many are not mature enough to make. A teenager who dislikes school at fifteen may regret leaving it by twenty, when returning is much harder.\n\nCompulsion has limits, however, when it takes a single form. Some adolescents learn poorly in a classroom yet thrive when given practical responsibility, and forcing them to sit through two more years of lessons may produce resentment and disruption rather than knowledge. A better arrangement is the one used in Germany and Switzerland, for example, where young people may leave full-time school for an apprenticeship that combines paid work with regular attendance at a college. An apprentice mechanic who sees why calculations matter in the workshop often returns to them with more interest. Such schemes keep them learning while giving them a wage and a sense of purpose, although they depend on employers being willing to offer enough places.\n\nYoung people should not be allowed to abandon education at sixteen. I believe the requirement until eighteen should nevertheless be for education or training, with full-time schooling as one option among several.",
"b278":"The search for life beyond the Earth is among the most ambitious projects that public money supports. To its critics, such spending is hard to defend while poverty, disease and pollution remain unsolved at home. I believe a modest share of national budgets is well spent on this research, although it should not be allowed to compete with urgent needs.\n\nThe objection is easy to understand. A space telescope or a mission to Mars costs an enormous sum, and its results may be decades away, if they come at all. A government that funds it is choosing not to spend the same money on hospitals, clean water or flood defences, whose benefits are immediate and certain. For a country struggling to feed or house its people, that choice would indeed be difficult to justify. The argument is weaker, however, for wealthy nations, where space science takes a very small part of public spending and cutting it would do little to solve those problems.\n\nThose in favour argue that the search repays its cost in ways that are not obvious at first. Looking for life elsewhere requires scientists to study how planets become habitable or lose that ability, and this knowledge has improved our understanding of the Earth's own climate. Venus, for example, shows how a planet can overheat when carbon dioxide builds up in its atmosphere. Beyond this practical gain lies a question that people have long asked, namely whether we are alone. Even though an answer may be distant, a society that stops asking such questions is poorer for it.\n\nEarthly problems deserve the larger part of public funds, and they already receive it. This is why I believe that looking for life on other planets is a reasonable use of a small amount of money, not a waste of it.",
"b279":"Sport fills stadiums, television schedules and school timetables, yet opinions differ about how much it really matters. Some regard it as a significant part of social life, while others see it as a hobby and nothing more. I think the first view is the more convincing.\n\nFor the person taking part, sport is indeed a form of leisure. Someone who plays badminton on a Saturday morning or jogs after work is doing so for enjoyment and fitness, much as another person might garden or paint. Those who hold this view argue that the attention given to professional matches is out of proportion, since the result of a game changes nothing in the lives of the spectators. They have a point when public money is spent on stadiums while hospitals wait for funds. What their argument overlooks, however, is the effect of sport on people collectively.\n\nThat collective effect is considerable. A population that exercises regularly is healthier, which reduces the burden on medical services. School sport and local leagues build a habit of activity that often lasts into adult life. Sport also unites people. Local clubs bring together neighbours of different ages, incomes and backgrounds who might otherwise have no contact, and a town's team often gives its residents a shared identity. At the national level, events such as the Olympic Games or the football World Cup unite people who agree on little else. Its influence is not wholly positive, given that rivalry between supporters sometimes turns into violence, but even this shows how much more than a pastime it has become.\n\nTo the individual player, sport may be simply an enjoyable way to spend free time. Taken as a whole, though, it improves public health and strengthens communities, and in my view that amounts to an important social role.",
"b280":"Taking a year out between school and university, whether to work or to see the world, has become common in many countries. I think that, for most students, this break does their later studies more good than harm, provided the time is spent with some purpose.\n\nThe main benefit is that students arrive at university knowing why they are there. Many eighteen-year-olds choose a course because a teacher or parent suggested it, and discover in the first term that it does not suit them. A year in a workplace can test that choice in advance, whereas a term on the wrong course wastes time and fees. Months as an office junior in a legal firm, for instance, may show a future law student that the profession appeals far less than expected. Students who have earned a wage, managed their own money or travelled alone also tend to be more organised and self-reliant, qualities on which independent study depends. Tutors rarely chase late work, so these habits matter from the first week.\n\nThe disadvantages concern momentum. Study habits weaken without practice, and subjects that build on earlier knowledge, such as mathematics or languages, can be hard to resume after twelve months away. The first term may then be spent relearning what was once familiar. There is also a risk that a temporary job, with its regular income, makes returning to lectures and a student budget less attractive. The longer the job lasts, the more likely the break is to become permanent. These dangers are real, although they mostly affect students who drift through the year without a plan or a confirmed university place.\n\nA year away from the classroom can blunt academic skills, yet it more often sharpens motivation and maturity. On the whole, I believe the advantages for a student's studies are greater than the drawbacks.",
"b281":"For several decades astronomers have listened for signals from distant stars, so far without success. A more controversial proposal is to stop waiting and transmit messages of our own, which some scientists welcome and others regard as reckless. In my view, the danger is remote, but it is serious enough that we should go on listening before we decide to call.\n\nAdvocates of sending messages argue that contact would be the greatest discovery in history. A civilisation able to receive and answer a signal would probably be far older than ours, and might have solved problems that still defeat us, in energy or medicine for instance. They add that silence offers little protection. Radio and television broadcasts have been leaking into space for about a century, so any nearby listener could already detect us. This point has some force, although those accidental signals are weak and fade quickly, whereas a deliberate beam aimed at a particular star is far easier to notice.\n\nThe cautious side draws on what we know from our own past. When a technologically advanced society has met a weaker one on Earth, the weaker has usually suffered, even where no harm was intended. We have no means of knowing how another species would behave, and a message cannot be recalled once it has left. Critics also ask who has the authority to speak for the whole planet. At present a single research team could make that choice on behalf of billions of people. The risk may be very small, but its consequences would fall on all of humanity.\n\nCuriosity about other life is natural, and listening satisfies it at no risk. Deliberate messages are another matter, and I believe they should wait until we know more and until nations have agreed on them together.",
"b282":"Astronomers have found thousands of planets beyond our solar system, and it seems increasingly plausible that some of them support life. Whether humanity should announce its presence to any intelligent beings out there is a separate matter. I think the cautious scientists have the stronger argument, although the risk they describe is remote.\n\nThose in favour of sending messages see contact as one of the greatest discoveries our species could make. A civilisation able to receive and answer a signal would probably be older than ours. Even a brief exchange might teach us a great deal about science or about how societies survive their own technology. Supporters also point out that the Earth has been leaking radio and television broadcasts for about a century, so staying silent now would achieve little. This argument is weaker than it sounds, since those accidental signals fade quickly with distance, whereas a deliberate, focused transmission is designed to be noticed.\n\nOpponents base their case on our own history. When technologically advanced societies have met less advanced ones, for example during the European colonisation of the Americas, the weaker side has usually suffered, even where no harm was intended. We know nothing about the intentions of another species, and a message cannot be recalled once it has been sent. A second concern is who decides. At present a handful of researchers could transmit on behalf of the whole planet, without the consent of the people who would share the risk. Admittedly, the distances are so vast that any reply might take centuries to arrive.\n\nGiven how little is known, in my view the sensible course is to keep listening rather than to broadcast. Deliberate messages should wait until there is broad international agreement on whether to send them and what they should say.",
"b283":"Asked what they want to be, many teenagers name a career that few people ever reach. Whether adults should encourage such ambitions or temper them is a matter of disagreement. I believe the decision belongs to the young person, but that it should be made with honest information about the odds.\n\nSupporters of free choice argue that a career imposed by others rarely leads to a happy working life. People live with the result for decades, so it seems fair that they should make the choice themselves. Ambition also has value in itself. A teenager who dreams of becoming a professional musician will practise daily and learn discipline, and these habits remain useful even if the dream is not realised. Freedom has a cost, though, when it is exercised without knowledge. A sixteen-year-old may not realise how few places exist in an orchestra, or how long it takes to earn a living from art.\n\nIt is this problem that advocates of realism have in mind. In fields such as professional sport, acting and fashion, a small number of people succeed and a large number give up after years of low and uncertain income. Parents and teachers who point this out are being responsible, not unkind. Being realistic also means looking at one's own abilities. A pupil who struggles with science, for instance, should think carefully before committing to medicine, however attractive the profession appears. The danger of this view is that caution turns into discouragement. Adults cannot predict which young people will succeed, and advice that amounts to choosing the safest option can waste talent.\n\nFreedom and realism are therefore compatible. On balance, I think young people should aim at the career they want, while preparing a second route, such as a qualification they can fall back on, in case the first does not open.",
"b284":"Private companies have already carried a small number of paying passengers to the edge of space, and regular trips may follow within a generation. I believe that this would be a negative development on balance, at least while such journeys remain as polluting and as exclusive as they are today.\n\nMy principal objection is environmental. A rocket launch burns an enormous quantity of fuel to give a few people several minutes of weightlessness. It also releases soot directly into the upper atmosphere, where it lingers far longer than emissions at ground level. At a time when ordinary travellers are being asked to fly less, it is hard to justify a new form of leisure with a much larger footprint per passenger. The cost also means that the experience would be limited to the very rich. Tourism of this kind benefits few people while its environmental cost is borne by all.\n\nSupporters reply that today's luxury often becomes tomorrow's ordinary service. Air travel, for instance, was once reserved for the wealthy, and the money those early passengers paid helped to fund safer and cheaper aircraft. In the same way, income from tourists could finance reusable rockets that later carry satellites or scientific instruments at lower cost. This is a fair point, although it depends on cleaner fuels being developed, and there is no guarantee that they will be. Unless that happens, more launches simply mean more damage.\n\nSpace tourism may eventually bring technical benefits, but these are uncertain whereas the pollution is not. In my view, it should be regarded as a harmful development until it can be offered without such a heavy cost to the atmosphere.",
"b285":"Sponsors' logos now cover stadiums, shirts and television screens, and major competitions are run as commercial enterprises. In my view, this is a positive development on the whole, because corporate money has raised the standard of sport, although it needs to be kept in check.\n\nThe main benefit is financial. Staging a large event requires stadiums, security, broadcasting and prize money, and few sports could pay for these from ticket sales alone. Sponsorship fills the gap. The Olympic Games, for example, depend on a group of international companies whose contributions help to fund athletes and facilities in poorer nations as well as rich ones. The same income has allowed many athletes to train full-time instead of fitting practice around another job, which is one reason why performances keep improving. This money is unevenly spread, however. It flows towards the most popular sports and the most famous teams, while minority sports often continue to struggle.\n\nThere are costs when profit becomes the main purpose. Match schedules are sometimes arranged to suit television audiences rather than players, who may be asked to compete too often. More matches mean less time to recover, so injuries become more likely and careers may be shortened. The choice of sponsor is the other concern. When gambling firms or producers of sugary drinks sponsor teams followed by children, the message conflicts with the healthy image of sport, and young fans may come to see these products as normal. These problems are real, but they can be addressed through rules on scheduling and advertising, without removing business from sport.\n\nCommercial involvement has given sport resources that it could not have found otherwise. Its effects are largely beneficial, I believe, provided that governing bodies, not sponsors, continue to decide how the games are played.",
"b286":"The claim that students should meet the whole cost of a degree rests on a premise: that higher education enriches the graduate and leaves the rest of society unaffected. I disagree with both the premise and the conclusion, although I accept that students can fairly be asked to pay a share.\n\nGraduates do gain personally from their studies. A degree usually leads to better pay, more interesting work and greater security, and it would be odd if the person who enjoyed these rewards contributed nothing towards them. This is the reasonable core of the argument, and it explains why many countries charge tuition fees. It supports a contribution, however, not the full cost. Courses such as medicine or veterinary science are so expensive to run that, if fees covered everything, only the children of wealthy families could consider them.\n\nThe idea that society gains little from universities does not survive a visit to a hospital or a school. The surgeon, the teacher and the engineer who designed the local bridge were all trained partly at public expense, and those who use their services have benefited whether or not they went to university themselves. Full fees would also reduce the supply of such people. Many of the most useful occupations, including nursing and social work, are modestly paid, so their holders could not recover the full price of their training. Charging it would simply leave these posts unfilled. The public return is admittedly harder to see for some degrees than for others, which is one reason a mixed system is appropriate.\n\nBecause a degree benefits both its holder and the community, its cost should be divided between them. I disagree, therefore, that students should pay in full, and would limit fees to part of what a course costs.",
"b287":"A student with a phone can now find in seconds what once required a morning among the shelves. Some conclude from this that libraries have outlived their purpose, but I disagree, because much of what a library offers is not available online.\n\nIt is true that the internet has taken over one of the library's old functions. Basic facts, dictionary definitions and introductory explanations are quicker to look up on a screen, and few students would now walk to a building to consult an encyclopaedia. If libraries were only stores of general information, the argument against them would be convincing. Much serious material, however, is not freely accessible. Academic journals, specialist databases and recent textbooks are costly, and it is the library's subscription that allows a student to read them without paying.\n\nA library is also a place and a service, not merely a collection. Many students have nowhere suitable to work at home, particularly those who share a bedroom or live in a noisy flat, and for them a silent reading room with reliable internet makes serious study possible. Librarians add something that a search engine lacks, which is judgement. A first-year undergraduate beginning a research project, for example, may be faced with thousands of search results of very uneven quality, and a trained librarian can point to the sources worth trusting. Libraries do need to adapt, though. Rows of rarely borrowed books may now be less useful than group study rooms and larger digital collections, and the best libraries are already making that change.\n\nOnline access has changed what students need from a library without removing the need itself. As long as reliable sources cost money and quiet space is scarce, I believe libraries will remain necessary.",
"b288":"Throughout the last century, commentators confidently predicted that machines would shorten the working week and leave ordinary people with long hours of leisure. In my view, this forecast has proved accurate only in a narrow sense: technology has saved a great deal of time, but most of that time has not become free.\n\nThere is no doubt that individual tasks now take far less effort. Washing machines and dishwashers have removed hours of domestic labour, and jobs such as paying a bill or booking a ticket, which once meant queuing in person, are done on a phone within minutes. In factories and offices, software performs calculations and paperwork that used to occupy whole departments. If free time were simply the time saved on each task, the prediction would have been fulfilled long ago.\n\nWhat the forecasters overlooked is that expectations rise as quickly as efficiency does. When a report can be produced in an hour rather than a day, employers tend to ask for more reports, not to send staff home early. The same devices that speed up work also carry it beyond the office. A manager can send a message late in the evening and expect a reply, so many employees now answer emails at the dinner table or on holiday. Hours that were once clearly their own have become an extension of the working day. This pattern is not universal, however. Some countries, such as Germany and the Netherlands, have turned higher productivity into shorter working weeks, and there the gains have been more visible.\n\nSo far, then, the prediction has been only partly right. Technology has delivered the means to work less, but whether people gain leisure from it depends on decisions made by employers, governments and individuals rather than on the machines themselves.",
"b289":"English has become the working language of science, aviation, business and much of the internet, and its reach is still growing. I agree that the benefits of this will go on exceeding the costs, although those costs are real and fall mainly on smaller languages.\n\nA shared language lowers the barrier to cooperation, and this advantage is unlikely to disappear. A researcher in Seoul can read a paper written in São Paulo on the day it appears, and a pilot landing in a foreign country speaks to the control tower in terms both understand. For individuals, English opens doors that would otherwise stay shut. A Vietnamese engineer who speaks it well, for example, can apply to firms in Singapore or Germany rather than only those at home. These gains are not shared equally, however, because good English teaching remains expensive and those who can afford it pull further ahead.\n\nThe disadvantages concern what is displaced. When parents conclude that English is the language of opportunity, they may stop passing on their own, and many minority languages are now spoken only by the elderly. The loss is serious, as songs, stories and ways of describing the world disappear with them. Native English speakers also enjoy an unearned advantage. A scientist who writes in a second language, for instance, must spend extra time and money on editing before a journal will accept the same findings. Yet these harms are not an inevitable result of English itself. Countries such as the Netherlands show that a population can be fluent in English while continuing to live, study and write in its own language.\n\nFor these reasons I agree that the spread of English will remain beneficial on balance. The outcome depends, though, on governments protecting local languages in schools and public life rather than treating bilingualism as a threat.",
"b290":"The rate at which animal species are disappearing has risen sharply in modern times, and human activity is the principal reason. The damage takes different forms on land and at sea, but in both it follows from how we obtain food and space, and the remedies must begin there.\n\nOn land, the chief cause is the loss of habitat. As populations grow, forests are cleared for farms, wetlands are drained for housing and roads cut the remaining wild areas into fragments too small to support large animals. Orangutans in Borneo, for example, have lost much of their forest to palm oil plantations. At sea, the main cause is overfishing. Modern fleets catch fish faster than they can breed, and their nets also kill turtles, dolphins and sharks that were not their target. When one species is removed in such numbers, the animals that feed on it decline as well.\n\nBecause these causes are economic, the solutions must alter what is profitable. Governments can set aside larger protected areas on land and at sea, where neither logging nor fishing is permitted, and link them so that animals can move between them. Such reserves succeed only if local people gain from them, which is why paying communities to guard wildlife works better than fencing them out. The other essential measure is a limit on catches. Fishing quotas based on scientific advice give stocks time to recover, since enough adult fish are left in the water to breed. Quotas work, however, only when patrols and satellite tracking enforce them on the open sea.\n\nExtinction cannot be undone, and some species will be lost whatever is done now. Most, however, could still be saved if the habitats they depend on were given firm protection and wild populations were harvested no faster than they can recover.",
"b291":"Phones and tablets have put games, videos and social media into people's pockets, where they are available at any moment of the day. Some observers consider this constant access damaging to users and to the wider community. I agree that it carries risks, but I disagree that it is harmful in itself.\n\nThe harm that critics describe is real for those who cannot limit their use. Mobile games are built to hold attention, with rewards and notifications that draw the player back, and some people lose hours they intended to spend on work, study or sleep. Because the device is carried into the bedroom and the classroom, there is no natural point at which the activity stops. Society is affected when this becomes common. A family sitting in a restaurant with each member looking at a separate screen, for example, is sharing a table but not a conversation. These effects are most serious among children, whose self-control is still developing.\n\nFor most users, however, portable entertainment is a modest pleasure that fills time which would otherwise be wasted. A short game or an episode of a series makes a long commute or a hospital waiting room easier to bear, and it does no harm to others. Many games are played with friends or relatives who live far away, so they maintain relationships instead of replacing them. Grandparents and grandchildren in different cities can share a word game each evening when visits are rare. The benefit depends on moderation, and manufacturers could do more to support it through tools that show users how long they have played.\n\nHandheld entertainment causes damage when it displaces sleep, work and conversation, and children in particular need limits. Used in moderation, though, I believe it does little harm, so the fault lies in excessive use and not in its availability.",
"b292":"Managers have long assumed that the surest way to get more from their staff is to pay them more. I disagree that money is the best motivator in the workplace. Fair pay is a necessary condition for effort, but beyond that point other things move people more.\n\nPay clearly matters, and it would be unrealistic to pretend otherwise. People work in order to live, and employees who feel underpaid tend to do as little as they can while looking for another job. A bonus can also raise output where results are easy to measure, as with a salesperson paid on commission. The effect has limits, though. A pay rise is welcome for a few months and then becomes the normal salary, so its influence on daily effort soon fades. Financial incentives can even distort behaviour, encouraging staff to pursue whatever is counted and neglect whatever is not.\n\nWhat sustains effort over years is the experience of the work itself. Employees who are trusted to make decisions, who can see that their work is useful and who receive recognition for doing it well are inclined to give more than their contracts require. Many nurses and teachers, for instance, could earn more in other occupations, yet they stay and work hard because they believe in what they do. The opportunity to learn and to be promoted is another strong motive, especially for younger staff. A well-paid employee with a hostile manager and no prospect of development will rarely perform well for long, however generous the salary. None of this replaces a decent wage, since praise cannot make up for pay that is unfair.\n\nMoney, then, prevents dissatisfaction more effectively than it creates enthusiasm. Once a fair salary is in place, the strongest motivation comes, in my view, from interesting work, respect and the chance to progress.",
"b293":"Every government has to decide how much of its budget serves the present and how much serves the decades ahead. I agree that spending on the young is the most effective way for a country to prepare for what is coming, although it cannot be the only form of preparation.\n\nThe case rests on a simple fact: the children of today will be the workers, taxpayers and leaders of the period in question. Whatever challenges arrive, whether new technologies or a changing climate, they will be met by people who are currently at school. Money spent on their health, schooling and skills therefore keeps paying back for decades, longer than most roads or machines last. South Korea, for instance, was a poor country with few natural resources, and its heavy commitment to education is widely regarded as the basis of its later prosperity. An educated generation can also adapt to problems that have not yet been foreseen, which no fixed plan can do.\n\nOther investments cannot be neglected, however. Well-trained graduates achieve little if there are no jobs, reliable power supplies or transport links to make use of their abilities. Many will simply emigrate, and a country that has paid for their training then sees another economy gain from it. Even so, this is an argument for balance rather than for a different priority. Infrastructure built today will eventually need to be maintained, improved and replaced, and only a capable younger generation can do that.\n\nA country that educates and protects its young people is equipping itself for a future it cannot predict in detail. Other forms of investment remain necessary, but I believe they work best as support for this one, not as substitutes for it.",
"b294":"One proposal for reducing accidents is to require every motorist to pass a driving test each year. Although regular checks on drivers have some merit, I disagree that annual testing would be the best way to make road travel safer.\n\nThere is a reasonable idea behind the proposal. Most people take a test once, in their late teens, and are then permitted to drive for decades without further assessment. During that time traffic rules change, bad habits develop, and eyesight and reactions decline with age. Some form of reassessment would identify drivers who are no longer fit to be on the road. Doing this every year, however, would be out of proportion to the problem. Examining every motorist in a country annually would require a large number of examiners and would cost drivers time and money, while a competent driver's skills hardly change within twelve months.\n\nThe deeper weakness is that most accidents are caused by choices rather than by lack of ability. Drivers who speed, use their phones or drive after drinking generally know how to drive well, and they would have no difficulty behaving perfectly for half an hour in front of an examiner. A test measures what a person can do, not what he or she does on an empty road late at night. Behaviour of this kind responds to enforcement, such as speed cameras, roadside breath tests and the loss of a licence for repeated offences. Retesting is better reserved for those who present a clear risk, including drivers convicted of serious offences and those above a certain age.\n\nSome reassessment is sensible, and a test every ten years would be hard to object to. Yearly tests for all drivers, in my view, would cost a great deal and change little, and stricter enforcement would save more lives.",
"b295":"Long commutes from suburbs and villages are a major source of traffic, and it has been argued that the answer is to persuade more people to live in cities. I agree that compact urban living reduces the need to travel, but I do not think it is the best solution when pursued alone.\n\nLiving in a city does shorten journeys. When homes are close to workplaces, shops and schools, many trips can be made on foot or by bicycle, and enough passengers live along each route to justify frequent buses and trains. In Copenhagen, for instance, a large share of residents cycle to work, which would be impossible if they lived far outside the city. Suburban and rural households, by contrast, usually need a car for almost every errand, and their daily drive into the centre is what fills the main roads each morning. In this respect the proposal identifies a real cause of congestion.\n\nIt does not follow that drawing more people into cities would solve the problem. Many cities already struggle to house and move their existing populations, and additional residents would add to crowded trains and push up rents unless infrastructure grew at the same pace. Building new lines and homes takes years, so for a long period congestion would simply move from the motorway to the station platform. A more balanced policy would also move some jobs and services outwards. People who need or prefer to live in suburbs and small towns, such as farmers and families wanting more space, could then work and shop near home rather than travelling to the centre.\n\nEncouraging city living is therefore one useful measure rather than a complete answer. On the whole, I believe transport problems are best addressed by reducing the distance between homes and jobs, wherever people choose to live.",
"b296":"Large international companies bring together staff of many nationalities, and it is sometimes said that no better school of culture exists. I disagree. Working for such an organisation teaches something about other cultures, but living in a foreign community and learning its language teaches considerably more.\n\nA multinational does offer regular contact with people from different backgrounds. Employees learn that colleagues from other countries may approach meetings, deadlines and disagreement in unfamiliar ways. A manager in a German firm with a Japanese partner, for instance, soon discovers that a decision which one side expects to settle in a single meeting may require lengthy consultation on the other. Knowledge of this kind is practical and memorable. It is narrow, however. Such companies usually have a common working language, generally English, and a uniform corporate culture, so that what staff mostly encounter is the professional behaviour of well-educated colleagues.\n\nCulture in the fuller sense lies outside the office. It includes how families are organised, what people find funny and how they celebrate and mourn. These things are learned by living among ordinary people, not by exchanging emails with an overseas branch. Someone who spends a year in a small Spanish town, shopping in the market and talking to neighbours, will understand Spain better than a colleague who deals with Madrid from a London office. Language is central here, because many attitudes and assumptions are carried in words that do not translate neatly. Its forms of address for older people, for instance, show how respect is expressed. Few people can move abroad, though, and for many an international workplace is the most realistic contact.\n\nA job in a global company is therefore a useful introduction to other cultures, though hardly the best one. Deep understanding, I believe, comes from daily life in another society and from speaking its language.",
"b297":"Calls for the state to guarantee healthy eating grow louder each time new figures on obesity appear. Against this stands the older belief that people must look after themselves. My own view is that diet is chiefly a personal duty, and that the government's part, though real, is narrow.\n\nPeople who want the government to take charge usually begin with those least able to choose. Children eat what adults give them, and families on low incomes may live in districts where takeaways are plentiful and greengrocers are not. Public authorities can do a good deal here. Finland, for instance, has long provided a free cooked lunch to all schoolchildren, and food safety laws protect consumers from dangers they cannot detect themselves. In these areas the state is acting where individual choice is absent or uninformed. The word 'ensure', however, promises more than any authority can deliver for the adult population.\n\nThe reason is that eating well is a daily practice, not a service that can be supplied. It involves planning meals, shopping carefully, cooking and resisting temptation, and none of these can be done on another person's behalf. Countries that have run health campaigns for decades still find that many citizens ignore the advice, which shows how little official effort achieves without personal commitment. There is also a cost to shifting responsibility upwards. If people come to regard their weight as the government's problem, they have less reason to change their own behaviour. Individual duty does depend on accurate information, however, which is why honest labelling and basic nutrition lessons at school remain a proper public task.\n\nGovernments should feed schoolchildren well, keep food safe and tell the truth about nutrition. Beyond that, to my mind, a healthy diet is something each adult has to achieve personally, because it cannot be achieved for them.",
"b298":"Traffic jams cost cities time, money and clean air, and one proposed remedy is for the state to make all public transport free at every hour of the day. I agree that governments should make buses and trains more attractive, but I do not think this particular policy is a sensible way to do it.\n\nThe proposal has some appeal. Removing fares would lower the cost of living for students, pensioners and low-paid workers, and all-night services would be valuable to people such as nurses and cleaners who finish work after midnight. Some drivers would also leave their cars at home. The weakness lies in the timing. Congestion occurs during a few hours in the morning and evening, when buses and trains are already full, so extra passengers at those times would make public transport less comfortable without removing many cars. Night services, though useful, run when the roads are empty anyway.\n\nThe larger objection is cost. A system without fares must be funded entirely from taxation, and the sums involved would have to come from other services or higher taxes. That money would ease congestion more effectively if it were spent on new lines and dedicated bus lanes, since capacity and speed are what draw motorists out of their cars. Experience also suggests that drivers respond more to the cost of driving than to the price of the alternative. London, for instance, reduced traffic in its centre by charging vehicles to enter rather than by making the Underground free. Free travel could still be offered to those who need it most, although this is a matter of welfare rather than traffic management.\n\nUniversal free transport, then, would be generous but poorly aimed. For congestion specifically, I believe governments would achieve more by expanding services and charging for road use at busy times.",
"b299":"Sculptures in squares and murals on blank walls are increasingly seen as a way of making urban areas more appealing, and some argue that the state should pay for more of them. I agree that public art is worth some government money, but only as a small part of a wider effort to improve the places where people live.\n\nArt in public places can give a town a character that it would otherwise lack. A striking statue or a painted wall turns an anonymous street into somewhere people recognise, and residents often take pride in it. The Angel of the North, for instance, a large steel figure beside a main road in northern England, has become the symbol of its region. Unlike a gallery, such work costs nothing to see, so it reaches people who would not normally seek out art. This effect is not guaranteed, though, because a piece chosen without consulting local people may be ignored or actively disliked.\n\nWhat makes a town attractive, however, is mostly something more ordinary. People judge a place by whether its streets are clean, its buildings are in good repair, its parks are safe and its centre has shops and cafes that are open. A statue placed in a square surrounded by litter and empty shopfronts does little to change that impression, and residents may reasonably resent the expense while basic services are neglected. Public money is limited, and housing, transport and maintenance have a stronger claim on it. Art adds most where these foundations are already in place.\n\nGovernments are therefore right to fund paintings and statues in public places, though modestly and with the involvement of the community. Such spending, in my view, should complement the everyday upkeep that makes a town pleasant, and cannot replace it.",
"b300":"It is often said that advertising exists mainly to make people buy things they have no real use for. Although this happens, I disagree that it is the main purpose. Most advertising is concerned less with whether people buy than with which company they buy from.\n\nThe bulk of commercial advertising is competition between brands for customers who intend to spend anyway. Someone who needs a bank account, a mobile contract or a week's groceries will purchase these regardless, and firms advertise so that the money goes to them rather than to a rival. Supermarkets, for instance, fill their advertisements with the prices of milk, vegetables and other basic goods, which few would describe as unnecessary. In markets of this kind advertising creates no new demand at all. It simply directs existing demand, and in doing so it gives companies a reason to keep prices low and quality high.\n\nIt would be naive, of course, to pretend that advertisers do not also encourage excess. Fashion labels persuade people to replace clothes that are still wearable, and makers of sweets and toys aim their messages at children, who cannot judge them critically. This side of the industry deserves the criticism it receives. It is nevertheless only one part of a much larger activity, which includes job vacancies, public health campaigns, charity appeals and announcements by local tradespeople. The same techniques that sell luxury watches are also used to persuade drivers to wear seat belts. Judging all advertising by its most wasteful examples gives a misleading picture.\n\nAdvertising certainly sells some things that people could do without. I believe, however, that its principal purpose is more ordinary than the statement suggests: to help one seller be chosen over another by customers who were going to buy in any case.",
"b301":"News bulletins are dominated by war, crime and disaster, and it is frequently suggested that journalists should make more room for encouraging stories. I agree with this suggestion, on the condition that positive reporting is added to serious coverage and is not used as a substitute for it.\n\nThe strongest reason for including more good news is accuracy. Sudden, harmful events are easy to report, whereas gradual improvements make poor headlines, so audiences hear about every plane crash but little about decades of safer air travel. The result is a picture of the world that is darker than the facts justify. More attention to what is going well would correct this imbalance. It would also be useful, because accounts of success show what can be done. An article on how one city reduced road deaths, for instance, gives other councils a method to copy and gives readers a reason to demand the same.\n\nThis does not mean that the media should become cheerful for its own sake. Their first duty is to tell the public what it needs to know, and much of that is unwelcome. Corruption, pollution and failing services are put right only when they are exposed, so a broadcaster that softened its coverage to keep viewers happy would be doing them a disservice. There is a further risk that 'good news' turns into light entertainment, with stories about rescued kittens replacing analysis. Positive stories deserve space only if they are significant and properly investigated, which is the standard applied to any other story.\n\nI believe, in short, that the media should report progress as carefully as they report failure. More good news would give audiences a truer and more hopeful view of the world, as long as it does not crowd out the difficult stories that hold power to account.",
"b302":"Work takes up a large share of adult life, and for some people it is the main source of purpose. Even so, I disagree with the claim that a career is the most important part of a person's life, and still more with the idea that life has no meaning without a satisfying one.\n\nA fulfilling career is certainly worth having. People who enjoy their work gain more than an income from it. They have a sense of progress, the respect of colleagues and the feeling that their abilities are being used. A scientist working on a new vaccine or a teacher who sees pupils succeed may find in the job much of what makes life seem worthwhile. Those who are stuck in work they dislike, by contrast, often carry their frustration home with them. Work therefore matters a great deal, although this is different from saying that it matters most.\n\nMeaning comes from several sources, however, and for most people relationships stand above employment. Raising children, caring for elderly parents and keeping close friendships give life a purpose that does not depend on a job title. Someone who works in a warehouse to pay the bills and spends the weekends coaching a children's football team, for instance, may be more content than a successful executive who rarely sees his family. The claim also ignores those who do not have careers at all. People who are retired, who stay at home to bring up children or who cannot work because of illness do not lead empty lives.\n\nSatisfying work is a real good, and those who have it are fortunate. It is, to my mind, one part of a meaningful life rather than its foundation, which lies chiefly in the people we care for.",
"b303":"Coverage of murders and assaults can alarm the public, and there are occasional fears that it inspires imitation. Some people conclude that such stories should be kept out of newspapers and television altogether. I disagree, since the public has a legitimate interest in knowing about violent crime, although I accept that the manner of reporting needs restraint.\n\nSuppressing this kind of news would leave people less safe, not more. Residents told of a series of attacks in a local park can avoid it at night, and parents can decide whether children should walk home alone. Witnesses, too, may come forward only because they saw an appeal in the press. Reporting also allows the public to judge how well the police and courts are performing. If violent offences went unmentioned, a government could claim that the streets were safe and citizens would have no means of checking. Rumour would fill the gap, and it is usually worse than the truth.\n\nThe worries that prompt the proposal are nonetheless real. Constant, dramatic coverage can convince people that violence is far more common than it is, so that elderly viewers, for example, become afraid to leave home in neighbourhoods that are quite safe. Detailed descriptions of how a crime was carried out, or coverage that makes the offender famous, may also tempt a small number of disturbed individuals to copy it. These dangers, however, arise from sensational treatment rather than from reporting as such. A brief, factual account that withholds the method and gives little attention to the criminal informs the public without producing either effect.\n\nViolent crime is part of what the public needs to know about its own society. For this reason I believe it should continue to be reported, but soberly and without the drama that turns information into fear or encouragement.",
"b304":"A cynical view holds that effort at work has a single explanation, which is the pay that follows it. I disagree. Money is a powerful reason for working hard, and for people on low incomes it may be the dominant one, but it is far from the only motive.\n\nThe financial motive should not be underestimated. Most people have no choice but to earn a living, and many put in extra hours because they need the overtime. A taxi driver supporting a family, for instance, may work late mainly because the rent is due. For workers in this position, effort rises and falls with the pay on offer. The statement goes wrong, however, in its word 'only'. If pay were the sole motive, people would stop making an effort as soon as they had enough, and that is not what happens.\n\nMuch hard work is done for other reasons. Volunteers give long hours to charities and receive nothing. Parents work hard at raising children, and students study through the night for examinations, without any payment. Within paid employment, too, people are driven by pride in doing a job properly, by the wish to be respected by colleagues and by interest in the work itself. Doctors in public hospitals often stay long after their shifts have ended because a patient needs them, not because they will be paid more. Wealthy entrepreneurs who could retire in comfort frequently continue to work as hard as before, which suggests that achievement matters to them more than income. Such motives count, although they are easier to act on once basic needs are met.\n\nMoney explains a good deal of human effort without explaining all of it. People also work hard out of duty, pride, ambition and care for others, and I believe these motives are often the stronger ones.",
"b305":"Thousands of people are killed on the roads each year, and tougher penalties for bad driving are frequently proposed as the answer. I disagree that this is the only way to make roads safer. Penalties have a place, but the design of roads and vehicles has probably saved more lives than any court.\n\nStrict punishment is effective against deliberate risk-taking. Drivers who know that they will lose their licence for drinking, or pay a heavy fine for using a phone at the wheel, are more likely to think before they act. Countries that enforce such laws firmly, with frequent roadside testing, have seen drink-driving become socially unacceptable as well as illegal. Punishment has little power, however, over the ordinary mistakes that cause many collisions. A tired parent who misjudges a bend, or a new driver who fails to see a cyclist, is not weighing up the penalty beforehand, and no sentence would have prevented the error.\n\nBecause human error cannot be eliminated, the more reliable approach is to make errors less deadly. Road engineering does this in several ways. Replacing a dangerous junction with a roundabout slows traffic at the point of conflict, and a physical barrier between opposite lanes prevents head-on crashes. Separate paths keep cyclists and pedestrians out of the way of lorries. Vehicle design has contributed just as much, for example through seat belts, airbags and automatic braking systems. These improvements protect careful and careless drivers alike, though they are expensive, and poorer countries cannot be expected to rebuild their road networks quickly.\n\nHarsher penalties deter the reckless minority, yet they do nothing for the driver who simply makes a mistake. I believe safer roads come from combining firm enforcement with engineering that forgives human error, so punishment is one tool among several, not the only one.",
"b306":"Calls for heavier fines and longer driving bans tend to follow every serious accident that reaches the news. Although such penalties can change behaviour, I disagree with the claim that they are the sole means of making roads safer, because training, enforcement and public attitudes matter at least as much.\n\nA penalty deters only if drivers expect to receive it. In many places the chance of being stopped for speeding is small, so doubling the fine changes little, whereas a modest fine that is almost certain has a marked effect. Speed cameras illustrate the point. On a stretch of road where every vehicle is measured, most motorists keep to the limit, although some simply slow down for the camera and accelerate afterwards. Consistent enforcement of existing laws would therefore achieve more than making those laws harsher.\n\nSafety also depends on what drivers know and believe before they set off. A demanding driving test, with lessons at night and on motorways, produces more competent motorists than one that can be passed after a few hours of practice. Young drivers, who are involved in a large share of serious crashes, benefit from restrictions in their first year, such as a ban on carrying passengers late at night. Public campaigns have a part to play as well. Wearing a seat belt became normal in many countries less through fear of fines than because attitudes changed. Education of this kind works slowly, and it will not reach the determined offender, for whom penalties remain necessary.\n\nStricter punishment is a useful measure against drivers who knowingly endanger others, but it is far from the only one. In my view, roads become safer mainly when existing rules are reliably enforced and when motorists are better trained and more aware of the risks.",
"b307":"Urban populations in many parts of the world are increasing faster than the cities themselves can adapt. This growth brings certain benefits, but for most residents its main effect is pressure on housing and public services, which only careful planning can relieve.\n\nThe effects are felt first in the housing market. When the number of households rises more quickly than the number of homes, prices and rents climb, and families either pay more for less space or move to distant suburbs. Public services are stretched in the same way. Trains and buses become overcrowded, school classes grow larger, and patients wait longer to see a doctor. Growth is not wholly negative, since a larger city offers more jobs and a wider choice of shops and entertainment. These advantages mean little, however, to someone who spends hours commuting to a flat that is too small.\n\nMaintaining the quality of life depends on planning for growth rather than reacting to it. Authorities should build transport, schools and hospitals before new districts are occupied, and should concentrate new housing around railway stations so that residents are not forced onto the roads. A suburb that opens with a rail link and a school already in place, for instance, adds far less to the queues elsewhere. Governments can also slow the growth of the largest cities by investing in smaller ones. If a regional city offers good universities, hospitals and employment, fewer people will feel obliged to move to the capital. Although this takes years to produce results, it addresses the cause of overcrowding rather than its symptoms.\n\nRapid growth lowers living standards mainly when it outpaces the homes and services a city provides. Where infrastructure is planned ahead and growth is shared among several cities, residents can continue to live well.",
"b308":"A lack of affordable homes in major cities leads to overcrowding, long commutes and, at worst, homelessness. I agree that these consequences are serious and that governments must lead the response, but I disagree that they are the only ones capable of solving the problem.\n\nGovernment action is indispensable for a simple reason: the state controls the conditions under which homes are built. It decides which land may be developed, how tall buildings may be and how quickly permission is granted. It is also the only body with both the resources and the motive to house people on low incomes, since private developers naturally build what is most profitable, which usually means expensive flats. The council housing built across Britain in the decades after the Second World War, for example, housed millions of families whom the market had failed to serve. Without intervention of this kind, a shortage tends to persist however high prices rise.\n\nThis does not mean that the state can succeed alone. In most countries, private companies build the great majority of homes, and they possess the capital, labour and expertise that no public authority could easily replace. Governments achieve most when they direct these efforts rather than try to substitute for them, for instance by releasing public land to builders on condition that a share of the new homes is affordable. Ordinary residents have a part to play as well, because local opposition to new construction is one of the main reasons that too little is built. Where neighbours accept new buildings nearby, permission is granted sooner and homes follow.\n\nThe housing shortage will not be solved without firm government action, and in that sense the statement contains an important truth. I believe, however, that lasting solutions depend on the state working with developers and local communities, not acting alone.",
"b309":"The proposal to abolish advertising rests on two claims: that it does nothing useful and that it sometimes does harm. I accept the second claim but not the first, and for that reason I disagree that society would be better off if advertising were banned.\n\nTo say that advertising has no useful purpose ignores what happens in markets without it. Where sellers cannot publicise their prices, buyers have little idea whether they are paying too much, and firms feel less pressure to offer a better deal. Where opticians or airlines, for example, are free to advertise their prices, customers can compare them in minutes and the expensive providers must respond. Advertising also carries money into areas of life that people value. Football clubs, music festivals and art exhibitions depend heavily on sponsors, whose only reward is having their name displayed. Although this dependence is not ideal, the events would be fewer and the tickets dearer without it.\n\nThe claim that advertising can be damaging is much harder to dismiss. Images of flawless bodies leave many teenagers unhappy with their own appearance. The pressure is hard to escape when the same pictures appear on most screens they use. Loans and betting are sometimes promoted to exactly the people who can least afford them. Borrowers already short of money may then fall into lasting debt. These harms are specific, however, and they call for specific remedies. Rules requiring honest claims, limits on what may be shown to children and clear labelling of altered photographs address the damage directly, while a ban would also sweep away everything that is harmless.\n\nAdvertising is neither as worthless nor as innocent as the two sides of this debate suggest. Because its benefits are real and its harms can be controlled, I believe regulation would serve society far better than abolition.",
"b310":"Human beings learn by imitation from infancy, and the habit does not end with childhood. The statement suggests that the appeal of fashion and of consumer goods in general is evidence of this instinct. I agree to a considerable degree, though imitation explains fashion far better than it explains the success of every popular product.\n\nClothing offers the clearest support for the claim. No practical reason exists for trouser legs to be narrow one decade and wide the next, yet millions of people change their wardrobes accordingly. What they are responding to is other people. A style appears on a well-known figure, is taken up by those who wish to seem up to date, and then spreads to the majority, who do not want to look out of place. The same process can be seen when a particular brand of water bottle or trainer suddenly becomes essential in a school. Its popularity depends on who else owns it rather than on the object itself.\n\nCopying is only part of the story, however, where other goods are concerned. Many products become popular simply because they work well, and people recommend them for that reason. Households across the world own refrigerators, for example, because they keep food fresh, and the fact that the neighbours have one is beside the point. Even in fashion, imitation is balanced by the opposite impulse. Some people deliberately dress differently from the crowd, and each new trend begins with someone who refused to follow the last one. The desire to stand out coexists with the desire to fit in.\n\nFashion and many consumer trends do reveal how readily people follow one another. I believe the statement is largely correct, provided we remember that usefulness and the wish to be different also shape what people buy.",
"b311":"A viewer of almost any evening bulletin will notice that it consists largely of things going wrong. Critics argue that this emphasis on crises and failures damages both individual audiences and the wider community. I agree only in part: the imbalance does cause harm, but attention to problems is also the most valuable service that journalism provides.\n\nThe harm to individuals is mostly psychological. A person who checks the headlines several times a day receives a steady stream of conflict, accidents and warnings, very little of which he can do anything about. Over time this can produce anxiety and a feeling of helplessness. Society is affected in turn, because people who believe that everything is getting worse lose trust in institutions and in one another. They may stop voting or volunteering on the assumption that nothing can be improved, despite real progress in areas such as public health and literacy that seldom reaches the front page.\n\nThe reporting of problems is not, however, harmful in itself. A community can only deal with dangers it knows about. When journalists reveal that a river is being polluted or that a hospital is failing its patients, for example, the unwelcome news is what forces those responsible to act. Warnings of an approaching storm or a spreading illness save lives. A press that preferred pleasant stories would leave the public comfortable but exposed. The damage comes from proportion and tone, since a problem reported calmly, with its causes and possible remedies, informs people without frightening them.\n\nThe negative emphasis of the news is therefore harmful when it is relentless and offers no context. The remedy, to my mind, is fuller and calmer reporting of problems alongside achievements, not less scrutiny of what goes wrong.",
"b312":"Predictions that technology will empty the classroom have been made about radio, television and the internet in turn. Now that artificial intelligence can tutor individual learners, the claim is being made again for 2050. I disagree, because the classroom is likely to change in form while surviving in substance.\n\nThere is little doubt that teaching will look different in a few decades. Software can already set exercises at the right level for each pupil, mark them instantly and explain mistakes, and by 2050 it will do so far better. Lectures by outstanding teachers are available to anyone with a connection, which makes it unnecessary for every teacher to deliver the same explanation in person. Adults taking professional courses may well study mostly online. For children, however, the situation is different, because their difficulty is rarely a shortage of information.\n\nWhat young learners need is someone who knows them and expects something of them. A teacher notices the pupil who has stopped trying, settles a quarrel, and persuades a reluctant class that a subject is worth the effort, and a program does none of this convincingly. The months when schools closed during the pandemic showed how much depends on physical presence. Many pupils fell behind when lessons moved online, for example, particularly those without a quiet home or parental help. The teacher of 2050 is therefore likely to spend less time presenting facts and more time guiding, while still standing in a room with a group of students.\n\nTechnology will take over many of the routine tasks of teaching by 2050. The basic arrangement of a teacher working with a class in person will, I believe, still be recognisable, since learning in childhood depends on human relationships.",
"b313":"People remain in employment for decades, and they do so for a mixture of reasons. I agree that, for most of them, money is the most important of these. Other motives are real, but they generally operate once the need for an income has been met.\n\nThe clearest evidence is what people do when they cannot afford to stop. Most households depend on wages to pay for housing, food and their children's education, and very few have savings that would last more than a few months. Many workers who would like to retire stay on because their pensions are too small. A sixty-year-old shop assistant with a mortgage still to pay, for instance, is unlikely to be at work chiefly for the company of colleagues. The same pressure keeps people in jobs they find dull or tiring. Whatever else work offers them, it is the salary that makes leaving impossible.\n\nOther motives nevertheless influence how long people stay, particularly those who are financially comfortable. Work gives structure to the day, contact with other people and a sense of being useful. Many people miss these after retiring more than they expected to, and some take up part-time work again for that reason. Some remain in the workforce because they find their profession absorbing. University professors and doctors, for example, often continue well beyond the age at which they could draw a pension. These factors matter, although they tend to decide the question only for those who have a choice. For a lawyer with large savings, interest may be the main reason to continue, while for a cleaner it rarely is.\n\nWork provides company, purpose and status as well as pay. Income remains the main reason why most people keep working, I believe, because it is the one need that cannot be met in any other way.",
"b314":"When a city has too few homes for the people who need them, the damage extends well beyond high prices. I agree that the social consequences are severe, but in my view the claim that government alone can remedy them is only partly true.\n\nA housing shortage reaches into almost every area of life. Families unable to afford adequate space share cramped flats, where children have nowhere quiet to study and illness spreads easily. Over several years this can hold back a child's schooling and health, so the effects last long after the family has moved. The other serious consequence falls on the city as a whole. Workers on whom it relies, such as nurses, teachers and bus drivers, are pushed to distant suburbs or leave altogether. Hospitals and schools then struggle to fill posts, and the services that residents depend on become weaker. Few urban problems cause harm on this scale.\n\nWhether the state is the only possible source of a solution is less clear. It has powers that others lack: it can change planning rules, tax empty properties and fund housing for those the market does not serve. Public money and public officials do not lay bricks, however. The homes themselves are largely built by private firms, financed by banks and, in many places, managed by charities. Government policy succeeds when it makes it worthwhile for these organisations to build more and at lower prices. A city that simply waits for the authorities to construct every home it needs is likely to wait a long time. The state's role, though leading, is that of an organiser rather than a sole provider.\n\nHousing shortages do serious social harm, and governments bear the main responsibility for ending them. They will succeed, I think, only by enlisting builders, lenders and non-profit bodies in the task.",
"b315":"Any attempt to name the single greatest invention runs into the difficulty that later discoveries are built on earlier ones. The internet has transformed modern life more quickly than almost anything before it. Nevertheless, I disagree that it is the most important invention in history, since it rests on older breakthroughs that mattered more.\n\nThe case for the internet is easy to understand. Within a few decades it has changed how people work, shop, learn and stay in touch, and it has made information that was once held in distant libraries available to anyone with a phone. A student in a small town can now follow lectures from a leading university, and a small business can sell to customers abroad. Few inventions have reached so many people so fast. Speed of adoption, however, is not the same as importance, and much of what people do online is a faster version of what they did before.\n\nMore fundamental inventions are those without which the others could not exist or human life would be far harder. Electricity is one example: without it there would be no internet, and also no refrigeration, lighting or modern hospitals. The printing press, similarly, first made knowledge cheap enough to spread beyond a small elite, which is the same achievement the internet is praised for today. Vaccines and clean water systems, such as the sewers built in nineteenth-century cities, have saved more lives than any communication tool. A society could lose the internet and still function, though with great inconvenience, whereas losing any of these would be a catastrophe.\n\nThe internet is certainly the defining invention of the present age. In my view, though, its dependence on earlier discoveries, and its smaller effect on survival and health, mean that it cannot be ranked above them all.",
"b316":"The father who looks after the children while the mother earns the family income was once a rarity, but he is becoming a more familiar figure. This change has economic and social causes, and I believe it is a positive development for parents and children alike.\n\nChief among the reasons is that women's earning power has grown. In many countries women are now as well qualified as men, and in a considerable number of couples the mother has the higher salary or the more secure post. When childcare is expensive and one parent has to stay at home, it makes financial sense for the lower earner to do so. Attitudes have shifted too. Younger men are generally more willing to take on domestic work, and countries such as Sweden offer paid leave reserved for fathers.\n\nThis arrangement benefits families because it allows them to decide according to their circumstances and not according to custom. A woman who has spent years training as a surgeon, for example, does not have to abandon her career, and her skills are not lost to society. The household also keeps the larger of its two incomes, which matters with children to support. Children gain as well. They build a close relationship with their fathers, and they grow up seeing that caring and earning are not tied to one sex. The picture is not entirely positive, however. Men at home can feel isolated in parent groups made up mostly of mothers. A father may also find it hard to return to work after several years away, although mothers have long faced the same difficulty.\n\nRising female earnings and changing expectations explain why more fathers are staying at home. Because it lets each family divide work and care in the way that suits it best, I think the trend is a welcome one.",
"b317":"Descriptions of the ideal society have differed widely, from communities with no private property to ones governed by philosophers. No real society will reach perfection, but I believe the element that matters most is fairness, meaning that the same rules apply to people whatever their wealth or origins.\n\nFairness comes first because most other goods depend on it. People will accept hard work, taxes and even personal disappointment if they are convinced that the system treats their neighbours no better than themselves. Once they suspect that jobs, court decisions or university places go to those with money or connections, cooperation gives way to resentment. A wealthy country in which a few families control most opportunities is unlikely to be called ideal by the majority. Fairness does not require that all citizens end up with the same income, though. Differences that reflect effort and talent are widely tolerated, whereas those that reflect privilege are not.\n\nMoving towards such a society requires institutions that cannot easily be bent. Courts must be independent of politicians, and public officials should be appointed through open competition rather than through personal ties. Equal treatment also has to begin early, since a child from a poor district who attends a failing school has lost the contest before it starts. Good public schooling and health care, as provided in the Nordic countries, for example, narrow that gap considerably. Laws alone are not enough, however, unless citizens themselves refuse to pay bribes or to ask for favours, and that habit is formed slowly through education and example.\n\nA perfect society will remain an aspiration, yet in my view the closest approach to it is one in which rules apply equally to all. It can be built only gradually, through impartial institutions, equal early opportunities and citizens who expect fairness of themselves.",
"b318":"Anyone curious about life in a distant country can choose between watching it and reading about it. I agree that film and video give a more immediate picture of how people live, but I think written documents are needed to understand what that picture means.\n\nMoving images convey things that are hard to put into words. A few minutes of footage from a market in Marrakesh or a fishing village in Vietnam show at once how people dress, what their homes look like and how a street sounds. A written account of the same scene would take pages and still leave the reader to imagine most of it. Film is also open to viewers who would not read a long book. Young children and adults with limited literacy can follow it without help, so it reaches a far wider audience. For gaining a first impression of everyday life, then, the camera is the better instrument.\n\nWhat the camera records, however, is only the surface. It shows a wedding ceremony but not why the family arranged it as they did, what it cost them or how such customs have altered over a generation. These matters are explained in writing: in histories, diaries, letters and the studies of anthropologists who have lived among a community for years. Film can also mislead. A documentary maker selects the most striking scenes, such as festivals and rituals, so that viewers may take the exceptional for the ordinary. Written sources are not free of bias either, although a text allows the author to state evidence and qualifications that a short film has no time for.\n\nVideo is the better way to see how others live, and writing the better way to understand it. I agree with the statement only in part, therefore, and believe that a serious learner should use both.",
"b319":"In many households, children now spend their afternoons in front of a screen instead of on a playing field. The reasons lie both in the appeal of the games and in the conditions of modern childhood, and I believe the shift is a negative one for their health and development.\n\nComputer games are designed to be hard to resist. They offer constant rewards, new levels and the company of friends online, and they are available at any hour without the need to leave the bedroom. Sport asks for more effort and offers less immediate satisfaction. Circumstances outside the child's control matter as well. In crowded cities there are few safe places to play, and parents who worry about traffic prefer their children to stay indoors. Schools under pressure to raise examination results have often cut the time given to physical education, while homework fills the hours that earlier generations spent outside.\n\nThe consequences are largely harmful. A child who sits for most of the day is more likely to gain weight and to develop poor posture and eyesight. Habits of inactivity formed early also tend to continue into adult life, when they are harder to break. Team sports teach lessons that are difficult to learn alone in a bedroom, such as cooperating with others, following a coach's instructions and losing gracefully. Children who miss these experiences may find group work at school harder. Games are not entirely to blame, since many develop problem-solving skills and quick reactions. The difficulty arises when gaming replaces physical activity rather than accompanying it, as increasingly happens.\n\nChildren choose screens because games are attractive and opportunities for sport have narrowed. Although gaming has some merits, I think a childhood with little physical exercise is a clear change for the worse, and one that parents and schools should work to reverse.",
"b320":"A generation ago, most people met the news once or twice a day, in a newspaper or an evening bulletin. It now reaches them continuously, and it shapes their decisions and moods far more than it used to. I believe this change is broadly positive, although it demands more care from audiences than the old arrangement did.\n\nThe main reason for the growing influence of news is that it has become hard to leave behind. A phone delivers headlines to its owner within minutes of an event, and social media ensures that even people who do not look for news see what their friends have shared. Events themselves also travel further. A decision taken by a central bank on another continent, for instance, can alter mortgage payments at home within weeks, so that distant stories have become personal ones. News matters more partly because more of it now concerns us directly.\n\nIn most respects this is a welcome development. Citizens who know what their government is doing are better placed to judge it, and officials behave more carefully when they expect to be reported. A council that knows its spending will be examined in the local press is less likely to waste public money. The difficulty is that speed and accuracy pull in different directions. Reports published in the first hour are often wrong, and outlets competing for attention tend to favour alarming stories, which leaves some readers anxious rather than informed. These costs are real, but they depend on how news is consumed and can be reduced by relying on a few trustworthy sources.\n\nNews has grown more powerful because it is constant and because the world it describes is more closely connected. On balance, I think a public that knows more is better off, provided that people learn to read with some scepticism.",
"b321":"The contents of an ordinary city supermarket basket may have crossed several borders before reaching the shelf. This is the result of urban demand and cheap transport, and I believe that, despite its environmental cost, it is on the whole a positive trend.\n\nFood travels so far chiefly because cities cannot feed themselves. As urban populations have grown, the farmland around them has been built over, and what remains cannot supply millions of residents. Their food has to come from somewhere else, and the larger the city, the further its suppliers tend to be. Cheap transport is the other reason. Refrigerated shipping and container freight have made it inexpensive to move produce across oceans, so retailers buy from wherever a crop grows best and is in season. Grapes on sale in London in January, for example, are likely to have come from South Africa or Chile.\n\nThe main benefit of this system is a diet that is varied, affordable and reliable. A poor harvest in one region no longer means empty shelves, because supplies can be drawn from another, and families on modest incomes can afford fresh produce in winter. Farmers in poorer countries gain as well, since exports give them access to customers far wealthier than those at home. This income supports rural jobs that local markets could not pay for. The cost is the fuel burned in transport, although this is smaller than it appears when goods travel by sea rather than by air. Tomatoes grown nearby in heated greenhouses may use more energy than those shipped from a sunnier climate.\n\nLong supply chains exist because cities are large and transport is cheap enough to supply them throughout the year. Although food flown in by air is hard to defend, I think the trend as a whole has done more good than harm.",
"b322":"Much of what people eat today is grown, processed and packed in different countries before it is sold. This pattern is driven mainly by cost, and in my view it is a negative development, because the price on the label does not reflect the damage it causes.\n\nThe chief reason is that producing food where it is cheapest saves more money than transporting it costs. Land, labour and climate vary greatly between countries, and large retailers, which buy in huge quantities, search the world for the lowest price. Processing follows the same logic. Fish caught in the North Atlantic, for instance, may be sent to Asia to be filleted and then shipped back to European shops, simply because wages there are lower. Trade agreements have removed many of the tariffs that once made such journeys uneconomic, and customers, who rarely look at the country of origin, reward whichever product is cheapest.\n\nAlthough these savings are real, they are possible only because part of the cost is paid by someone else. Lorries, ships and aircraft carrying food release large amounts of carbon, and produce that spends weeks in transit has to be picked unripe, chilled and heavily packaged. Flavour and freshness suffer as a result. Small farmers, meanwhile, struggle to compete with imports and many give up their land. The country then depends on distant suppliers, whose deliveries a war or a failed harvest can interrupt. Some crops, such as coffee and bananas, cannot be grown in cooler countries, and importing these is reasonable. The objection, however, is to moving food that could be produced nearby.\n\nDistance has become cheaper than local labour and land, which is why so much food now crosses the world. While some trade of this kind is unavoidable, I believe that its current scale harms both the environment and local agriculture.",
"b323":"Governments spend heavily on schools and universities and comparatively little on what young people do outside them. I disagree that education receives too much attention, although I believe that leisure provision for the young has been neglected and deserves greater public investment.\n\nThe suggestion that education is overemphasised is difficult to accept. A country's prosperity rests on the skills of its workforce, and for an individual child, good schooling is the most reliable route to a secure adult life. Many schools are, if anything, short of money, with large classes and ageing buildings. What can fairly be criticised is not the amount spent but the narrowness of what it buys. Where pupils spend every evening on homework and extra tuition, as happens in several East Asian countries, the pressure can harm their health without improving what they learn.\n\nFree-time activities answer this problem, and they merit more funding than they usually receive. Sport, music, drama and youth clubs develop confidence, teamwork and physical fitness, none of which is easily taught at a desk. They also give teenagers somewhere safe to go. In a neighbourhood with no sports ground or youth centre, for instance, bored adolescents are more likely to spend their evenings on the street or in front of a screen. Although private clubs exist, their fees exclude the children who would gain most, so public provision is the only way to reach them. This spending need not come out of the education budget, however, since leisure facilities are cheap in comparison with schools.\n\nEducation is not overvalued, and its funding should be protected. I agree, nevertheless, that governments ought to spend more on young people's leisure, treating it as a complement to schooling and not as a rival.",
"b324":"Relocating businesses and their staff to rural areas is sometimes proposed as a way of relieving the pressure on overcrowded cities. I disagree that this would solve their traffic and housing problems, although a limited version of the idea has some value.\n\nIn principle, the reasoning is sound. If fewer people worked in a city, fewer would need to live in it or travel across it, and demand for both homes and road space would fall. For some industries the move makes sense. Factories require large sites and heavy lorries, and they gain little from a central location, which is why many have already moved to cheaper land outside cities. Offices are a different matter. Banks, law firms and technology companies cluster together because they depend on clients, suppliers and a large pool of skilled applicants nearby, and few would accept the loss of these advantages.\n\nEven where relocation is possible, it tends to move problems rather than remove them. Employees cannot simply be transferred like equipment. Many have partners with jobs in the city and children settled in schools, so some would resign and others would commute long distances by car, which would increase traffic. The countryside, for its part, lacks the housing, roads, hospitals and schools that thousands of new residents would need. Building all of this would consume farmland and create the very congestion and shortages that the policy was meant to cure. A more realistic alternative is to direct growth towards medium-sized towns, such as regional capitals, which already have basic infrastructure and room to expand.\n\nMoving some industry out of major cities may ease the pressure slightly, but I do not believe it can solve problems on this scale. Better public transport and more homes within cities, together with stronger regional towns, are in my view more likely to succeed.",
"b325":"In many households, the evening meal at which parents and children once met has become an occasional event. This decline in shared time has two main sources, the demands of work and the pull of personal screens, and its consequences are felt both by individuals and by the family as a unit.\n\nMuch of the explanation is that paid work now occupies more of the day. In cities where housing is expensive, both parents usually need full-time jobs, and long commutes add further hours away from home. Work also follows people home, since a manager can send a message late at night and expect an answer. The time that remains is often spent apart, even under one roof. A teenager watching videos in her bedroom while her father scrolls through the news downstairs, for instance, is at home but hardly with her family. Technology is not solely to blame, of course, as it merely fills the gaps that tiredness leaves.\n\nThe effects on the individual are easy to overlook because they arrive slowly. Adults who see little of their relatives lose the people most willing to listen to their worries, and stress that conversation might have eased tends to build up. For the family, the cost is a loss of familiarity. Parents who rarely talk to their children may not notice trouble at school or a change in friends until it has become serious, and elderly relatives can feel forgotten. These outcomes are not inevitable, though. A family that protects even one unhurried meal a day can remain close despite busy schedules.\n\nLonger working days and private screens have reduced the hours families spend together, leaving individuals with less support and relatives with a weaker knowledge of one another. How serious the damage becomes depends largely on whether the remaining time is deliberately shared.",
"b326":"Smartphones and tablets have put a telephone, a library and a camera into almost every pocket. Critics claim that these devices do more harm than good. I disagree, because the difficulties they cause come mainly from how they are used, whereas their benefits are built into what they are.\n\nWith a mobile device, communication and information are no longer tied to a place. A traveller who is lost can consult a map, and a patient in a remote area can speak to a doctor by video. Relatives living on different continents can see one another daily at almost no cost. Mobile devices have also made ordinary life safer. A driver whose car breaks down at night, for example, can call for help at once rather than wait for a passing stranger. These gains are easy to overlook only because they have become so familiar.\n\nThe disadvantages are just as familiar. Constant notifications break concentration, and many people check their phones so often that conversations, meals and even sleep are interrupted. A student who glances at every message, for instance, may take an evening over work that needs an hour. The other main drawback is that employees can be reached at any hour, which blurs the line between work and rest. Someone who answers emails late at night returns to the office tired, without having been paid for the extra time. While these are genuine costs, they are not inevitable. A device can be silenced or left in another room, and families and employers can agree on limits, such as keeping phones away from the dinner table.\n\nMobile technology has brought habits that many users would be wise to control. I believe, however, that its advantages remain considerably greater, since the drawbacks can be limited by sensible use while the benefits cannot be replaced.",
"b327":"Teenagers tend to care more about the approval of their classmates than about that of any adult, and they adjust their clothes, habits and ambitions accordingly. This influence can work in a young person's favour, but I believe its drawbacks are greater, because it is strongest at the age when judgement is least developed.\n\nPeer pressure is sometimes a force for good. A student who joins a group of hard-working friends is likely to study more, simply because revising is what the group does on a Sunday afternoon. The same mechanism draws shy teenagers into sports teams, bands and volunteering. A girl who would not have auditioned alone may join the school choir because her friends have, and the confidence she gains can last. These gains depend, however, on the group a young person happens to fall into, which is largely a matter of chance.\n\nThe harm arises when the group's standards are poor, and adolescents are badly placed to resist them. The fear of being excluded is intense at fifteen, so a teenager may start smoking, miss lessons or join in bullying a classmate while privately knowing that it is wrong. Some of these decisions, such as leaving school early, have consequences that last far longer than the friendship. Social media has extended this pressure beyond the school gates. Teenagers now compare their looks and possessions with those of classmates late into the evening, and those who fall short can feel inadequate. Although confident teenagers with supportive parents often withstand it, many others do not.\n\nThe desire to fit in can raise a young person's efforts as easily as it can lower them. The damage, however, tends to last longer than the benefit and falls on those least able to refuse, which is why, in my view, the disadvantages of peer pressure outweigh its advantages.",
"b328":"Raising the price of fuel is often presented as the simplest answer to environmental damage, on the grounds that people use less of whatever costs more. I agree that higher prices have a useful effect, but I disagree that they are the best solution, since they address only one kind of harm and work poorly without alternatives.\n\nThe logic behind the proposal is sound as far as it goes. Coal, oil and gas are burned in power stations, factories, ships and homes, and their price rarely reflects the pollution they cause. When that cost is added through taxation, every user has a reason to economise. A manufacturer may invest in more efficient machinery, and a household may insulate its roof instead of turning up the heating. This approach is attractive because it leaves the choice of method to those who know their own circumstances best. Its success depends, however, on a cleaner option being available, and where there is none people simply pay more and carry on.\n\nA further weakness is that many environmental problems have little to do with fuel. Rivers are poisoned by fertiliser and factory waste, forests are cleared for cattle, and the seas are filling with plastic. None of these would be much affected by dearer energy. They call for different tools, such as limits on fishing, laws against dumping and protected status for threatened land. Even for climate change, price alone is a blunt instrument. It falls hardest on poorer families, who spend a larger share of their income on heating and cooking, unless the revenue is returned to them in some form.\n\nExpensive fuel can therefore contribute, particularly when the revenue is invested in clean energy. In my view, though, it is one measure among several and cannot by itself be called the best way to protect the environment.",
"b329":"English, Spanish and Mandarin gain new speakers every year, while hundreds of smaller languages are no longer being passed on to children. The spread of shared languages brings obvious benefits, but I believe the trend as a whole is a negative one, because those benefits do not require the loss that accompanies them.\n\nThe growth of a few major languages has made the world easier to move around in. Scientists publish in English and are read everywhere, pilots and air traffic controllers use it to avoid fatal misunderstandings, and a company in Vietnam can negotiate with one in Brazil without an interpreter. For individuals, knowing a widely spoken language opens the door to universities and jobs abroad. These are real gains. They are unevenly shared, however, since people who grow up speaking a dominant language enjoy an advantage that others must spend years of study to match.\n\nThe decline of smaller languages is a different matter, and it is here that the damage lies. A language is the main record of a community's history, songs, humour and knowledge of its surroundings, and much of this has not been written down. When children stop learning it, for example because school and television use only the national language, they can no longer talk easily with their own grandparents, and what the older generation knows disappears with them. This loss is permanent. Although linguists can record its vocabulary, a language no longer spoken at home rarely returns. The loss is also unnecessary, because people are perfectly able to speak two languages.\n\nA common language is valuable, but it should be an addition to people's mother tongues and not a replacement for them. Since the present trend is erasing small languages instead of supplementing them, I think it is, on balance, a negative development.",
"b330":"Older people are often the last to adopt devices that younger generations take for granted. This is unfortunate, because they stand to gain a great deal from modern technology, and the obstacles that keep them from it can be removed with patient teaching and better design.\n\nThe greatest benefit for elderly users is connection. Many retired people live alone and limited mobility can make visits rare. A video call allows a grandmother to see her grandchildren every week, not just on special occasions. Technology also supports independence. Groceries and medicines can be ordered for delivery, bills paid without a journey to the bank, and a doctor consulted from the living room. Wearable devices, such as watches that detect a fall and alert a relative, allow frail people to remain safely in their own homes for longer. These advantages are greatest for those who are housebound, although most older people would find some of them useful.\n\nEncouraging the elderly to use such devices depends on addressing the reasons for their reluctance, which are usually fear of making mistakes and fear of fraud. Free classes in libraries and community centres, taught slowly and in small groups, give learners the chance to practise without embarrassment. Family members can help by setting up a device and showing one function at a time, rather than explaining everything in a single afternoon. Phones with larger text, simpler menus and clear warnings about suspicious messages are also far less intimidating. Even with this support, some older people will prefer familiar methods, and services should not be withdrawn from them.\n\nTechnology can reduce the isolation of old age and help people stay independent. Older users will take it up more readily when they are taught patiently, protected from fraud and offered devices designed with their needs in mind.",
"b331":"A generation ago, most of what a child saw and heard before the age of ten came from parents, relatives and a few neighbours. That is no longer the case, and it is claimed that influences beyond the home now count for more than the family does. I agree to a large extent, at least once children reach school age.\n\nOutside influences now reach children earlier, and for more of the day, than they once did. In many households both parents work, so infants spend long hours in nurseries and older children in after-school clubs. The time left for family life is short. The internet has gone further by bringing the outside world into the home itself. A twelve-year-old with a phone, for example, may spend the evening in her bedroom following video creators and messaging classmates while her parents sit in the next room. Her tastes, her vocabulary and her idea of what is normal are being shaped by people her family has not met.\n\nIt would be a mistake to suppose that parents have become unimportant. The first years of life are still spent largely in their care, and it is then that a child learns to speak, to trust and to control his or her temper. These foundations affect how later influences are received. A teenager who feels secure at home is better able to resist pressure from friends than one who does not. Family influence has therefore narrowed rather than vanished, and it remains strongest where parents make time to talk to their children despite their working hours.\n\nSchools, friends and screens now occupy most of a child's waking life, and I believe their combined effect has overtaken that of the family. Parents still lay the foundations, but others build most of what stands on them.",
"b332":"Printed newspapers were for generations the main way in which people learned what was happening in the world. I disagree with the claim that they will keep this position, although I think the organisations that publish them will continue to matter a great deal.\n\nThe printed paper cannot match what readers now expect from news. A story published online reaches a phone within minutes and is corrected and extended as events develop, while a newspaper reports it the following morning. Online news is also free or cheap, and it arrives through devices that people already carry with them. Habits follow convenience. Younger adults in particular have grown up without buying a daily paper, and there is little reason to think they will start doing so in middle age. As sales fall, advertising revenue falls with them, which forces titles to raise prices or close, and the decline feeds itself.\n\nWhat will survive is not the paper but the journalism behind it. Much of the reliable reporting found online still comes from newsrooms that began in print, such as those of The Guardian or The New York Times. Their websites now reach far more readers than their printed editions did. In this sense the claim contains some truth. Trained reporters and editors remain the most trustworthy source of news, even though their work is read on a screen. There are also places where print retains its lead, including rural areas with poor internet coverage, and it remains popular among older readers. These are exceptions, however, and they are shrinking.\n\nFor most people, then, the newspaper as a physical object is likely to become a minority habit. I believe its lasting contribution will be the professional journalism it created, which will go on reaching the public online.",
"b333":"The steady movement of people from villages to towns and cities is one of the defining changes of the past century, and it is emptying the countryside in rich and poor countries alike. Although this shift carries real costs for the communities left behind, I believe it is, on balance, a positive development.\n\nThe main reason is that cities offer people more productive work than the land can. A small farm can support only so many adults, whereas a city concentrates factories, offices and services that pay regular wages. A young woman who leaves a rice-growing village for a garment factory, for instance, may earn far more than her parents do and send part of it home. Public services are a second gain, because hospitals, secondary schools and clean water are far cheaper to provide to people who live close together. A family that once travelled half a day to see a doctor may find a clinic within walking distance. These gains depend on cities having jobs and housing to offer, however, and where they do not, migrants exchange rural poverty for a slum.\n\nThe countryside pays a price. Those who leave tend to be young and ambitious, so villages are left with an ageing population, and schools, shops and bus routes close as the number of users falls. While these losses are serious, they are not a reason to keep people where opportunities are scarce. Fewer farmers can still feed a country when machinery replaces labour, and the money sent home by migrants often keeps rural households afloat.\n\nUrban migration improves more lives than it harms, and I believe the decline of rural populations is the cost of that improvement rather than a reason to resist it. The sensible response is to support the villages that remain, not to hold people in them.",
"b334":"When large numbers of pupils leave school unable to order a meal in the language they have studied for years, it is tempting to decide that they were incapable of learning it. I disagree with that verdict, and I think compulsory language teaching should stay, at least until the middle years of secondary school.\n\nWhat looks like inability is usually the result of how languages are taught. In many classrooms, a large group shares one teacher, each pupil speaks for only a few moments in a lesson, and assessment is mainly of written accuracy. Few people would learn to swim under equivalent conditions. The same children often pick up English phrases from songs, games and online videos, which shows that the capacity exists when a language is heard often and used for something they care about. Some pupils, such as those with severe dyslexia, do face real obstacles with spelling and reading. Even they, however, can usually manage a course that concentrates on listening and speaking.\n\nCompulsion is also defensible because fluency is not the only thing these lessons provide. A pupil who has struggled through basic Spanish has learnt how another language organises time, politeness and gender, and understands her own grammar better as a result. She has also had the experience of being a beginner, which makes her more patient with foreigners speaking her language. These gains do not require a high grade. They do depend on the lessons lasting long enough to matter, although there is little point in holding an unwilling sixteen-year-old in a class he has stopped attending in spirit.\n\nThe poor results that prompt this proposal are an argument for reforming language teaching, not for abandoning it. I believe schools should keep the subject compulsory in the earlier years and let older pupils decide whether to continue.",
"b335":"Childhood has become more demanding than it was a generation ago. Pupils are expected to excel at school, to be popular among their peers and to own the right things, and these three pressures share causes that parents, schools and governments can address.\n\nOne cause is that competition has moved earlier in life. Places at good universities and secure jobs have become harder to obtain, so parents and schools push children to prepare from a younger age. In some countries, pupils of seven or eight already attend evening classes and sit regular tests. A second cause is that screens now reach children directly. The comparison that once ended at the school gate continues at home through social media, where a child's appearance and friendships are on permanent display. Companies use the same channels to sell. An eight-year-old watching videos online, for instance, may see the same toy promoted by several presenters in a single afternoon.\n\nReducing these pressures requires adults to limit the competition rather than ask children to cope with it. Schools could test less often in the primary years and report progress in words instead of rankings. Pupils would then be compared with their own earlier work, which lowers anxiety without lowering standards. Governments can restrict advertising aimed at young children, as Sweden has long done on television, and require social media companies to enforce their own age limits. A ten-year-old who cannot open an account is spared both the comparison and much of the marketing. Such steps achieve less, however, unless parents support them and act together, since few children accept rules that none of their friends follow.\n\nThe pressures on children are largely the product of adult anxieties and commercial interests reaching into early life. They can be eased when schools, legislators and parents each accept responsibility for the part they control.",
"b336":"Because a warming climate affects every country, the question of who should act on it is often answered by pointing at ordinary people and their daily habits. I disagree with this view for the most part. Individuals have a part to play, but the main responsibility belongs to the governments and companies that shape the choices available to them.\n\nIndividual responsibility has an obvious appeal. Emissions are the result of what people buy, eat and drive, and a household that flies less or wastes less energy does reduce its own share. Such choices are limited, however, by circumstances that no single person controls. A commuter in a town with no reliable bus service has little alternative to the car, and a tenant cannot decide how the building is heated or where its electricity comes from. Asking people to change while leaving these conditions untouched produces guilt rather than results.\n\nGovernments and corporations, by contrast, decide what those conditions are. A state can tax carbon, close coal-fired power stations and invest in railways, and a single law of this kind alters the behaviour of millions at once. Large companies are equally influential, since energy producers, airlines and manufacturers account for a large share of emissions and choose the technology they use. When Denmark committed itself to wind power, for example, it was public policy and industrial investment that changed the country's energy supply, not the goodwill of separate households. Individual pressure still matters here, because politicians and firms respond to voters and customers, but its effect is indirect.\n\nPersonal effort is therefore worth making, yet it cannot carry the weight that this argument places on it. I believe that climate change will be brought under control only when those with the power to change whole systems accept the leading role.",
"b337":"Public sports grounds and swimming pools are expensive to build, and many councils struggle to maintain those they already have. One suggestion is that businesses should supply them for the neighbourhoods in which they operate. I agree that large firms should be encouraged to do this, but I do not think it can be made a general duty.\n\nThe case for corporate involvement is strongest where a company is large and has drawn heavily on its surroundings. A factory or office park relies on local roads, local workers and the patience of residents who live with its traffic and noise, so giving something back is a reasonable expectation. A firm that already owns a football pitch or gym for its staff, for example, can open it to nearby schools in the evenings at little extra cost. This argument applies, however, only to businesses with land and money to spare.\n\nTurning the idea into a rule would create more problems than it solved. Most companies are small, and a bakery or an accountancy practice with a dozen employees cannot be expected to fund a tennis court. Such firms often work on narrow margins, and an extra obligation of this kind could push some of them to cut jobs or close. Provision would also follow profit rather than need. Prosperous towns with several major employers would acquire excellent facilities, while poorer districts, where inactivity and poor health are most common, would receive nothing. Although businesses should contribute, the fairer route is through the taxes they pay, which councils can then spend where facilities are most needed.\n\nCompanies with the means to share or build sports facilities deserve praise and incentives. In my view, though, this cannot replace public provision, which remains the only way to serve every community and not merely the fortunate ones.",
"b338":"Video games are played in almost every country, by grandparents as well as schoolchildren, yet many parents regard them as a waste of time at best. I disagree with this judgement for the most part, since games can teach useful skills and become damaging mainly when they are played to excess.\n\nThe belief that games teach nothing does not survive a look at what players actually do. Strategy and building games require children to plan ahead, manage limited resources and try again after failure. In Minecraft, for example, players design structures and solve practical problems together, often with friends in other countries, and many pick up English along the way. Fast-moving games train attention and quick decision-making. This learning is admittedly informal and no substitute for reading or mathematics, but it is far from worthless. Some schools now use similar games in lessons because pupils concentrate on them willingly.\n\nWorries about harm are better founded, although the harm depends on quantity and content rather than on gaming itself. A child who plays late into the night will be tired at school, and one who rarely goes outside misses exercise and time with friends in person. Stopping is not easy, because many titles are built to keep players engaged for as long as possible. Content is the other concern. Violent games designed for adults are plainly unsuitable for young children, who may be frightened by scenes they are too young to put in context. These are good reasons to supervise, to set limits and to check age ratings. They are not reasons to forbid an activity that, in moderation, is no more dangerous than television.\n\nParents are right to be watchful, but mistaken, I believe, in dismissing computer games altogether. Chosen sensibly and limited in time, games offer children real intellectual and social benefits alongside the enjoyment.",
"b339":"The belief that better technology will bring crime down rests on a simple idea: people offend less when they expect to be caught. I agree with this prediction to a large extent, although the fall is likely to be uneven and smaller than optimists suppose.\n\nThe likelihood of detection tends to deter offenders more than the severity of punishment. A person considering a robbery is influenced less by the length of a possible sentence than by the odds of escaping one. Technology is changing those odds. Cameras in streets and shops, number-plate recognition and records of card payments mean that an offender now leaves a trail that is difficult to erase. Facial recognition at an airport, for example, can pick out a wanted person among thousands of travellers. As such systems spread, offences that are planned in advance, including theft, vandalism and vehicle crime, should become steadily less attractive.\n\nNot all crime is the product of calculation, however, and this is where the prediction reaches its limit. Assaults committed in anger or under the influence of alcohol are rarely preceded by any thought of consequences, and violence within the home takes place where no camera records it. Offences of this kind are unlikely to fall merely because detection has improved. Technology is also only as effective as the institutions that use it. Footage is useless if no officer has time to examine it, and forensic evidence loses its value when laboratories and courts take years to deal with a case. Although these factors do not cancel the benefit, they slow it considerably.\n\nCrime that depends on not being identified will probably decline as detection improves, while impulsive and hidden offences will be less affected. I believe, therefore, that the statement is broadly correct, provided that police and courts have the resources to use the new tools.",
"b343":"Economic growth and environmental harm have gone together for so long that many people regard the second as the unavoidable price of the first. I disagree with the claim in its absolute form. Development has usually damaged nature in the past, but in my view the link can be weakened considerably by the way an economy grows.\n\nThere is no denying the historical pattern. Industrial expansion requires energy, land and raw materials, and for two centuries these have come from burning coal and oil, clearing forests and mining. Rivers in manufacturing regions have been polluted by factory waste, and cities that grew quickly, such as Beijing or Delhi, have suffered from severe smog. Poorer countries face the hardest choice, since the cheapest route to higher incomes is still the dirtiest one. While their governments may wish to protect the environment, they are understandably reluctant to slow the growth that lifts people out of poverty.\n\nWhat this pattern shows, however, is how economies have developed, not how they must. Growth increasingly comes from services, software and design, which need fewer physical resources than steel or cement, and the energy behind it can be produced cleanly. Several European countries have expanded their economies while their carbon emissions fell. Prosperity also pays for protection, because wealthier societies can afford sewage treatment, cleaner engines and national parks that poorer ones cannot. Many rich cities have cleaner air today than when they were poorer. None of this happens automatically. It depends on regulation that makes polluters bear the cost of the harm they cause, and where such rules are absent, growth continues to do damage.\n\nThe word 'always' is therefore too strong. Some environmental cost is likely whenever an economy expands, but I believe that sensible policy and cleaner technology can keep it small enough for growth and nature to coexist.",
"b344":"The gap between the pay of those who run large organisations and those who work in them has become one of the most debated features of modern economies. Some regard it as the price of good leadership, while others see it as an injustice. I think a substantial difference is justified, but not one of the size now common.\n\nDefenders of high salaries argue that they reflect the scale of the job. A director's decisions affect thousands of employees and large sums of other people's money, and a single poor judgement about where to invest can cost more than the whole board is paid. People capable of bearing that responsibility are scarce, and organisations compete for them internationally. If a company refuses to match what rivals offer, it risks losing its most able managers. On this view, generous pay is what the market for a rare skill requires.\n\nCritics reply that results are produced by the whole workforce rather than by the person at the top. A supermarket chain, for example, earns its profit through the cashiers, drivers and warehouse staff who keep the shelves full, and many of them struggle to pay their rent. The other objection is that executive pay has little connection with merit. Directors sometimes receive large rises in years when wages are frozen or jobs are cut. This is possible because their salaries are often approved by committees made up of other highly paid executives, not set by an open market. Although a director's work is harder to replace than a cashier's, the difference is surely not as great as present pay suggests.\n\nBoth sides are therefore partly right. I believe that directors should earn considerably more than ordinary staff, provided that their pay rises and falls with the organisation's long-term performance and with the wages of the people they lead.",
"b347":"From polluted rivers to shrinking forests, some form of environmental harm can be found in almost every country. In my view, its root cause is that those who damage nature rarely pay for doing so, and effective solutions must begin by correcting this.\n\nMost damage arises because polluting is cheaper than the alternative. A factory that releases waste into a river saves the cost of treating it, while the people downstream bear the consequences. The same is true on a smaller scale. A motorist does not pay for the fumes the car produces, and a shopper pays nothing extra for packaging that will sit in a landfill for decades. Rising populations and growing demand for goods multiply these effects, but they are not the underlying problem. Where harm costs nothing, both businesses and consumers have little reason to avoid it, even if they would prefer a cleaner world.\n\nBecause the cause is largely economic, the response should be as well. Governments can tax pollution, so that a company finds it cheaper to clean up than to carry on, and they can fine those who break the rules heavily enough to deter them. Charges on plastic bags, for example, have cut their use sharply in countries such as Ireland at very little cost. Public money should also make the cleaner option the easier one. Commuters leave their cars at home when trains are frequent and reliable, and households recycle more when waste is collected from the door. Although laws of this kind work only if they are enforced, they change behaviour far more reliably than appeals to conscience.\n\nThe environment suffers mainly because harming it is free. I think the most effective remedy is therefore to attach a price to pollution and to make responsible choices cheap and convenient.",
"b348":"Languages spoken by only a few hundred elderly people fall silent every year, and it is sometimes said that the world is simpler for it. I disagree with this view. Fewer languages would make life only slightly easier, and the cost to the communities concerned is considerable.\n\nThe practical argument has some force. Translation is expensive, misunderstandings can be serious, and a country with dozens of tongues must decide which to use in its courts and schools. If humanity spoke a handful of languages, such difficulties would shrink. The convenience is easily exaggerated, though. People do not need to abandon their own language in order to communicate with outsiders, because they can learn a second one, as much of the world already does. Nor does a shared language guarantee harmony, given that many bitter conflicts have been fought between people who understood each other perfectly.\n\nWhat is lost, on the other hand, matters a great deal to those who lose it. A language ties people to their ancestors and to each other, and communities whose language has been suppressed often describe the experience as a loss of identity. This is why groups such as the Welsh and the Maori have invested so heavily in teaching their languages to a new generation. Knowledge is lost too. Small languages often contain detailed knowledge of local plants, weather and farming that exists nowhere else. Little of it is written down, so it dies with the last speakers. Although not every language can be saved, and speakers should not be pressed to keep one they no longer want, indifference is the wrong response.\n\nThe disappearance of languages removes a minor inconvenience at the price of something that cannot be recovered. For that reason, I believe it does matter, and that communities wishing to keep their languages alive deserve practical support.",
"b349":"Actors, singers and athletes now comment regularly on elections, climate policy and foreign conflicts, subjects far removed from the work that made them famous. In my view this is, on balance, a positive development, although it carries a risk that should not be ignored.\n\nThe chief benefit is attention. Many important issues receive little coverage because they are complicated or distant, and a well-known face can bring them before millions who do not follow the news. When a famous footballer campaigns for free school meals, for example, a problem that officials had discussed for years suddenly becomes a matter of public debate. Celebrities are also citizens, with the same right as a shopkeeper or a teacher to say what they think. It would be strange to deny that right to people merely because they are widely heard.\n\nThe danger lies in the gap between fame and knowledge. Success in film or sport gives a person an audience but no particular understanding of economics or medicine, and admirers may accept a star's opinion more readily than that of someone qualified. This is most serious in matters of health, where a careless remark about a treatment can lead followers to make harmful choices. Public debate may also become shallower if positions are adopted because a favourite singer holds them. These risks are real, although they depend largely on how the audience responds. Where people treat a celebrity's statement as a reason to find out more, rather than as the final word, the influence is healthy.\n\nFamous people who speak on public issues sometimes mislead, but more often they draw attention to matters that deserve it. I believe their involvement does more good than harm, as long as their views are weighed like anyone else's.",
"b350":"University timetables, reading lists and examinations can easily fill every waking hour of a student's week. I agree that undergraduates should make room for other pursuits, as long as these support their degree instead of competing with it.\n\nA degree alone no longer distinguishes one graduate from another. Employers interviewing candidates with similar grades look for evidence that a person can work with others, organise an event or cope with responsibility, and lectures rarely provide it. A student who has run the finances of a university society, for instance, or worked at weekends in a busy café, can describe real situations in which she solved problems and dealt with difficult people. Such experience also helps students discover what kind of work suits them before they commit to a career. An undergraduate who volunteers at a legal advice centre may find that the law is less appealing in practice than in theory, which is far better learnt at twenty than at thirty.\n\nActivities outside the library benefit study itself, too. Long hours of reading without a break produce diminishing returns, and students who play a sport or an instrument often return to their books with clearer minds. There is a limit, however. Courses such as medicine or engineering leave little spare time, and a student who takes on a demanding job in order to pay rent may see her grades suffer. The aim should be a few commitments chosen with care rather than a diary so crowded that the degree becomes an afterthought.\n\nAcademic work must remain the priority, since it is the reason for being at university. Even so, I believe students who spend some of their time on sport, work or voluntary activity leave better prepared than those who only study.",
"b351":"The ability to alter the genes of plants, animals and people is among the most powerful tools science has produced. Supporters expect it to cure disease and reduce hunger, while opponents fear damage that cannot be undone. I believe the benefits are likely to be considerable, but only if the technology is introduced under strict public control.\n\nOptimists can point to what has already been achieved. The insulin on which diabetic patients depend, for example, has for decades been made by genetically modified bacteria. Gene editing is now being used to treat inherited blood disorders such as sickle cell disease, which were once managed but not cured. In agriculture, crops have been developed that tolerate drought or resist pests, which allows farmers to harvest more while spraying less. These examples suggest that the technique is a practical means of solving old problems, although each new application still has to prove its safety separately.\n\nThe fears of critics concern what happens once altered organisms leave the laboratory. A modified crop can spread its genes to wild relatives, and unlike a chemical spill, a living organism reproduces and cannot simply be cleaned up. The consequences for insects, soil and other species may take years to appear. Editing human embryos raises a different worry, since the changes would pass to future generations who had no say. If wealthy parents could buy advantages for their children, inequality might become inherited. While these dangers are serious, they arise from particular uses of the technology rather than from the science as a whole.\n\nBoth sides are therefore partly right: genetic engineering can relieve suffering, and it can also cause harm that is hard to reverse. In my view, it should be welcomed in medicine and farming, with careful testing of each use and firm limits on altering human inheritance.",
"b352":"Clean streets, tidy parks and unpolluted streams benefit those who live beside them more than anyone else. I agree that governments should require residents to take some responsibility for their surroundings, as long as the authorities continue to provide the services that individuals cannot.\n\nThe principle is sound because local damage is mostly caused locally. Litter, dumped furniture and dog mess are left by people who live or pass nearby, and councils then spend public money removing what need not have been dropped. Making residents answerable changes this. In Japan, for example, pupils clean their own classrooms and neighbours take turns to sweep shared streets, and the result is public space that is noticeably cleaner than in many wealthier cities. People who have maintained a place themselves are also less inclined to spoil it, so the habit tends to last.\n\nHow governments impose this duty matters a great deal. Fines for littering and for failing to sort household waste are reasonable, since they ask for nothing beyond ordinary care. Compulsion should stop, however, where the task exceeds what a household can do. A resident cannot repair a sewer, clear an illegal dump left by a business or reduce the fumes from a nearby main road. It would be unjust to hold a neighbourhood responsible for such problems. There is a risk, too, that officials use the idea of personal duty as an excuse to cut street cleaning and waste collection. Responsibility works only if bins, recycling points and regular collections are provided, because rules that cannot be followed are simply resented.\n\nGovernments are right, in my view, to expect people to look after the places where they live. That expectation should nevertheless be matched by proper public services, so that the duty placed on citizens is one they can actually meet.",
"b353":"It is sometimes argued that quality sells itself and that a company with a worthwhile product has no need to promote it. I disagree with most of this argument, because a product cannot be bought by people who do not know it exists.\n\nQuality matters only after a customer has tried a product, and advertising is usually what brings about that first trial. A new brand of running shoe may be better than anything on the market, but it sits on a shelf beside dozens of established names, and shoppers with limited time choose what they recognise. Without some form of promotion, the firm depends on chance discovery, which is too slow to cover the costs of production. Competitors, meanwhile, are advertising. Many well-designed products have failed for this reason while inferior ones with larger marketing budgets succeeded. A good product is therefore necessary for lasting success, but it is rarely sufficient.\n\nThe claim is more convincing where reputation can spread without help. A neighbourhood bakery or a village mechanic, for example, relies on personal recommendation, and customers who are satisfied do the advertising for nothing. Online reviews have extended this effect, allowing a small hotel to attract guests from abroad on the strength of its ratings alone. Even here, though, the business has had to make itself visible by listing its services on a website or a booking platform, which is promotion in another form. Word of mouth also works slowly and locally. A company hoping to sell across a whole country cannot wait for praise to travel from one household to the next.\n\nA product that meets real needs will keep its customers, and no campaign can rescue a bad one for long. To win those customers in the first place, however, I believe most businesses still need to advertise.",
"b354":"As companies recruit across borders, a foreign degree is often presented to families as the surest route to a high salary. It can be a real advantage, but in my view it is one good path among several, and for many young people a less expensive one will serve equally well.\n\nSupporters of overseas study argue that it provides what international employers want most. A graduate who has spent three years at a university in Britain or Australia has usually acquired fluent English, the confidence to work with people from different backgrounds, and a qualification that recruiters recognise. These qualities are hard to obtain at home, and multinational firms in cities such as Ho Chi Minh City or Jakarta do pay a premium for them. The advantage is weaker than it once was, however. So many students now return with foreign degrees that the certificate alone no longer guarantees an interview, and those who studied at little-known institutions may find their investment hard to recover.\n\nOthers maintain that there are more reliable routes. A student who attends a strong local university, completes internships and learns English well can enter the job market years earlier and without debt. By the time her classmates return from abroad, she has experience to show and contacts among local employers, which they lack. In fields such as law, accountancy or medicine, a domestic qualification is the one that counts, because professional rules differ from country to country. A foreign law degree may require further examinations before its holder can practise. These alternatives depend on self-discipline, though, as the student must arrange the experience herself.\n\nStudying abroad suits those entering international careers who can gain admission to a respected institution. For most others, I believe a good local degree combined with work experience and strong English offers a better return.",
"b356":"Clothes worn a handful of times, phones replaced while they still work and furniture too cheap to be worth repairing have become normal in many societies. This habit of discarding has clear commercial and cultural causes, and its consequences are felt well beyond the household bin.\n\nGoods are thrown away chiefly because replacing them is now cheaper and easier than mending them. Mass production has lowered prices so far that a new kettle may cost less than an hour of a repairer's time. Manufacturers add to the problem by designing goods that cannot easily be opened or fixed, such as phones with sealed batteries, because their profits depend on repeated sales. The second cause is the pressure of advertising and fashion. Consumers are encouraged to see last year's model as out of date although it performs perfectly well, and new purchases become a source of status. Clothing chains, for instance, now release new styles every few weeks, so a shirt can seem old after a single season.\n\nThe problems this creates begin with waste. Landfill sites fill up, plastic that is not collected ends up in rivers and oceans, and discarded electronics release toxic metals when they are dumped or burned. Much of this rubbish is shipped to poorer countries, where those who sort it are exposed to its dangers. Less visible, but equally serious, is the waste of resources. A product that is thrown away early represents oil, water, minerals and energy that were used to make it and must be used again for its replacement. Recycling, despite its benefits, recovers only a fraction of these materials.\n\nA throwaway culture, then, arises because cheap goods, deliberate design and constant marketing make discarding the easy option. The price is paid in pollution and in lost resources.",
"b357":"Teachers in several countries say that they now spend more time keeping order than teaching. In my view, the main causes are weaker boundaries at home and schools that have lost the means to enforce their rules, so solutions must restore both.\n\nPoor behaviour usually starts with what children have learnt to expect from adults. Parents who work long hours may have little energy left to insist on bedtimes, homework or good manners, and some avoid conflict by giving in. A child who is seldom refused anything at home arrives at school unprepared to wait, share or accept an instruction. Schools then find it difficult to respond. In many systems, teachers can no longer remove a disruptive pupil from the room without lengthy paperwork, and parents sometimes side with their child against the school. Not all misbehaviour has this origin, since some reflects boredom with lessons pitched at the wrong level, but inconsistent discipline allows minor problems to grow.\n\nThe remedy I would suggest is consistency, beginning with a small number of clear rules that every member of staff applies in the same way. Banning mobile phones during lessons, for example, works only if all teachers enforce it, because a rule that depends on the individual teacher invites argument. Parents belong in the same system, and should be invited in after the first serious incident instead of receiving a letter after the tenth. Punishment alone is not enough, however. Pupils who disrupt lessons repeatedly often have difficulties with reading or problems at home, and access to a counsellor or extra tuition can remove the cause as well as the symptom.\n\nDisorder in classrooms grows where neither home nor school sets firm limits. I think it can be reduced when rules are few, consistently applied and supported by parents, with help for the pupils who struggle most.",
"b358":"Courts in many countries are dealing with a growing number of offenders who are still in their teens. This trend has less to do with young people becoming worse than with the conditions in which many of them now grow up, and the remedies follow from those conditions.\n\nOne cause is that many teenagers have too little adult attention and too little to do. Parents who work long hours cannot easily know where a fifteen-year-old spends the evening, and schools that exclude difficult pupils hand them still more empty time. A second cause is the pull of older peers. A boy who is failing at school may find that a local gang offers him the status, money and sense of belonging that nothing else in his life provides. Poverty alone does not explain the trend, however, as most teenagers from poor families break no laws.\n\nIf idle, unsupervised time is the problem, the most promising response is to fill it. Youth clubs and sports teams cost little compared with courts and custody, and they place teenagers in regular contact with adults who notice when something is going wrong. Schools offer the other main solution. They can keep troublesome pupils in supervised education rather than sending them home, and offer practical training to those who see no point in academic lessons. A teenager learning a trade such as plumbing has a legal way to earn money and less reason to look to a gang. Although punishment must remain available for serious offences, locking up a sixteen-year-old tends to introduce him to more experienced criminals.\n\nRising youth crime is largely the product of teenagers left to themselves at the age when guidance matters most. It will fall when communities give them somewhere to go, someone to answer to and a believable route into adult work.",
"b359":"Burgers, fried chicken and similar meals can now be bought within minutes of most homes, often for less than the cost of cooking. This change has obvious attractions, but I believe its disadvantages are considerably greater, mainly because of what it does to public health.\n\nCheap fast food brings practical benefits. It offers a hot, filling meal to people with little money or time, such as students, night-shift workers and parents returning late from work. For a family on a tight budget, a meal that costs less than cooking at home is a real help. The industry is also a source of employment. Many young people with few qualifications find their first job in a fast-food outlet, where they learn to keep to a timetable and deal with customers. These advantages should not be dismissed. They hold, however, only when such meals are eaten occasionally rather than as the basis of a diet.\n\nThe lower the price and the nearer the outlet, the less occasional fast food becomes, and this is where the harm lies. Meals of this kind are typically high in fat, salt and sugar and low in fibre, so regular consumption contributes to obesity, diabetes and heart disease. The burden also falls unevenly. In poorer districts, takeaway shops often outnumber places selling fresh produce, and for a family living there the unhealthy option is both the easiest and the cheapest. Although an adult is free to choose what to eat, the eventual cost of treatment is shared by all taxpayers.\n\nCheap and plentiful fast food saves time and money in the short term. Weighed against the long-term damage to health and the public expense that follows, these gains are modest, and for that reason I think the drawbacks clearly outweigh them.",
"b360":"Salaries and prices, once considered private matters in many cultures, now come up freely in conversation among friends and colleagues. This change reflects both rising living costs and a new openness encouraged by the internet, and I believe it is, with some reservations, a healthy one.\n\nPeople talk about money more because it occupies more of their thoughts. Where rents, school fees and food prices rise faster than wages, working out how to manage becomes a shared daily problem, and comparing notes is a natural response. Online life has reinforced the habit. Job websites publish typical pay for different positions, and on social media people openly describe what they earn, what they paid for a flat or how they invest. Younger adults who have grown up with this openness see little reason to treat income as a secret, although older generations in the same family may still find the subject uncomfortable.\n\nThe trend is largely beneficial because information about money is useful only when it circulates. An employee who learns that a colleague doing the same job is paid more, for instance, is in a position to ask for a fair rise, whereas silence mainly protects employers who underpay. Friends who discuss prices and savings can likewise avoid bad deals and learn habits that schools rarely teach. The danger lies in the tone of these conversations rather than in their subject. When talk about earnings turns into boasting or constant comparison, it produces envy and encourages people to judge one another by income alone.\n\nMoney has become an everyday topic because financial pressure is widely felt and information about pay is easier to find. Provided that such conversations are practical and not competitive, I think they do more good than harm, since openness helps ordinary people make better decisions.",
"b361":"The family dinner cooked from local ingredients is giving way, in many places, to meals bought from a counter and eaten quickly. I agree that this shift is harmful at each level the statement mentions, although fast food is more a symptom of modern pressures than their only cause.\n\nFor individuals and their families, the loss is partly nutritional and partly social. Traditional cooking in most cultures is built around vegetables, grains and modest amounts of meat, whereas a typical fast-food meal supplies a great deal of fat and salt and little else. A person who eats it several times a week is more likely to gain weight and to develop related illnesses. The household changes too. Preparing and sharing a meal is, in many homes, the one daily occasion when parents and children talk without distraction. When each member eats separately, that habit weakens, although long working hours are as much to blame as the food itself.\n\nSociety as a whole pays in two ways. Poor diets raise the incidence of conditions like diabetes, and the expense of treating them falls on public health systems and therefore on taxpayers. The second cost is cultural. Recipes are usually passed on by cooking alongside an older relative, so a generation that seldom cooks has little to hand down. Dishes that once marked a region or a festival, such as the rice cakes Vietnamese families prepare together at New Year, risk becoming something bought in a shop. This decline is not inevitable, however, since many countries still protect and celebrate their cuisine.\n\nFast food offers convenience, but where it displaces home cooking, people's health, family life and shared traditions all suffer. That is why I agree with the statement, while recognising that the remedy lies in giving families more time as well as better food.",
"b362":"Lecture halls in engineering are still filled mostly with men, while courses in literature and languages attract far more women. I agree that this pattern should be changed, but only in part. The pressures that produce it deserve to be removed, but equal numbers should not become a target in themselves.\n\nChange is desirable to the extent that the pattern reflects expectation instead of preference. Girls who are good at mathematics are still sometimes told that physics is a subject for boys, and boys who enjoy poetry may be teased for it. Choices made under such pressure are not free ones. Scientific and technical careers are among the better paid, so steering women away from them contributes to the gap in earnings between the sexes. Teams that design products for the whole population also work better when they include both. A voice-recognition system tested mainly by male engineers, for instance, may perform less well with women's voices.\n\nIt does not follow that schools and universities should aim for an even split in each subject. Once young people have seen what each field involves and feel welcome in it, some differences in what they choose may remain, and there is nothing wrong with that. Quotas or financial incentives designed to push women into science, or men into the arts, would simply replace one form of pressure with another. A student who is persuaded into a degree she does not enjoy is unlikely to do well in it. What can reasonably be asked of educators is equal encouragement, visible role models and career advice that makes no assumptions, although results from such measures tend to come slowly.\n\nThe imbalance should be addressed where it stems from stereotypes and discouragement. Beyond that point, I believe students' own choices should be respected, whatever proportions they produce.",
"b363":"Emails, chat messages and video calls have replaced the meeting room as the normal channel of communication in a large number of organisations. I believe this shift brings more benefits than drawbacks, though certain kinds of conversation are still better held in person.\n\nThe main advantage of communicating online is efficiency. A question that once required a scheduled meeting can be settled in a short message, and colleagues in different cities or time zones can contribute without travelling. A design team in London, for example, can review a drawing with a factory in Shenzhen on the same afternoon, which would previously have meant days of delay or an expensive flight. Written messages also leave a record. Staff can check what was agreed without relying on memory, and those who were absent can catch up easily. These gains in speed and clarity are considerable, although they are greatest for routine coordination.\n\nThe disadvantages concern what a screen fails to convey. Tone is easily misread in a brief email, and remarks that would have been softened by a smile can cause offence. Although video calls help, participants still miss much of the body language that signals doubt or disagreement, so problems may surface later than they should. Constant messages are a second drawback. An employee who stops to answer every notification rarely gets an uninterrupted hour, and work that needs concentration takes longer. These weaknesses matter most in sensitive situations, such as giving criticism or negotiating a contract. They matter far less when the purpose is simply to share information, which accounts for most workplace communication.\n\nOnline tools save time, cut travel and include people who could not otherwise take part. In my view these advantages outweigh the loss of personal contact, provided that organisations continue to meet face to face when trust or delicate judgement is involved.",
"b364":"The model of a husband who earns and a wife who keeps house has given way, in many countries, to marriages in which both partners work. It is now argued that supporting the family financially is a duty the two share. I agree that the responsibility is joint, although I do not think this means both must be in paid work at every stage.\n\nThere are strong reasons for treating income as a shared obligation. In most cities a single salary no longer covers housing, schooling and healthcare comfortably, so two wages protect the family against hardship. They also protect it against bad luck. If one partner falls ill or is made redundant, the household still has money coming in. Shared earning is fairer to both sides as well. It relieves one person of the pressure of being the sole provider and gives the other financial independence, which matters greatly if the marriage ends.\n\nA shared responsibility is not the same as an identical contribution, however. Families go through periods in which it makes sense for one partner to earn less or nothing. A couple with a newborn baby, for example, may decide that one of them will stay at home for a year or two, and the same may apply when a relative needs care. Raising children and running a household are work, even though they are unpaid, and they make the other partner's career possible. What matters is that such arrangements are agreed between the two rather than assumed on the basis of gender.\n\nProviding for a family is, in my view, a duty that belongs to both spouses equally. How they divide paid and unpaid work between them should nevertheless be a matter for each couple to settle.",
"b365":"In wealthy societies a trip to the shops is often planned as a day out rather than an errand. I believe this is mainly a positive development, provided that the enjoyment comes from the outing itself and not from spending beyond one's means.\n\nShopping for pleasure gives people an accessible place to spend time together. Modern shopping centres combine stores with cafes, cinemas and play areas, so a family or a group of friends with different interests can share an afternoon under one roof. In the city where I live, for example, many teenagers meet at the largest mall at weekends because it is safe, air-conditioned and free to enter. Most of them leave having bought little more than a drink. Markets serve a similar purpose. Visitors to a Christmas market in Germany go for the lights, the food and the company rather than for the goods on sale. Since nothing has to be bought, such outings are open to people with little money.\n\nThe danger lies in confusing entertainment with consumption. Retailers design their stores to prolong visits and prompt unplanned purchases, and someone who shops to lift a low mood can easily buy things that are soon forgotten. I have seen friends return from a sale delighted with clothes they later admitted they had no use for. This habit wastes money and, on a larger scale, resources. It is not an inevitable result of enjoying shops, however. A person who sets a budget, or who simply likes looking, gains the pleasure without the cost, much as a visitor enjoys a gallery without buying a painting.\n\nTreating shopping as recreation is a problem only when the recreation depends on buying. For most people it is a sociable and inexpensive pastime, which is why I think the trend is more positive than negative.",
"b366":"The typical family of two or three generations ago, with a working father, a mother at home and several children, is no longer the norm in many societies. Both the shape of the household and the tasks of its members have altered. I think these changes are largely positive, though they have brought some losses that should be recognised.\n\nThe most visible change is in size and composition. Couples marry later and have fewer children, grandparents more often live separately, and single-parent households have become common. Roles inside the home have shifted at the same time. Women now work outside it in large numbers, and in many families they earn as much as their husbands or more. Men, for their part, are expected to share the cooking and the care of children, tasks their own fathers seldom performed. Decisions that used to belong to the head of the household, such as where to live or how to spend savings, are increasingly taken jointly.\n\nMost of these developments have improved family life. Women who earn their own income have more say and more security, and children benefit from fathers who are closely involved in their upbringing. A girl who sees both parents working and both cooking grows up with a wider sense of what she may become. The gains have come at a price, however. When both parents work long hours, time together is scarce and children may spend their evenings alone. Smaller, scattered families also leave elderly relatives with less company, unless they live close enough to be visited regularly.\n\nFamilies today are smaller, more equal and more varied than they were. In my view this is a change for the better overall, although it requires parents to guard the time they spend with their children and with their own parents.",
"b367":"Oil companies are now working in places that were once thought too distant or too hostile to exploit, from Arctic waters to deep rainforest. Supporters stress the income this brings, while opponents warn of the harm to fragile regions. I believe the environmental risks are the weightier consideration in most such locations.\n\nEconomically, the case for drilling is a real one. For a country with few other resources, a new oilfield can pay for roads, hospitals and schools that would otherwise be unaffordable. Norway shows how such income can also be saved and invested for future generations. The other benefit is local. Drilling creates skilled jobs and brings ports, airstrips and power supplies to areas that had none, and these can remain useful to residents after the oil is gone. These gains depend, however, on honest and capable government. Where revenue is poorly managed, the profits go to foreign firms and a small elite, and the surrounding communities see little of them.\n\nThe environmental objections are stronger precisely because the sites are remote. Such regions are often among the last untouched habitats, and their wildlife, for example Arctic seals and polar bears, has nowhere else to go. Roads and pipelines built to serve a well also open these areas to further disturbance. Distance makes accidents harder to contain as well. A spill in icy or deep water may spread for weeks before equipment and crews can reach it, and oil breaks down very slowly in cold conditions. Although modern technology has reduced some of these dangers, it has not removed them, and the damage from a single failure can last for decades.\n\nOil revenue is valuable but temporary, whereas a ruined ecosystem may not recover. Drilling in remote areas should therefore, in my view, be permitted only under strict conditions, with the most sensitive regions left alone.",
"b368":"Queues of slow-moving vehicles have become a daily feature of life in places as different as large capitals and small market towns. The problem arises when too many people try to use the same roads at the same time, and the most effective responses are those that change when and how people travel.\n\nCongestion is caused less by the number of cars than by the way they are used. Most journeys are concentrated into two short periods, when offices open and schools begin and when both close, so roads that are quiet at noon are full at rush hour. The second cause is that many of these cars carry only a driver. People drive alone because the alternatives are weak: buses are infrequent or stuck in the same traffic, and housing estates are often built far from workplaces. A handful of commuters therefore occupy the road space that a single bus would need for fifty passengers.\n\nBecause the cause is a pattern of behaviour, the solutions must alter that pattern. Buses become attractive once they are given their own lanes, as passengers then see them moving past stationary cars. Cities such as London and Seoul have drawn commuters out of their cars in this way. Employers can help by allowing flexible starting times or occasional home working, which spreads demand across the day at almost no cost. If even a small share of staff travel an hour earlier or later, the morning peak becomes noticeably lighter. Building additional roads, however, tends to bring only temporary relief, although a bypass can be justified where heavy lorries pass through a town centre.\n\nTraffic jams are the product of shared habits rather than of a shortage of tarmac. They ease when public transport is made faster than driving and when fewer people need to travel at the same hour.",
"b369":"Urban transport is moving in opposite directions at once. In some cities the bicycle is giving way to the car, while in others cycle lanes are taking space that once belonged to traffic. I believe the second trend is the healthier of the two.\n\nThe difference is largely explained by the stage of development a city has reached. Where incomes have risen recently, as in many Asian cities, a car is the first major purchase a family can make. It offers comfort, protection from heat and rain, and a visible sign of success. Cycling in such places is associated with a poorer past. Wealthier cities have already lived through the results of mass car ownership. Copenhagen and Amsterdam, for example, have spent decades building separated cycle paths because congestion and polluted air had made driving a poor way to cross town. Residents there choose bicycles because they are often the quickest option rather than because a car is beyond their means.\n\nOf the two developments, the return to cycling brings greater benefits. A bicycle takes up a fraction of the road space of a car, produces no exhaust, and gives its rider daily exercise, so a city that cycles is quieter, cleaner and fitter. The car remains the better tool for long distances, heavy loads and people with limited mobility, and no city can do without it. For the short trips that make up most urban travel, however, it is an inefficient choice. Its advantages also shrink as more people share them, since a road full of cars moves slowly whoever is driving.\n\nCities where bicycles are replacing cars are, in my view, heading in the better direction. The growth of car use elsewhere is understandable, but it leads towards problems that richer cities are now paying to undo.",
"b370":"In a number of countries, people are becoming heavier and less fit with each decade. I think the explanation is that daily life now demands very little physical effort while offering more food than the body needs. Reversing the trend means building activity back into ordinary routines.\n\nThe chief cause, in my opinion, is the disappearance of movement from everyday life. Work that once involved lifting, walking or standing is now largely done seated at a screen, and journeys that were made on foot or by bicycle are made by car. Leisure has followed the same path, with television, video games and phones replacing sport and outdoor play. Diet is the second cause. Calorie-dense snacks and sweet drinks are cheap and within reach at all hours, and they are often easier to obtain than a cooked meal. People therefore consume more than their grandparents did while burning less, and although a gym membership can help, relatively few keep it up.\n\nSince the cause lies in routine, the most promising measures are those that alter routine rather than relying on willpower. City authorities can make walking and cycling the easy choice by building safe lanes, wide pavements and parks close to housing. Copenhagen, for example, has shown that when cycling is quick and safe, a large share of commuters will choose it. Schools are the other place to act. They should give pupils daily physical education and a choice of sports, as a child who finds one enjoyable is likely to stay active as an adult. These measures work slowly, however, and they need the support of honest food labelling.\n\nWeight has risen and fitness has fallen because modern life asks so little of the body. I think the answer is to design towns, schools and workplaces so that moderate daily activity happens almost without being planned.",
"b371":"Companies selling sweets, toys and games increasingly address their advertising to children instead of to the adults who pay. Parents often object that this is unfair, while advertisers regard it as ordinary marketing. In my view the parents are right, at least where young children are concerned.\n\nThose who defend such advertising argue that children are consumers like any other. They have preferences, they often receive pocket money, and learning to deal with commercial messages is part of growing up in a market economy. Advertisers add that the final decision rests with parents, who are free to refuse. On this view an advertisement only tells a family what is available, and the adults remain in control of the money. While this reasoning has some force for teenagers, who are capable of recognising a sales pitch, it is far weaker for a five-year-old.\n\nParents' objection is that advertising to young children exploits an unequal contest. A small child cannot reliably tell where a cartoon ends and a commercial begins, and tends to believe what a friendly character says. The advertiser is, in effect, using the child to put pressure on the parent. A mother at a supermarket checkout, for instance, faces a tired child asking for the chocolate bar seen on television that morning, and each refusal carries a price in tears. An adult facing the same message would at least know that something was being sold. Although parents can and should say no, it is unreasonable to design a system that requires them to do so many times a day.\n\nAdvertising to adolescents can be defended as part of normal commercial life, but persuading children too young to understand persuasion is a different matter. I believe it is unfair, and that it should be restricted by law.",
"b372":"Whether an undergraduate stays in the family home or moves to another city is partly a matter of national custom and partly one of cost. I believe that, where a family can afford it, living away brings benefits that exceed the drawbacks.\n\nIndependence is the principal gain, and it is hard to acquire under a parent's roof. A student in a shared flat has to budget for rent and food, cook, wash clothes and settle disputes with housemates, and there is no parent to do these things for him. Mistakes are made, but they are small and instructive ones, such as running short of money in the last week of the month. By graduation, such a student is usually better prepared for working life than one whose meals and laundry have been taken care of. Moving also widens choice. A school-leaver who is willing to relocate can apply for the best course in her subject rather than merely the nearest one, and she will meet people from regions and backgrounds unlike her own.\n\nThe disadvantages are chiefly financial and emotional. Rent in university cities is high, and students without family support may need loans or long hours of paid work, which can affect their studies. Some first-year students also feel lonely and miss the comfort of familiar people. These costs are real, although they are often temporary. Homesickness usually fades once friendships form, and universities offer counselling and shared halls designed to ease the transition. Debt is the more serious concern, and for a student whose local university is strong, staying at home may well be the wiser decision.\n\nLeaving home to study is expensive and at first unsettling. Even so, I think the maturity, wider choice of courses and broader social experience it provides outweigh those costs for most students who have the means.",
"b373":"For centuries, anyone who wanted to learn something had to find the right book, and often the right library. Most of what people need to know is now published online, and I believe this shift has brought far more benefit than harm, despite the unreliable material that comes with it.\n\nKnowledge published online no longer depends on where a person lives or what they can afford, and this is its greatest advantage. A student in a rural school, for example, can now watch a university lecture or consult an encyclopedia that her school could not have purchased. The other main benefit is that online material can be kept up to date. An error is corrected as soon as it is found and new findings are added within days, while a printed mistake stays on the shelf for years. People without reliable internet access remain excluded, however.\n\nThe principal drawback is that the internet has no editor. A book normally passes through a publisher, who checks it before it is printed, but anyone may upload a claim about medicine or history, and it will appear beside the work of specialists. Readers who cannot tell the two apart may be misled, sometimes with serious consequences for their health. A second drawback is shallow reading. A search returns a short answer in seconds, so people often skim a paragraph where they would once have worked through a chapter, and they understand less. Although these risks are real, they are problems of skill rather than of the medium itself. Schools that teach pupils to check who wrote a page, and to read at length, greatly reduce them.\n\nSince the main weaknesses of online knowledge can be reduced by teaching people to read carefully, while its reach far exceeds anything that printing achieved, I think the advantages clearly outweigh the disadvantages.",
"b374":"History records a long list of wars begun by kings, generals and presidents, almost all of them men. Some conclude from this that female rule would bring peace and better government, but I disagree, because the behaviour of leaders owes more to the systems they work within than to their sex.\n\nThe argument that men are responsible for past violence overlooks how few women were permitted to hold power at all. When nearly every ruler is male, nearly every war will have been started by a man, whatever the cause. The women who did govern were not noticeably more peaceful. Margaret Thatcher, for instance, led Britain into the Falklands conflict, and queens in earlier centuries defended and extended their territory much as kings did. Leaders of either sex tend to go to war when they are threatened, when they see an advantage, or when no parliament or court is able to restrain them.\n\nThere is, nevertheless, a sound point behind the claim. Governments drawn from only half the population ignore a great deal of experience, and those with more women in senior positions often give greater attention to matters such as health, childcare and education. Decisions are usually better when the people making them do not all share the same background. This is an argument for balance, though, rather than for replacing one group with another. A cabinet composed solely of women would be as narrow as the all-male ones it succeeded, and it would face the same pressures and temptations.\n\nPeace and good government depend on accountable institutions and on leaders chosen for their judgement. I believe the world would benefit from many more women in power, but not from the assumption that either sex is naturally fitter to rule.",
"b375":"As corporations have grown and family firms have declined, a job in a company with thousands of staff has become the normal experience of work. Such employers offer security and opportunity that small businesses can rarely match, but often at the price of personal recognition and independence.\n\nThe chief attraction of a large organisation is financial security. A multinational bank or manufacturer typically pays a higher salary than a local firm and adds benefits such as health insurance, a pension and paid parental leave. Employment tends to be more stable as well, since a big company can survive a poor year that would close a small one. The other main advantage is room to develop. A large employer has the resources to train its staff properly and enough departments for them to move upwards or sideways without resigning. An accountant who tires of auditing, for example, may transfer to the finance team of another division.\n\nThe drawbacks are mostly a matter of scale. In a business that employs tens of thousands, an individual's contribution is hard to see, and employees may feel that their work makes little difference to the result. A good idea from a junior member of staff can pass through several layers of management before anyone acts on it. Roles are usually narrow as well. A marketing assistant might spend years on one small task, whereas a counterpart in a small firm would deal with customers, budgets and suppliers in the same week. Not all large employers are alike, however, and those that organise staff into small teams avoid some of these problems.\n\nLarge companies give their employees better pay, training and prospects than most small firms can, while offering less variety, influence and personal attention. Which matters more depends on the individual and on the stage of his or her career.",
"b376":"The word \"new\" may be the most frequently used claim in modern marketing, attached to everything from phones to breakfast cereal. Companies stress novelty because it is the easiest way to attract attention, and I believe the habit does more harm than good.\n\nNovelty is emphasised because it gives customers a reason to buy something they already own. In wealthy markets most households have a phone, a television and a full wardrobe, so sales depend on persuading people that what they have is out of date. A claim of newness suggests improvement without the company having to prove any, and it appeals to a natural curiosity about what has changed. It also helps a brand stand out among rivals that are almost identical. A toothpaste described as a new formula, for instance, catches the eye on a crowded shelf although few buyers could say what the formula does.\n\nThe effects of this emphasis are mostly negative. Because the advertising rewards the appearance of change, firms release frequent minor updates, and consumers replace goods that still work well. The result is unnecessary spending for households and a growing volume of discarded electronics and clothing. The constant promise of something better also leaves people dissatisfied. A phone that pleased its owner a year ago begins to seem slow or dull once its successor is advertised. There is a positive side, however, since competition to offer something new does drive real innovation, such as safer cars. The difficulty is that advertising uses the same word for a real advance and for a change of packaging.\n\nBusinesses advertise newness because it is a cheap and effective way of creating demand. While it sometimes signals real progress, in my view it more often encourages waste and restlessness, which makes it a negative development overall.",
"b377":"From the office desk to the living room sofa, few parts of daily life are now untouched by computers and the networks that connect them. I believe that the benefits of this change outweigh its disadvantages to a large extent, although the balance is clearer at work than it is at home.\n\nIn working life, the gains are hard to dispute. Tasks that once took days, such as preparing accounts or sending documents abroad, are finished in minutes. Information that used to be held by a few specialists is now available to any employee who needs it. This efficiency has changed where work can be done as well as how fast. An architect can share drawings with a client in another country, and a small business can sell to customers it will not meet in person. The benefit is unevenly spread, however, since workers whose jobs are automated may struggle to find new ones without retraining.\n\nAt home and in leisure time, the picture is more mixed. Technology has made everyday chores simpler and entertainment far more varied. The difficulty is that the same devices carry work into the evening and compete for attention that used to go to the people in the room. A family dinner at which each person is looking at a screen, for instance, is a familiar scene. Leisure spent online also tends to be sedentary and solitary. An evening of streamed films or video games replaces the walk, the sport or the visit to friends that once filled it. These costs come from habits that households can change rather than from the technology itself.\n\nI think, then, that the advantages of information technology are considerably greater than its drawbacks, most clearly in the workplace. The problems it causes at home are real, but they are largely matters of habit.",
"b378":"In many cultures, bringing up a child is treated as the work of a whole household, with aunts, uncles and grandparents sharing the task. I agree that children generally gain from this wider involvement, as long as the parents remain the ones who make the main decisions.\n\nA child surrounded by several caring adults has more sources of attention and support than two parents can supply alone. An uncle who enjoys fishing or an aunt who speaks another language introduces interests the parents may not have. A teenager who cannot discuss a problem at home may also find it easier to confide in a relative. Parents gain as well, and their children gain through them. Relatives who collect a child from school or look after her during an illness relieve parents of a strain that would otherwise affect the whole home. Rested parents tend to be more patient ones. This benefit is clearest in countries such as Vietnam or Italy, where several generations often live close together.\n\nThe arrangement works less well when the adults disagree about how a child should be raised. If a grandmother allows sweets and late bedtimes that the parents forbid, the child receives mixed messages and learns to play one adult against another. Relatives may also hold outdated views about discipline or about what girls and boys ought to do, and parents can find it awkward to object to someone who is helping them. Involvement should therefore mean support rather than shared authority. Parents know their child best and bear the responsibility, so their rules ought to prevail.\n\nA wide circle of relatives gives children affection, variety and security that a small household struggles to match. This is why I agree with the statement in most cases, although its truth depends on relatives respecting the parents' lead.",
"b380":"Few people expect a chief executive to earn the same as a new recruit, but there is far less agreement about how wide the difference ought to be. I agree that senior leaders in large companies deserve higher pay than other employees. My reservation is about the way that pay is decided, not about the principle.\n\nHigher pay at the top is justified chiefly by accountability. An executive answers for the whole company: when a product fails or a factory has to close, it is the chief executive who must explain the decision to shareholders, staff and the press. The role demands long hours and constant availability, and it can end abruptly if results disappoint. A pay scale that rises with responsibility also motivates others. Ambitious managers work for promotion partly because the next level is better rewarded, and a firm that paid all grades alike would struggle to persuade its best people to accept the heaviest burdens.\n\nAgreement in principle does not mean that any amount is acceptable. In some corporations executives have received large bonuses in years when the company lost money or dismissed staff, which rewards position rather than achievement. Pay of that kind damages morale, because employees can see that the sacrifices are not shared. A better arrangement is to give much of an executive's reward in shares that cannot be sold for several years, so that it depends on the company's lasting health. Some Japanese firms, for instance, have traditionally kept the gap between the boardroom and the shop floor far narrower than American ones without obvious harm to their performance.\n\nLeading a large company is demanding, risky work, and it merits a higher salary. I believe, however, that the difference should be earned through long-term results, and that it should not grow while ordinary wages stand still.",
"b381":"Large cities attract people with the promise of work and variety, but the daily experience of living in one is often harder than newcomers expect. The most serious difficulties concern housing and travel, and I believe governments should respond by making smaller towns a realistic alternative.\n\nThe first problem is the cost of somewhere to live. Because land in a big city is limited and demand keeps rising, rents and prices climb faster than wages, and many residents hand over a large share of what they earn for a small flat. Those who cannot afford the centre move to distant suburbs, which creates the second problem. A nurse or shop assistant may spend a large part of each day on crowded trains or in slow traffic, time that is taken from sleep, family and leisure. High salaries compensate some professionals for these pressures, although people on ordinary incomes tend to feel them in full.\n\nEncouraging people to settle in smaller towns would ease both problems at once. Housing there is cheaper, distances are short, and every household that leaves reduces competition for space in the city. People will move only if work is available, however, so encouragement must mean more than advertising a quieter life. Governments can relocate public offices, offer lower business taxes to firms that set up in towns, and improve rail and broadband connections so that city employers can be served from a distance. A software developer who works online, for instance, could then live wherever the cost of living suits her. Without such investment, a move would simply exchange high rents for unemployment.\n\nGiven the pressure on housing and transport in major cities, I believe an active policy of drawing people towards smaller towns is justified. Such a policy will succeed only if jobs and good connections come first.",
"b382":"Computer games were once regarded as a pastime for teenagers, yet a large share of players today are adults with jobs and families. This popularity is easy to explain, and I believe it is a broadly positive development as long as gaming does not replace other parts of a full life.\n\nAdults turn to games mainly because they offer a kind of rest that other entertainment does not. After a day of work in which effort is rarely rewarded immediately, a game provides clear goals, quick feedback and a sense of progress. It also demands attention, which makes it easier to stop thinking about the office than it is while watching television. Convenience matters as well. A game can be played for half an hour at home, at no extra cost, at whatever time the children are finally asleep. Few other hobbies fit so easily around a job and a family, since sport or an evening class requires a fixed timetable.\n\nJudged as a hobby, gaming compares well with the alternatives. It is more active than passive viewing, since players solve problems, plan ahead and often cooperate with others. Strategy games, for example, require the kind of sustained concentration that few other evening activities ask of a tired adult. The risks appear when play becomes excessive. Someone who regularly plays late into the night will sleep badly, exercise little and have less time for a partner or children. Although a small minority of players do lose control in this way, the same could be said of many pastimes, and it is not a reason to condemn the activity as a whole.\n\nAdults play because games are an absorbing, affordable and convenient way to relax. Provided that it remains one interest among several, I think this is a harmless and often beneficial use of free time.",
"b384":"Almost all governments accept some duty to conserve wildlife, but there is disagreement over how far that duty extends. One side holds that every wild species deserves protection, and the other that efforts should concentrate on a selected few. In my view, all should be protected in principle, though not with equal effort.\n\nThose who favour protecting every wild animal argue that nature works as a connected whole. Predators, prey, insects and scavengers each perform a function, and because these relationships are only partly understood, it is risky to decide that a particular creature can be spared. Vultures in India, for instance, were given little attention until their numbers collapsed and rotting carcasses began to spread disease. Supporters of this view also make a moral point, namely that an animal's right to exist should not depend on its appeal to humans. On this argument, a beetle has the same claim as a panda. The difficulty is that no country has the money or staff to defend every species within its borders.\n\nThis is the starting point for the opposing view. Since resources are scarce, its advocates would direct them to animals that are closest to extinction, or to those such as elephants and tigers whose survival attracts public support and funding. Some would add that certain species need no help, or even require control. Rats and wild boar, which thrive near human settlement, can damage crops and other wildlife. These are reasonable observations, although they justify setting priorities rather than withdrawing protection altogether. An animal that is common today may become rare quickly once it is ignored.\n\nThe two positions are closer than they first appear. I believe the law should protect all wild animals from needless harm, while active conservation is focused on the most threatened species and on the habitats that many others share.",
"b386":"Burgers, fried chicken and pizza are now sold on almost every high street, and the industry behind them is frequently blamed for a range of social ills. I agree that it has harmed both the environment and the way people eat, but I think its effect on family life has been overstated.\n\nOn the environment and diet, the criticism is difficult to dispute. Fast food depends heavily on beef, and cattle farming requires vast areas of land, which in parts of South America has been obtained by clearing rainforest. Each meal is also served in disposable packaging, such as cups, boxes and bags, much of which becomes litter within minutes. As for eating habits, the food is designed to be cheap, filling and high in fat, salt and sugar. Regular customers, and children in particular, come to prefer these flavours and to expect large portions, which helps to explain rising obesity in many countries. The industry is not solely responsible, since people choose what they eat, although heavy advertising aimed at the young makes that choice less free than it appears.\n\nThe charge that fast food has weakened families is less convincing. Critics argue that shared home-cooked meals have been replaced by food eaten separately and in a hurry, so that parents and children talk less. This has happened in some households, but the cause lies mainly in long working hours and crowded schedules rather than in the restaurants themselves. A takeaway may even give tired parents an evening together at the table that they would otherwise have spent cooking and clearing up.\n\nTwo of the three accusations are therefore well founded. In my view, the industry deserves criticism for its environmental and nutritional costs, while the decline of family meals has deeper causes.",
"b387":"Few professional teams or athletes today compete without a company's name on their kit. Supporters of this arrangement see it as what keeps sport alive, whereas critics believe it corrupts it. In my view sponsorship does more good than harm, though its benefits are spread very unevenly.\n\nThose in favour argue that sponsorship gives athletes the means to compete. Training full time requires coaching, equipment, travel and medical care, and in most disciplines prize money covers little of this. A swimmer or a cyclist with a sponsor can give up a second job and concentrate on performance, which raises standards for the whole sport. Clubs benefit in the same way. A local football team whose shirts carry the name of a nearby business can afford pitches and youth coaches that membership fees would not pay for. The case is strongest at this modest level, where the sums are small and the sponsor asks for little in return.\n\nOpponents reply that money follows popularity instead of merit. Companies want large audiences, so funds pour into a handful of sports, such as men's football and motor racing, while women's teams and minority disciplines struggle for attention. Wealthy clubs attract the richest sponsors, buy the best players and win more often, which makes competitions predictable. There is also the question of who is paying. Shirts that advertise gambling firms place betting in front of young fans every week. These criticisms seem to me justified, but they describe sponsorship that is badly distributed and poorly regulated rather than sponsorship that is wrong in principle.\n\nSport would be poorer and smaller without commercial backing, and that is why I believe sponsorship deserves support. Its harmful effects are real, but they can be reduced by sharing income more fairly and excluding unsuitable advertisers.",
"b388":"From village football teams to the Olympic Games, sport now relies heavily on money from businesses that want their names seen. Supporters regard this income as indispensable, while critics worry about the influence it buys. I believe sponsorship benefits sport on the whole, provided that governing bodies decide which sponsors are acceptable.\n\nThose in favour argue that sponsorship pays for things that ticket sales and public funds cannot. A local firm that buys the shirts for a youth team, for instance, allows children to play who might otherwise be unable to afford the kit. At the highest level, sponsors enable athletes to train full time, and this has raised standards in sports with small audiences, such as rowing or gymnastics, which attract little television revenue. Major events would also be far more expensive to attend without commercial backing. The benefit is uneven, though, since companies naturally prefer popular sports and famous competitors.\n\nThe disadvantages arise from the fact that a sponsor expects something in return. Match times may be arranged to suit advertisers rather than players or spectators, and clubs can become dependent on a single company whose withdrawal leaves them in difficulty. The choice of sponsor is a further concern. When betting firms or makers of sugary drinks appear on the shirts of players admired by children, sport lends its healthy image to products that are far from healthy. These are serious objections. They are, however, arguments for regulating sponsorship and not for rejecting it, because the money itself does a great deal of good.\n\nCommercial sponsors have made sport better funded and more widely played, at the price of some independence. Because that price can be limited by clear rules on who may sponsor and on what terms, I think the practice does more good than harm.",
"b389":"Every generation inherits habits and ceremonies that were shaped by conditions very different from its own. Some argue that most of these have outlived their usefulness and should be allowed to disappear. I disagree for the most part, since the majority still serve a purpose even when the original reason for them has gone.\n\nA custom does not need a practical function in order to be relevant. Families in Vietnam, for instance, still travel long distances to gather at the Lunar New Year, a festival that grew out of the farming calendar and now matters just as much to office workers. What it provides is a fixed occasion on which relatives meet, the old are honoured and children learn where they belong. Modern life, with its long working hours and scattered families, makes such occasions more valuable rather than less. Everyday courtesies, such as greeting elders first or sharing food with guests, do similar work at very little cost.\n\nSome traditions, on the other hand, do deserve to be abandoned. Practices that restrict people's choices, such as arranging marriages for teenage girls or expecting daughters to leave school early, conflict with values that most societies now hold. Others have simply become a burden, as when families fall into debt to pay for a lavish wedding because relatives expect it. Although these examples support the statement, they are a small part of any culture. They also show that the sensible response is to reform a custom, not to discard the whole inheritance. Weddings can be made simpler without ceasing to be celebrated.\n\nCustoms that cause harm should be changed or dropped, but they are the exception. For the rest, I believe their worth lies in the ties they create between people and with the past, a need that modern life has not removed.",
"b390":"Timetables and budgets in many schools and universities now lean heavily towards science and technology, with drama and literature treated as optional extras. This shift has understandable causes, but in my view it is a negative development when it goes as far as neglecting the arts altogether.\n\nThe reasons are largely economic. Governments regard engineers, doctors and software developers as the source of future growth, and they fund institutions accordingly. Parents think along similar lines, since a degree in computing appears to lead to secure employment in a way that a degree in theatre does not. Science is also easier to measure. A school can show its success through examination results in mathematics, while the confidence a pupil gains from performing in a play appears in no league table. Head teachers under pressure direct money and hours to the subjects that are counted.\n\nThis preference becomes harmful once the arts are pushed aside. Literature teaches young people to understand lives unlike their own and to express a complicated idea clearly, and drama teaches them to speak in front of others and to cooperate. Employers in most fields, including scientific ones, ask for these abilities. A talented engineer who cannot explain a proposal to colleagues, for instance, is less useful than one who can. The other loss falls on students whose strengths lie in language or performance. With no subject in which they can succeed, they may leave school believing they have little talent. Science does not deserve less support, although its growth should not come at the expense of everything else.\n\nInstitutions favour science because it promises jobs and produces results that are easy to count. I believe the emphasis is reasonable but the neglect is not, since an education without the arts leaves students less articulate and less able to understand other people.",
"b392":"Politicians are usually judged by growth figures, so it is unsurprising that many treat rising national income as their main task. Others maintain that advances in areas such as health, justice and the environment deserve the same attention. I agree with this second view, since wealth is a means and these other gains are among the ends it should serve.\n\nGovernments that put the economy first have a reasonable case. Almost everything a state wishes to do, from building hospitals to paying pensions, requires revenue, and revenue grows only when businesses and employment do. A country that neglects its economy soon finds that it cannot fund its other ambitions. The weakness of this position is that it treats national income as proof of national wellbeing, when the two can diverge sharply. Output may rise while rivers become polluted and most of the new wealth goes to a small group.\n\nThose who value other kinds of progress argue that a country should be judged by how its people live. Clean air, honest courts, good schools and equal treatment of women and minorities all improve daily life directly. Bhutan, for instance, is known for assessing its policies against the happiness of its citizens instead of production alone. Social progress often strengthens the economy as well, because educated, healthy people who trust their institutions work and invest more effectively. Firms invest more readily where contracts are enforced and workers are literate. Such aims are harder to pursue, however, in the poorest states, where raising incomes may have to come first for a time.\n\nEconomic growth provides the resources a nation needs, but it is not a complete measure of success. I believe, therefore, that progress in health, education, fairness and the environment should be pursued with equal seriousness, except perhaps where extreme poverty leaves no choice.",
"b393":"Skills that were sufficient at the start of a career can be out of date within a few years, particularly in fields shaped by new technology. Whether the employer or the employee should bear the cost of keeping up is disputed. I believe companies should fund training that their work requires, while individuals should pay for learning that mainly serves their own plans.\n\nThe view that businesses should pay rests on who benefits first. When a hospital introduces new equipment or a software firm adopts a new programming language, the organisation cannot function unless its staff learn to use it, and the gain appears directly in its results. Paying for courses also helps a firm to keep its people. Staff who are given the chance to develop tend to stay longer, which saves the expense of recruiting and settling in replacements. Employers' reluctance is understandable in one respect, though. An employee trained at company expense may leave for a competitor, taking the investment elsewhere.\n\nPeople who place the responsibility on the individual point out that skills belong to the person who holds them. A qualification raises its owner's salary and employability for life, whoever the employer is, so it seems reasonable that the owner should contribute. An accountant who takes a master's degree to move into management, for example, will carry that advantage to every future post. This argument is convincing for learning of this kind, which goes beyond the current role. It is much less convincing when training is simply a condition of doing one's existing job.\n\nResponsibility for professional development is best divided according to purpose. Employers should finance what their own operations demand, and in my view workers should meet the cost of courses chosen chiefly to advance or change their careers.",
"b394":"Soft drinks, breakfast cereals and many packaged snacks contain far more sugar than most buyers realise, and the effects on weight and teeth are well known. I agree that raising the price of such products is a sensible way to cut consumption, as long as it forms part of a wider policy.\n\nPrice is one of the few tools that change behaviour on a large scale. Shoppers respond to cost even when they ignore health warnings, as the long decline in smoking after repeated tobacco tax rises suggests. A levy on sugar also acts on producers. When Britain introduced a charge on sweetened drinks, for example, many manufacturers reduced the sugar in their recipes to avoid it, so consumers drank less sugar without changing brands. A tax of this kind works best on drinks, however, where cheap unsweetened alternatives are easy to find.\n\nOpponents raise two objections, neither of which is decisive. The first is that such a tax weighs most heavily on poorer households, who spend a larger part of their income on food. This is true, yet the same households suffer most from diabetes and tooth decay, and they stand to gain most if consumption falls. The second objection is that adults should be free to eat as they please. A higher price does not remove that freedom. It merely asks buyers to bear some of the medical costs that their choices impose on others. Although the tax alone will not solve the problem, it strengthens the effect of labelling and education.\n\nMaking sugary products dearer is neither a complete answer nor an unfair one. Combined with clearer labels and limits on marketing to children, it would reduce the amount of sugar people consume, which is why I agree with the proposal.",
"b395":"The decision to start a family is being postponed in many countries, with first-time parents in their late thirties no longer unusual. This change reflects new priorities among both sexes, and I believe its effects on families and on society are mixed.\n\nSeveral pressures combine to produce the delay, but the common thread is that adulthood itself now begins later. Young people spend their twenties gaining qualifications, changing jobs, and many do not settle with a long-term partner until their thirties. Men and women alike now want to travel, save and feel established before taking on a responsibility that lasts two decades. The cost of raising a child strengthens this caution, since rent, childcare and school expenses can be hard to meet on an early-career salary.\n\nWithin the family, the main effect is a more secure start for the child. Parents in their late thirties have usually finished training and built up savings, so they are less likely to struggle with rent or childcare fees. They also tend to bring more patience to the task than they would have had at twenty-five. Grandparents are older too, however, and can offer less practical help. For society, the main consequence is a falling birth rate. Couples who begin late have less time for a second or third child, so families become smaller across the whole population. Fewer births mean fewer workers to pay the taxes that fund pensions and hospitals, a problem already visible in countries such as Japan and Italy. Although there is a short-term gain, as more women remain in skilled work, it does not offset this long-term imbalance.\n\nLater parenthood, then, follows naturally from longer education, later partnerships and high living costs. In my view it tends to give individual children a secure start, while leaving families smaller and populations older.",
"b396":"How much freedom teenagers should have to challenge the adults who teach them is a question on which schools differ widely. Some fear that criticism will weaken a teacher's authority, but I believe that pupils who are taught to question respectfully learn more, and that discipline need not suffer as a result.\n\nSupporters of questioning argue that it improves both learning and teaching. A student who asks why a historical account should be trusted, or who points out a step in a proof that does not follow, is thinking rather than copying. Teachers benefit as well, because honest comments reveal which explanations failed and which activities wasted time. Some schools, for instance, ask classes to complete a short anonymous form at the end of term, and many teachers adjust their lessons in response. This kind of feedback works, however, only when it is directed at the lesson and not at the person.\n\nThose on the other side worry about what happens when that distinction is lost. Adolescents are still learning to control their tempers, and an invitation to criticise may be heard by some as permission to argue about every instruction or to mock a teacher in front of the class. Order is difficult to restore once it has gone, and the quieter pupils are the ones who lose most. This concern is especially strong in cultures where respect for elders is central to education. It is a reasonable fear, although it describes criticism that has been badly managed rather than criticism as such.\n\nRespect that depends on silence is fragile, whereas respect earned by a teacher who can answer hard questions is durable. In my view, schools should therefore encourage pupils to challenge ideas and to comment on lessons, within clear rules about tone and timing.",
"b397":"For most listeners, music is something to enjoy on the way to work or at the end of a long day. Some people conclude that it is simply a pastime, while others see it as a force that shapes how societies think and feel. In my view, the second group is closer to the truth.\n\nThose who regard music as entertainment are describing how it is actually used. People choose songs because they like the melody, play them in the background while cooking or driving, and rarely stop to consider what the lyrics mean. The industry reflects this, since streaming services are designed to supply a pleasant, continuous sound for whatever mood the listener is in. Seen from this angle, a pop song is no more significant than a television comedy. This account is accurate, although it describes the individual listener and says little about what happens when many people share the same music.\n\nIt is in that shared experience that the wider influence lies. A national anthem sung in a stadium, or a hymn at a funeral, binds strangers together in a way that speech seldom does. People who have little else in common feel, for a few minutes, that they belong to the same group. Music has also carried political messages to audiences that would not have read a pamphlet. Protest songs, for example, became part of the American civil rights movement because they could be sung by a crowd and remembered afterwards. Not every song has this kind of weight, and most are forgotten within a year, but the ones that last help to define a generation.\n\nMusic is certainly entertaining, and that is reason enough to value it. I think, however, that its capacity to unite people and to express what they believe gives it an importance well beyond amusement.",
"b398":"Complaints about having too little time for family, friends and rest are heard in almost every profession. The difficulty has less to do with poor personal organisation than with the way modern work is arranged, and the remedies must therefore involve employers as well as individuals.\n\nThe principal reason is that work no longer has clear boundaries. A generation ago, most employees left their tasks behind when they left the building. Laptops and smartphones now allow messages to arrive at any hour, and many workers feel obliged to reply in case they seem less committed than their colleagues. Rising living costs are a second reason. Where rents and food prices grow faster than wages, some people take on overtime or a second job simply to pay the bills. A delivery driver who also works weekend shifts in a restaurant, for instance, has very little free time left to protect. The result is that evenings and weekends, though officially free, are seldom entirely so.\n\nBecause the cause lies largely in workplace culture, this is where change should begin. Companies can set rules about contact outside working hours, as France has done through a legal right to disconnect, and can judge staff by what they achieve rather than by how long they stay. Flexible schedules and occasional remote working give back the hours otherwise lost to travel. Individuals have a part to play too. Small habits, such as switching off notifications in the evening and protecting fixed times for exercise or family meals, restore a boundary that technology has removed. Such personal measures work only when managers respect them, however.\n\nA healthier balance is possible, but it depends on employers and employees accepting that rest is a condition of good work and not a reward for it.",
"b399":"Urban life has removed most of the physical effort that earlier generations took for granted, and many city residents now pass entire days almost without moving. The causes lie in the way city work and city space are organised, which is also where the solutions are to be found.\n\nThe main cause is that modern urban routines leave neither the need nor the time for activity. Most jobs in a city are done sitting at a desk, and the journey to them is made sitting in a car, bus or train. An office worker who leaves home early and returns late in the evening has little energy left for a run. The city itself offers few invitations to move. Parks are distant and busy roads make cycling feel unsafe, while lifts, escalators and delivery services remove the small efforts that once filled the day. Gyms exist, although their fees and fixed locations put them beyond the reach of many residents.\n\nSince lack of time is the central obstacle, the most useful solutions build exercise into things people already do. Safe cycle lanes and pleasant walking routes allow the commute itself to become a workout, as it has for many commuters in the Netherlands. City councils should also bring facilities closer to homes, for instance by installing free outdoor exercise equipment in neighbourhood parks and opening school sports halls in the evening. A facility that is free and close to home can be used without planning or membership. Public campaigns have a place, but information alone rarely changes habits unless the active choice is also the convenient one.\n\nCity dwellers are inactive mainly because their work, transport and surroundings require so little of their bodies. They will move more when streets, workplaces and parks make exercise a normal part of the day rather than an extra task.",
"b400":"Remote work is usually discussed from the employee's point of view, with its promise of flexible hours and no commute. This has led some to assume that companies gain nothing from it. I disagree, because employers benefit in measurable ways, even though the arrangement creates management problems that they must solve.\n\nFor a business, the most direct gain is financial. A firm whose staff work at home for part of the week needs fewer desks, and can move to smaller premises with lower rent, heating and cleaning bills. For a company in a city centre, where office space is among its largest expenses, the saving can be considerable. Recruitment improves as well. An employer in an expensive capital city is no longer restricted to people who can afford to live nearby, so it can hire a talented programmer from a provincial town or even from abroad. The same flexibility helps it to keep experienced staff who might otherwise leave, for example after having children.\n\nIt would be wrong, though, to pretend that employers bear no costs. Managers find it harder to judge effort when they cannot see their teams, and new recruits learn the company's methods more slowly without colleagues beside them. Shared ideas may also suffer, since chance conversations seldom happen online. These difficulties explain why some organisations have called staff back to the office. They are not, however, evidence that only workers benefit. They show that the gains to the company depend on good organisation, such as clear targets and regular days when the whole team meets in person.\n\nHome working is not a gift from employers to their staff. In my view, both sides profit from it, and companies that manage it carefully gain lower costs and a wider, more stable pool of staff.",
"b401":"Opening a bank account or joining a social network now begins with the same step, which is handing over a name, an address and a telephone number. Whether this is desirable depends on what the details are used for, but in my view it is a positive development where the information is needed to provide a service.\n\nOnline banking shows the practice at its best. A bank must know who its customers are, and it is obliged by law to protect what it holds. Because identity is confirmed in advance, a customer can transfer money, apply for a loan or check a balance at midnight without visiting a branch. The personal details are doing useful work here, since a code sent to a registered phone number is what stops a thief from emptying the account. This arrangement is safe only if customers play their part, for instance by refusing to reveal passwords to callers who claim to be from the bank.\n\nSocial networks are a different matter, because their business depends on knowing as much as possible about their users. A birthday, a home town and a phone number are requested at registration, although a profile could function without most of them. Such details are then used to target advertising, and those displayed publicly can help a stranger to locate or impersonate a user. Teenagers, who tend to share freely, are particularly exposed. The benefit of keeping in touch with friends is real, but it does not require this much disclosure, and users are wise to supply the minimum and to check their privacy settings.\n\nWhere a service cannot work without personal information, as with banking, supplying it online is clearly worthwhile. I think the trend is beneficial overall, provided that people learn to refuse requests for details that serve the company rather than themselves.",
"b402":"Portable devices are frequently blamed for shortened attention, poor sleep and neglected relationships. These complaints have some basis, but I disagree with the claim that phones and tablets do more harm than good, because it overlooks how much people now accomplish with them.\n\nA single device in a pocket has replaced a remarkable range of separate tools. It is a map, a camera, a bank branch, a dictionary and a means of calling for help, and it costs far less than those things did individually. The practical effect is greatest for people who previously had little access to such services. A farmer in a remote area, for example, can check market prices, receive payments and consult a weather forecast on a basic smartphone. For students, a tablet holds more books than a school library could offer. Advantages of this kind are easy to forget because they have become ordinary.\n\nThe disadvantages that critics describe are real, but they concern the manner of use rather than the devices as such. Applications are designed to hold attention, and many people check their screens far more often than they intend to. Sleep suffers when a phone is taken to bed, and conversations suffer when one is placed on the dinner table. Children are especially vulnerable, since they have not yet learned to limit themselves. Although these problems deserve to be taken seriously, each of them has a simple remedy, such as switching off notifications or keeping devices out of bedrooms. A tool that is harmful when overused is not, for that reason, harmful on the whole.\n\nI therefore remain unconvinced that these devices do more harm than good. They have put information, services and safety within reach of ordinary people at very low cost, and I believe this outweighs the damage caused by careless use.",
"b403":"Loud telephone conversations on a bus or in a reading room are a common source of irritation, and some people would like phones to be prohibited in such places. I disagree with a general ban, since the real problem is noise, which can be dealt with by far narrower rules.\n\nA prohibition would remove much that is useful to prevent a little that is annoying. Most of what people do on a phone in public is silent. A passenger buys a ticket, checks when the next train leaves or reads the news, and a shopper compares prices or pays at the till. In a library, students use their phones to search the catalogue and photograph a page. None of this disturbs anyone, and some of it is necessary, as when a parent must be reachable by a child's school. A ban would also be hard to enforce in shops and on crowded trains, where staff have better things to do than police pockets. A rule that is openly ignored also weakens respect for notices that do matter.\n\nWhat does deserve regulation is behaviour that spoils a shared space for others. Speaking loudly, playing videos without headphones and letting a phone ring repeatedly are discourteous anywhere, and in a library they defeat the purpose of the building. Targeted rules work well here. Many railway companies, for instance, set aside quiet carriages, and most libraries already ask visitors to silence their devices and take calls outside. Such measures depend on people's cooperation, however, so clear signs and an occasional reminder from staff are needed to make them effective.\n\nPhones themselves do no harm in public places, while thoughtless use of them does. For that reason I believe that quiet zones and simple rules of courtesy are a better answer than prohibition, which would punish the considerate majority.",
"b404":"Few institutions divide animal lovers as sharply as the zoo. To its critics it is a place of confinement, while its defenders see it as a refuge for species that might otherwise vanish. I believe both descriptions can be accurate, and that the answer depends on which animals are kept and how.\n\nThe accusation of cruelty has substance. Many zoo animals are adapted to ranges far larger than any enclosure can offer. An elephant that would walk great distances each day in the wild, or a polar bear accustomed to open ice, has little to do in a paddock. Such animals often develop repetitive pacing and other signs of distress. Conditions vary widely, however. A modern wildlife park with large enclosures bears little resemblance to a roadside row of cages, and smaller species such as reptiles, frogs and many birds appear to cope well.\n\nThe conservation argument is equally serious. Breeding programmes in zoos have saved animals that had almost disappeared from the wild, such as the Arabian oryx, which was later returned to its native desert. Zoos also pay for work beyond their gates. Part of the money from ticket sales goes to research and to field projects that protect animals where they still live wild. These benefits should not be exaggerated. Only a minority of zoo animals belong to endangered species, and few of those bred in captivity are ever released. Protecting habitats remains the more effective way of saving wildlife, although it is of no help to a species whose habitat has already gone.\n\nZoos are neither simply prisons nor simply sanctuaries. On the whole, I think they are justified when they keep animals that can live well in captivity and contribute to conservation, rather than displaying large mammals for entertainment.",
"b405":"A message sent to a colleague today is likely to read far more like speech than a business letter of two generations ago. This relaxed style has spread mainly because of the tools people now write with, and I believe it brings more advantages than disadvantages.\n\nThe chief cause is that writing has taken over much of the work once done by conversation. People send a text where they would once have telephoned, and reply to emails within minutes, so their sentences are short, direct and close to the way they talk. Messaging apps encourage this by rewarding speed over polish. Social attitudes have shifted as well. Many workplaces are less hierarchical than they were, and employees who address a manager by her first name see little reason to write to her in ceremonial language. Although informality now dominates everyday writing, formal conventions survive in fields where precision is essential, such as law and academic publishing.\n\nThis change makes written communication more efficient. Plain, direct wording is quicker to produce and to read, so a question that once needed a formal letter and a week's wait is now settled in a few lines. The other main benefit is that informal writing is more inclusive. People with less education can deal confidently with banks, councils and employers when letters are written in everyday language instead of official phrases. The chief disadvantage is that young people who write only casually may struggle when a formal register is required, as in a job application. This risk is real, although schools can reduce it by teaching both styles and when each is appropriate.\n\nInformal writing has grown because technology has made writing resemble talk. Its gains in speed and clarity outweigh its costs, in my view, provided that people keep the ability to write formally when the occasion demands it.",
"b406":"Stories of entrepreneurs who abandoned their studies and went on to build famous companies are often told as proof that formal education is unnecessary. The truth is more modest: some people do succeed after leaving early, for reasons that have little to do with dropping out itself, and those reasons reveal what achievement really demands.\n\nPeople who leave education and prosper have usually left for something, not merely from something. Bill Gates, for example, gave up his place at Harvard because he had already started a software business and judged that the opportunity would not wait. Such individuals tend to possess an unusual skill, a clear plan and the confidence to act on it, and they gain several years of practical experience while their peers are still in lectures. It should be remembered, however, that these cases are visible precisely because they are rare. The many who leave school without a plan and struggle to find stable work do not appear in magazine profiles.\n\nWhat success requires, then, is not the absence of a certificate but qualities that a classroom does not automatically provide. The most important is persistence, since almost any business or career involves early failures that have to be absorbed. Closely related is a willingness to keep learning independently, whether from customers, colleagues or mistakes, long after formal teaching has ended. A person with both can recover from a poor start and adapt when circumstances change. Although neither quality depends on a degree, education often strengthens them, and for professions such as medicine or engineering it remains the only route in.\n\nSuccessful dropouts are best understood as determined people who found a faster path, not as evidence that study holds others back. Drive and continued learning matter most, and they can be developed inside a university as easily as outside it.",
"b407":"Being told to sit still and do nothing sounds like odd advice for people who already feel they have too little time. I agree that a daily period of complete rest is a valuable way of easing stress. I do not believe, however, that it is the best method for every person or every kind of pressure.\n\nThe recommendation makes sense because much modern stress comes from uninterrupted stimulation. From the moment they wake, many people move between tasks, messages and screens without a pause, and even their breaks are filled with news or social media. A short period with no goal at all allows the body to slow down and the mind to stop reacting. Someone who sits quietly on a bench for a while after lunch, for example, with the phone left in a bag, often returns to work calmer and clearer. The practice costs nothing and requires no skill. Its value depends, though, on the person being able to let thoughts pass.\n\nFor some people, idleness has the opposite effect. Those whose tension comes from a particular worry, such as debt or a conflict at work, may find that an empty half hour simply gives the worry more room. They are usually better served by activity that absorbs their attention. A brisk walk, a swim or a conversation with a trusted friend releases physical tension and interrupts anxious thinking in a way that sitting still may not. Rest also leaves the source of the stress untouched. While a pause can restore energy, an employee with an impossible workload needs the workload reduced.\n\nDoing nothing for a while each day is a simple and underused remedy, and I agree that most people would benefit from it. Whether it is the best one depends on the individual and on the cause of the stress.",
"b408":"The idea that future criminals can be identified in the nursery is an old one, and modern genetics has given it new life. In my view, inherited traits play only a minor part in criminal behaviour, and because upbringing matters far more, most children at risk can be steered away from crime.\n\nGenes may influence temperament, but temperament is not destiny. Some children are born more impulsive, more aggressive or less fearful than others, and these qualities can make rule-breaking more likely. They do not, however, lead in one direction only. A fearless, restless boy raised by attentive parents may become a firefighter, while the same boy, neglected and surrounded by violence, may end up in court. Levels of offending also rise and fall within a single generation, which is far too quickly for heredity to explain. What a child inherits, then, is at most a tendency whose outcome depends on the surroundings in which it develops.\n\nSince surroundings are decisive, prevention is possible in a great many cases. Early signs such as persistent aggression or truancy can prompt help for the family, for example through home visits by nurses, parenting classes or extra support at school. Children who learn to control anger and who have at least one reliable adult in their lives are much less likely to offend later. Predictions about individual children are often wrong, though, and a pupil who is labelled a future criminal may be treated with suspicion and come to fit the description. Help should therefore be offered to struggling families generally, without singling out particular children as dangerous.\n\nBiology supplies, at most, a disposition, and the conditions of childhood decide what becomes of it. This is why I think most young people can be kept from crime, provided that support reaches their families early and without stigma.",
"b409":"Teachers in many countries report that holding a class's attention has become harder than it was a generation ago. The difficulty stems mainly from the phones students carry and the sleep they lose, and both can be addressed if schools and families act together.\n\nThe most powerful cause is the smartphone. Applications are designed to interrupt, and a teenager who receives messages throughout the day learns to expect a new stimulus every few minutes. A forty-minute explanation of photosynthesis cannot compete on those terms, so the mind drifts even when the device is switched off. The same habit damages sleep. Many adolescents continue scrolling late into the night and then arrive at an early lesson too tired to follow it, which no amount of willpower can fully correct. Although boredom in class is hardly new, the combination of constant distraction and tiredness is.\n\nBecause the causes are habits, the remedies must change habits as well. Schools can require phones to be handed in or locked away during the day, as some countries, such as France, already do, so that pupils are not relying on self-control alone. Without interruptions, pupils are likely to find a full lesson easier to follow. Sleep has to be protected at home. Parents can keep devices out of bedrooms at night and set a regular time for lights out, so that a teenager arrives at school rested enough to listen. These steps will not work unless they are applied consistently, since a rule enforced in one classroom and ignored in the next teaches students that it is negotiable.\n\nPoor concentration among today's students is less a failing of character than a predictable response to devices built to capture attention and to nights cut short. Firm limits on phones at school and at night would restore much of the focus that has been lost.",
"b410":"Unpaid placements have become a compulsory part of many degree courses, from journalism to engineering. Both parties gain from the arrangement, but I believe students benefit more, as long as the placement involves real training and is not simply free labour.\n\nFor students, the value lies in what a lecture hall cannot offer. A few months inside a company show how the theory they have learned is applied under deadlines and budgets, and how colleagues behave when something goes wrong. This kind of knowledge is hard to teach in a classroom, and it gives the remaining terms of study a clearer purpose. They also leave with something to put on a CV and with people who can recommend them. A trainee accountant who has already helped to prepare a client's annual accounts, for instance, is a far stronger candidate than a classmate with identical grades and no experience.\n\nCompanies gain as well, though less than is sometimes assumed. They receive extra hands at no cost and a chance to observe potential recruits over several weeks, which is more reliable than an interview. Against this must be set the time experienced staff spend supervising someone who knows little and will soon leave. The balance changes when a firm gives interns routine tasks that need no supervision, such as photocopying or data entry. In that case the business gets free work and the student learns almost nothing. Unpaid placements can also exclude those who cannot afford to work without a wage, unless the university helps with travel and living costs.\n\nWhere a placement is properly designed, the company gets modest help and an early look at future staff, while the student acquires experience that shapes an entire career. In my view, that makes students the greater beneficiaries, and universities should check placements carefully to keep it so.",
"b411":"University is for most people the last period of life in which they are free to study whatever interests them. Some students use this freedom to explore beyond their degree, while others prefer to concentrate on it entirely, and I believe a limited amount of wider study is worth the time it takes.\n\nThose who look outside their main subject argue that knowledge does not divide as neatly as timetables do. A medical student who takes a course in ethics, or an engineer who learns a foreign language, gains something that the core programme does not supply and that later work will demand. Such choices also protect against an uncertain job market, because few graduates remain in one narrow field for a whole career. A chemist who has also studied some programming or accounting, for example, has more than one direction in which to move. The risk, however, is that curiosity turns into distraction if a student collects courses without finishing any of them well.\n\nThe opposing view is that a degree is demanding enough to deserve full attention. Subjects such as law or medicine involve a great deal of material, and the class of degree awarded can decide which employers will consider an application. A student who spends evenings on philosophy seminars may reach the final examinations less prepared than classmates who did not, and a disappointing result is hard to correct afterwards. This reasoning is strongest in the final year, although it is less convincing earlier in a course, when the workload is lighter.\n\nDepth and breadth compete for the same hours, and the degree must come first. Even so, in my view one or two carefully chosen outside courses make a graduate more capable and more adaptable, and students should take them while they can.",
"b412":"Taking a year away from study before university was once unusual, but it is now common in countries such as Britain and Australia. I agree that this break generally makes people better students afterwards, although the benefit depends on the year having some purpose.\n\nTime spent working or travelling tends to produce a maturity that school cannot. A young person who has held a job in a restaurant or a warehouse has had to arrive on time, deal with difficult customers and live on what he or she earns. Someone who has travelled independently has solved problems without a parent or teacher nearby. When these people begin their degree, they are generally more organised and less likely to be overwhelmed by living away from home. They also know why they are there. An eighteen-year-old who has spent months in low-paid work, for example, often returns with a sharper sense of what a qualification is for.\n\nThe year can clarify the choice of course as well. Many pupils select a subject at seventeen with little idea of what the related career involves. A period in a hospital, an office or a school may confirm the decision or prevent an expensive mistake. These gains are not guaranteed, however. A year spent largely at home without plans teaches little. Some students, despite good intentions, find it hard to return to essays and examinations after a long absence, particularly in subjects like mathematics where skills fade quickly. A few lose the wish to study and do not come back, which may or may not be the right decision for them.\n\nA gap year is not valuable in itself. I believe it does benefit later study when it is planned, because students return older in outlook, surer of their direction and more willing to work.",
"b413":"Most teenagers can name five singers at once, whereas five figures from their nation's past may take rather longer. I believe this gap says more about how the two kinds of fame reach young people than about any lack of curiosity, and it can be narrowed.\n\nThe main reason is exposure. International celebrities appear on a young person's phone many times a day, in videos, advertisements and friends' posts, and the entertainment industry spends heavily to keep them there. Historical figures have no such promotion. They are met mainly in textbooks, where a general or a reformer is often reduced to a list of dates to be memorised before an examination. Stars also seem relevant in a way that the dead do not, because following them is a shared activity among friends. A student who knows nothing about a popular band may feel left out, while ignorance of a nineteenth-century statesman carries no social cost.\n\nIf the problem is presentation, the solution is to tell these stories better. Schools could teach history through people rather than events, asking pupils to examine the difficult choices a leader faced instead of memorising the outcome. A class that debates whether a king was right to go to war, for example, is likely to remember him long after the examination. The media can help as well, since a well-made film or television drama reaches audiences that a textbook cannot. The musical Hamilton, for instance, made an eighteenth-century American politician a subject of conversation among teenagers. Such projects need care, however, because a drama that distorts the facts for effect teaches the wrong history.\n\nYoung people are not indifferent to the past, only rarely given a reason to care about it. In my view, vivid teaching and good storytelling on screen would supply that reason.",
"b414":"Cities draw newcomers with the hope of higher wages and wider choices, yet many find that the life they arrive at is harsher than the one they imagined. The difficulties are chiefly those of cost and insecurity, and governments can ease them through housing and transport that reach all residents.\n\nThe hardest part of city life for most people is making an income cover its expenses. Rent takes the largest share, and food, transport and childcare all cost more than in the countryside. New arrivals often begin in casual work, such as construction or street selling, where pay is irregular and a week of illness can mean an unpaid bill. Unable to afford proper housing, many settle in crowded districts on the edge of the city, far from their jobs and poorly served by water, drainage and clinics. Well-paid professionals escape most of these pressures, although they too lose hours to traffic and breathe the same polluted air.\n\nGovernments can do most by concentrating on the needs that residents have in common. The first is affordable housing. Building or subsidising low-cost homes close to employment brings rents down, and it spares workers the long journeys that come with living on the edge of the city. Transport is the other priority, because cheap, frequent buses and trains benefit cleaners and executives alike. Bogotá, for example, built a network of rapid bus lanes that connected outlying neighbourhoods to the centre at a low fare. Such investment is expensive, and it works only if poorer areas are served first rather than last.\n\nUrban life is difficult mainly because it is costly and, for many, insecure. It improves for the whole population when public money is spent on the homes and transport that every resident relies on.",
"b415":"Rising incomes across Asia, Africa and Latin America are allowing millions of families to buy a car they could only dream of a generation ago. This is a welcome sign of prosperity, but it is creating problems that these countries are poorly equipped to handle, and I believe the answer lies in acting before car dependence becomes fixed.\n\nThe central problem is that vehicle numbers are growing much faster than the systems needed to manage them. Streets laid out for pedestrians, bicycles and motorbikes are suddenly filled with cars, so journeys across cities such as Hanoi or Nairobi slow to walking pace at busy hours. Buses are caught in the same queues, which makes public transport less attractive. Air quality is the second problem. A large share of the cars are older second-hand models with poor emission controls, and slow traffic keeps their engines running for longer. Wealthier countries faced these difficulties too, although they had decades in which to adapt.\n\nThe most effective solutions take advantage of the fact that habits are still forming. If governments invest early in metro lines and fast bus networks, the new middle class may come to see public transport as normal rather than as something to escape. Separate bus lanes are especially useful, because they keep services moving when roads are full. Car ownership can also be managed through taxes and registration fees, an approach Singapore has used for decades. Higher charges on older, more polluting vehicles would also clean the air. These policies will be unpopular with new car owners, so they need to be accompanied by visible improvements in the alternatives.\n\nIn my view, developing countries cannot and should not prevent people from buying cars. They can, however, avoid the worst consequences by building good public transport now and by using taxes to manage car numbers.",
"b416":"A growing number of people cannot find an address, remember a telephone number or pass a spare ten minutes without reaching for a phone. Relying on useful tools is natural, but I believe that dependence on this scale is a negative development, because it weakens abilities that people still need.\n\nDependence first costs people skills that they used to practise daily. Someone who follows spoken directions on every journey does not form a picture of the city in his mind, and a person who looks up every fact has little reason to retain any. This matters because understanding is built on what is already remembered. A student who has to search for each date and definition, for instance, will struggle to see how the events of a period connect. Although it could be argued that every technology replaces a skill, as calculators replaced mental arithmetic, phones are replacing many abilities at once, including the ability to tolerate boredom.\n\nHeavy reliance also leaves people exposed when the technology fails or is absent. A flat battery or a lost signal can leave a traveller unable to pay, navigate or contact anyone, since every one of those functions lives in the same device. There is an emotional side to this as well. Many users feel uneasy when separated from their phones, check them without any purpose, and find it hard to concentrate on a book or a conversation. Although such habits fall short of addiction in most cases, they reduce the quality of attention that work and relationships require.\n\nThe internet and the phone are excellent servants, and the trouble begins only when people can no longer manage without them. Because that point has been reached by many, I think the trend is harmful and worth resisting through deliberate periods offline.",
"b417":"Hospitals and technology firms in wealthy states depend heavily on professionals who were trained elsewhere, often in countries with far fewer resources. Whether this amounts to theft or to ordinary migration is disputed. I believe that individuals have every right to move, but that rich governments act unfairly when they actively recruit from places that cannot spare their graduates.\n\nThe accusation of stealing rests on who pays and who benefits. A poor country may spend years of public money educating a doctor, only to see her leave for a foreign hospital shortly after qualifying. The receiving nation obtains a trained professional without having paid for the training, while the patients she would have treated at home go without care. Some African states, for instance, have lost a large share of their nurses to Britain and other richer health systems. The loss is hardest to bear in medicine, where shortages cost lives, and less serious in fields such as computing.\n\nThose who call this a natural movement stress that workers are not the property of their governments. Engineers and scientists have long gone where their skills are best rewarded, and it would be unjust to forbid a graduate from seeking a better salary and safer conditions for her family. Migration can also help the country left behind. Many professionals send money home, and some return later with experience and contacts that would have been impossible to gain locally. Although these benefits are real, they depend on the migrant staying connected to home, and they do little to fill an empty rural clinic in the meantime.\n\nNeither description is entirely accurate, in my view. People should remain free to work abroad, but wealthy countries ought to train more of their own specialists and to compensate poorer nations whose graduates they employ.",
"b418":"Applications for degrees in the natural sciences have been falling in a number of countries, even as demand for scientific skills grows. I believe the explanation lies mostly in how a scientific career now looks to an eighteen-year-old, and that the long-term effects of this trend are a cause for concern.\n\nStudents are turning away from science largely because the path it offers seems long and uncertain. Becoming a researcher usually means a first degree, then a doctorate, then several years on temporary contracts before any permanent post appears. Meanwhile, newer courses such as marketing, design or international business promise an earlier start in working life and require less mathematics. Tuition fees sharpen the comparison, since families paying for a degree naturally ask how soon it will pay them back. It is not that young people have lost interest in how the world works, but that interest alone rarely settles so costly a decision.\n\nThe consequences reach well beyond the campus. With fewer graduates in chemistry, biology and physics, hospitals find it harder to staff their laboratories and manufacturers struggle to recruit people who can develop new products. A nation in this position gradually becomes a buyer of other countries' technology rather than a producer of its own. Problems that require home-grown expertise also suffer. A country facing rising sea levels or a new crop disease, for instance, needs its own specialists who understand local conditions. Scarcity may eventually push salaries up and draw students back, although that correction takes many years, during which a great deal of progress is lost.\n\nScience is being passed over because it asks for a long commitment and gives little security in return. In my view, the resulting shortage of trained people will weaken healthcare, industry and a country's independence unless such careers are made more attractive.",
"b419":"Spending a few months teaching in a village school or helping to build homes abroad has become a common choice for school-leavers and students from rich nations. Their motives are a mixture of idealism and self-interest, and I believe that, in most cases, the volunteers gain more than the communities they set out to help.\n\nTwo motives stand out. Many young people have a sincere wish to be useful and are aware of how comfortable their own upbringing has been. Having seen poverty only on a screen, they want to give something back in person, and physical work such as building a house offers a result they can see. There is a practical motive too. Universities and employers look favourably on applicants who have shown initiative and worked in difficult conditions, and a gap year spent in Nepal or Kenya, for example, stands out on an application. Such a trip is far easier to arrange than it once was, although the agency fees can be high.\n\nThe communities do receive something. A new classroom or a term of English conversation has value, and visitors bring money into the local economy. The benefit is limited, however, by the volunteers' lack of skill and the shortness of their stay. An eighteen-year-old with no training is rarely an effective teacher or builder, and paid local workers could often do the job better. Children may also become attached to helpers who leave after a few weeks. The young visitors, by contrast, return with greater independence, a wider view of the world and a stronger application, all of which last.\n\nYoung people volunteer abroad out of goodwill and concern for their future prospects. While host communities gain modestly, I think the experience does more for the volunteers themselves, unless projects are long-term and designed around what local people actually need.",
"b420":"Few people dispute that children ought to grow up knowing how to sort and reuse what they throw away. The disagreement is over who should teach them, and I believe that the habit is formed mainly at home, with schools providing the understanding that makes it last.\n\nThose who look to parents argue that recycling is a daily routine rather than a subject. A child learns it by rinsing a bottle after dinner and putting it in the right bin, until doing so requires no thought. Parents are present at exactly these moments, and they also set the example. If the adults in a household throw everything into one bag, a lesson delivered in a classroom is unlikely to survive the journey home. This argument has a limit, though, because it assumes that parents themselves recycle, and many do not know the local rules.\n\nSupporters of schools point to this weakness. A school reaches every child in a community, whatever the habits of the family. A pupil whose parents do not recycle will still meet the practice there. Teachers can also explain what parents often cannot: where waste goes, why some materials can be processed again and what is lost when they are buried. Many primary schools in Japan, for example, have pupils sort their own lunch waste and clean their classrooms, so that the principle is practised as well as taught. While such programmes are valuable, a child spends only part of the day at school, and a routine that stops at the gate rarely becomes a habit.\n\nBoth institutions are needed, since the school can supply knowledge to every pupil and the family can turn it into everyday behaviour. Because recycling is in the end something done in kitchens rather than classrooms, I think the larger share of the responsibility rests with parents.",
"b421":"Global brands now build factories, open offices and sell their products in economies that were once closed to them. Their arrival brings clear economic gains to developing countries, but it also carries costs that are easy to underestimate.\n\nThe main advantage is the investment and employment that these firms provide. A foreign car manufacturer or electronics company brings capital that local businesses cannot raise, and its plants may employ thousands of people at wages above the local average. Vietnam, for example, has attracted large electronics producers whose factories have created work for young people from rural provinces. The benefit extends beyond the pay packet. Employees learn modern production methods and management practices, local suppliers have to raise their standards to win contracts, and the government collects taxes and export earnings. How far this happens depends on the host country, however, since skills are transferred only when local staff are promoted beyond the assembly line.\n\nThe disadvantages arise from the unequal strength of the two sides. A multinational can choose among many countries, and governments eager to attract it may offer long tax holidays or tolerate weak labour and environmental rules. The host country then gains less revenue than it expected, while workers and nearby residents bear the cost of low standards. There is also a risk of dependence. If the company later moves to a cheaper location, a town built around its factory can lose most of its jobs at once. Local firms that supplied the plant lose their main customer at the same moment, so recovery is slow.\n\nMultinational companies therefore offer developing countries capital, jobs and expertise, while exposing them to lost tax revenue, weak regulation and sudden withdrawal. The balance between the two depends largely on how firmly the host government negotiates and regulates.",
"b422":"Opinion is divided over what the public should find when it walks into a museum or gallery: the story of its own region, or objects gathered from across the world. Both positions have merit, but in my view the two aims are compatible, and the best institutions build on a local core while regularly bringing in work from abroad.\n\nThose who favour local content argue that it is the one thing a museum can do uniquely well. The pottery, tools and photographs of a particular town cannot be seen anywhere else, and they give residents a sense of how their community came to be. Once such objects are scattered or lost, that record cannot be rebuilt. Tourists arrive with the same interest. A visitor to Hoi An, for instance, wants to learn how its merchants once lived, not to look at a modest collection of European paintings. This argument is strongest for small museums, though it carries less weight for major galleries in capital cities.\n\nSupporters of international collections reply that a museum should widen horizons as well as record local life. For schoolchildren and for adults who cannot afford to travel, the nearest gallery is often the only place where they will meet the art of other civilisations. Touring exhibitions of foreign treasures also tend to draw people who would not otherwise visit, and the income they generate can help to fund local displays. A town museum that borrows a famous collection for a season may find that the new visitors come back for its own galleries. A gallery that relied wholly on borrowed exhibitions, however, would neglect the heritage that only it can protect.\n\nA permanent collection rooted in local history, enriched by temporary exhibitions from other countries, answers both concerns. I believe museums should not be asked to choose between the two.",
"b423":"Theatres and concert halls in many countries are filled largely by older audiences, and young faces are rare. I believe the explanation has more to do with price and habit than with taste, which means that the situation can be changed.\n\nCost is the most immediate barrier. A ticket for a play or an orchestral concert can cost as much as a month of a streaming subscription, which offers unlimited films and music at home. For a student or someone in a first job, the comparison is difficult to ignore. Unfamiliarity matters just as much. Young people who were not taken to the theatre as children often assume that it is formal, slow and intended for someone else. They may feel unsure, for example, about how to dress or when to applaud, and an evening that begins with that anxiety is easy to avoid. This is less true of pop concerts and festivals, which many young people attend eagerly despite high prices.\n\nThat contrast suggests what would work. Venues could offer cheap tickets to people under a certain age, as many European theatres already do, filling seats that would otherwise stay empty. A student who can see a play for the price of a cinema ticket has less reason to stay at home. Schools should also take pupils to live performances regularly, so that the first visit happens early and in the company of friends. Once teenagers have sat through a concert or two, the customs of the theatre stop seeming strange and returning alone feels easier. Lower prices alone will not be enough, though, unless the experience itself feels welcoming to a newcomer.\n\nYoung people stay away from plays and classical concerts mainly because these seem expensive and unfamiliar. Cheaper tickets and early visits through school would, I think, bring many of them in.",
"b424":"In many labour markets, experienced workers now find themselves applying for the same posts as people who have just left university. This competition creates real hardship for older applicants, but it can be eased if governments and employers change the way they train and recruit.\n\nThe main problem is that older candidates tend to lose, and the consequences of losing are heavier for them. Employers often assume that a younger recruit will accept lower pay and learn new software more quickly. A fifty-year-old accountant who is made redundant, for instance, may send out dozens of applications and receive few replies. Unlike a graduate, this person is likely to have a mortgage and children to support, so a long period without work can damage a whole family. The economy loses as well, because knowledge that took years to acquire is left unused. A firm that replaces its experienced staff with newcomers has nobody to train them, and avoidable mistakes increase.\n\nBecause the difficulty lies partly in skills and partly in attitudes, both need attention. Governments can fund short, practical courses that allow mid-career workers to update their technical knowledge, as Singapore does through its adult training credits. Such schemes are effective only if the courses match what local employers are looking for, which means companies must help to design them. Recruitment itself can also be made fairer. Removing dates of birth from application forms, and judging candidates on a work-related task, gives older applicants a chance to be assessed on what they can do. Although no law can remove prejudice entirely, measures of this kind make it harder to act on.\n\nCompetition between generations is unlikely to disappear while people work for longer. Its worst effects can be reduced, however, when older workers are helped to keep their skills current and are assessed on ability rather than age.",
"b425":"Machines that clean floors, assemble cars and sort parcels have moved from science fiction into ordinary homes and workplaces. I believe this is a positive development on the whole, although its benefits will be fairly shared only if displaced workers are helped to move into new roles.\n\nThe strongest argument for robots is that they take over work which is dull, exhausting or dangerous. On a production line, a machine can lift heavy parts and repeat the same movement all day without injury or loss of accuracy, leaving people to supervise, repair and design. The same principle applies at home on a smaller scale. A robotic vacuum cleaner or lawnmower, for example, hands back time that a busy household can spend on rest or on one another. For elderly people who wish to remain in their own homes, such help may make the difference between independence and residential care. Robots are less suited, however, to tasks that call for judgement or sympathy, and a machine cannot comfort a patient.\n\nThe obvious objection concerns employment. When a warehouse installs automatic sorting, the staff who did that job are no longer needed, and those with few qualifications are affected first. This hardship is real and should not be brushed aside. Past waves of automation suggest, though, that new kinds of work tend to appear as old ones vanish, since machines must be built, programmed and maintained. The difficulty is that a redundant packer does not become a technician overnight. Whether automation benefits such workers depends on retraining that governments and employers are willing to fund.\n\nRobots relieve people of labour that few would choose to do, and they make homes and factories safer and more productive. My view is therefore favourable, provided that the transition is managed with care.",
"b426":"Walls and doors have disappeared from many workplaces, replaced by shared desks at which dozens of people sit within sight and earshot of one another. Although this layout has real attractions for employers, I believe that for most kinds of work its drawbacks are greater.\n\nThe case for open-plan offices is mainly one of cost and contact. A single large room holds far more staff than the same floor divided into private offices, so a company in an expensive city centre can cut its rent. Supporters also argue that removing barriers encourages people to talk. A junior designer who can see that a senior colleague is free, for example, is more likely to ask a quick question than one who has to knock on a closed door. These gains are genuine, although the second depends on the work being collaborative in the first place.\n\nThe disadvantages fall on the activity that offices exist for, which is concentrated thought. Writing a report, checking figures or solving a technical problem requires long periods without interruption, and an open room supplies the opposite: ringing phones, nearby conversations and constant movement. Many employees respond by wearing headphones, which removes the very openness the design was meant to create. Privacy suffers as well, since a difficult call to a client or a confidential talk with a manager has to be held in front of an audience. Some staff therefore put off sensitive conversations, and problems are left to grow. While quiet rooms and booths can soften these problems, they are usually too few.\n\nOpen-plan offices save money and make casual contact easier, but they do so at the expense of concentration, on which the quality of most office work depends. On that basis I believe the disadvantages outweigh the advantages, except in teams whose work is mostly discussion.",
"b427":"Through social media, young people now follow the daily lives of singers, athletes and online personalities more closely than any earlier generation could. In my view, the growing power of these figures as role models is mainly a negative development, even though a few of them use it well.\n\nThe central problem is that fame rewards the wrong qualities. Celebrities become visible because of their looks, their wealth or their ability to attract attention, and these are the things their followers learn to admire. A teenager who spends hours watching an influencer's luxury holidays and flawless photographs may come to judge her own life and appearance against a standard that is carefully staged. Much of this content is also advertising, though it is rarely labelled as such. Followers are encouraged to buy the clothes, cosmetics or diets that a star is paid to promote. The influence is therefore commercial as much as personal.\n\nIt would be unfair to ignore the famous people who set a better example. Some athletes speak openly about the discipline behind their success or about their struggles with mental health, and such honesty can help young fans to cope with their own difficulties. The footballer Marcus Rashford, for instance, used his reputation to campaign for free school meals for children in Britain. Although these cases show that celebrity can be put to good use, they remain exceptions. They do not alter the fact that the people a child sees every day, such as parents and teachers, are being displaced by strangers whose lives are largely a performance.\n\nAdmiring a talented person is harmless, and sometimes inspiring. The problem is the scale of the influence, and I believe that when celebrities matter more to the young than the adults around them, the result is more harm than good.",
"b428":"Distance learning has grown from a minority option into a route chosen by huge numbers of working adults. The claim that such study can never match attendance in person is, in my view, too sweeping: it holds for some subjects and some learners, but not for all.\n\nFor a motivated adult studying a subject based on reading and discussion, an online course can be just as effective. Someone taking a master's degree in accounting or history while employed full-time can watch lectures in the evening, submit written work and receive detailed comments from a tutor. Institutions such as the Open University in Britain have awarded respected degrees in this way for decades. The quality of what is learned depends on the design of the course and the effort of the student rather than on the building in which it happens. Indeed, a well-organised online programme may serve its students better than a crowded lecture hall where few can ask a question.\n\nThe claim is far stronger where learning depends on physical practice or close supervision. Nursing, dentistry and engineering involve skills that have to be performed under an instructor's eye, and no video can replace this. Younger students also tend to need the routine and companionship of a campus. An eighteen-year-old left alone with a laptop is more likely to fall behind than a professional who has chosen to study and is paying for it personally. Even these limits are not fixed, though, since many programmes now combine online theory with short periods of practical training on site.\n\nWhether a course works at a distance depends on what is being taught and who is learning it. I disagree, therefore, with the idea that online study is inferior by nature, while accepting that for practical disciplines and for many school-leavers the campus remains the better choice.",
"b429":"A leading actor or singer can earn more from a single film or tour than a prime minister earns in an entire term of office. This gap follows from the different ways in which the two groups are paid, and I believe it is not the negative development it may first appear to be.\n\nEntertainers are paid by the market, and the market for their work has become global. A popular song can be streamed in almost every country at once on a service such as Spotify, and a film is shown in thousands of cinemas. Even a small payment from each customer therefore adds up to an enormous sum. Advertisers then pay extra to attach their products to a famous face. Politicians, by contrast, receive a salary fixed by the state and drawn from taxes. Voters are reluctant to see that salary rise, and a minister who proposed a large increase would be accused of greed. Their pay is therefore held down by public opinion rather than set by demand.\n\nSeen in this light, the difference is largely acceptable. Money earned by a performer is given willingly by fans, whereas a politician's income is taken from citizens who have no choice in the matter. It is also healthy that public office is not a route to great wealth, since modest salaries tend to attract people who are motivated by service. There is a limit to this argument, however. If the pay is so low that only the rich can afford to stand for election, or if officials are tempted to accept bribes, the public loses.\n\nEarnings reflect the size of an audience, not the value of a job. For that reason, I think the gap is tolerable, provided that politicians are paid enough to keep capable and honest people in public life.",
"b430":"Far more children are seriously overweight today than a generation ago, and the trend shows little sign of slowing. The causes lie mainly in what children eat and how little they move, and both can be changed if parents, schools and governments act together.\n\nThe main cause is a change in the food that surrounds children. Processed snacks, sugary drinks and fast food are cheap, heavily advertised and available on almost every street, whereas fresh ingredients take time and money to prepare. Parents who both work long hours often rely on ready meals, so a child may grow up regarding crisps and fizzy drinks as ordinary daily food. This diet is combined with a much less active childhood. Many children are driven to school and spend their free time on phones and games consoles rather than outdoors, so the extra calories are not burned off. Although family habits and genes also play a part, they cannot explain a rise that has taken place within a few decades.\n\nSince children do not choose their own environment, the solutions must come from the adults who shape it. Schools are the obvious place to begin. They can serve balanced lunches, remove vending machines selling sweets, and make sport or active play part of each school day. Governments can support these efforts by restricting the advertising of junk food to children and, for example, by taxing sugary drinks as the United Kingdom has done. They will achieve little, however, unless parents reinforce them at home by cooking simple meals, limiting screen time and being active with their children.\n\nChildhood obesity is the product of cheap, unhealthy food and inactive routines, not of any failing in children themselves. It will decline only when schools, governments and families work together to change the conditions in which children eat and play.",
"b431":"The number of applicants for each university place has risen sharply in many countries, and admission to the best institutions has become a contest that begins years in advance. Some rivalry raises standards, but I believe competition at its present intensity does more harm than good.\n\nThere is a case for competition. When places are scarce, pupils have a strong reason to work hard, and the habits they form, such as planning their time and persisting with difficult material, are useful long after the examination. Universities, in turn, receive students who are better prepared for demanding courses. These benefits hold, however, only up to the point at which effort still reflects learning. Beyond it, students are no longer studying to understand a subject but to outscore one another by a fraction of a mark.\n\nThat point has been passed in a number of systems. In South Korea and China, for example, a single entrance examination shapes much of adolescence, and teenagers attend private classes late into the evening for years. The first cost falls on health. Sleep, sport and friendships are sacrificed at the age when young people need them most, and the pressure to outperform classmates leaves many anxious and exhausted before they reach university. The other cost is to fairness, because families who can pay for tutoring buy an advantage that others cannot. Capable young people from poorer homes are therefore pushed out by rivals who are better coached and not necessarily more talented. Although scholarships can soften this effect, they rarely match years of paid preparation.\n\nA degree of selection is healthy, and no system can offer every applicant a first choice. The current race for places is nevertheless, in my view, a negative development, because it damages young people's wellbeing and rewards family income as much as ability.",
"b432":"Events such as the Olympic Games are regularly praised as occasions on which nations set aside their differences. I agree that international sport encourages goodwill between peoples, but in my view it is a modest aid to peace and not a means of achieving it.\n\nSport brings together people who might otherwise meet only as rivals or strangers. Athletes from countries with hostile governments compete under the same rules, share accommodation and often show each other open respect, and spectators around the world watch them do so. These images matter because hostility between nations feeds on the belief that foreigners are fundamentally different. Sport can also give diplomats an opening. At the 2018 Winter Olympics, for example, athletes from North and South Korea marched together at the opening ceremony, a gesture that accompanied a brief period of talks between the two governments. Such moments do not settle disputes, though they make conversation easier to begin.\n\nThe limits of this influence are equally clear. Wars are caused by disputes over territory, resources and power, and none of these is altered by a football match. Two countries may applaud each other's athletes in the stadium and resume their quarrel the following week, because the interests that divide them are unchanged. Sport can also be turned to less friendly purposes. Governments have at times used tournaments to display national strength or to improve their image abroad, while continuing the policies that caused tension. In such cases the event lends respectability to a regime rather than restraining it. Sport therefore reflects the state of relations between countries more often than it changes them.\n\nInternational competitions create contact and sympathy between ordinary people, which is valuable in itself. Lasting peace, however, depends on political agreement, and I believe sport can support that process without replacing it.",
"b433":"Hunger remains a daily reality for millions of people, and genetically modified crops are often presented as the answer. I agree that they can make a useful contribution, but I believe they address only one part of a problem whose roots are largely economic and political.\n\nGenetic modification is most valuable where farming conditions are harsh. Scientists can now give a plant qualities that conventional breeding would take decades to produce, such as resistance to drought, pests or salty soil. A farmer whose maize survives a dry season harvests a crop where there would otherwise have been none, and uses less pesticide in the process. Some varieties are also designed to be more nutritious. Golden rice, for instance, was developed to supply vitamin A to communities whose diet lacks it. As the climate becomes less predictable and the world's population grows, such crops are likely to matter more, provided that they are properly tested and that seeds remain affordable for small farmers.\n\nHigher yields alone, however, will not end hunger, because scarcity is seldom the real cause of it. The world already grows enough to feed its population, yet a great deal is lost through poor storage, wasted by consumers or priced beyond the reach of the poor. Famines tend to occur in regions affected by war or failed government, where food cannot reach those who need it. In such places a better seed changes little. Investment in roads, storage and fair markets would do more, although these measures attract less attention than a scientific breakthrough.\n\nGenetically modified foods deserve a place in the response to food shortages, particularly in areas with difficult growing conditions. In my view, though, they are one tool among several, and they will help the hungry only if poverty, waste and conflict are tackled as well.",
"b434":"Many shoppers insist that advertisements merely irritate or amuse them and have no bearing on what they buy. I disagree with this view, because the influence of advertising works largely below the level at which people notice it.\n\nThe claim rests on how people experience advertisements, not on how they behave afterwards. Few viewers feel persuaded by a short film about a soft drink, and most forget it within the hour. What remains is familiarity. Weeks later, facing a row of similar bottles, the shopper picks the one whose name comes to mind first and regards the choice as a personal preference. Companies understand this well. Firms such as Coca-Cola continue to advertise heavily although almost every potential customer already knows the product, which would make little sense if the spending had no effect on sales. An amusing advertisement is in fact the most effective kind, since people remember it and repeat it to others.\n\nThis does not mean that consumers are helpless. For costly or important purchases, a buyer usually looks beyond the advertisement, reading reviews of a washing machine or asking friends about a holiday destination. At this stage a clever slogan counts for little against the experience of people who have used the product. Advertising has often done its work before this careful stage begins, however, by determining which brands are considered at all. A family choosing a car, for instance, will compare three or four makes they have heard of and ignore those they have not. The decision is their own, but the shortlist was partly written for them.\n\nPeople are right that an advertisement seldom makes them buy something on the spot. I believe they are mistaken, however, to assume that it leaves their decisions untouched, since it shapes what they recognise, trust and consider.",
"b435":"Life in a big city brings opportunities, yet it also exposes residents to difficulties that people in smaller places rarely meet. The chief ones are polluted surroundings and social isolation, and in my view governments would do better to tackle them directly than to persuade people to leave.\n\nCity dwellers breathe air filled with fumes from traffic and industry, and live with constant noise from roads, building sites and neighbours. Over the years this takes a toll on the lungs and heart, and on sleep. Green space is scarce, so children have few places to play and adults few places to rest. The second problem is less visible. Although millions of people live side by side, many know hardly anyone in their own building, and long working hours leave little time for friendship. Newcomers and the elderly are particularly likely to feel alone in a crowd.\n\nMoving people to smaller towns appears to offer an easy answer, but it has limits. Most people live in cities because the jobs, universities and hospitals are there, and a town that lacks them will not hold new arrivals for long. A policy of encouragement makes sense where towns already have employers who need staff, or for those able to work remotely. For the majority who must stay, however, the better course is to improve the city itself. Cleaner buses, restrictions on the most polluting vehicles, and new parks and community centres address the problems where they occur. London's low-emission zone, for example, has reduced exhaust fumes without asking a single resident to move.\n\nI believe, then, that governments should offer smaller towns as an option but should not treat relocation as the main solution. The priority should be cleaner and more sociable cities for the people who depend on them.",
"b436":"Television used to be watched by a whole household in one room, but personal screens and streaming services have made viewing a largely solitary habit. This change has obvious attractions, yet I believe that what has been lost is worth more than what has been gained.\n\nThe appeal of watching alone is control. Each person can choose what interests them, start it at a convenient moment and pause it at will, without negotiating with anyone. Someone who works night shifts, for example, can follow a series at breakfast instead of missing it. The other advantage is concentration. A demanding film or a documentary in a foreign language is easier to follow when no companion is talking or checking a phone. A difficult scene can also be replayed without irritating anyone. These are real conveniences, although they are conveniences of the kind that make life smoother without making it richer.\n\nThe cost is that a shared activity has become a private one. When parents and children watched the same programme, they laughed at the same jokes and talked about the story afterwards. Those conversations were often a way of raising subjects that are difficult to discuss directly. Although they still share a house, each member of the household may now sit in a separate room with a separate screen. A second drawback is that solitary viewing tends to last longer. Streaming services start the next episode automatically, and with nobody there to suggest a break, one programme easily becomes four. Sleep and exercise are usually what is given up.\n\nWatching alone offers freedom of choice, and for people who live by themselves the question hardly arises. For families and friends, however, I think the loss of a shared experience outweighs that convenience, which is why occasional viewing together is worth protecting.",
"b437":"A person with an internet connection can now tour a foreign museum, listen to another country's music and watch its films without leaving home. Some conclude that visiting those countries has lost much of its purpose. I disagree with this conclusion, because learning about a culture is not the same as experiencing it.\n\nThe internet is an excellent source of information about other societies, and its value should be recognised. Travel is expensive and beyond the means of many people, so online material may be the only window they have on life elsewhere. Someone curious about Japan, for instance, can study its history, follow cooking demonstrations and hear the language spoken by native speakers. What the internet provides, however, is a selected and edited version, usually the most attractive or most unusual aspects, chosen by whoever made the video.\n\nBeing present in a country teaches things that cannot be transmitted through a screen. A visitor has to order food, ask for directions and work out how people queue, greet each other and behave on public transport. These small difficulties reveal the unwritten rules of a society, and they involve real people who answer back, which no recording does. Travel also engages the senses. The heat of a street market, the smell of the food and the noise of the traffic form an impression that stays in the memory long after facts are forgotten. Although a short holiday spent entirely in a resort offers little of this, a traveller who makes some effort to mix with local people gains an understanding that reading alone does not give.\n\nOnline resources are best treated as preparation for a journey rather than a substitute for one. Travel remains important, I believe, for anyone who wants to understand another culture and not merely know about it.",
"b438":"Many illnesses treated in modern hospitals are linked to smoking, heavy drinking or poor eating, and some argue that patients responsible for their own condition should be charged more. Although I understand the frustration behind this proposal, I disagree with it, because it would be both unfair and unworkable.\n\nThe idea has an obvious appeal. Health budgets are limited, and money spent on preventable diseases cannot be spent on patients who fell ill through no fault of their own. The difficulty, however, lies in deciding who is to blame. A doctor cannot say with confidence whether a particular heart attack was caused by diet, by stress or by an inherited weakness. Nor is lifestyle purely a matter of choice. A person on a low wage who works night shifts has less time and money for fresh food and exercise than a well-paid professional, so the charge would fall hardest on the poor.\n\nA fairer way to make people bear the cost of unhealthy habits is to tax the products themselves. Duties on tobacco, alcohol and sugary drinks, such as those already charged in many European countries, collect money from consumers in proportion to what they consume. They require no judgement about any individual patient, and the revenue can be directed to the health service. This approach also discourages the habit before the illness develops, whereas a penalty at the hospital door arrives too late to change anything. Although taxes of this kind do not reach people who simply overeat or avoid exercise, they are far more practical than judging patients one by one.\n\nFor these reasons I disagree that patients with unhealthy lifestyles should pay more for treatment. Responsibility for health is real, but it is better encouraged through prices and education than through punishment at the point of care.",
"b439":"Thin plastic carrier bags are used for the few minutes it takes to carry shopping home and then thrown away, yet they remain in the environment for generations. I agree that they should be banned, although a ban will only help if shoppers reuse whatever replaces them.\n\nThe reason for prohibiting these bags is that the harm they cause is out of all proportion to their usefulness. They are too light and too cheap to be worth collecting for recycling, so a large share ends up in rivers, drains and the sea, where turtles and seabirds swallow them or become entangled. In cities with poor drainage they also block the channels that carry away rainwater, which worsens flooding. A charge reduces the number of bags taken, but it leaves them in circulation. An outright ban is simpler to enforce, and countries such as Rwanda and Kenya, which have introduced one, have noticeably cleaner streets and waterways as a result.\n\nA ban does have costs. Paper bags require more energy and water to manufacture than plastic ones, and a cotton bag must be used many times before it does less damage than the bags it replaces. If people simply collect a new cloth bag on every visit, the environment gains little. The benefit therefore depends on habit as much as on law, which is why shops should be encouraged to reward customers who bring their own bags. This objection, however, is an argument for designing the ban carefully rather than for abandoning it, since a sturdy bag carried for years is clearly better than hundreds of disposable ones.\n\nPlastic shopping bags offer a small convenience at a very high environmental price. For this reason I believe governments are right to ban them, provided that the law is accompanied by efforts to make reusable bags a habit.",
"b440":"International tournaments such as the football World Cup are often praised as a peaceful outlet for national pride and even as a means of bringing rival countries together. I agree that they can do some good in both respects, but I believe that describing them as essential greatly overstates their influence.\n\nThere is something in the claim that sport offers a harmless form of patriotism. During a World Cup, people wave flags, sing anthems and celebrate their country with an intensity that in other settings might seem aggressive. Because the contest is governed by rules and ends with a handshake, these feelings are expressed and then put away. Fans from rival nations frequently mix in the host cities with good humour. This release is not guaranteed to be safe, however. Violence between supporters has spoiled a number of tournaments, and a defeat can sharpen resentment rather than relieve it.\n\nThe idea that such events ease tensions between governments is harder to defend. Political disputes arise from conflicts over territory, trade or security, and ninety minutes of football leaves those causes untouched. Sport has at times opened a door. Table tennis matches, for example, helped to restore contact between China and the United States in the 1970s. In that case, though, both governments already wanted better relations and used sport as a convenient first step. Where the will is absent, competitions are more likely to reflect hostility, as the Olympic boycotts of the Cold War showed. Lasting improvements depend on diplomacy, trade and ordinary contact between citizens.\n\nInternational sporting occasions bring people together and allow national feeling to be expressed in a mostly friendly way. To my mind, this makes them a welcome addition to international life, but not a substitute for the patient political work on which peace actually depends.",
"b441":"Learning another language takes years of effort, and some children show little enthusiasm for it. This has led to the suggestion that the subject should be optional at school. I disagree, because pupils are too young to judge what they will later need, although the way languages are taught deserves rethinking.\n\nChildren cannot foresee the value of a skill they do not yet have. A twelve-year-old who drops English may discover ten years later that the course or job she wants requires it. By then the easiest years for learning have passed, and catching up alongside work or study is hard. There is a wider benefit too. Studying another language shows pupils that people elsewhere express ideas, and sometimes see the world, differently. A child who learns that a familiar phrase has no exact equivalent elsewhere begins to question assumptions that had seemed universal. These gains depend, of course, on reaching a level at which the language is usable.\n\nThe strongest objection is that children who are forced to study learn very little. It is true that many leave school after years of lessons unable to hold a simple conversation, and that this time could have gone to other subjects. This is a criticism of method, however, rather than of compulsion. Where lessons concentrate on grammar drills and written tests, poor results are to be expected. In countries such as the Netherlands, where pupils spend much of their time speaking and listening, most school-leavers use English comfortably. Some flexibility is reasonable, for example over which language is studied, but that is different from allowing pupils to study none.\n\nMaking languages optional would let children give up an advantage before they understand its worth. I disagree with the proposal for that reason, and would keep the subject compulsory while teaching it in a more practical way.",
"b442":"Cooking, sewing and woodwork have been disappearing from school timetables as pressure on examination results grows. Academic achievement must remain the main purpose of schooling, but I disagree that practical skills should be left to families, because many families are no longer in a position to teach them.\n\nThe proposal assumes that every home contains an adult with both the ability and the time to pass these skills on. That was once a reasonable assumption, but it is less true today. In many households, both parents work long hours and rely on ready-made meals and cheap replacement goods, so they may not be able to cook from basic ingredients or repair a garment themselves. A child from such a home will learn these things at school or not at all. Leaving the matter to parents therefore widens a gap between pupils, whereas a weekly lesson gives each of them the same start.\n\nPractical subjects also support the academic aims they are accused of displacing. Measuring timber for a shelf applies geometry, and adjusting a recipe involves ratio and basic chemistry, so abstract ideas become easier to grasp when they are used. There is a benefit for motivation as well. Pupils who struggle with written work often discover in a workshop or kitchen that they are competent, and this confidence can carry over into other lessons. A teenager who has built a sound shelf, for instance, has evidence that patient effort produces results. Time is limited, of course, and these subjects should occupy a small part of the week rather than compete with mathematics or languages for equal hours.\n\nFamilies who can teach their children to cook and make things should certainly do so. Schools must still provide such lessons, I believe, since they give all pupils useful abilities and strengthen academic learning instead of weakening it.",
"b443":"Screens are now present in most classrooms, and some people argue that they should take over from the printed page entirely. I disagree with this proposal, because films, computers and games are valuable additions to books but poor substitutes for them.\n\nNewer media can certainly teach things that books cannot. A film of a volcanic eruption or a computer simulation of the solar system shows in seconds what paragraphs of description struggle to convey. Educational games, such as those that reward pupils for solving sums against the clock, turn repetitive practice into something children do willingly. Used in this way, technology makes lessons more vivid and reaches pupils who find reading difficult. Its strength, however, lies in capturing attention and illustrating ideas, which is only part of what education requires.\n\nSustained reading develops abilities that these media do not. Following an argument across several pages, holding its stages in mind and judging whether the conclusion is justified is slow work, and it is the foundation of study in most subjects at higher levels. A book also leaves the reader in control of the pace, free to stop, reread and think, while a film moves on regardless and a game rewards speed over reflection. Writing benefits too. Children who rarely read at length tend to write less fluently, since vocabulary and sentence structure are absorbed largely from the page. They meet far fewer complete, carefully built sentences in a film or a game than in a chapter of a novel. This is not an argument against screens, only against relying on them alone.\n\nEach medium does something the others cannot, so the sensible question is how to combine them. Books should, in my view, remain at the centre of schooling, with films, computers and games used wherever they explain or motivate better.",
"b444":"Warnings about the need to save energy have been repeated for decades, yet households in most countries use more electricity than their parents did. This growth has identifiable causes, and understanding them suggests how people might be persuaded to consume less.\n\nThe main cause is that electricity is both cheap and invisible. A family pays for it weeks after using it, in a single bill that does not show which appliance was responsible. There is therefore little connection between leaving the air conditioning on and the cost of doing so. The number of devices in a typical home has also multiplied. Televisions, routers and chargers draw power day and night, even when they are not in use, and rising incomes have made air conditioners and tumble dryers ordinary purchases. Although individual appliances have become more efficient, this gain has been cancelled out because people own more of them and run them for longer.\n\nSince waste results from a lack of information and incentive, encouragement should supply both. Smart meters that display consumption and its cost as it happens allow residents to see, for example, what an hour of electric heating adds to the bill. Once the figure is in front of them, many people switch off appliances they would otherwise have ignored. Pricing can reinforce this message. If the rate per unit rises once a household passes a reasonable level of use, careful families pay less and wasteful ones have a reason to change. Publicity campaigns have a place, although they tend to work only when they are supported by measures that affect people's finances.\n\nPeople use too much electricity largely because nothing in their daily lives tells them they are doing so. Clear information and sensible pricing would, I believe, change that more reliably than further warnings.",
"b445":"Of all the facts a shopkeeper might know about a customer, the year of birth is probably the most revealing. I agree to a large extent that age shapes shopping habits more than any other single factor, though it does not act alone.\n\nAge matters so much because it influences both what people buy and how they buy it. Needs change over the decades as a person's body, home and responsibilities change. A couple in their thirties, for example, may spend on children's clothes and toys, while the same couple at seventy buy little of either. Method changes as well. Someone who grew up with a smartphone will compare prices and order on it, while many older people prefer to pay in cash at a counter. Few other characteristics reach so widely. Gender or nationality may tell a retailer something about what a customer likes, but less about how, where and how often that customer buys.\n\nThe obvious rival to age is income. A wealthy pensioner and a poor one clearly fill different baskets. Income, however, determines how much is spent more than the pattern of spending. A well-paid graduate of twenty-five and a manager of fifty-five on the same salary will use their money quite differently. The first is likely to spend on rent, travel and technology, for instance, and the second on a mortgage, a family car and savings for retirement. Income is also partly a product of age, as earnings usually rise through a career and fall after it. The claim is weakest in cases of real poverty, where necessity leaves little room for preference of any kind.\n\nWealth, family and personality all leave their mark on how people shop. Age, however, runs through each of these, and that is why I think it remains the most reliable single explanation of shopping behaviour.",
"b446":"For many people in their twenties, a free Saturday is as likely to be spent in shops, or on shopping websites, as in a park or a sports club. Several features of modern life explain this, and I believe the trend is, on the whole, a negative one.\n\nShopping has become a leading pastime largely because it is the easiest one available. Shopping centres are open late, sheltered from the weather and close to public transport, and they require no booking, equipment or skill. Online stores are more convenient still, since browsing can fill any idle moment on a phone. Social media supplies a second reason. When friends post pictures of new outfits or gadgets, buying becomes a way of taking part, and a purchase can be shared and admired within minutes. Shopping thus meets a need for company and novelty, although it does so at a price.\n\nThat price is the reason the trend concerns me. Leisure built around purchasing is expensive, and young adults who are trying to save for housing or repay student loans can least afford it. Small sums spent every weekend are easy to overlook, yet over a year they may amount to money that was badly needed elsewhere. Waste is the other concern. Cheap clothing bought for amusement, such as items worn once for a photograph, is quickly discarded, and much of it ends up in landfill. While an occasional afternoon of shopping with friends does no harm, a problem arises when it becomes the default, so that pleasure and consumption are hard to separate.\n\nConvenience and online pressure have made shopping a favourite recreation of the young. It offers company and excitement, but because these come with debt and waste attached, I think it is an unhealthy habit for a generation to form.",
"b447":"Tobacco kills more people than any other legal product, and it is understandable that some would like to see it outlawed. While I share the aim of ending smoking, I disagree that an outright ban is the right way to achieve it, because prohibition would create new harms without removing the old one.\n\nThe case for a ban is a strong one. Nicotine is addictive, so most smokers are not making a free choice each time they light a cigarette, and many started as teenagers. A habit taken up at fifteen can therefore lead to cancer or heart disease decades later. The harm also reaches others. People nearby, including children, breathe the smoke without having agreed to do so, and the cost of treating smokers falls on health services that non-smokers also pay for. Although these facts justify firm action, they do not show that making tobacco illegal would work.\n\nThe difficulty is that a law cannot end an addiction overnight. Millions of dependent smokers would still want cigarettes, and criminal gangs would be glad to supply them, as happened with alcohol when the United States prohibited it in the 1920s. Illegal tobacco would be untaxed, unregulated and sold to anyone, including the young. Governments have better tools. High taxes, plain packaging, advertising bans and smoke-free public places have steadily reduced smoking in countries such as Australia and Britain, and they have done so without turning smokers into offenders. A more gradual measure, raising the legal age of sale each year, could eventually close the market to new customers rather than punishing existing ones.\n\nTobacco deserves no protection, but the test of a policy is whether it reduces harm. I believe governments should keep tightening the controls that are already working, and I do not think a sudden ban would serve public health as well.",
"b448":"Many people reach for their headphones when they feel tense, and it is sometimes claimed that relieving anxiety is the chief purpose of music. While I accept that this is one of its most common uses, I disagree that it is the main one.\n\nThere is no doubt that music can calm the mind. A slow, familiar piece steadies breathing and draws attention away from whatever is causing worry, which is why people play quiet songs after a difficult day or before trying to sleep. Hospitals and dental surgeries use background music for the same purpose. For a commuter on a crowded train, a favourite album creates a private space in which the pressures of the day recede. This function has probably grown as modern life has become more hurried, though it describes only one kind of listening, usually done alone.\n\nMuch of the music people value has little to do with relaxation. Football supporters sing to show loyalty, soldiers march to drums, and guests dance at weddings to celebrate. None of these groups is trying to feel calmer. Music is also a means of expression, both for those who make it and for those who recognise their own feelings in it. A teenager who listens to an angry or sorrowful song, for instance, is often seeking company in that emotion rather than an escape from it. Religious ceremonies in most cultures depend on chants and hymns, whose purpose is worship. If stress relief were the principal function, it would be hard to explain why so much music is loud, fast or deliberately sad.\n\nSoothing the listener is therefore one role among several. Music also unites groups, marks important occasions and gives shape to feelings, and I believe these purposes are at least as central as the comfort it brings to the individual.",
"b449":"Standing up to address an audience is something many adults dread, and a number of educators argue that the remedy is to begin practising in childhood. I agree with them and believe that speaking in public deserves a regular place in the school curriculum.\n\nThose who hold this view point to how much of adult life depends on speaking well. Job interviews, meetings, sales pitches and community gatherings all require a person to organise ideas and deliver them clearly to listeners. Schools, however, have traditionally assessed pupils almost entirely through writing, so a student can leave with excellent grades and still be unable to explain an idea aloud. Supporters also note that the fear of public speaking grows with age. A nine-year-old who regularly talks to the class for two minutes about a favourite book, for instance, treats the task as ordinary, while an adult facing a first presentation may be paralysed by it.\n\nThese arguments seem to me sound, and there is a further reason to teach the skill at school. Preparing a talk forces a pupil to select the main points, put them in order and anticipate questions, which deepens understanding of the subject itself. It is also a matter of fairness. Children from confident, talkative families tend to acquire this ability at home, whereas others get the chance only if school provides it. The teaching needs care, though. Forcing a very shy child to perform before thirty classmates without preparation can increase anxiety, so practice should start in small groups and build up gradually. It should also remain a modest part of the timetable and not displace reading and writing.\n\nSpeaking to an audience is a skill that adult life demands and that improves only with practice. It should therefore be taught to all pupils, I believe, beginning early and gently.",
"b450":"Constant notifications, dozens of passwords and devices that need updating have led some people to feel that technology has complicated life beyond reason. There is truth in this feeling, but I disagree that the answer is to give technology up, since doing so would create far greater difficulties than it removed.\n\nIt is fair to say that modern life demands more attention than it used to. A worker may receive messages on several platforms at once, each expecting a prompt reply, and the boundary between office and home has become unclear. Everyday tasks have multiplied as well. Paying for parking, for example, can now require downloading an application, creating an account and remembering yet another password. Complexity of this kind is tiring, and the wish to escape it is understandable. Much of it, though, comes from poor design and from the habit of saying yes to every new service, rather than from technology as such.\n\nAbandoning technology would not bring back a simpler life, because the simplicity people imagine did not really exist. Washing clothes by hand, travelling for days to deliver a message and queuing at a bank were time-consuming in their own way. Modern medicine, clean water and reliable food supplies all rest on advanced equipment, and few would choose to live without them. A more realistic course is selective use. People can remove applications they do not need, switch off alerts and keep certain hours free of screens, while retaining the tools that serve them. Someone who checks messages twice a day instead of every few minutes keeps the benefit of fast communication without the constant interruption.\n\nFeeling overwhelmed by technology is reasonable, but giving it up is not a solution. The sensible response, I believe, is to use it more deliberately, because its absence would make life harder instead of simpler.",
"b451":"Cinemas and streaming services in most countries are dominated by films made elsewhere, above all in the United States. Some welcome this as a source of entertainment and insight, while others fear that it weakens local culture. I believe the benefits are greater, provided that domestic film-making is given room to survive.\n\nPeople who approve of foreign films see them as an inexpensive way of meeting other societies. A viewer in Europe who watches an Iranian family drama, for example, sees ordinary people whose worries resemble their own, a picture that news reports rarely provide. Hearing the original language adds to this, since viewers learn how people really speak. Local film-making can benefit as well. Directors borrow techniques from abroad, and audiences who have seen the best foreign work expect more from films made at home, which pushes standards up. This case is weaker when nearly all imported films come from a single country and tell similar stories.\n\nThe opposing view is that imports crowd out what is made at home. A domestic production with a small budget struggles to compete with the marketing of a Hollywood blockbuster, and cinemas naturally give their screens to whatever sells most tickets. If local films are no longer made, a country's own stories, humour and language gradually disappear from the screen, and young people may come to treat foreign lifestyles as the norm. Although this concern is legitimate, the remedy is support rather than restriction. South Korea long required its cinemas to show domestic films for part of each year, and its film industry is now admired around the world.\n\nForeign films enrich audiences far more than they harm them, and the risk they pose to local culture can be managed. In my opinion, governments should keep their screens open while helping their own film-makers to be seen.",
"b452":"Every education budget is limited, and how it is divided between technology and staff says a good deal about what a school system values. While up-to-date computers clearly have a use, I believe that most of the money should go to recruiting, training and keeping good teachers.\n\nThose who would prioritise computers point to the world pupils will work in. Almost every profession now depends on digital tools, and children who have only used slow, shared machines at school start at a disadvantage, particularly if there is no computer at home. Good equipment also gives a class access to resources that no single school could provide, such as recorded lectures, virtual science experiments and practice exercises that adjust to each pupil's level. For schools in remote areas, this may be the only way to offer certain subjects at all. The case is therefore strongest where technology fills a gap that nothing else can fill.\n\nThe argument for spending on teachers is that equipment achieves little on its own. A laptop cannot notice that a child has stopped trying, explain an idea in a second way when the first has failed, or persuade a reluctant teenager that a subject is worth the effort. These are the things that determine whether pupils learn, and they depend on having enough well-trained staff who are paid enough to stay in the profession. Computers also lose their value quickly. A machine is out of date within a few years, whereas a skilled teacher improves with experience. Even the best software is only as useful as the person who decides how to use it in a lesson.\n\nSchools need working computers, but they need capable teachers more. In my view, technology should be funded once classrooms are properly staffed, since it supports good teaching rather than replacing it.",
"b453":"Some individuals seem to take charge of a group effortlessly, which leads many to assume that leaders are born and cannot be made. Others regard leadership as a craft like any other. While temperament gives certain people an early advantage, I believe the abilities that matter are mostly learned.\n\nThose who see leadership as innate can point to differences that appear in childhood. In almost any playground, a few children organise the games while the rest follow, long before anyone has taught them how. Traits such as confidence, energy and ease with strangers do seem to be partly inherited, and they make a person more likely to be noticed and chosen. This view explains who tends to put themselves forward. It explains less well who succeeds afterwards, since many confident people prove to be poor leaders once they hold responsibility.\n\nThe opposing view holds that effective leadership consists of skills, and skills can be practised. Delegating tasks, giving clear instructions, listening to criticism and making decisions with incomplete information are all learned through experience and feedback. Armies have long worked on this assumption. Officer academies such as Sandhurst take recruits of varied character and train them systematically to command, with considerable success. In business, too, a quiet specialist who is promoted often becomes a capable manager after some years of guidance and mistakes. Natural charm may shorten this process, but it cannot replace it, and a person relying on charm alone is soon found out.\n\nA talent for attracting followers may be present from birth, yet attracting followers is only the beginning of leading them well. In my opinion, the qualities that make a leader effective are developed through training and experience, and are therefore open to far more people than is commonly assumed.",
"b454":"As the planet warms, a disagreement has emerged over where effort should go: into slowing the process or into coping with its effects. I believe this is a false choice, since both are required, but that reducing emissions must remain the priority.\n\nThose who favour adaptation argue from realism. A certain amount of warming is already unavoidable because of gases released over the past two centuries, and international promises to cut emissions have repeatedly fallen short. It therefore seems sensible to spend money on measures whose benefits are local and certain, such as flood barriers, drought-resistant crops and cooler buildings. Bangladesh, for example, has greatly reduced deaths from cyclones by building shelters and warning systems. Such projects protect people now, whatever other countries decide to do. Their weakness is that they treat symptoms, and the cost of treating them rises with every additional degree.\n\nThe argument for fighting climate change begins from that weakness. Adaptation has limits that prevention does not: a sea wall can be raised only so far, and no technology allows farmers to grow food where rain no longer falls. The burden is also uneven, as wealthy nations can afford to defend their coasts while poorer ones, which have contributed least to the problem, cannot. Cutting emissions through cleaner energy and transport addresses the cause and benefits every country at once. Although it demands cooperation that has proved difficult to secure, the alternative is to accept changes that may eventually exceed any society's capacity to adjust.\n\nLearning to live with a warmer climate is necessary, because some change can no longer be prevented. It is not a substitute for limiting that change, however, and in my view governments should adapt where they must while continuing to treat the reduction of emissions as their first duty.",
"b455":"The claim that shoppers simply buy what they need, and that advertising could therefore disappear without loss, assumes that people already know what is on sale. I disagree with this view to a large extent, because need explains why we buy, while advertising often explains what we buy and from whom.\n\nNeed is clearly the starting point of most purchases. A family whose washing machine has broken will replace it whether or not a single advertisement reaches them, and few people have to be persuaded to buy bread or soap. In such cases the decision to spend is made before any marketing is seen. This reasoning only goes so far, however, since knowing that a machine is required does not tell the family which models exist, what they cost or where they are sold. That information has to come from somewhere, and for most consumers it comes from advertising.\n\nThe case for advertising is stronger still when a product is new. People cannot feel a need for something they have not heard of, and a small company with a better or cheaper product has no other way of reaching customers who are loyal to established brands. A new airline offering low fares on a route, for example, can only fill its seats if travellers learn that the service exists. Without promotion of this kind, large firms would keep their customers by default and prices would be likely to rise. Although a good deal of advertising goes beyond information and tries to create wants rather than meet needs, that is an argument for regulating it, not for abandoning it.\n\nNeed may prompt a purchase, but it does not guide one. For that reason I believe advertising remains necessary, both for buyers who want to choose well and for businesses that deserve the chance to be chosen.",
"b456":"Schools differ widely in when they introduce a foreign language: some begin with five-year-olds, while others wait until secondary school. Each approach has its logic, but I believe an early start is preferable, provided that the language is taught by people who speak it well.\n\nAdvocates of an early start emphasise how readily young children absorb sounds. A child of six imitates pronunciation without embarrassment and picks up phrases through songs and games, whereas a teenager often hesitates to speak for fear of sounding foolish in front of classmates. Beginning early also means more years of contact with the language, so that by adolescence it feels familiar instead of foreign. In countries such as the Netherlands, where English is introduced in primary school, most young adults use it comfortably. Early learning does depend, however, on the quality of teaching, because a poorly pronounced model is copied as faithfully as a good one.\n\nThose who favour a later start argue that older pupils learn more efficiently. A twelve-year-old can understand a grammatical rule, use a dictionary and study independently, and may therefore cover in one year what a small child takes several to learn. They add that the first years of school should be devoted to reading and writing in the mother tongue, which is the foundation for everything else. This reasoning has some force, although it treats the two languages as rivals. A few short lessons a week take little time from literacy, and what young children gain from them is not grammar but confidence and a good ear.\n\nOlder learners are faster, but younger ones are bolder and have more time ahead of them. I think languages should therefore be introduced in the early years of primary school, as long as the teachers are properly trained.",
"b457":"International travel is often praised for bringing nations closer, yet crowded resorts and resentful residents suggest a less comfortable picture. Although tourism can cause friction, I disagree that this is its main effect. In most cases it leaves visitors and hosts with a better understanding of each other.\n\nDirect contact is one of the few things that reliably corrects a stereotype. Someone who has stayed in a family-run guesthouse in Morocco, for instance, and shared a meal with its owners, is unlikely to accept crude generalisations about that country afterwards. The effect works in both directions, since hosts also meet foreigners as individuals and not as the figures they see in the news. Such encounters are small, but across a great many journeys they shape how whole populations regard one another. How much is learned depends on the kind of trip, and a tourist who spends a week inside a resort comes home knowing little more than before.\n\nTension does arise where visitor numbers overwhelm a place. In cities such as Venice and Barcelona, residents have protested against crowds, noise and rents pushed up by holiday lettings, and careless behaviour at religious sites causes offence in many countries. These grievances are real. They are directed, however, at the scale and management of tourism rather than at the visitors' nationality, and they rarely affect relations between governments. A local council can respond by limiting numbers or regulating short-term rentals, which is a matter of planning and not of diplomacy. Irritation of this kind is also quite different from hostility towards another culture.\n\nTourism sometimes strains the patience of the communities that receive it, but it seldom sets countries against each other. I believe its more lasting result is familiarity, which makes prejudice harder to sustain.",
"b458":"City authorities faced with jammed roads usually reach first for construction, whether of flyovers, ring roads or metro lines. Others argue that managing demand for travel achieves more than building, and in my view they are closer to the truth, although neither approach is sufficient without the other.\n\nSupporters of infrastructure have a simple and partly sound argument. A growing city needs more capacity, and one whose population has grown sharply cannot function on the streets it had when it was far smaller. New bridges and tunnels remove particular bottlenecks, and an underground railway can carry far more people per hour than any road. The weakness of this view appears when the investment goes mainly into roads. Although extra lanes make driving easier for a time, they encourage more people to drive, and within a few years the wider road is as slow as the old one. Cities such as Houston have widened their motorways repeatedly without ending congestion.\n\nThose who favour other solutions start from this experience. They hold that traffic reflects how people choose to travel, so that choice should be influenced. Charging motorists to enter the centre at busy times, as Stockholm does, cuts traffic quickly and far more cheaply than a new road. Flexible working is a second tool. When staff start at different times or work from home, the morning peak is spread out. Such measures have limits of their own, however. A charge is fair only when drivers have another way to travel, and that alternative, whether a tram line or a bus corridor, is itself infrastructure.\n\nFor this reason, I believe the best answer combines the two views. Investment should go into public transport rather than additional road space, and it should be accompanied by pricing and working arrangements that make leaving the car at home the sensible choice.",
"b459":"Caring for parents in their old age is regarded as a moral duty in most cultures, and some would go further and make it a legal one. I disagree with this proposal, because a law of this kind would be unfair to many families and would do little for the elderly people it is meant to protect.\n\nSupporters of legal responsibility argue that it simply enforces a debt. Parents spend two decades feeding, housing and educating their children, so it seems just that the favour is returned when they become frail or poor. Countries such as China and Singapore already accept this logic and allow neglected parents to claim maintenance from their adult children. While this reasoning has moral force, it assumes that every family is close and every child able to pay.\n\nIn practice, neither assumption holds. Some adults were neglected or mistreated by the very parents they would be compelled to support. A court would have to investigate painful family histories to decide who was exempt, which would be slow and distressing for both sides. Many others are willing but lack the means. A factory worker raising two children in a small flat, for instance, cannot house a mother with dementia or pay for a nursing home, however much he loves her. Forcing him to do so would push a second household into hardship. A more reliable approach is for the state to fund basic care through taxation and to help relatives who do take on the role, with carers' allowances and paid leave.\n\nChildren ought to look after their ageing parents, and most do so without being told. In my view, though, this duty should remain a matter of conscience supported by public services rather than an obligation enforced by law.",
"b460":"It is common to see a child of eight or nine absorbed in a smartphone for most of an evening. The reasons have as much to do with adults as with children, and I believe the habit is a negative one when it reaches several hours a day.\n\nChildren spend so long on their phones partly because the applications are built to keep them there. Videos play one after another without a pause, and games reward players for returning every day, so stopping requires self-control that young children have not yet developed. A child who intends to watch one short clip can easily watch a dozen. Parents contribute as well. A phone is an easy way to keep a child quiet in a restaurant or on a long journey, and although this begins as an occasional solution, it can become a routine. Adults who are often on their own devices also set an example that children copy, and they are in a weak position to impose limits.\n\nThe effects of such heavy use are mostly harmful. Time on a screen is taken from sleep, homework, sport and conversation with the family, all of which a growing child needs. Late-night scrolling, for instance, leaves pupils tired and inattentive in lessons the next morning. Content matters too. Children may come across material unsuitable for their age, and those on social media tend to compare themselves unfavourably with others. A phone can of course be used well, to read or to practise a language, and moderate use does little harm. Hours every day, however, leave too little room for anything else.\n\nChildren are drawn to smartphones by clever design and convenient parenting. The result, in my view, is damaging at this level of use, and limits set by parents are the most effective remedy.",
"b461":"As urban populations expand, planners must decide whether new homes should rise above existing streets or spread across the surrounding land. Both approaches provide housing, but I believe that building upwards is the better solution for most large cities, if it is planned with care.\n\nThe main strength of tall buildings is that they keep a city compact. When thousands of households live within a small area, it becomes worthwhile to run frequent trains and buses, and shops, schools and clinics can be reached on foot. Singapore, for example, houses most of its residents in high-rise estates built around metro stations, and many of them manage without a car. Compactness also spares the farmland and forest at the city's edge. These gains depend on what accompanies the towers, however, since blocks built without parks, daylight or reliable lifts become places that people wish to leave.\n\nBuilding outwards has an obvious appeal, because it offers what many families want: a house with a garden and some distance from the neighbours. Land on the fringe is also cheaper, so homes can be built quickly and sold at lower prices. The difficulty is that the costs appear later and are borne by others. Low-density suburbs are too thinly populated to support good public transport, which leaves residents dependent on cars and adds to congestion on the roads into the centre. Pipes and roads must be extended over long distances, and the countryside lost to them cannot be recovered. Although expansion may suit a small town with land to spare, it serves a city of several million poorly.\n\nFor large and growing cities, then, I think height is the wiser choice, since it houses more people on less land and keeps daily life within reach. It succeeds, though, only where towers come with green space and good transport.",
"b462":"Each year, large numbers of young people leave their own countries to take degrees elsewhere, often at considerable expense to their families or governments. It is sometimes claimed that the home country gains greatly from this. I agree to a large extent, although the benefit depends on whether these students eventually return.\n\nThe main gain for the home country is expertise that it could not easily produce itself. A student who trains at a leading overseas university learns current methods in fields such as medicine or engineering, and brings back not only knowledge but also ways of working and professional contacts. Returning graduates often go on to teach, to set up companies or to reform the institutions they join, so that what one person learned abroad spreads to many who stayed at home. South Korea and China, for instance, have drawn heavily on returning scientists and engineers in building their technology industries.\n\nThese benefits are not automatic, however. Many of the most able students are offered good jobs in the country where they studied and decide to stay. The home country has then paid for their schooling and lost their working lives. Poorer nations suffer most from this, since the gap in salaries is widest for them. Even so, the loss is rarely total. Emigrants send money to their families, invest in businesses at home, and sometimes return later in their careers with experience and savings. Although governments cannot compel anyone to return, they can improve the odds by attaching a period of service at home to the scholarships they award.\n\nSending students overseas is therefore an investment with a real risk attached. I believe it brings substantial benefits to the home country, but only where there are good reasons for graduates to come back.",
"b463":"When crime rises, the demand for more officers on patrol is usually the first to be heard. Visible policing does have an effect, but I disagree that it is the best way to reduce crime, since it reaches only certain offences and none of their causes.\n\nPatrols work well against crime that happens in public. An officer on foot in a shopping street or outside a railway station makes pickpockets, vandals and violent drunks think twice, and residents feel safer when they can see the police. The effect is strongest when officers are concentrated in the few locations where trouble is known to occur rather than spread thinly across a city. Even then, the benefit is partly local. Some offenders move to the next district, and the calm lasts only as long as the patrols do, which makes this an expensive form of protection to maintain.\n\nA large part of modern crime, meanwhile, is beyond the reach of any patrol. Domestic violence takes place behind closed doors, and fraud is now committed largely online, for instance through fake messages that persuade victims to transfer their savings. Dealing with such offences requires detectives, specialists in digital evidence and social workers, not uniformed officers on street corners. More fundamentally, policing responds to people who have already decided to break the law. Drug addiction, unemployment among young men and failing schools produce new offenders each year, and unless these conditions are addressed, extra police will merely be kept busy.\n\nAdditional officers can make particular streets safer, and they deserve a place in any plan. On balance, though, I think that a lasting reduction in crime depends more on skilled investigation and on tackling the conditions that lead people to offend.",
"b464":"At fifteen, a pupil in one country may already be studying only three or four subjects, while a pupil of the same age elsewhere is still taking ten. Early specialisation produces depth, yet I believe a broad curriculum serves most teenagers better until they are close to leaving school.\n\nSystems that specialise early do so in order to prepare students thoroughly for what comes next. A fifteen-year-old who drops other subjects to concentrate on mathematics and the sciences reaches a much higher level in them by eighteen, and can begin a university course without a preliminary year. Universities can teach faster, since they need not repeat school material. This is the logic behind qualifications such as A-levels in England. It works well for those who know what they want, though far fewer do at that age than the system assumes.\n\nThe case for breadth rests on how little teenagers know about themselves and about the working world. Interests change between fifteen and twenty, and a pupil who gave up languages or science at fifteen may find that door closed when a new ambition appears. A wide curriculum postpones the decision until it can be made with better information. It also produces adults who can follow a scientific argument and write a clear paragraph, whatever their profession. A lawyer who cannot read a graph, for instance, is at a disadvantage when a case turns on financial evidence. Although the most able specialists progress more slowly under such a system, this cost can be reduced by letting older pupils spend more of their time on the subjects they prefer.\n\nDepth matters, but it can be acquired later, whereas a subject abandoned at fifteen is seldom taken up again. For this reason I think schools should keep the curriculum wide and allow gradual specialisation only in the final years.",
"b465":"Many of the world's languages are spoken only by small communities and may disappear within a few generations unless they receive support. Whether public money should be spent on keeping them alive is disputed, but I believe a modest investment is justified.\n\nOpponents of such spending point to the other demands on a national budget. Hospitals, roads and schools benefit the whole population, whereas a language with a few thousand speakers serves very few people. They add that economic opportunity lies in the national language, which is why parents in minority communities often encourage their children to master it. On this view, a government cannot keep a language alive once its own speakers have stopped using it. This last point has some force, although it overlooks the fact that many communities abandoned their languages under pressure and not by choice.\n\nThose in favour of investment argue that a language is more than a means of communication. It carries songs, oral history and detailed knowledge of the local landscape, and when its last fluent speaker dies, much of this is lost for good. The measures required are relatively cheap, such as recording elderly speakers, training teachers and offering some primary lessons in the mother tongue. Wales shows that these measures can work, since schooling and broadcasting in Welsh have helped to bring the language back into daily use. Support of this kind succeeds only where the community itself wants it, and it should accompany the national language rather than replace it.\n\nThe cost of preserving a minority language is small beside what disappears with it. Where speakers are willing to take part, funding such efforts is, in my view, a sound use of public resources.",
"b466":"Recent disruptions to global trade have revived the old idea that a nation should feed itself from its own farms. I agree that every country needs a secure domestic supply of basic foods, but in my view cutting imports to a minimum would leave most populations worse off.\n\nSelf-reliance in food is essentially a matter of security. A country that depends on foreign suppliers for its staple foods is exposed whenever a war, a poor harvest or an export ban interrupts deliveries. When grain shipments from Ukraine were blocked in 2022, for example, bread prices rose sharply in several importing countries in North Africa and the Middle East. Poorer households, which spend much of their income on food, were hit hardest. These benefits are real, although they apply mainly to essentials such as rice, wheat and cooking oil, not to every item on a supermarket shelf.\n\nBeyond those essentials, a policy of importing as little as possible would be expensive and, for many nations, impossible. Climate and land decide what can be grown efficiently. Britain could produce bananas or coffee only in heated greenhouses at great cost, while a small, crowded state such as Singapore has almost no farmland at all. Trade allows each country to buy what others grow more cheaply, which lowers prices and gives consumers a varied diet throughout the year. It is also a form of insurance. A nation that relies wholly on its own harvest has nowhere to turn when drought or flooding ruins it, whereas one with several trading partners can make up the shortfall.\n\nA sensible government will protect its capacity to grow staple crops and keep reserves for emergencies. I believe, however, that treating imports as something to be minimised confuses security with isolation, and that a mix of home production and varied trade serves people best.",
"b467":"Comparing a comedian with a chemist may seem strange, yet both are sometimes said to serve society equally well. I recognise the real value of entertainers, but I disagree that their contribution matches that of scientists.\n\nEntertainment answers a need that should not be dismissed. People cannot work without rest, and a good film, concert or football match gives them pleasure, relief from routine and something to share with others. During periods of hardship this matters a great deal. In a long lockdown, for instance, many people relied on music, television drama and comedy to stay cheerful and feel connected. The best performers do more than amuse. A powerful play or song can change how an audience sees a social problem, and it often reaches people who would not read a report on the subject.\n\nThe work of scientists, however, affects life at a more basic level. Vaccines, clean water, electricity and reliable food supplies all rest on research, and without them there would be little leisure in which to enjoy a performance. Scientific knowledge is also cumulative. A discovery about a disease remains useful to every later generation, whereas even a brilliant show is enjoyed and then replaced by the next one. Each finding also becomes the starting point for further work, so the benefit grows over time rather than fading. Although an individual scientist may add less to public happiness than a famous singer, the profession as a whole could not be removed without society ceasing to function.\n\nA world without entertainers would be dull and poorer in spirit, while a world without scientists would be dangerous and short of necessities. For this reason, I believe both deserve respect, but they are not of equal importance.",
"b468":"Whether boys and girls should be taught together is an old argument that has not disappeared, even though mixed schools are now the norm in most countries. Single-sex education has some real attractions, but in my view co-educational schools prepare young people better for adult life.\n\nSupporters of separate schools claim that pupils concentrate better without the other sex present. Teenagers are self-conscious, and some are less willing to answer a question or attempt something difficult when they are worried about how they appear. It is also said that subject choices are freer. In a girls' school, for example, physics is simply a subject and not a boys' subject, and the same is true of languages or drama in a boys' school. These are genuine advantages, although they owe a good deal to the fact that many single-sex schools are selective and well funded.\n\nThe argument for mixed schools is that education involves more than examination results. Men and women study together at university, work together and raise families together, and school is the natural place to learn to treat one another as colleagues and friends. Pupils who have spent years in mixed classes tend to find this ordinary, while those who have not may arrive at eighteen with little idea of how the other half of their generation thinks. Mixed schools are admittedly not free of distraction or stereotyping. These problems are better addressed by good teaching, however, than by keeping the two groups apart.\n\nSingle-sex schools can give some pupils confidence, and parents should be free to choose them. Even so, I believe that learning alongside the opposite sex is itself an important part of education, and that mixed schools should remain the standard.",
"b469":"Advertising reaches people on their phones, on their streets and in their homes, so it is natural to ask whom it serves. Some regard it as useful to both companies and customers, whereas others see mainly harm. In my view the benefits are greater, although they are shared less evenly than its supporters suggest.\n\nThose who defend advertising point first to what it does for firms. A business that cannot make itself known cannot grow, and this is especially true of small or recently founded companies. A family restaurant that promotes a lunch offer on social media, for instance, may fill tables that would otherwise stand empty. Individuals gain as well, because advertisements tell them what is available and at what price, which makes comparison easier. It also pays for much of what they enjoy without charge, including television channels, websites and local newspapers.\n\nCritics reply that these gains come at a cost. Advertisements are designed to persuade rather than to inform, often by suggesting that happiness, popularity or beauty can be bought. People who are constantly shown things they do not own may feel dissatisfied, and some borrow money to keep up. Businesses can suffer too, since a firm that must spend heavily on promotion to match its rivals passes that expense on to its customers. Smaller companies that cannot afford such spending may lose sales whatever the quality of their products. These objections carry most weight where the audience is young or the product is harmful, such as gambling. They are less convincing, however, for an adult choosing between two brands of car.\n\nBoth sides are describing something real. On balance, however, I think advertising does more good than harm to companies and the public alike, provided that the law restricts it where people are least able to resist.",
"b470":"Booking a medical appointment or paying a bill increasingly requires a smartphone, and many people in their seventies and eighties find these devices hard to manage. Their difficulty has understandable causes, and it can be reduced by better teaching and more considerate design.\n\nThe main cause is that older people met this technology late in life. Those who are now retired spent their working years with paper, telephones and face-to-face service, so they lack the background knowledge that younger users absorbed gradually. Ideas that seem self-evident to a teenager, such as swiping, icons or the difference between an application and a website, have to be learned from the beginning. Physical changes add to the problem. Small text is hard to read with weaker eyesight, and touch screens respond poorly to unsteady fingers. A person who repeatedly presses the wrong button may conclude that the device is beyond them and stop trying.\n\nBecause the causes are a lack of familiarity rather than a lack of ability, patient instruction is the most effective remedy. Community centres and libraries can run small classes in which older people practise tasks they really need, for example making a video call to a grandchild or ordering a prescription. Relatives can help too, provided that they explain each step slowly instead of simply doing it themselves. Design matters as well. Phones with larger buttons and simpler menus already exist, and official websites could follow their example. Although these measures will help many, some people will remain unable to cope, so banks and government offices should keep a telephone or counter service available.\n\nOlder people struggle with technology because it arrived after their habits were formed and was not designed for them. With training, simpler devices and continued offline services, most of this difficulty can be overcome.",
"b471":"Working couples with young children have to decide who will look after them during the day, and the choice usually falls between a nursery and a relative. Each has clear merits, but I believe a good childcare centre is the better option once a child is about three, with grandparents in a supporting role.\n\nParents who favour childcare centres value what trained staff and other children can offer. A nursery teacher knows which games develop speech, coordination and early number skills, and plans the day around them. Just as importantly, a child in a large group learns to share toys, wait for a turn and settle disagreements. These habits are hard to practise with one adult at home, and they ease the move to primary school. Despite these strengths, individual attention is limited. One adult responsible for many toddlers cannot comfort each of them promptly, and fees in cities such as London or Sydney can consume much of a salary.\n\nThose who prefer grandparents point to affection and trust. A grandmother cares for one or two children she loves, in a familiar home, and can adapt to a child who is unwell or simply tired. Such security makes a child calmer and readier to explore. Family care also passes on language and customs, which matters greatly to migrant families, for example. A grandfather who speaks the family's first language to a toddler offers something a nursery rarely can. Family care has clear limits, though. Older relatives may lack the energy to keep up with an active four-year-old, and sometimes follow rules that conflict with the parents' own.\n\nBoth forms of care serve children well at different stages. In my view, relatives are ideal for babies, while a nursery prepares older pre-school children better, and families fortunate enough to have both should combine them.",
"b472":"Some people devote years to discovering who their ancestors were, whereas others regard this as time taken from the living. Both attitudes are understandable, and I believe they conflict less than they appear to, because knowledge of the past is most valuable when it is passed on to those who come next.\n\nAdvocates of family research see it as a way of understanding themselves. Knowing that one's grandparents survived a war or left their village to find work explains a great deal about a family's values, habits and even its silences. There can be practical benefits as well. A record of conditions such as heart disease or diabetes among earlier relatives helps doctors assess the risks faced by their descendants. Unless someone collects these stories and facts, they disappear when the oldest members of a family die.\n\nCritics reply that time and attention are limited and should go to people who can still benefit from them. A father who spends his weekends in archives is not spending them with his children, and no discovery about the eighteenth century will pay for their education or protect the environment they inherit. From this point of view, the past cannot be changed, while the future depends on what is done now. This argument is strong when research becomes an obsession. It is less convincing against a moderate interest, since an evening spent recording a grandmother's memories, for instance, takes little away from anyone and gives her grandchildren something they will value later.\n\nThe needs of present and future generations should come first, as they are the only ones that can still be met. Even so, I think a modest effort to preserve a family's history is itself a gift to those generations and not a distraction from them.",
"b473":"Crime has been part of every known society, which leads some people to assume that governments can do little to prevent it. Others maintain that far more could be done. I believe the second group is right, because crime levels respond clearly to the conditions people live in.\n\nThose who are pessimistic point to human nature. Greed, jealousy and anger are not created by governments and cannot be legislated away, so there will be people who steal or strike out whatever the law says. They also note that prevention has a disappointing record: harsher sentences, public campaigns and new police powers have often been introduced with great confidence and little result. This scepticism is reasonable as far as it goes. It shows that crime cannot be abolished, however, not that it cannot be reduced, and the difference between those two claims is large.\n\nThe case for prevention begins with the fact that crime varies greatly from one city to another and from one decade to the next. Human nature is the same everywhere, so such differences must come from circumstances, and circumstances can be changed. Glasgow, for example, was long known for knife violence, and it became considerably safer after police, teachers and health workers began working together with young men at risk. Simpler measures help as well, including better street lighting, secure locks and treatment for drug users who steal to fund their habit. None of these is cheap or quick, and success depends on sustained effort rather than a single law, but the results are real.\n\nAlthough no society will be free of crime, the experience of safer cities shows that prevention works where it is taken seriously. Much more can be done, in my view, and pessimism should not become an excuse for inaction.",
"b474":"On a car-free day, a city closes its centre to private vehicles and invites residents to walk, cycle or take the bus instead. Supporters see this as a practical way of cleaning the air, but I agree with those who regard it mainly as a useful demonstration and think that permanent measures achieve far more.\n\nThe argument for car-free days is that their effect is immediate and visible. When traffic disappears from the main streets, levels of exhaust fumes fall within hours, and residents experience a quieter, cleaner city. Bogotá, for example, has held such a day annually for many years, and the event has helped to build support for its cycle routes and bus network. This shows where the real value lies. A single day changes attitudes and may persuade some drivers to try another way of getting to work. It does little for the air on the remaining days of the year, when the cars return.\n\nCritics therefore argue that pollution must be tackled by measures that operate continuously. A charge for driving into the centre, such as the one London has applied for two decades, discourages unnecessary journeys every working day rather than once a year. The money raised can also be spent on buses and trains, which gives drivers a realistic alternative. Stricter emission standards are the other main tool. They reduce the fumes from each vehicle that remains on the road, and they reach sources that a car-free day ignores, including lorries and buses with old diesel engines. Although these policies are more expensive and slower to introduce than an occasional street closure, their benefits accumulate.\n\nCar-free days are worth holding, since they let people see what a less congested city could be like. Lasting improvements in air quality, I believe, depend on pricing, regulation and better public transport.",
"b475":"When a child breaks the law, it is not obvious who should answer for it: the child who acted or the adults who raised him. In my opinion, young offenders should face consequences suited to their age, while parents should be held responsible only where their own neglect contributed.\n\nPunishing the child rests on a simple principle, which is that people learn responsibility by answering for their own actions. A fourteen-year-old who steals a bicycle knows that it is wrong, and if someone else pays the penalty, he learns that offending is free. Victims also expect the person who harmed them to make amends. A fine paid by a parent does not give them that, whereas an apology from the offender often does. The punishment should fit a young person, however. Unpaid work teaches more than a prison cell, and very young children, who cannot yet understand the harm they cause, need guidance rather than penalties.\n\nThe argument for punishing parents is that children are largely what their upbringing makes them. Adults who let a child roam the streets at night, or who ignore repeated warnings from the school, have failed in a duty, and a sanction may force them to take it seriously. In some countries, for instance, courts can fine parents whose children persistently miss school or cause damage. This approach has clear limits. Many parents of young offenders are doing their best in difficult circumstances, and a fine takes money from a household that may already be struggling. A determined teenager can also deceive the most careful mother or father.\n\nResponsibility for a child's crime is shared, but it is not shared equally in every case. I believe the young person should bear an age-appropriate penalty, and that parents should be supported first and punished only when they have plainly neglected their role.",
"b476":"Most people rely on two circles of support, the relatives they were born among and the friends they have chosen. I agree that family generally matters more, chiefly because its commitment is more durable, but friends meet needs that relatives often cannot.\n\nWhat sets family apart is that its bonds do not depend on convenience. Friendships are usually built on shared circumstances, such as a classroom, an office or a hobby, and many fade when those circumstances end. Parents, brothers and sisters remain connected whatever happens. This permanence shows most clearly in a crisis. Someone who loses a job or falls seriously ill can normally expect relatives to offer a room, money or months of care. Even a close friend, by contrast, has a household of his or her own to put first. A friend's help is therefore real but usually more limited. This is not true of every home, admittedly, and people raised in neglectful or violent families may depend on friends instead.\n\nFriends, however, provide something different in kind. They are chosen for shared interests and outlook, so a conversation with them can be freer than one with a parent who still sees an adult as a child. A teenager worried about a relationship, for example, will often confide in a classmate before a mother or father. Friends also widen a person's world by introducing new ideas, tastes and opportunities that a family, being similar by nature, rarely supplies. A colleague may suggest a career that no relative had considered, or a flatmate may introduce a sport, a cuisine or a political view that is new.\n\nA full life needs both kinds of relationship, and neither substitutes for the other. When the two are weighed, though, I believe family carries more weight for most people, since its loyalty is the least conditional.",
"b477":"Factories and power stations are responsible for much of the pollution in the air and water, and there is disagreement about how governments should make them cleaner. Some favour higher taxes on industry, while others prefer different methods. I believe a tax works well when it is charged on pollution itself, but that it needs to be supported by regulation.\n\nThose who support taxation argue that companies respond to costs more reliably than to appeals. If a firm must pay for every tonne of carbon or waste it releases, cleaner equipment that once looked too expensive becomes the cheaper choice. This reasoning is sound, provided that the tax is tied to emissions. A general increase in the tax paid by an industry gives a careful producer no advantage over a careless one, and both may simply pass the cost on to customers.\n\nCritics of this approach believe that other measures achieve more. Legal limits, backed by inspections and fines, can prohibit the most dangerous practices outright, which matters when a substance is too harmful to be permitted at any price. Mercury discharged into a river, for instance, should be banned, not taxed. Governments can also offer grants or cheap loans for clean technology. Many smaller firms want to improve but lack the capital to replace old boilers or filters, and for them a tax only adds to costs they cannot escape. These alternatives have their own weakness, however, since standards and subsidies are costly to administer and give companies no reason to go beyond the minimum required.\n\nNeither approach is sufficient alone. In my view, a tax on emissions should be the main instrument, because it rewards every reduction, while strict legal limits deal with the pollutants that are too dangerous to tolerate.",
"b478":"In many countries the age at which people leave work is rising, sometimes by choice and sometimes by necessity. I agree that it is a good idea for older people to carry on working when they are able and willing to, provided that the decision remains theirs.\n\nRemaining in work tends to benefit older people themselves. A job supplies more than a salary. It gives structure to the week, regular contact with other people and the sense of being useful, all of which are easily lost on retirement. A retired teacher who continues to tutor a few pupils, for instance, often remains more active and more cheerful than a former colleague who stopped abruptly. Without such commitments, the days can lose their shape, and loneliness becomes a real risk. These benefits depend on the kind of work, however. Heavy manual labour, such as construction or farm work, can harm an ageing body, and people in those occupations have good reason to stop earlier.\n\nSociety gains as well. Experienced employees carry knowledge that cannot be found in manuals, and they are well placed to train younger colleagues. In ageing countries such as Japan, where the workforce is shrinking, companies increasingly rely on staff in their late sixties to fill positions that would otherwise be empty. Public finances benefit too. Each additional working year means taxes paid and pension payments delayed, which matters when fewer workers must support a growing number of pensioners. The fear that older employees block the way for the young seems to me overstated, since the two groups rarely compete for the same roles.\n\nWork in later life is good for individuals, for employers and for the public purse when health permits it. I believe it deserves encouragement, as long as it remains a choice and not an obligation.",
"b479":"Children's eating habits are formed early and tend to last, so it matters a great deal who shapes them. Some regard school as the most effective place to promote good nutrition, while others see this as a task for parents. I believe the home has the greater influence, though schools can strengthen what families begin.\n\nThose who favour schools point out that they reach nearly all children, whatever their background. A canteen that serves vegetables, fruit and water in place of chips and fizzy drinks guarantees at least one balanced meal on each school day. Children are also more willing to try unfamiliar food when classmates are eating it. Lessons help as well. Teaching pupils what the body needs gives them knowledge some would not acquire at home. In Japan, for instance, pupils eat the same freshly cooked lunch together and learn about its ingredients. Although such schemes are valuable, school provides only one meal a day for part of the year.\n\nParents, by contrast, control most of what a child eats and set the example. Children copy what they see. If the adults at home cook regularly, eat together at the table and treat sweets as an occasional pleasure, these routines come to seem normal long before a child can understand a lesson on vitamins. A pupil who learns about healthy eating in class but returns to a kitchen stocked with snacks receives two conflicting messages, and the daily one usually wins. Not all parents have the time, money or knowledge to do this well, however, which is exactly why schools remain important.\n\nBoth settings matter, but they are not equal. In my view, parents lay the foundation of a child's diet through daily example, and the best that schools can do is to support families and to protect children whose homes provide less guidance.",
"b480":"A century ago most people bought what the local shop stocked, did the work their parents had done and married someone from nearby. Today almost every decision, from breakfast cereal to career, comes with a long list of alternatives. I agree that this abundance has gone too far in small matters, but I do not think it is excessive where it counts.\n\nIn everyday consumption, choice has clearly passed the point of usefulness. A shopper looking for toothpaste, for example, may face a whole shelf of products that differ only in packaging and in claims that are hard to verify. Comparing them takes time and attention that could be better spent, and whatever is chosen, the buyer is left wondering whether another option was better. The same is true of streaming services, where an evening can be lost scrolling through titles. Such variety benefits the companies that compete for attention rather than the people who must sort through it.\n\nThe picture changes when the decisions are important ones. The freedom to choose a field of study, an occupation, a place to live or a partner is not a burden to be reduced, even though it can cause anxiety. Earlier generations, and women in particular, were often denied these choices, and few would wish to return to that position. Having many options here means that a person's life can fit his or her abilities and wishes instead of the accident of birth. Although such decisions are difficult, the difficulty is the price of something valuable.\n\nWe do have more options than we need on supermarket shelves and screens, and I agree that they waste our time. The wider choices modern life offers about how to live, however, are in my view a gain that should not be confused with clutter.",
"b481":"Parents who can afford it often hire a private tutor in the belief that individual attention is the surest route to progress. Others maintain that people learn best in the company of fellow learners. I believe that group lessons are the better setting for most learning, although individual tuition is hard to match when a learner has a specific difficulty.\n\nThe strength of one-on-one teaching is that it wastes no time. The tutor sees at once what the learner has misunderstood, goes back over it, and moves on only when it is secure, so that the pace suits the student exactly. Nothing is taught that is already known. A child who has fallen behind in fractions, for example, can often catch up in a few weeks of private lessons after a year of confusion in class. Such teaching is expensive, however, and its intensity is tiring over long periods.\n\nGroup lessons offer something that a single tutor cannot supply, which is other learners. Students hear questions they had not thought to ask, compare their answers and explain ideas to one another, and explaining is one of the most effective ways of understanding. Subjects that depend on discussion or cooperation, such as literature, languages and team sports, can hardly be taught otherwise. A class also keeps people going. Friendly competition and a sense of shared effort bring learners back each week when their enthusiasm fades, whereas a student working alone can easily give up. These advantages weaken as groups grow, because in a very large class the quieter members receive little attention and may fall behind unnoticed.\n\nEach format does something the other cannot. For everyday learning, though, I think a group of moderate size is superior, with individual lessons kept for the moments when a student is stuck.",
"b482":"Professional athletes are among the most closely watched people in the world, and children copy what they see. Some regard these sportspeople as admirable examples, while others think their conduct sets a poor standard. I believe the influence is positive on the whole, though it comes from what athletes do in training more than from how they live.\n\nSupporters of the first view point to the nature of sporting success. Reaching the top of tennis or athletics is not a matter of luck, and young fans can see that years of practice, sacrifice and patience lie behind each medal. An athlete who returns from a serious injury, for example, shows a teenager that setbacks can be overcome. Lessons of this kind are more persuasive when they come from a hero than from a teacher, provided that the hero behaves as well as he or she plays.\n\nCritics have plenty of evidence as well. On the field, footballers dive to win penalties and shout abuse at referees. Children repeat this behaviour in school matches the following week, because it appears to be what winning requires. Off the field, some stars make headlines for drunken incidents, reckless spending or doping. When such players keep their contracts and their sponsors, the lesson seems to be that talent excuses bad conduct. These criticisms are justified, although they concern a visible minority. Most professionals train quietly and attract little publicity, precisely because good behaviour is not news.\n\nAthletes are not chosen for their character, and not all of them will be models of virtue. In my view, what young people can usefully take from them is the dedication their careers demand, and in that respect the example they set is a good one.",
"b483":"The sight of a group of friends sitting together in silence, each looking at a phone, has convinced many observers that smartphones are ruining social life. In my view this is an exaggeration, because these devices have changed the way people interact far more than they have reduced it.\n\nSmartphones have made it much easier to stay in contact. People now exchange messages with friends and relatives throughout the day, share photographs within seconds and remain part of each other's lives after moving to another city or country. A grandmother who sees her grandchildren on a video call each week, for instance, has a closer relationship with them than letters would have allowed. Online contact is admittedly thinner than time spent in the same room. It supports existing friendships well, but it is a poor way of forming deep new ones.\n\nWhere the critics are right is in describing what happens when a phone intrudes on company. A person who glances at a screen during a meal signals that the conversation is not worth his full attention, and the other person usually responds by saying less. Repeated often enough, this makes time together shallower. The habit is strongest among young people, some of whom find it more comfortable to type than to talk. This damage is caused by manners, however, and manners can change. Many families already keep phones away from the table, and friends increasingly agree to put them aside when they meet.\n\nSmartphones have multiplied the contact people have with one another while sometimes lowering the quality of their time together. To say that they are destroying social interaction goes too far, I think, since the harm they cause comes from habits that are easily corrected.",
"b484":"Spectators often explain a champion's victories by pointing to natural gifts such as speed, height or strength. Others maintain that qualities of mind and preparation matter more. I believe physical ability is the entry requirement for elite sport, but that it is rarely what separates the winner from the rest.\n\nIt would be foolish to deny the role of the body. Certain sports reward particular builds so heavily that no amount of effort can compensate for lacking them. Professional basketball is dominated by exceptionally tall players, for example, and the best distance runners tend to be light with a large lung capacity. At school level, the child who is naturally quick or strong wins races with little training, which is why talent seems decisive to casual observers. As athletes rise through the ranks, however, this advantage shrinks, because at the top nearly all competitors are physically gifted.\n\nWhat distinguishes champions at that level is largely mental. One factor is determination. Years of disciplined practice turn raw ability into reliable skill, and only athletes with unusual persistence can sustain such a routine through injury and defeat. Composure matters as much. A tennis final or a penalty shoot-out is often decided by who can think clearly under pressure rather than by who is stronger. A player who has prepared for such moments, for instance by rehearsing penalties in front of a noisy crowd, is less likely to freeze. Neither quality can create a champion from someone without the basic physical capacity, although together they explain why many gifted youngsters do not fulfil their promise.\n\nPhysical talent opens the door to sporting success, and without it the highest levels are out of reach. Among those who possess it, though, I believe dedication and mental strength are the more important factors.",
"b485":"Most adults can recall both a teacher they feared and a teacher they liked, and can say which of them made them work harder. The answers differ, which is why this debate continues. In my view, warmth motivates students more reliably than severity, but only when it is combined with firm expectations.\n\nPeople who favour strictness argue that young learners do not work hard of their own accord. A teacher who insists on silence, punctual homework and careful presentation creates a classroom in which effort is normal and time is not lost to disruption. Pupils in such a class know exactly what is required of them, and many take pride in meeting a demanding standard. The difficulty is that strictness motivates through fear of consequences. It works while the teacher is watching, yet it seldom produces any wish to study the subject once the pressure is removed, and anxious pupils may stop asking questions altogether.\n\nFriendliness is defended on different grounds, namely that students work hardest for people they respect and trust. A teacher who knows pupils by name, shows interest in their ideas and treats mistakes as a normal part of learning makes them willing to attempt difficult tasks. A teenager who is weak at writing, for instance, is far more likely to hand in a draft to someone who will respond with encouragement than to someone who will ridicule it. Friendliness has its own risk, though. A teacher who wants above all to be liked may accept poor work and lose control of the room, and pupils quickly cease to take such a teacher seriously.\n\nThe most effective teachers are neither harsh nor indulgent. I believe that a friendly manner is the better source of motivation, provided that it rests on clear rules and high standards that pupils are expected to meet.",
"b486":"For most of the last century, the morning paper was how people found out what had happened in the world. Television, radio and above all the internet have since challenged that position. Although newspapers retain certain strengths, I believe these newer media are now the more effective way to follow the news.\n\nThose who remain loyal to newspapers value the care that goes into them. An article printed in a respected daily has usually been checked by an editor, and the writer has had several hours to establish what happened and why it matters. Readers are also given a sense of proportion. The layout of a page shows which stories the editors consider most significant, so a reader with ten minutes to spare knows where to begin. These are real merits. They come at the price of speed, however, and a paper printed at midnight cannot describe what happened at dawn.\n\nSupporters of other media argue that news is, by definition, about what is new. A website or television channel can report an earthquake or an election result within minutes and correct its account as the facts become clearer. These media can also show events, not merely describe them. Footage of a flooded town, for instance, conveys the scale of a disaster in a way that a column of text struggles to match. Their weakness is that speed invites error, and rumours spread easily on social media unless users take care over which sources they trust.\n\nNewspapers still offer considered writing, and I would not wish to see them disappear. As a way of learning what is happening, though, I think broadcast and online media serve people better, because they are quicker and more vivid.",
"b487":"Secondary schools are judged mainly on examination results, and activities that do not lead to a grade are easily squeezed out. Requiring teenagers to do unpaid work in their communities would be one way of correcting this imbalance. I agree that such service should be compulsory, as long as it is modest in scale and students have some choice over what they do.\n\nThe main reason for making service compulsory is that it teaches what classrooms cannot. A sixteen-year-old who helps at a food bank each week, for example, or coaches younger children at football, has to turn up on time, follow instructions and deal with people of different ages and backgrounds. Such habits are difficult to acquire from a textbook. If the scheme were voluntary, it would tend to attract those who are already public-spirited and miss the pupils who would gain most.\n\nThe usual objection is that work done under compulsion is not really volunteering and may be done resentfully. Although this objection has some force, it applies mainly to badly run schemes, in which pupils are simply sent to pick up litter and learn more cynicism than goodwill. Workload is a further concern for students who already face heavy examinations or have part-time jobs. Both problems can be reduced by good design. If schools offer a range of placements, keep the hours low and schedule them within the school week rather than at weekends, most pupils will find something they are willing to do.\n\nCommunity service gives young people experience and a sense of responsibility that academic study alone does not. Provided that it is flexible and well organised, I believe it deserves a required place in every high school programme.",
"b488":"When documentaries can show a snow leopard hunting in its own mountains, the old justification for zoos, that people had no other means of seeing exotic animals, has largely disappeared. Even so, I disagree that there is no longer any need for them, because well-run zoos now serve purposes that have little to do with display.\n\nThe most important of these is the protection of species that would otherwise be lost. Many habitats are shrinking so fast that some animals cannot survive in them, however much we might wish them to remain free. Breeding programmes in captivity keep a healthy population alive until it can be returned. The Arabian oryx, for instance, was hunted to extinction in the wild and exists there today only because zoos bred it and released the offspring. Such programmes take years, and the money visitors pay funds them. A film may inform viewers about an endangered animal, but it cannot preserve its genes.\n\nThis defence applies only to institutions that take such work seriously. A great many establishments still keep large, intelligent animals in conditions that cannot meet their needs. Elephants walk long distances every day in the wild, and dolphins range across open sea; confined to a paddock or a tank, they often show signs of distress. Keeping animals in this way purely to attract paying visitors is hard to justify now that the public can learn about wildlife by other means. Such places should close or be converted, and the species that suffer most in captivity should not be held unless their survival depends on it.\n\nThe claim that zoos have outlived their purpose is true of those that exist only to entertain. Zoos devoted to conservation are a different matter, and I believe they will remain necessary for as long as natural habitats continue to disappear.",
"b489":"As more women reach senior positions in business and politics, it is sometimes claimed that they lead better than men do. I disagree with this generalisation, as I believe the qualities of a good leader belong to individuals and not to either sex.\n\nThose who make the claim point to traits often associated with women. Female managers are said to listen more carefully, to consult before deciding and to show greater concern for the wellbeing of their staff. Leaders such as Jacinda Ardern, the former prime minister of New Zealand, have been widely praised for combining firmness with empathy during national emergencies. There may be some truth in the observation, since girls in many cultures are brought up to cooperate while boys are encouraged to compete. Upbringing is not destiny, however, and a tendency across a large group says little about any one person.\n\nOrdinary experience suggests that effective and ineffective leaders are found among both sexes. Most people have worked under a considerate male supervisor and a harsh female one, or the reverse. What distinguishes the good ones is a set of skills that anyone can develop: clear thinking, honesty, the ability to explain a decision and the courage to take an unpopular one. These skills are learned through experience and feedback, not given at birth. The stronger argument for promoting women is a different one. Organisations that draw their leaders from only half the population waste talent, and a mixed leadership team is likely to consider a wider range of views.\n\nWomen deserve equal access to positions of authority, and many will excel in them. When they do, I believe it will be because of their individual abilities, not because their sex makes them better leaders.",
"b490":"Conservation charities and government agencies devote considerable money and publicity to saving threatened wildlife, and some critics feel that the effort has gone too far. I disagree. The resources involved are modest beside the scale of the losses, although I accept that they are not always spent on the right species.\n\nThe claim of excess does not survive a comparison with what is being lost. Populations of wild animals and birds are falling in almost every region as forests are cleared, wetlands drained and farmland sprayed with chemicals. Against these pressures, the sums given to conservation are small, far smaller than what the same governments spend on roads or defence. Nor is this spending a luxury. Insects and birds pollinate crops and control pests, and forests full of wildlife store carbon and regulate rainfall. When such species decline, farmers and taxpayers eventually bear the cost, so protecting them is better understood as maintenance than as charity.\n\nWhere the critics have a point is in the way attention is distributed. Public campaigns concentrate on a few attractive animals, such as the giant panda and the tiger, because these raise donations easily. Meanwhile, less appealing creatures that matter more to the functioning of an ecosystem, including bats, frogs and many insects, receive little help. Large sums can thus be spent on keeping a few famous animals alive in reserves while their wider habitat continues to shrink. This is a reason to direct funds more intelligently, towards protecting whole habitats rather than individual species, but it is not evidence that the total is too high.\n\nEfforts to protect wildlife could certainly be better targeted. Given how quickly the natural world is being depleted and how much human welfare depends on it, however, I believe the present level of commitment is too low rather than too high.",
"b491":"Photographs of film stars on holiday and reports of their marriages, illnesses and quarrels fill newspapers and websites every day. I agree, for the most part, that such private matters should not be published, although there is a narrow class of cases in which the public has a right to know.\n\nFame is earned through a particular piece of work, and it does not follow that the rest of a person's life belongs to the audience. An actress is known for her films, not for her medical history or her children's school. When photographers wait outside a celebrity's home or follow a car through the streets, the result is constant anxiety for the person concerned and for relatives who did not choose public life. Princess Diana, for example, was pursued by photographers for years, and her treatment is widely remembered as a warning. The usual reply is that stars accept this attention as the price of success. That argument is weak, since agreeing to be interviewed about a new album is quite different from agreeing to be watched at a funeral.\n\nThere are, however, situations in which a private matter becomes a public one. If a singer who campaigns against drugs is secretly using them, or a sports star who advertises a charity has been taking money from it, fans are being misled and deserve to be told. What justifies publication in such cases is the link between the private conduct and the public role, rather than mere curiosity. A divorce, a pregnancy or a stay in hospital has no such link, however many readers would like the details.\n\nCelebrities sell their talent, not their families or their health. I believe, therefore, that the media should leave their private lives alone unless disclosure serves a real public interest, which gossip does not.",
"b492":"Anyone who follows current affairs has to decide where to direct limited attention. Many people concentrate on events at home, whereas others argue that what happens abroad deserves greater priority. In my opinion, domestic news is the natural place to start, but it can no longer be understood without the international picture.\n\nThe preference for national news is easy to justify. Decisions made by a person's own government determine the taxes he pays, the schools his children attend and the hospital that treats him. Domestic news is also the only kind on which most citizens can act, since they can vote, protest or write to a local representative. A proposed change to the retirement age, for instance, is something a reader can both feel and influence. Events in a distant country, however serious, seldom offer that opportunity.\n\nThose who emphasise international news reply that borders now matter less than they did. A conflict in an oil-producing region raises fuel prices on the other side of the world, and a virus that appears in one city can close schools on every continent. Citizens who know little about such developments are poorly placed to judge their own leaders' foreign and economic policies. Following world affairs also guards against a narrow outlook. A reader who sees how other societies deal with housing shortages or an ageing population can judge proposals at home with more than local experience. The difficulty is that foreign coverage is often brief and dramatic, so awareness does not necessarily lead to understanding.\n\nThe two kinds of news are less separate than the question implies. I believe a well-informed person should follow national affairs closely while paying steady attention to events abroad, because the second increasingly explains the first.",
"b493":"Dances, festivals and crafts that were once performed for a community are now frequently staged for paying visitors. Critics say this empties them of meaning, while supporters argue that it keeps them alive. In my view tourism can preserve a tradition, but only when the people to whom it belongs remain in control of how it is presented.\n\nThose who fear the effects of tourism have good grounds. Once a ritual becomes a source of income, it tends to be shortened, simplified and timed to suit tour buses. A ceremony that used to mark the harvest once a year may be repeated every evening in a hotel courtyard, and the performers come to regard it as a job. Younger members of the community then grow up seeing it as a show for outsiders. Something important is lost in this process, although \"destroyed\" is perhaps too strong a word, since the songs, costumes and skills themselves do survive.\n\nThe opposing argument begins from the fact that many traditions are disappearing for reasons unrelated to tourism. As young people move to cities, few of them see any reason to spend years learning a craft that cannot support them. Visitors change that calculation. Water puppetry in Hanoi, for example, attracts audiences from abroad every day, and the ticket income allows troupes to train new puppeteers in an art that might otherwise have faded. These benefits depend on local people deciding what to share, what to keep private and how the earnings are used.\n\nCommercial performance does change a tradition, but a changed tradition is better than a forgotten one. Provided that communities set the terms themselves, I believe tourism does more to protect their heritage than to damage it.",
"b494":"Companies spend enormous sums on advertising, yet the people it is aimed at often claim to have stopped noticing it. Both observations contain some truth. I believe, however, that advertising remains highly persuasive, largely because it has changed its methods in response to our inattention.\n\nThere is good reason to think that people filter out much of what they are shown. A commuter passes dozens of posters on the way to work and could probably describe none of them by the time she arrives. Viewers leave the room during commercial breaks, and internet users install software to block banners or simply learn not to look at the edges of the page. When messages arrive from every direction, ignoring them becomes a habit. This is true, however, mainly of advertising of the traditional, interrupting kind, which has lost a good deal of its force.\n\nThose who consider advertising successful can point to the behaviour of firms themselves. Businesses measure their returns carefully, and they would not keep paying for promotion that brought no extra sales. What has changed is the form it takes. Much of it is now directed at individuals on the basis of what they have searched for, so a person who looks up running shoes will see them offered again for days. Other advertising is disguised as recommendation, such as a popular video maker praising a skin cream to followers who trust her. These approaches work precisely because they do not feel like advertisements. Their power is not unlimited, though, since no campaign can rescue a product that disappoints the people who try it.\n\nWe have learned to ignore the billboard, not the message. Advertising, I would argue, still shapes a great deal of what we buy, and it does so most effectively when we fail to recognise it.",
"b495":"A common complaint about education is that it fills students' heads with information and leaves them unable to do anything with it. I agree that this criticism is justified at many schools and universities, but I do not think it applies equally at every level, nor that facts and skills can be neatly separated.\n\nThe complaint is most accurate for the later years of education. Secondary pupils often spend months memorising dates, formulae and definitions for examinations, only to forget most of them soon afterwards. Many leave school unable to manage a budget, speak in public or work effectively in a team. Universities can be little better. A business graduate, for example, may be able to describe several theories of management without having once prepared a set of accounts or negotiated with a client. Employers then have to teach what the degree did not. More project work, placements and assessed presentations would address this gap.\n\nAt primary level, however, the charge is weaker, because the basic facts taught there are themselves the tools of practical life. A child who knows multiplication tables and how words are spelt is acquiring skills, not trivia. It is also a mistake to treat knowledge as the enemy of ability. People cannot think critically about history, or solve an engineering problem, unless they already know a good deal about the subject. A mechanic or a nurse relies on a large store of facts every working day. The real fault lies less in teaching facts than in stopping there, and in testing only whether they can be repeated.\n\nEducation in its later stages does lean too heavily towards information that is memorised and soon lost. In my view, the remedy is not to teach fewer facts but to require students to apply them far more often.",
"b496":"The bicycle is an old invention that has returned to favour, with city authorities from Paris to Hanoi encouraging residents to ride. Its critics point to real drawbacks, but I believe that for short journeys in towns it is one of the best forms of transport available, provided that riders are given safe space on the road.\n\nThe reasons for regarding the bicycle as a modern solution begin with the city itself. A bicycle produces no fumes and almost no noise, and it takes up a fraction of the space a car requires. In a crowded centre this makes it quick, since a cyclist is not held up by the congestion that traps drivers. Health is the other reason. Cycling builds exercise into the daily routine of people who might not otherwise find time for it, so the journey to work replaces a visit to the gym. In Copenhagen, for instance, a large share of residents cycle to work or school.\n\nThose who emphasise the disadvantages are thinking of different conditions. Safety is the most serious concern. A cyclist has no protection in a collision, and on roads shared with lorries and fast traffic a small mistake by a driver can cause severe injury. Weather is the other drawback. Rain, summer heat and steep hills make riding unpleasant, and arriving at an office soaked or exhausted is hardly practical for someone with a meeting to attend. These objections are valid, although the first of them is a failure of road design rather than of the bicycle itself.\n\nCycling will not suit every journey, and it is less attractive in bad weather. Within those limits, I think it deserves its reputation as clean, healthy and efficient, and cities that build protected lanes will find the remaining objections much reduced.",
"b497":"Before a new drug is given to patients, it is normally tried on mice, rabbits or monkeys to find out whether it is safe. Many regard this as a regrettable necessity and others as a wrong that no benefit can excuse. I believe that such testing remains acceptable, but only where no reliable alternative exists.\n\nSupporters of the practice argue that it prevents greater harm. A substance that seems promising in a laboratory dish may damage the liver or the heart of a living body, and this can be discovered only in a whole organism. Without that step, the first to be exposed to an unknown risk would be human volunteers. Vaccines against polio and treatments for diabetes, for example, were developed with the help of animal experiments, and millions of people are alive because of them. This argument justifies testing for serious medical purposes, though not the routine repetition of experiments whose results are already known.\n\nOpponents reply that the ability to suffer, rather than membership of a species, is what matters morally. Laboratory animals feel pain and fear, cannot consent and gain nothing from the procedure. Critics also question its reliability, since a drug that is harmless to a mouse may still injure a human being. If results transfer poorly, much of that suffering yields little knowledge. These points have already led to progress, as computer models and cultures of human cells can now replace animals in some early tests. They cannot yet reproduce a whole body, however, and until they can, a complete ban would bring new medicines close to a halt.\n\nTesting drugs on animals involves real suffering and should not be defended lightly. It is justified for the present, I believe, as a last resort, on condition that researchers use as few animals as possible and that funding for alternatives continues.",
"b498":"People form an impression of a stranger within moments, and clothing is usually the first evidence they use. I agree that dress can reveal something about a person's cultural background, but I disagree that it is a reliable guide to character.\n\nClothing has long been one of the most visible signs of where someone comes from. A sari, a kilt or a Vietnamese ao dai identifies a national tradition at a glance, and items such as a turban or a headscarf often indicate religious belief. Within a single society, what people wear can also point to their generation or to a group they wish to belong to. These signals are weaker than they used to be, however. Jeans, trainers and T-shirts are now worn on every continent, so in daily life dress tells us less about culture than it does at weddings, festivals and other formal occasions.\n\nCharacter is far harder to read. It is tempting to assume that a neatly dressed person is organised or that someone in bright colours is outgoing, and there may sometimes be a loose connection. Clothes are shaped by many other factors, though. Income limits what people can buy, employers impose uniforms and dress codes, and a great many people simply wear what is comfortable. A nurse and a bank clerk, for instance, dress as their jobs require, whatever their temperament. The deeper problem is that qualities such as kindness, honesty and patience show themselves in behaviour over time rather than in a wardrobe. An interviewer who rejects a capable candidate because of an unfashionable suit has learned nothing about that person's ability.\n\nDress offers some clues to cultural background, particularly on traditional occasions, yet it says little that can be trusted about personality. For this reason I agree with the statement only in part.",
"b499":"A cucumber wrapped in plastic film and a small toy sealed inside a large box are familiar sights in modern shops, and someone has to deal with the rubbish they generate. Although shoppers can make a difference at the margins, I believe the main responsibility lies with the companies that design and sell these products.\n\nThe argument for holding retailers and manufacturers responsible is that they make the decisions. Packaging is chosen long before a product reaches the shelf, often to make it look larger or more attractive rather than to protect it. A company that changes one design removes waste from millions of purchases at a stroke, whereas persuading millions of customers to change their habits takes years. Some supermarkets, for instance, now sell loose fruit and vegetables and offer refill stations for rice or detergent. Such changes do carry a cost, and firms are unlikely to make them voluntarily unless regulation or public opinion pushes them.\n\nThat pressure is what the other side has in mind. Those who place the duty on consumers argue that companies supply whatever sells, so heavily packaged goods would disappear if people refused to buy them. There is some truth in this, since shoppers who choose the unwrapped option send a signal that retailers notice. The difficulty is that the choice is often absent. When every brand of biscuits on the shelf comes in a plastic tray inside a wrapper inside a box, the customer can only take it or go without. Nor can those on tight budgets afford to pay more for a greener alternative.\n\nConsumers should use what influence they have, but it is limited by what is offered to them. Responsibility, I think, belongs chiefly to producers and supermarkets, because they alone decide how goods are packaged.",
"b500":"Governments looking for ways to make their populations healthier often consider building more gyms, pools and playing fields. Critics reply that such spending changes little and that the money is better used elsewhere. I think new facilities are worthwhile in places that lack them, but that other measures would do far more.\n\nSupporters of more sports facilities start from a sound premise: regular exercise reduces the risk of heart disease, diabetes and depression, and people cannot exercise without somewhere to do it. In a crowded district with no park or public pool, a new leisure centre removes a real obstacle, especially for teenagers and for families unable to afford private clubs. The weakness of the argument is that provision does not guarantee use. New facilities tend to attract people who are already active, while those most at risk stay away through lack of time, confidence or interest.\n\nThe opposing camp argues that health is determined by more than sport. Diet has at least as great an effect on weight and long-term illness as exercise does, so policies such as taxing sugary drinks or improving school meals reach people who would not enter a gym. A balanced school lunch benefits a child every day, whether or not that child enjoys games. Activity itself can also be increased more cheaply by building it into daily life. In cities such as Copenhagen, for instance, safe cycle lanes mean that many residents exercise simply by travelling to work. These measures are less visible than a new sports hall, although their effects are broader.\n\nSports facilities have a place in public health policy, particularly where communities have nowhere to exercise. On their own, however, they reach too few of the people who need help, and I believe prevention through better diet and active daily routines should take priority.",
"b501":"The printed newspaper was once the only detailed source of news available, and its decline has been rapid. Some now argue that it has no remaining purpose. I agree that most readers can manage perfectly well without it, but I do not think it has become unnecessary for all of them.\n\nFor the majority, the practical case against print is strong. The news in a paper is several hours old by the time it is delivered, whereas the same publisher's website is updated throughout the day and costs less to read. Digital editions can also be searched, enlarged and carried on a phone. There is an environmental argument as well, since printing and transporting millions of copies every night consumes trees, ink and fuel. A screen has costs too, although one device replaces thousands of printed pages. A commuter who once bought a paper at the station kiosk now loses nothing by reading the identical articles on a screen.\n\nThe claim goes too far, however, when it is applied to society as a whole. Access to online news depends on a reliable connection, a suitable device and the confidence to use it, and these cannot be assumed. Many elderly people, for example, have read the same daily paper for decades and would find it hard to follow the news through an application. Rural areas with weak internet coverage are in a similar position. Print also has one quality that screens lack, which is that it ends. A reader finishes the paper and puts it down, while a website keeps offering more.\n\nPrinted newspapers are no longer needed in the way they once were, and their numbers will continue to fall. In my view, though, they still serve a minority of readers well enough that it is too early to call them redundant.",
"b502":"Meat has been part of the human diet for as long as records exist, yet it is increasingly blamed for modern diseases. I disagree with the general claim that it harms health. In my view, the danger lies in the quantity and kind of meat consumed, not in meat itself.\n\nEaten in moderate amounts, meat is a valuable source of nutrition. It supplies complete protein, iron in a form the body absorbs easily, and vitamin B12, which plants do not naturally provide. These nutrients matter most to growing children, pregnant women and older people, who can become anaemic or lose muscle when their diet lacks them. Lean meat and fish, such as chicken breast or salmon, deliver these benefits with relatively little saturated fat. A person can certainly obtain most of the same nutrients from plants, although doing so requires careful planning and sometimes supplements.\n\nThe concern about meat is justified only in relation to particular eating patterns. Diets heavy in red meat and in processed products like bacon, sausages and ham have been linked by health authorities to heart disease and bowel cancer. Such products are also high in salt and saturated fat, which strain the heart when eaten daily. Quantity is the other concern. In many wealthy countries, portions have grown so large that meat pushes vegetables and whole grains off the plate. This is a problem of excess rather than of the food itself. The traditional Japanese diet, for example, includes fish and small quantities of meat alongside rice and vegetables, and is associated with long life.\n\nMeat becomes harmful when it is processed, fatty or eaten in large amounts every day. As part of a varied diet rich in plants, however, it does more good than harm, which is why I disagree that it is bad for health.",
"b503":"Subjects that demand abstract reasoning, mathematics and philosophy among them, are the ones pupils most often dread. It has been suggested that they should therefore be a matter of choice. I disagree, because difficulty is a poor test of whether a subject belongs in the core curriculum, though I accept that the two subjects are not equally essential.\n\nThe fact that children find a subject hard is often a sign that it is teaching them something they could not learn elsewhere. Mathematics trains pupils to reason step by step and to handle quantities, and adults need these abilities whenever they compare loans, read a graph or check a bill. If the subject were optional, many children would drop it at the first setback, long before they could judge what they were giving up. Compulsion protects children from a decision they are too young to make.\n\nPhilosophy is a somewhat different case. Although its questions about right and wrong, evidence and argument are valuable, few would claim that a person cannot function in daily life without formal lessons in it. There is room for flexibility here: schools might require a short introductory course and leave further study to those who enjoy it. What should not decide the matter is difficulty alone. Where a subject is hard, the better response is to teach it differently, for example through practical problems and discussion rather than abstract rules, and to give extra support to those who fall behind.\n\nMaking a subject optional because it is demanding would teach children to avoid challenges at the very age they should be learning to meet them. I believe mathematics must remain compulsory throughout school, while philosophy can reasonably be required only at an introductory level.",
"b504":"Few cities can afford to keep every structure that has survived from earlier centuries, so decisions have to be made about which ones to protect. I agree that some old buildings deserve preservation more than others, although I think the grounds for choosing between them should be wider than is often assumed.\n\nAge alone does not make a building valuable. What matters is what it can tell later generations, and by this measure the differences are large. A medieval cathedral, a royal palace or the house in which a national figure was born is a rare record of how people once worshipped, governed or lived. Although an ordinary warehouse may be equally old, it is often one of hundreds, of no particular design and in poor condition. Since restoration is expensive and urban land is needed for housing and transport, it is reasonable to spend money where the historical or architectural significance is greatest. Treating all old buildings equally would in practice mean protecting none of them properly.\n\nThe risk in this approach is that only grand buildings are judged worth saving. Much of a town's character comes from modest structures, such as a row of mill workers' cottages or a covered market, which record the lives of ordinary people more faithfully than any palace. Once demolished, they cannot be rebuilt with any authenticity. Importance should therefore be assessed not only by experts in architecture but also by the community that lives among these buildings. Even so, this is an argument about how to rank buildings rather than against ranking them, because some selection is unavoidable.\n\nPreservation involves choices, and I believe it is right that certain buildings are given priority. Those choices should, however, reflect local memory and everyday history as well as fame and beauty.",
"b505":"The age at which children first sit down to formal lessons varies by several years from one country to another. Some systems begin at four or five, whereas others deliberately wait until seven. I believe the later start is wiser, provided that the years before it are spent in good-quality nurseries and not simply at home.\n\nStarting school at four or five appeals to many parents because young children learn quickly, and these years should not be wasted. A child who begins reading and counting at four has a head start, and early schooling is thought to matter especially for children from disadvantaged homes, who may hear few books read aloud. School also lets teachers identify problems with hearing, speech or behaviour before they become serious. A speech difficulty noticed at four can be treated before reading suffers. These are real benefits, although they come from trained adults in a stimulating setting, not from desks and worksheets.\n\nSupporters of a later start believe that formal instruction before seven asks children to do things for which many are not ready. Sitting still, holding a pencil correctly and decoding print are hard for a typical five-year-old, and those who fail at them may decide early that they are bad at school. Play, by contrast, develops language, imagination and the ability to cooperate, which are the foundations of later learning. Finland, for example, begins formal schooling at seven and still produces pupils who read and calculate well. A late start works, however, only where good pre-school provision is available to all families, because otherwise poorer children simply lose two years.\n\nYoung children do need stimulation from an early age, but that is different from formal teaching. In my view, they are better served by play-based learning until about seven, after which they can take up academic work with confidence.",
"b506":"In many countries young people may leave school at sixteen, and some of them move straight into low-skilled work. The proposal that all those under eighteen should remain in full-time education is a response to this. I agree with it in principle, on condition that education is understood to include vocational training as well as academic study.\n\nThe strongest argument for keeping teenagers in education until eighteen is what awaits those who leave earlier. Although jobs open to unqualified sixteen-year-olds provide a wage, they are generally insecure and offer little prospect of advancement. Two further years of study give a young person the chance to gain a recognised certificate and the maturity to make better decisions about a career. A minimum leaving age also protects teenagers from pressure to start earning before they are ready. That pressure falls hardest on those from poor families, where a second wage is tempting and a capable pupil may leave to help with the bills.\n\nThe proposal becomes less convincing if full-time education means only the classroom. Some sixteen-year-olds are tired of academic lessons, have little hope of passing further examinations and would spend two more years bored and disruptive, to their own cost and that of their classmates. For them, an apprenticeship that combines paid work with college training is a far better route. Germany's apprenticeship system, for instance, prepares large numbers of young people for skilled trades. Compulsion should therefore apply to learning rather than to a particular building, and should leave room for exceptions such as young athletes or performers with established careers.\n\nLeaving education at sixteen with nothing to show for it harms most of those who do so. I believe young people should continue learning until eighteen, whether in a school, a college or a properly supervised apprenticeship.",
"b507":"Painters, composers and musicians rarely earn a steady income from their work, and the question of who should support them is an old one. Some look to the state, while others prefer private patrons, businesses or the market. I believe public money should go to the kinds of art that these other sources are unlikely to pay for, and no further.\n\nAdvocates of state funding regard the arts as a public good. An orchestra, a sculpture in a city square or a national theatre benefits people well beyond those who buy tickets, and it contributes to a country's sense of itself. The market, by contrast, rewards what is popular today. Experimental work and traditional forms with small audiences, such as folk opera, would struggle to survive on sales alone, yet much art that is now treasured was not profitable when it first appeared. Public grants do carry a risk, as committees may favour safe or officially approved work.\n\nSupporters of alternative funding object that taxpayers should not pay for art they have not chosen to see, especially when hospitals and schools are short of money. Artists who depend on ticket sales, commissions or online supporters, they argue, stay close to their audiences and free from political influence. Popular music and commercial cinema show that creative work can thrive without subsidy. Private money has limits of its own, however. Corporate sponsors tend to back famous names and uncontroversial projects, and wealthy donors are concentrated in large cities, so talent elsewhere may go unsupported.\n\nNeither source is sufficient alone. Artists who can attract buyers or sponsors should rely on them, while governments, in my view, should reserve their limited funds for valuable work that the market would otherwise allow to disappear.",
"b508":"The bill for staging a World Cup or an Olympic Games now runs into billions, and it is paid largely by taxpayers. Some regard this as money thrown away, while others insist that the returns justify it. I think the critics are closer to the truth, although hosting need not be wasteful if costs are kept under control.\n\nDefenders of hosting emphasise the economic stimulus. Visitors fill hotels and restaurants, construction creates jobs, and worldwide television coverage acts as an advertisement for tourism and investment. Germany's 2006 World Cup, for instance, is widely remembered for improving the country's image abroad. New roads and rail links also remain after the final whistle. These claims tend to be overstated, however. Many of the jobs are temporary, ordinary tourists often stay away to avoid the crowds, and transport improvements could have been built without a tournament attached.\n\nThe financial case against is stronger. Budgets set at the bidding stage are regularly exceeded, and the organising bodies rather than the host keep much of the income from broadcasting and sponsorship. Montreal took decades to pay off the debt from its 1976 Olympics, and Brazil built World Cup stadiums in cities with no major club to fill them. Every sum spent in this way is unavailable for schools, hospitals or housing, which would benefit citizens for far longer than a month of sport. The balance changes only when a country already has most of the venues it needs. Los Angeles in 1984 relied on existing stadiums and finished with a surplus, whereas hosts that start from nothing rarely recover their outlay.\n\nLarge sporting events can lift a nation's mood and reputation, but they seldom repay what they cost. Unless a host can rely mainly on existing facilities, I believe public money is better spent on services that last.",
"b509":"As pressure grows on schools to prepare teenagers for employment, subjects with no obvious commercial use come under suspicion, and literature is usually among the first to be questioned. I disagree with the view that reading novels and poems wastes students' time, although the way literature is often taught gives that view some support.\n\nLiterature offers a kind of knowledge that other subjects do not. A novel places the reader inside the mind of someone of a different age, country or century, whose motives are not the reader's own. A teenager reading 'To Kill a Mockingbird', for example, learns more about how prejudice feels from within a community than a textbook could convey. This capacity to imagine other lives is useful in any career that involves people. Close reading also develops precise use of language. Students must explain exactly how a writer achieves an effect, sometimes in a single line, and that discipline carries over into their own essays and reports.\n\nCritics are right, however, that these benefits are often lost in practice. When classes spend a year dissecting a single set text for an examination, and pupils memorise approved interpretations instead of forming their own, literature becomes an exercise in recall. Many then leave school resolved not to open a novel again. The fault lies in the method, though, not in the subject. Where students are given some choice of books and are encouraged to argue about them, they generally respond with interest. Time devoted to literature is wasted only when it is taught as a list of answers.\n\nNovels and poems give teenagers insight into other people and command of their own language, neither of which is a luxury. For these reasons I believe literature should remain part of the high school curriculum, taught in a way that invites students to think.",
"b510":"Whether boys and girls learn best together or apart has been debated for as long as both have been educated. Single-sex schools offer certain academic advantages, but I believe mixed schools serve young people better, because they prepare them for the world they will live in.\n\nThe case for separate schooling is mainly about concentration and confidence. Teenagers are very aware of the opposite sex, and without that distraction some pupils pay closer attention in lessons. Supporters also argue that each sex feels freer to pursue subjects it might otherwise avoid. A girl in an all-girls school, for instance, may choose physics without being one of only two girls in the class, and a boy may join the choir without fear of being teased. The good results of such schools are hard to credit to separation alone, however, since many single-sex schools are selective or fee-paying and admit able pupils to begin with.\n\nMixed schools reflect ordinary life, in which men and women study, work and raise families side by side. Pupils who share classrooms from an early age learn to treat one another as colleagues and friends rather than as strangers. They hear different points of view in discussion and become used to cooperating on projects, as employers will later expect. Young people who have had little contact with the other sex before university may find those relationships awkward at first. Co-education is not free of problems, as teachers must make sure that neither group dominates lessons, but this is a matter of classroom management and not a reason for separation.\n\nSingle-sex education can suit particular pupils, especially those who lack confidence in certain subjects. As a general policy, mixed schooling seems to me the wiser choice, and I think its social benefits outweigh any small academic gain from teaching the sexes apart.",
"b511":"Men are often described as the more competitive sex, whether in sport, business or politics, and this is commonly put down to nature. I agree that there is an observable difference in behaviour, but I believe upbringing and opportunity account for most of it, so the word 'naturally' goes too far.\n\nThe belief has some basis. In most societies men are more visibly drawn to open contests, and they are more likely to enter high-risk careers. Biology probably contributes something, since hormones such as testosterone are linked to risk-taking and the pursuit of status. Yet a biological tendency is not the same as a fixed trait. Individual variation is wide, and many women are far more driven than the average man, as any list of Olympic champions or company founders shows.\n\nMuch of the gap is better explained by what each sex is taught and permitted. Boys are typically praised for winning and given competitive games from an early age, while girls are more often rewarded for being cooperative and modest. An ambitious woman may be criticised as aggressive for conduct that would be admired in a male colleague, which teaches many to compete less openly. Opportunity matters too. Where women were long excluded from professional sport or senior management, their apparent lack of competitiveness reflected closed doors rather than weak motivation. As those doors have opened, the difference has narrowed. Girls now outperform boys in school examinations in many countries, and the rivalry for places on university courses in medicine is as intense among women as among men.\n\nMen may be somewhat more inclined by nature to seek out contests, although the evidence for this is modest. For the most part, in my view, competitiveness is shaped by expectations and chances, and it will continue to even out as these become more equal.",
"b512":"Every budget involves choices, and money given to saving tigers or rainforests is money that could have gone to hospitals or schools. While I understand why some would put people first in every case, I believe a modest share of public resources should be reserved for wildlife, since the two aims conflict less than they appear to.\n\nThe argument for giving priority to the human population is a moral one. In many countries people still lack clean water, basic medicines and a secure supply of food, and their suffering is immediate. A government that funds a breeding programme for a rare bird while its rural clinics have no staff is open to the charge that its priorities are wrong. This reasoning is strongest in the poorest states, where every public project competes for very limited funds. It is weaker elsewhere, because the amounts spent on conservation are usually too small to make a noticeable difference to health or welfare budgets.\n\nThose who defend spending on wild animals argue that the loss is permanent in a way that most human problems are not. A clinic can be built next year, but an extinct species cannot be brought back. They also point out that conservation often serves people directly. In Rwanda, for example, part of the income from visitors to the mountain gorillas is given to neighbouring villages, where it has paid for schools and water supplies. Protecting forests safeguards the rivers that farmers downstream depend on. Such benefits are not automatic, however, and projects that exclude local residents from their land can leave them poorer.\n\nHuman needs are urgent and deserve the larger share of any budget. Even so, I think it would be short-sighted to spend nothing on wildlife, because well-designed conservation protects something irreplaceable and frequently improves the lives of the people nearby.",
"b513":"Few features of school life cause as much anxiety as marks and examinations, and some educators would like to abolish them altogether. Others regard grades as indispensable. In my view, assessment is necessary, but it serves pupils well only when it is used sparingly and explained.\n\nOpponents of grading argue that it distorts the purpose of learning. Once a number is attached to every piece of work, pupils begin to study for the mark rather than for understanding, and teachers are tempted to teach only what will be tested. A child who repeatedly receives low grades may also conclude that he or she is simply not clever, and stop trying. Young children are particularly vulnerable to this, which is why countries such as Finland give few formal grades in the early years of school. These objections are serious, although they apply to constant ranking more than to assessment as such.\n\nSupporters of grades reply that pupils, parents and teachers all need to know how learning is going. Without some form of test, a pupil who has misunderstood fractions may not discover the gap until it has become difficult to close. Grades also provide a common standard. Universities and employers cannot interview every applicant at length, and a recognised qualification tells them something reliable about what a candidate can do. A mark alone, however, says little about how to improve, so it is most useful when it comes with specific comments from the teacher.\n\nBoth sides are right about something: frequent grading can discourage, while its absence leaves pupils and parents uninformed. For that reason I believe schools should keep tests and grades, but give them less often and accompany them with clear guidance.",
"b514":"Tobacco is sold under tight restrictions in most countries, and it is sometimes suggested that unhealthy food and drink should be removed from sale altogether. I largely disagree. In my view, a ban is justified only for the few ingredients that are harmful in any amount, and persuasion works better for the rest.\n\nProhibition has a poor record of changing behaviour. When the United States outlawed alcohol in the 1920s, drinking continued, and the trade passed into the hands of criminals. Something similar would be likely if popular products such as cola or crisps vanished from shops, since demand would not disappear with them. There is a question of principle as well. In a free society, adults are generally allowed to take risks that affect mainly themselves, and an occasional burger falls well within that freedom. A government that decided otherwise would need to police millions of ordinary purchases.\n\nThere are, nevertheless, cases in which a ban is sensible. Some substances offer no benefit and cause damage even in small doses. Industrial trans fats, for instance, were once common in margarine and baked goods, and Denmark removed them from its food supply by law without consumers noticing any loss. Rules of this kind target manufacturers rather than shoppers, which makes them easy to enforce. For products that are harmful only in excess, the better tools are honest labelling, restrictions on advertising to children and public education. These measures work slowly, although their effects tend to last because people come to choose differently of their own accord.\n\nA general ban on unhealthy food and drink would be unenforceable and would intrude on personal choice, so I disagree with the proposal as it stands. Legal limits should be kept for ingredients with no safe level, with information doing the remaining work.",
"b515":"Prize-giving ceremonies in most schools honour the pupils with the highest marks. A growing number of teachers feel that progress deserves the prize more than position does. Although excellence should be recognised, I think rewards for improvement do more good for more students.\n\nRewarding top performers has a clear logic. High marks usually reflect sustained effort as well as talent, and acknowledging them shows the whole school that academic achievement is valued. Scholarships for outstanding results, for example, give able students a reason to aim higher still. The difficulty is that the same few names tend to appear on the list each year. Once the rest of the class realises that the prize is out of reach, it stops motivating them, and it may even persuade average pupils that effort is pointless.\n\nRewards for improvement reach those pupils. A student who moves from failing to passing has often worked harder than the one who stays comfortably at the top. Recognising that effort tells every member of the class that progress is within their control. This message matters because persistence, more than initial ability, tends to decide how far people go in later life. Improvement prizes are not without problems, however. Progress is harder to measure fairly than a final score, and a strong student has less room to improve than a weak one. Teachers can reduce this unfairness by judging each pupil against his or her own earlier work rather than against the class average, and by keeping separate awards for the highest achievers.\n\nA sensible school will reward both kinds of success. If one had to take priority, however, I believe it should be improvement, since it encourages effort from the many and not only from the gifted few.",
"b516":"Fresh water is one of the few resources for which there is no substitute, and in many regions there is less of it each year. Some conclude that the state must manage its supply tightly, whereas others would leave it to private suppliers and the market. I believe firm public control is necessary, although it should concentrate on large users rather than on ordinary households.\n\nThe case for regulation begins with the fact that water is shared. A river or an underground reserve serves farms, factories and homes at once, so whatever one user takes is no longer available to the rest. Left to themselves, the largest users have little reason to hold back. A farmer who pumps groundwater freely to irrigate a thirsty crop, for example, lowers the level for every neighbouring well. Only an authority with the power to set limits and issue licences can prevent this kind of slow exhaustion, and such limits matter most in dry years, when competition is fiercest.\n\nOpponents of regulation argue that governments manage resources badly. State water companies, they point out, often lose large volumes through leaking pipes and charge prices too low to pay for repairs. A competitive market, in this view, would reward suppliers who waste less and would let prices signal scarcity to consumers. Although there is some truth in this, since a realistic price does make people more careful, a market serves those who can pay. Unless rules guarantee a basic supply at an affordable rate, poorer families may find themselves outbid by golf courses and bottling plants.\n\nBoth positions therefore capture part of the truth, but they are not equally strong. In my view, governments should keep strict control over how much water is taken and by whom, while using sensible pricing to discourage waste.",
"b517":"Irritation with advertising is widespread, and a few people would go as far as outlawing it in every form. I disagree with this proposal. Advertising causes real problems, but a total prohibition would remove a good deal that is useful and would be almost impossible to enforce.\n\nThe first difficulty is that so much of economic life relies on advertising. A new business has no customers until people hear of it, and a ban would leave established companies with a permanent advantage over any challenger. A family opening a café, for example, could not announce itself, so customers would go on using the chains they already knew. Much of the media would also collapse. Free television channels, most websites and many local papers are paid for by advertisers, and without that income they would have to charge their audiences or close. The people least able to pay would then have the least access to news and entertainment.\n\nA complete ban would also catch messages that few would wish to silence. Job vacancies, charity appeals, notices of concerts and government campaigns on road safety are all advertising. It is hard to see how a law could forbid a soft drink poster while permitting these, or how it would treat a shop sign or a celebrity mentioning a product online. The concerns behind the proposal are legitimate, however. Advertising aimed at young children, or promoting harmful products such as tobacco and gambling, does damage that outweighs any benefit. Many countries already restrict these categories, and that targeted approach has worked without the costs of a general prohibition.\n\nBanning all advertising would punish the useful along with the harmful. I believe a better course is to keep advertising legal and to tighten the rules wherever it exploits the vulnerable or misleads the public.",
"b518":"Universities are increasingly judged by how quickly their graduates find well-paid work. An older tradition sees them as places where knowledge is pursued whether or not it has a market value. In my opinion, this second purpose should remain the main one, although the two aims conflict less than is often supposed.\n\nThe demand for workplace preparation is reasonable. Students invest several years and a good deal of money in a degree, and most expect it to lead to a career. For professions such as medicine, engineering or accountancy, the university is the place where practical competence must be acquired. This model becomes a problem, however, when it is applied to every course. Employers' needs change quickly, and a syllabus designed around the skills in demand today may leave graduates poorly equipped a decade later.\n\nStudy pursued for its own sake is more durable. A student of philosophy or history learns to analyse difficult material, weigh evidence and construct an argument, and these habits transfer to almost any occupation. Universities are also among the few institutions in which research without immediate commercial use is carried out, and much of what later proves useful begins there. Work on the structure of DNA, for instance, was not undertaken to serve an industry, yet modern medicine relies on it. Companies are well placed to teach their own procedures once a capable graduate arrives, whereas they cannot supply the years of disciplined thinking that come before. Even so, academic departments should not ignore their students' futures, and offering work placements alongside a degree takes little away from the curriculum.\n\nTo my mind, the main function of a university is to advance knowledge and to train people to think rigorously. Graduates educated in this way are, in the long run, also the ones employers most need.",
"b519":"Much of what fills a household bin is not the product that was bought but the plastic, card and foil it came in. Opinion is divided over whether producers and retailers should cut this packaging or whether shoppers should simply choose goods that carry less of it. In my view, the main responsibility lies with the companies, although consumers have a supporting part.\n\nThe argument for placing the duty on shoppers is that businesses sell what people buy. If customers consistently picked loose vegetables over those wrapped in plastic trays, supermarkets would soon change what they stock. Sales figures are watched closely, and a line that stops selling is quickly redesigned or withdrawn. Such choices are only possible, though, where an alternative is on the shelf. A shopper in a mall where every brand of biscuits comes in three layers of wrapping cannot buy an unwrapped packet, however good his or her intentions.\n\nThis is why the stronger case points to those who make and sell the goods. Packaging is decided long before a product reaches the shop, by designers whose priorities are appearance and shelf life rather than waste. A single decision by a large manufacturer, such as replacing a plastic bottle with a refillable one, removes more rubbish than thousands of careful customers could. Retailers have similar power, since they can refuse to stock excessively wrapped lines or offer refill stations. Some packaging does protect food and prevent it from spoiling, so the aim should be to remove what is unnecessary, not all of it.\n\nConsumers can reward the better companies and should do so where they have the choice. The quantity of packaging is set at the factory and the store, however, and I believe that is where the obligation to reduce it should mainly rest.",
"b520":"When new homes are built in an established neighbourhood, the question arises of whether they should imitate the older houses around them or reflect the tastes of their owners. Both positions are reasonable, and in my view the right answer depends on how distinctive the area is.\n\nThose who favour traditional styles argue that the appearance of a street belongs to the whole community and not only to the person who owns a plot on it. Many towns owe their charm, and often their income from tourism, to a consistent style developed over centuries. In the old quarter of Hoi An in Vietnam, for example, a glass villa among the timber shophouses would damage something that cannot be replaced. A few such buildings would erode the character visitors come to see. Although this argument is strong in historic districts, it weakens in ordinary suburbs where the existing houses have no special merit.\n\nThe opposing view is that a house is first of all a place to live, and that owners should be free to design it for their own needs. Older styles were shaped by the materials and family life of their time. Someone building today may want larger windows, solar panels on the roof or an open interior, none of which fits easily behind a traditional facade. Freedom of design also allows architecture to develop, since the buildings now admired as heritage were themselves new once. A town that permits only copies leaves nothing of its own period to be valued later. Unlimited freedom has costs, however, because one oversized or garish house can spoil the outlook of a whole street.\n\nI believe local authorities should enforce traditional styles strictly only in areas of real historic value. Elsewhere, people should be allowed to build as they wish within simple limits on height and scale.",
"b521":"The idea that a machine might one day outthink its makers has moved from science fiction into serious scientific debate. Some welcome that prospect and others fear it. In my view, both reactions are reasonable, and which of them proves right will depend on how much control people keep over the systems they build.\n\nOptimists argue that greater intelligence, wherever it comes from, is the resource humanity most lacks. Problems such as designing new medicines or predicting how the climate will change involve more data and more variables than any team of researchers can handle. A system that reasons better than its human colleagues could shorten work that now takes decades, and the benefits would be shared by patients and farmers who have no interest in computing. This hope is realistic only if the machine's goals remain those its designers intended, which cannot simply be assumed.\n\nThat is the point at which the pessimists' case begins. A system more capable than its supervisors is, by definition, difficult for them to check: they may not understand why it reaches a decision or notice when it is wrong. An error in a system that manages a power grid or advises doctors, for instance, could spread widely before anyone corrected it. The other concern is employment. If computers can perform most intellectual tasks, large numbers of professional jobs may disappear faster than new ones emerge, and lawyers or accountants in mid-career cannot easily retrain. Although these fears are sometimes exaggerated in films, they describe risks that grow as machines are trusted with more important decisions.\n\nSuperior machine intelligence could therefore be either a great benefit or a serious threat. I believe it will be the former only if testing, regulation and human oversight advance as quickly as the technology itself.",
"b522":"People have more ways of keeping up with the news than at any time in the past, and opinion is divided over which is best. Some remain convinced that nothing matches a newspaper, whereas others prefer television, podcasts or news applications. In my view neither group is entirely right, because the most suitable medium depends on what a reader wants from the news.\n\nAdmirers of newspapers often mention the breadth of what they offer. Someone who turns the pages of a daily paper meets stories he would not have searched for alongside the politics he bought it for. He may find an article on farming, for instance, or a review of a play. Nothing on the page has been selected to match his previous habits. A newspaper also has a clear end, so the reader knows when he is reasonably well informed. A news feed, by contrast, has no last page. These advantages, however, are available only to people with the time and inclination to sit down and read.\n\nOther media suit the way many people now live. A nurse who works long shifts can listen to a news podcast on the bus, and a student can follow a developing story on her phone between lectures. Neither needs to set aside an hour, because the news fits into time that would otherwise be lost. Their drawback is that applications tend to show users more of whatever they clicked on before. Unless people make a deliberate effort to look further, they may end up with a narrower view of the world than a newspaper reader has.\n\nThere is, then, no single best method. I think newspapers serve those who want range and depth, while other media serve those short of time, and most people would be best informed by using both.",
"b523":"Laws designed to cut carbon emissions, from fuel taxes to bans on petrol engines, are changing the conditions in which companies operate. Many business owners see these rules as a burden, while others regard them as a source of new markets. I believe that, across the economy as a whole, they offer more opportunity than harm, though the gains and losses fall on different firms.\n\nThe fear that climate policy damages business is well founded in certain industries. A steel plant or an airline depends heavily on fossil fuels, and a carbon tax raises its costs at once, long before cleaner technology is ready at a comparable price. If competitors abroad face no such charge, the firm may lose orders or move its production overseas, taking jobs with it. Smaller companies feel the pressure most, because they lack the capital to replace vehicles and machinery quickly. These costs are real, although they are concentrated in a limited number of sectors and tend to fall as alternatives become cheaper.\n\nOther firms find that the same regulations create demand that did not exist before. When a government sets a date for ending sales of petrol cars, it guarantees a market for batteries, charging points and the engineers who install them. Danish manufacturers, for example, became world leaders in wind turbines after their government committed itself to renewable power. Efficiency rules bring a quieter benefit. A factory obliged to use less energy pays smaller bills each year, which often repays the cost of new equipment. Such rewards depend, however, on policies remaining stable enough for businesses to plan their investments.\n\nReform therefore produces both winners and losers. On balance, I think it is better understood as an opportunity, since the firms that adapt gain new markets while those that resist merely delay a change that is coming regardless.",
"b524":"Paid leave for new fathers, once unusual, is now offered by employers and governments in a growing number of countries. I agree that fathers should be entitled to such leave, because the weeks after a birth shape the whole family, although its length has to be affordable for employers.\n\nA newborn creates more work than one recovering adult can reasonably manage. A mother who has just given birth, particularly after surgery, needs rest, and a father who is at home can take over the cooking, the night feeds and the care of older children. His presence also matters to the baby. Fathers who change, bathe and settle an infant in the first weeks tend to remain involved parents, whereas those who return to work within days often come to see childcare as someone else's task. This benefit depends on how the time is used, of course, and leave treated as a holiday does little for the family.\n\nPaternity leave also has consequences beyond the home. When only women take time off after a birth, employers have a reason to regard young female candidates as a greater risk, and mothers' careers suffer accordingly. Sharing the absence between both parents weakens that assumption. In Sweden, for example, a portion of parental leave is reserved for fathers, and taking it has become ordinary rather than remarkable. The difficulty lies with small firms. A workshop with four employees cannot easily lose one of them for months, which is why the cost should be borne largely by the state.\n\nTime at home after a birth helps mothers recover, draws fathers into the care of their children and makes the workplace fairer to women. For these reasons I agree that fathers should have this entitlement, provided that public funding protects smaller employers.",
"b525":"As populations age, the question of who should pay for the years after work has become urgent in many countries. Some look to the state and others to the individual, and I believe a fair system needs both: a basic public pension, supplemented by what people save for themselves.\n\nThe argument for government support is that old age is a risk which individuals cannot fully control. Many workers earn too little to put money aside, and others see their savings reduced by illness, unemployment or inflation through no fault of their own. A woman who spent decades raising children and caring for elderly parents, for example, may reach retirement with almost nothing in her own name. A guaranteed income prevents the poverty that would otherwise await those who were unlucky rather than careless, and it spreads that risk across the whole population.\n\nThose who favour self-reliance are concerned mainly with cost and responsibility. In countries such as Japan and Italy, the number of retired people is rising while the workforce shrinks, so generous state pensions place a growing burden on younger taxpayers. It is also argued that people who expect to be provided for have less reason to save. If the state promises a comfortable income in old age, spending today's wages looks sensible, and the bill passes to others. These points carry weight, although they apply chiefly to those with enough income to save in the first place. Telling a low-paid cleaner to fund her own retirement is advice she is unable to follow, however willing she may be.\n\nNeither the state nor the individual can reasonably bear the whole cost of retirement. The most sustainable arrangement, in my view, is a modest pension guaranteed to all, with tax incentives that encourage those who can afford it to build additional savings of their own.",
"b526":"Most children draw without hesitation, yet few adults would call themselves artists. This gap lies behind the disagreement over whether good art is open to all or reserved for the gifted. In my view, almost anyone can learn to make competent and sincere work, but art of lasting quality usually requires an ability that practice alone does not supply.\n\nThe belief that anyone can produce good art rests on the fact that artistic skill is largely taught. Perspective, colour mixing and the handling of a brush are techniques, and a determined adult who attends evening classes for a few years will improve beyond recognition. Its supporters add that art is valued for what it expresses as much as for its polish. A grandmother's quilt or a song written for a friend's wedding can move people deeply, although neither maker would claim any special gift. Judged by sincerity and craft, then, quality is within most people's reach.\n\nThose who insist on talent are thinking of a different standard. Thousands of pianists master the same pieces with the same dedication, yet only a handful play them in a way that audiences remember. What separates them is hard to teach: an ear for phrasing, an eye for composition, or an instinct for the unexpected image. Mozart, for instance, was composing as a small child, before training could explain his fluency. Talent of this kind still depends on years of work, however, and a gifted person who does not practise will be overtaken by a diligent one. Ability sets the ceiling rather than doing the climbing.\n\nBoth views are therefore right about different things. Art that is honest and well made can come from almost anybody willing to learn, while work that changes how others see or hear, I believe, demands a rare aptitude as well as effort.",
"b527":"It is often said that a language and the culture behind it can only be learnt in the country where they belong. I agree that living abroad is the most effective way to learn both, yet I disagree that it is a necessity.\n\nImmersion offers something that no classroom can reproduce. A student living in Spain hears Spanish in shops, on buses and at the dinner table, and must use it to meet everyday needs. Progress is fast as a result, and vocabulary is remembered because it was needed. Customs are absorbed in the same way. How late people eat, how they greet an older neighbour or how directly they refuse an invitation are things one notices by taking part, not by reading about them. This advantage does depend on behaviour, however. Students who share a flat with compatriots and socialise in their own language can spend a year abroad and return with little more than they took.\n\nFor those who cannot travel, the alternatives are now far better. Learners can practise with native speakers through online exchanges, watch films and news broadcasts from the country, and follow courses taught by qualified teachers at home. Many people in Scandinavia, for example, speak excellent English without having lived in an English-speaking country. Cost matters here too. Study abroad is beyond most families' means, and a rule that made it essential would exclude them. Language learning would then become a privilege of the wealthy. What home study teaches less well is the unwritten side of a culture, which remains easier to grasp in person.\n\nTime abroad is a great advantage to any language learner, but it is an advantage, not a requirement. With good teaching and regular contact with native speakers, I believe students can learn a language and much of its culture without leaving home.",
"b528":"Each generation inherits the planet in the condition its predecessors left it, and has no say in the matter. The claim that people alive today ought to protect the environment for those who follow seems to me almost beyond dispute. I agree with it strongly, while recognising that the duty cannot fall equally on rich and poor.\n\nThe main reason is that environmental damage outlasts those who cause it. Carbon released this year will warm the atmosphere for many decades, and a species that disappears does not return. Future citizens will bear these costs without having shared in the benefits or been able to object. There is a basic unfairness in that arrangement. It resembles a tenant who enjoys a house for years and leaves the repairs to the next occupant. Acting now is also cheaper than acting later. Protecting a forest costs far less than attempting to restore one, and some losses, such as a drained wetland, cannot be reversed at any price.\n\nThis obligation has limits, and they concern the present generation's own needs. A farmer in a poor country who clears land to feed his family cannot reasonably be asked to put unborn strangers first. Development that lifts people out of poverty has a moral claim too. The answer, though, is to distribute the effort fairly rather than to abandon it. Wealthy nations, which have produced most of the pollution, can afford measures such as replacing coal with renewable power and helping poorer states to do the same. Where sacrifices are shared in this way, protecting the future need not mean neglecting the present.\n\nFor these reasons I agree that today's generation must act on behalf of the next. The steps should be taken first by those most able to afford them, but they should not be postponed.",
"b529":"The internet has placed lessons on almost any subject within reach of a child at home, and some conclude that school buildings and timetables have had their day. I strongly disagree, because schools provide a good deal more than information.\n\nOnline resources are valuable, but information is not the same as education. A website can explain photosynthesis clearly, yet it cannot notice that a particular child has misunderstood, or decide what that child should study next. Teachers do both. They also impose a structure that few children can create for themselves. A ten-year-old left alone with a laptop is more likely to drift towards games than towards grammar, whereas a timetable and a watching adult keep him at the task. Home education can work well where a parent has the time, knowledge and patience to act as the teacher. Most households, however, depend on two incomes, and no adult is free to play that part.\n\nSchool is also where children learn to live among people they did not choose. In the classroom and the playground they must share, wait, disagree politely and work with classmates from different backgrounds. These lessons are difficult to arrange at home. Schools serve a wider social purpose as well, since they give children from poorer families access to teachers, laboratories, sports facilities and, in many places, a daily meal. If learning depended on what each home could supply, such as a quiet room and a fast connection, the gap between rich and poor pupils would widen. The internet is therefore best seen as a tool that teachers use rather than a replacement for them.\n\nChildren today can learn a great deal online, and schools should make full use of that fact. They remain necessary, in my view, for the guidance, companionship and equal opportunity that a screen at home cannot offer.",
"b530":"Teenagers have long modelled themselves on singers, actors and sports stars, and social media have made such figures more visible than ever. The habit of imitating them grows out of ordinary needs of adolescence, but it can bring financial, personal and social costs.\n\nThe main reason young people copy celebrities is that they are still working out who they are. Adolescents are moving away from their parents' influence and looking for other models, and famous people appear to have what they most want, which is confidence, admiration and success. Dressing or speaking like a favourite star is a way of borrowing some of that status. It is also a way of belonging. When a whole class follows the same footballer or pop group, wearing similar trainers or adopting the same phrases shows membership of the group.\n\nSeveral problems can follow, although much imitation is harmless and soon outgrown. The most immediate is expense. Branded items worn by stars are costly, and a teenager who insists on them may put pressure on parents who can barely afford them, or feel ashamed when they refuse. A deeper difficulty concerns self-image. The appearance of famous people is produced by stylists, trainers and edited photographs, so those who measure themselves against it are likely to feel inadequate. While clothes are a matter of taste, behaviour can be copied just as easily, and this is more worrying. If an admired musician is seen smoking, driving recklessly or treating others with contempt, for example, some followers will conclude that such conduct is attractive.\n\nImitating the famous is a normal part of growing up rather than a sign that something is wrong. It becomes a concern only when it costs families money they lack or leads young people to judge themselves by standards that are not real.",
"b531":"Timetables are crowded, and it is tempting to argue that every hour should go to the subjects on which examinations are set. I disagree with the view that practical skills such as cooking have no place in school, although I accept that academic work should take up most of the week.\n\nThe priority given to academic subjects is justified. Examination results decide whether a pupil can enter university or a chosen profession, and schools would be failing their pupils if they neglected them. A teenager who misses the required grade in mathematics may find many careers closed. It does not follow, however, that the remaining hours are wasted. Pupils cannot concentrate on abstract material all day, and a lesson in which they make something with their hands often sends them back to their desks more attentive.\n\nPractical lessons matter because they prepare pupils for adult life, which examinations alone do not. A young person who can plan a cheap, healthy meal, repair a piece of clothing or understand a household bill will manage far better on leaving home. Families once passed on such knowledge, but where both parents work long hours this often does not happen. A student who arrives at university unable to cook, for instance, is likely to rely on expensive and unhealthy convenience food. Practical classes also give less academic children a chance to succeed at something. A boy who struggles with algebra but cooks well gains confidence, and that can change his attitude to school. An hour or two a week is enough, though, and much more would cut into essential study.\n\nExaminations rightly dominate the school week, but they are not the whole of an education. For this reason I believe a modest amount of practical teaching should be kept alongside academic subjects, not removed to make room for them.",
"b532":"People with degrees are convicted of crimes less often than those without, and it is tempting to infer that teaching prisoners would bring crime down more effectively than anything else. I agree that prison education is valuable, but I do not think it is the best single remedy.\n\nTeaching inmates makes sense because of what awaits them on release. Many prisoners left school early and read poorly, and a criminal record makes employers more reluctant to hire them. A man who has used his sentence to qualify as an electrician, for example, has a legal way of earning a living that he did not have before. Study can also change how prisoners see themselves. Completing a course is often their first experience of succeeding at something lawful, and a man who starts to see himself as a student is less likely to return to old companions. These gains are worth paying for, although they reach only those willing to take part.\n\nThe claim that this is the best method goes further than the evidence allows. Graduates may commit fewer crimes partly because of the stable homes and comfortable incomes that tend to accompany a degree, rather than because of the degree itself. A certificate will not keep a former prisoner out of trouble if he returns to addiction, debt or homelessness, so education has to be combined with treatment and housing support. Prison classes also arrive late. By definition they help people who have already offended, whereas better schools and youth services in poor districts could prevent some of those offences from taking place.\n\nEducation in prison lowers the chance that an offender will return, and it deserves more funding than it receives. In my view, though, it works as one part of a wider effort and cannot be called the best way to reduce crime.",
"b533":"Because the world that pupils will work in is changing faster than any syllabus can follow, some educators argue that the ideal curriculum teaches adaptability instead of fixed content. I agree that coping with change should be a central aim, but I believe it cannot be taught without a solid base of knowledge.\n\nThe argument against a static curriculum is persuasive. Facts and procedures that seemed essential a generation ago, such as how to use a library card catalogue, are now of little use, and today's pupils will probably hold jobs that do not yet exist. A school that only transmits settled information prepares them for a world that has already passed. What they need instead is the ability to learn unfamiliar things quickly, judge the reliability of new sources and solve problems for which no method has been given.\n\nAdaptability does not grow in a vacuum, however. A person can only think critically about a subject he or she knows something about, and the capacity to learn new material depends on what is already understood. An engineer retraining in renewable energy, for instance, relies on physics and mathematics learnt at school, both of which have changed very little. The same is true of reading, writing and numeracy, which are static in the sense that they do not go out of date. A curriculum made up entirely of general skills would therefore leave pupils with nothing to apply them to. The real choice is not between knowledge and flexibility, but about how the first is taught so that it produces the second.\n\nPreparing young people for change is the right goal for a modern curriculum. The way to reach it, in my view, is to teach lasting knowledge in a manner that trains pupils to question, connect and extend what they learn.",
"b534":"Basic literacy and numeracy are often described as the first step out of poverty, and some claim that six years of free schooling is the most effective measure a poor country can take. I agree with much of this claim, although such schooling reduces poverty only when other conditions are also met.\n\nPrimary education changes what a poor person is able to do. Someone who can read can follow the instructions on a packet of seed or medicine, understand a contract, and check that a trader's sums are correct. A farmer who can keep simple accounts, for example, is better placed to obtain a small loan and to see which crops make a profit. Making these years free matters as much as providing them. Even small fees lead poor families to keep children, especially girls, at home, and the households that drop out are the ones schooling was meant to help.\n\nSchooling is not sufficient by itself, however. Six years in a classroom achieve little if teachers are untrained or frequently absent, so that pupils leave unable to read a simple sentence. Attendance figures can then look impressive while almost nothing has been learnt. The other condition lies outside the school. A well-taught child will still remain poor as an adult unless there are jobs, roads and markets that reward what he or she has learnt. A literate farmer with no way of getting crops to a buyer, for instance, earns little more than an illiterate one. Education is therefore best understood as the foundation of development rather than a substitute for its other parts.\n\nFew investments do as much for the poor as six years of free, good-quality schooling. I believe it deserves priority in developing countries, while recognising that it works alongside investment in teachers, infrastructure and employment.",
"b535":"Teenagers who end up in trouble with the police often come from homes where discipline is either absent or harsh, so teaching parents how to bring up children seems an attractive remedy. I agree that it would help, but I do not believe it is the single best way to reduce youth crime.\n\nParenting classes can make a real difference, particularly when children are young. Mothers and fathers who learn to set consistent rules, to praise good behaviour and to punish without violence tend to raise children with more self-control. Such skills are not instinctive, and people who were badly treated in their own childhood may have had no model to follow. Although such courses are offered to new parents in several countries, their weakness is one of reach. The parents who most need guidance are frequently those least likely to attend, whether because of long working hours, addiction or distrust of the authorities.\n\nA further limit is that parents are only one influence among several once a child reaches adolescence. A fifteen-year-old spends most of his waking hours at school, with friends or online, and the opinions of his peers often count for more than those of his family. Even a capable and caring parent may be unable to compete with a gang that offers money and status in a neighbourhood with no jobs. Reducing youth crime therefore also requires measures outside the home, such as schools that keep difficult pupils engaged, safe places to spend the evening and realistic routes into work. Unless these are in place, better parenting can achieve only so much.\n\nTeaching parenting skills deserves support as one part of prevention, especially in the early years. I think youth crime will fall furthest, however, when help for families is combined with better schools and real opportunities for teenagers themselves.",
"b536":"Motorists in many countries already complain about what they pay at the pump, yet some argue that the charge should be considerably higher for the sake of the planet. While I accept that dearer petrol would reduce some driving, I disagree that it is the best way to tackle environmental problems, which extend far beyond private cars.\n\nMaking fuel more expensive does change behaviour to a degree. Drivers combine journeys, share lifts or choose a smaller car when the old one is replaced, and over time manufacturers respond by producing more economical engines. In European countries where petrol is heavily taxed, for instance, cars tend to be smaller than in the United States. The effect is limited, though, by how much choice people have. A nurse who works night shifts in a town with no late buses must drive whatever it costs. For such people a higher price is a penalty, not an incentive, and it weighs most heavily on rural and low-income households.\n\nThe larger objection is one of scale. Private vehicles are responsible for only part of the damage done to the environment. Electricity generation, heavy industry, aviation, farming and deforestation all contribute, and none of them would be touched by a tax paid by motorists. A policy aimed at one source cannot solve a problem with many. Within transport itself, other measures may achieve more: reliable public transport gives drivers a real alternative, and support for electric cars removes exhaust fumes rather than merely making them costlier. A fuel tax works best as a way of paying for these improvements.\n\nHigher prices for motorists have a place, then, provided that the revenue funds better alternatives. In my view, however, they do not deserve to be called the best solution when most environmental harm comes from elsewhere.",
"b537":"It is often claimed that societies are safer than they used to be and that technology deserves the credit. I agree only partly. Technical advances have made several traditional crimes harder to commit, but they have also opened the way to new ones, and other factors have contributed to the fall.\n\nTechnology has clearly reduced some kinds of offending. Security cameras in city centres, alarms in homes and tracking devices in cars raise the risk of being identified, while DNA testing allows police to solve cases that would once have gone unpunished. The decline of cash has had a similar effect. A shop that takes most of its payments by card, for instance, keeps little money in the till and is a poor target for an armed robber. In these areas, where crime depends on physical opportunity, the claim is persuasive, although careful offenders soon learn to avoid cameras or cover their faces.\n\nThe picture is less encouraging once crime that happens on a screen is taken into account. The same networks that protect a bank's vault allow fraudsters to empty accounts from another continent, and identity theft, online blackmail and the sale of illegal goods have all expanded. Many of these offences go unreported, because victims are embarrassed or doubt that the police can help, so official figures may overstate the fall in crime. It is also difficult to give technology sole credit for what has improved. Ageing populations, higher living standards and changes in policing are all likely to have played a part, and their effects cannot easily be separated.\n\nTechnology has made burglary, robbery and car theft riskier, and to that extent I agree with the statement. Much crime has moved online instead of disappearing, however, which is why I believe the claim is only partly true.",
"b538":"When Neil Armstrong stepped onto the lunar surface in 1969, he described the moment as a leap for all humanity. Sceptics point out that daily life on Earth looked much the same the next morning. I disagree with their conclusion, because the landing changed ordinary lives indirectly, through the technology that grew out of it.\n\nIt is true that the event itself gave most people nothing practical. No household's income, health or housing improved because twelve astronauts walked on the Moon, and the rocks they brought back were of interest mainly to geologists. Viewers around the world watched the broadcast with excitement, but they returned the next day to the same jobs, the same bills and the same worries. Judged only by its immediate results, the mission can fairly be called a spectacular gesture.\n\nIts lasting effects, however, came from the effort required to achieve it. Engineers had to build computers small and reliable enough to fit inside a spacecraft, and that demand helped to speed up the development of the microchips on which modern electronics depend. The wider space programme produced the satellites that now make weather forecasting, navigation and international broadcasting possible. A farmer checking tomorrow's rain or a driver following a map on a phone, for example, is using systems that grew out of the same work. Although some of these advances would probably have arrived without the Moon missions, they would almost certainly have arrived later.\n\nThe first footprint on the Moon did not alter anyone's life overnight. Even so, I believe the claim that it made little difference overlooks how much everyday technology can be traced back to the effort behind it.",
"b539":"When public money is short, spending on theatres, galleries and orchestras is among the first items to be questioned, and moving it to schools sounds like common sense. I disagree with this proposal, because it would do little for education while causing real damage to cultural life.\n\nEducation clearly has a stronger claim on public funds than the arts. Schooling is compulsory, it shapes every child's prospects, and overcrowded classrooms or poorly paid teachers are problems that most voters want solved before a new concert hall is built. If the choice were truly between a textbook and a painting, the textbook should win. The choice is seldom so simple, though. In most countries the arts receive a very small sum compared with what is spent on schools, so that even a severe cut would add little to the education budget.\n\nWhat such a cut would remove is considerable by comparison. Public subsidy is what keeps museums free or cheap, supports local theatres outside the capital, and allows orchestras to perform for audiences who could not pay commercial prices. Without it, culture becomes a luxury for the wealthy. Much of this spending is in fact educational. School parties visiting a national museum, for instance, learn history and science from objects that no classroom could hold, and young musicians and actors are trained through publicly funded programmes. The arts budget should not be immune from scrutiny, and grants to institutions that attract few visitors may deserve review. Savings of that kind are a matter of good management, however, not a way of financing schools.\n\nSchools deserve more money, but the arts are the wrong place to look for it. A government that reduced its small cultural budget would, in my view, weaken education in the broad sense while barely improving it in the narrow one.",
"b540":"The proposal that the state should pay every citizen a regular sum, whether or not they work, has attracted growing interest. I agree with its aim of ensuring that people do not go without essentials, but I disagree that an identical payment to the whole population is the best way to achieve it.\n\nThe attraction of a basic income is its simplicity and its dignity. Existing welfare systems often require claimants to fill in long forms and prove that they are looking for work, and some people in real need fall through the gaps. A payment made to all would reach them automatically. It would also give people room to make better decisions. A factory worker whose job has been replaced by a machine, for example, could afford to retrain instead of taking the first low-paid post available. Such security is valuable, although supporters tend to say less about how it would be financed.\n\nThe cost is the principal objection. Paying every adult enough to live on would require either very high taxes or cuts to services such as hospitals and schools, on which poorer families depend most. Much of the money would go to people who do not need it, and this is difficult to justify while budgets are limited. There is also the question of work. Most recipients would probably continue in their jobs, since few people are content with a bare minimum, but some reduction in the workforce is likely in unpleasant, poorly paid occupations. A more efficient approach is to direct support to those without adequate income, while making it easier to claim.\n\nNo citizen should be left unable to afford food and shelter, and governments have a duty to prevent this. They can meet that duty more fairly, in my view, through well-designed, targeted benefits than through a universal payment.",
"b541":"Global corporations now make, move and sell goods in almost every country, and the world's economies are more closely tied together than at any earlier time. Supporters claim that this process benefits all concerned. I agree that it has raised living standards for very many people, but I disagree that its effects are positive for all.\n\nThe gains are substantial and should be acknowledged. When a multinational opens a factory in a developing country, it brings capital, training and access to foreign markets that local firms could not have obtained alone. Wages in such factories are low by Western standards, yet they are often higher than the alternatives available nearby. Millions of people in China and Bangladesh, for example, have moved from subsistence farming into paid employment as a result. Consumers elsewhere benefit too, through cheaper clothing, electronics and food. A household on a modest income can now afford goods that were once beyond its reach. These advantages are widely spread, though not evenly, and they depend on governments enforcing decent working conditions.\n\nThe costs fall on particular groups, and for them the outcome can be plainly negative. A worker in an industrial town whose factory moves abroad loses not only a job but often the only kind of work the area offered. Cheaper goods are small compensation. Local businesses in poorer countries may suffer as well, since a family shop cannot match the prices of an international chain. When such a shop closes, the owner's living goes with it. While none of this cancels the overall benefit, it shows that averages conceal real losers.\n\nGlobalisation has made the world as a whole richer. I believe, nevertheless, that the claim that it benefits all is too strong, and that its rewards will be widely shared only if governments support those it leaves behind.",
"b542":"Burning oil, gas and coal remains the main source of the gases that are warming the planet, and their concentration rises every year that action is delayed. I agree that the international community must act without delay to bring consumption down. I would add only that the pace of reduction cannot be identical for every nation.\n\nUrgency is justified by the way the problem accumulates. Carbon dioxide stays in the atmosphere for generations, so each year of high emissions makes the eventual task harder and the damage greater. Coastal flooding, failed harvests and longer droughts are already affecting many regions. A coordinated response is necessary because no state can solve this alone. If one country cuts its use of fossil fuels while others expand theirs, the first bears the cost and the climate barely benefits. Binding international commitments are the only means of ensuring that effort in one place is not cancelled out in another.\n\nWhere the statement needs qualifying is in its demand that all nations cut consumption at once. Wealthy countries built their prosperity on cheap energy and have the money and technology to replace it. Poorer ones are in a different position. A country such as Ethiopia, where many homes still lack electricity, cannot be expected to reduce energy use that it has barely begun to enjoy. A fairer approach is for rich states to make the deepest cuts first, while financing solar and wind power elsewhere so that developing economies can grow without repeating the same mistakes. Unless such help is provided, poorer governments are unlikely to sign any agreement.\n\nImmediate, collective action on fossil fuels is therefore essential, and I believe every nation should be part of it. What each is asked to do, however, should reflect what it can afford and what it has already emitted.",
"b543":"Faced with fierce competition for good jobs, many families press teenagers to settle on a profession as soon as possible. I disagree that this is generally the best strategy. An early decision helps in a few professions with long training, but for most young people it carries more risk than benefit.\n\nEarly commitment has clear advantages in certain fields. Medicine, architecture and law demand many years of study, and a student who decides at sixteen can select the right school subjects and gain relevant experience. The same is true of careers in music or sport, where training has to begin in childhood. In these cases a late start can close the door. Such occupations are a minority, though, and the argument assumes that the young person has chosen correctly.\n\nThat assumption is often mistaken. Teenagers have seen little of working life, and their preferences are shaped by parents and whichever subjects they happen to find easy. A pupil who commits to engineering at fifteen may discover at twenty that she dislikes it, having already given up the subjects that would allow her to change direction. The job market adds a further uncertainty, because the demand for particular skills shifts faster than a degree can be completed. Roles in data analysis, for example, barely existed when today's senior analysts were at school. What employers reward in such conditions is adaptability: the ability to write clearly, work with numbers, solve unfamiliar problems and learn quickly. These are developed through a broad education rather than early specialisation, and they keep several careers within reach.\n\nCompetition does make preparation important, but preparing is not the same as narrowing. Except where a profession requires an early start, I believe young people are better served by keeping their options open until they know themselves and the market well enough to choose.",
"b544":"Large cities continue to attract young adults with the promise of education, work and independence, yet the same growth that creates these opportunities makes them harder to enjoy. The most serious difficulties concern housing and employment, and both can be eased by deliberate policy.\n\nThe cost of accommodation is the problem that young city residents feel most sharply. As more people compete for a limited number of flats, rents rise faster than starting salaries. A graduate in her first job may find that a room in a shared apartment is all she can afford. This affects more than comfort, because people without stable housing tend to postpone marriage and children. Employment adds to the pressure. Cities draw far more applicants than there are good positions, so many young people accept temporary or poorly paid work, sometimes unrelated to their qualifications.\n\nSince these problems are linked, the solutions should be too. City authorities can increase the supply of affordable housing by requiring new developments to include small, reasonably priced flats and by building rental homes near transport lines. Some cities, such as Vienna, have long provided good public housing for people on ordinary incomes, not only for the poorest. On the employment side, governments can offer tax incentives to companies that take on school-leavers and graduates in paid traineeships, which turn insecure first jobs into the start of a career. Although such schemes require public money, the cost of a generation unable to settle is likely to be greater. They will work, however, only if housing supply keeps pace with the number of new arrivals.\n\nYoung people in cities are held back less by lack of ability than by the price of a home and the scarcity of secure work. Where authorities address both, city life can again deliver what it promises.",
"b545":"Car ownership is rising across the world even though the harm that vehicles cause, both in the factory and on the road, is widely understood. I believe this is explained by rising incomes and the lack of practical alternatives, and that governments can limit the trend by changing both what driving costs and what it competes with.\n\nThe chief reason for the car's popularity is that it has become affordable to far more people. In fast-growing economies, millions of families are now able to buy a vehicle for the first time, and they see it as a reward for hard work and a sign of success. A car is also extremely convenient, offering privacy, comfort and a direct journey at any hour. In many cities this convenience is close to a necessity, because housing has spread outwards while bus and rail networks have not kept up. Environmental concern, although sincere, tends to give way when the alternative is a long and unreliable journey.\n\nControlling this growth requires making driving less attractive and the alternatives more so. Taxes on fuel and on the purchase of new vehicles raise the cost of ownership. Singapore, for example, requires buyers to bid for a permit before they may own a car at all. Such charges are fair only if people have another way to travel, so the revenue should be invested in frequent, affordable public transport and safe cycling routes. Electric cars reduce exhaust emissions, but their manufacture still consumes energy and raw materials. Encouraging shared vehicles and longer-lasting models would therefore help to limit production itself.\n\nCars are popular because they are affordable, convenient and, for many, hard to avoid. In my view, their numbers will be controlled only when governments make ownership more expensive and give people a realistic alternative.",
"b546":"Most societies respond to crime by locking offenders away, yet the number of people who offend again after release suggests that confinement alone changes little. I agree that education is, in most cases, the more effective response, although prison remains necessary for a minority of offenders.\n\nImprisonment does serve purposes that education cannot. It protects the public from people who are violent, it satisfies the reasonable demand of victims that wrongdoing has consequences, and the prospect of losing one's freedom may deter some from breaking the law. For murderers or armed robbers there is no realistic alternative. As a means of changing behaviour, however, prison performs poorly. An inmate convicted of a minor theft spends his sentence among more experienced criminals, loses his job and often his home, and comes out with fewer lawful options than before.\n\nEducation addresses the reasons people turn to crime, which is why it tends to work better. A large share of offences are committed by young men with few qualifications and no prospect of steady work, and teaching them a trade gives them something to lose. In countries such as Norway, sentences are organised around study and vocational training, and prisoners are expected to leave with a skill. A former prisoner who can earn a wage as an electrician or a cook has a practical reason to stay within the law. It is not a quick remedy, and it will not reach those who refuse to take part. Its results last, though, in a way that the effects of punishment seldom do.\n\nPrison will remain the right place for dangerous criminals. For the much larger group whose offences grow out of poverty and lack of opportunity, I believe education offers a better chance of ending criminal behaviour than a cell does.",
"b547":"Driving across a large city has become slow and frustrating, with queues that last well beyond the traditional rush hour. The causes are fairly well understood, and the most effective remedies are those that reduce the demand for road space rather than add to its supply.\n\nThe basic cause is that the number of vehicles has grown much faster than the roads that carry them. As incomes have risen, more households have bought cars, while streets in old city centres cannot be widened without demolishing the buildings beside them. The pattern of travel makes this worse. Most offices, schools and shops open at similar hours, so a large part of the population tries to reach the same districts at the same time.\n\nBecause space cannot easily be increased, the solutions must make better use of what exists. A charge for entering the centre at peak times, which cities such as Stockholm have introduced, persuades some drivers to travel at a different hour or by a different means. This works only if the alternative is good, so the revenue should pay for frequent buses with their own lanes and for reliable rail services. Employers can help by allowing flexible hours or some work from home, which spreads journeys more evenly through the day. If an office lets staff start at any time between seven and ten, for instance, far fewer of them join the eight o'clock queue. Although new roads may be justified in rapidly expanding suburbs, they seldom bring lasting relief in the centre.\n\nCongestion is the result of too many vehicles seeking the same space at the same time. It eases when cities manage that demand and offer travellers a convenient alternative to the car.",
"b548":"Every company, from a street café to a multinational, needs to persuade more people to buy what it sells. The methods used fall into a few broad groups, and I believe the most effective of them is also the least dramatic: improving the product and the service around it.\n\nThe most visible technique is advertising. Firms pay for television commercials, online banners and, increasingly, recommendations from social media personalities, all intended to make a brand familiar and desirable. A shopper choosing between two similar products tends to pick the name already heard, which is why even well-known companies keep advertising. A second approach is to compete on price through discounts, seasonal sales and offers such as two items for the price of one. Loyalty schemes work in a similar way by rewarding customers who return. Both methods can raise sales quickly, although the effect often fades once the campaign or the discount ends.\n\nLasting growth depends on something less easily bought. A customer who is pleased with a purchase comes back and tells friends and colleagues, and this kind of recommendation is trusted far more than any commercial. Online reviews have made the effect stronger, since a buyer can now check the experience of hundreds of others before spending anything. A restaurant with excellent food and attentive staff, for instance, may fill its tables every evening with almost no advertising, while a heavily promoted rival with poor service loses its customers after one visit. Quality alone is not enough, however, if few people know the product exists, so a new business still needs some promotion to be noticed.\n\nAdvertising and price cuts both have their place, particularly at the start. In the long run, though, I believe a company sells most by offering something good enough that its customers do the promoting for it.",
"b549":"A visitor walking down the main shopping street of almost any large city will now find much the same names above the doors. Some welcome this sameness as a sign of dependable quality, whereas others regret what it has replaced. In my view, the loss is greater than the gain, although the gain is real.\n\nThose who approve of the trend point to what global chains offer the customer. A shopper knows what to expect on price, quality and the right to return goods, whether the branch is in Hanoi or Madrid. This matters to travellers, who cannot know which local shops to trust. Large retailers such as IKEA or Zara can also sell well-designed products cheaply, because they buy in enormous quantities, and this has brought goods once reserved for the wealthy within reach of ordinary households. These are genuine benefits, though they matter most where local alternatives are worse or dearer.\n\nCritics reply that the same efficiency removes what made each place distinct. An independent bookshop or family tailor rarely survives when rents rise to the level an international brand can pay, and once such businesses close, the skills and tastes behind them tend to disappear as well. Travel loses some of its point too, since there is little to discover in a street that resembles the one at home. The other concern is economic. Profits earned by a chain leave the town for a distant head office, whereas a local owner spends much of what she earns nearby. While chains do create jobs, most are junior posts, with decisions taken elsewhere.\n\nInternational retailers have made everyday goods cheaper and more dependable, and that should not be dismissed. On balance, however, I think a world of identical high streets is a poorer one, and cities are right to protect space for local traders.",
"b550":"City dwellers are surrounded by sound from the moment they step outside, and in many streets, stations and shopping areas it has reached a level that makes conversation difficult. This noise has identifiable sources, most of which can be reduced by better design and firmer rules.\n\nTraffic is the largest single cause. Engines, horns and tyres on tarmac produce a constant background roar, and it is loudest where wide roads pass directly beside pavements, cafes and parks. Modern building materials make matters worse, because glass, concrete and steel reflect this sound instead of absorbing it. Commercial noise is the other main source. Shops play music through open doors to attract customers, restaurants compete to be heard, and announcements echo around stations. Each business turns up its own volume to be noticed above the rest, so the general level keeps rising.\n\nSolutions should begin with traffic, because that is where the greatest improvement can be made. Lower speed limits in central districts, streets closed to cars and a shift to electric buses all reduce noise at its source. Many European cities, such as Copenhagen, have found that pedestrian zones become noticeably calmer once engines are removed. Planting trees along the busiest roads softens what remains. For commercial premises, councils can set a maximum volume and fine businesses that exceed it. A clear limit also removes the reason for competing, since no shop needs to be louder than its neighbour. Such rules are effective only if inspectors enforce them, though.\n\nA busy city will not be silent, and few residents would want it to be. Much of the noise is nevertheless avoidable, and reducing it depends mainly on restraining traffic and holding businesses to reasonable limits.",
"b551":"Advertising now follows consumers from the television to the bus stop and onto their phones. I think its influence on what people buy is considerable, though far from total, and that the people most exposed to it need protection through both regulation and education.\n\nThe extent of the influence varies with the purchase and the purchaser. For cheap, frequently bought items such as snacks or washing powder, people rarely stop to compare, and the brand they have seen most often tends to win. Advertising also shapes wider habits by presenting certain goods as normal, so that owning the latest phone comes to seem a requirement rather than a luxury. Its hold is weaker when adults make large, considered purchases, as they usually consult reviews and friends as well. Children are a different matter. A six-year-old cannot tell a sales message from a cartoon, and teenagers are easily persuaded by online personalities who are paid to recommend products.\n\nProtective measures should therefore begin with honesty. Governments should require that claims in advertisements can be proved and should fine companies that cannot do so. Paid promotion on social media ought to be clearly labelled, so that young followers know when an opinion has been bought. Rules alone will not be enough, however, because new techniques appear faster than laws can be written. The second measure is education. Schools should teach pupils how advertisements work, for example by having them analyse a commercial and identify what it promises without stating. A teenager who has learned to ask who is paying for a message is harder to mislead, whatever form the next campaign takes.\n\nIn my view, advertising guides far more of our spending than we like to admit. Rules on honesty and consumer education would let people use the information it carries while resisting its pressure.",
"b552":"As life expectancy rises, more people spend their final decades needing help with daily tasks, and someone has to provide it. In my view, this duty cannot rest on relatives alone: families should remain closely involved, but the government must take responsibility for the nursing and the costs that exceed what a household can manage.\n\nRelatives are well placed to give the kind of care that cannot be bought. An elderly woman is likely to feel more secure when the person who visits or cooks is a daughter or grandson who knows her habits and history. In societies such as Vietnam and Japan, looking after one's parents is also understood as a return for the years they spent raising their children. Old people who are cared for in this way keep a respected place at home. This arrangement works, however, only while needs are modest. A person in their nineties with dementia may require attention day and night, and a son who gives up his job to provide it damages his own finances and health.\n\nAt this point the state has to step in. Governments can train and employ carers, fund home visits and run residential homes on a scale that no family could organise, and they can spread the expense across taxpayers. Public provision also protects those who have no children, or whose relatives live abroad. Without it, the quality of a person's last years would depend on family circumstances. State care has its own weakness, though, since staff who look after many residents cannot offer much personal attention.\n\nOld age now lasts too long, and can involve too much medical need, for families to carry it unaided. I believe governments should be held responsible for funding and delivering skilled care, while relatives continue to supply the affection and company that no public service can.",
"b553":"Feeding a growing population from a fixed area of farmland is one of the hardest tasks governments face, and genetic engineering offers one way of doing it. Opponents fear for human health and for the natural world. I agree that countries should use these crops, as long as their environmental effects are closely monitored.\n\nThe health objection is the weaker of the two. Genetically modified maize and soya have been eaten by vast numbers of people in North and South America for some three decades. Regulators in those regions have found no illness that can be traced to them. Each new variety is tested more thoroughly than crops produced by traditional breeding. Some are in fact designed to improve nutrition or to reduce the need for chemical sprays, which benefits farm workers as well as consumers. Although caution is reasonable where long-term effects are concerned, fear alone is a poor basis for rejecting a useful technology.\n\nConcerns about nature deserve more attention. Crops engineered to tolerate weedkiller, for example, have encouraged some farmers to spray more heavily, and resistant weeds have appeared as a result. Modified genes can spread to wild relatives, and planting a single high-yielding variety over huge areas reduces the diversity on which farming ultimately depends. These are genuine risks. They are, however, arguments for regulation rather than prohibition. Conventional intensive farming also damages soil and wildlife, and a crop that yields more per hectare may spare forests from being cleared for new fields. Rules requiring varied planting and independent monitoring would limit most of the harm.\n\nRejecting genetic engineering outright would deny farmers a valuable means of producing more food on less land. I believe the technology should be adopted, with each crop judged on evidence and with firm safeguards for the environment.",
"b554":"From the screen of a phone to the side of a bus, commercial messages now fill almost every space that people look at. I agree that this constant exposure distorts our sense of what matters and leaves many people worse off, though its power is not unlimited.\n\nAdvertising influences priorities because it rarely sells a product on its merits alone. It attaches the product to something people already want, such as admiration, youth or a happy family, and suggests that a purchase will supply it. Repeated day after day, this message teaches that problems are solved by buying. A man who feels perfectly content with his phone, for example, may begin to regard it as embarrassing once the newer model appears on every billboard he passes. His needs have not changed, but his idea of what is adequate has.\n\nThe effect on people's lives follows from this manufactured dissatisfaction. Households spend on goods that bring little lasting pleasure, sometimes on credit, and the time needed to earn that money is taken from family and rest. The pleasure soon fades, because the next campaign promotes a newer version. Parents feel this most when children, who cannot yet tell persuasion from information, ask for things the family cannot afford. It would be wrong, however, to treat advertising as wholly harmful. It tells consumers about new products and lower prices, and it pays for much of the journalism and entertainment that people receive free. Adults who understand how it works can also learn to discount it, although few manage to do so consistently.\n\nAdvertising is useful as a source of information, but most of it aims to create wants and not to meet them. For that reason I agree that, in its present volume, it pushes people's priorities in the wrong direction and does more harm than good.",
"b555":"Anyone who turns on a sports channel at the weekend will find far more matches played by men than by women. This imbalance has understandable commercial causes, but I believe broadcasters should work to reduce it, even though a rule of strictly equal airtime would be impractical.\n\nMen's sport dominates the schedule largely because it has had a long head start. Professional leagues for men have existed for more than a century, which has given them famous clubs, loyal supporters and rivalries that pass from parents to children. Women were excluded from many sports for much of that time, so their competitions are younger and less familiar. Channels depend on advertising and subscriptions, and they therefore buy the events that already attract the largest audiences. The result is a circle in which women's sport is shown less because fewer people watch it, and fewer people watch it because it is seldom shown.\n\nWhether airtime should be divided equally is a separate question. An exact split would be difficult to apply, since some sports have no professional women's league of comparable size and a private company cannot be forced to broadcast at a loss. A substantial increase is nevertheless justified. Audiences grow when coverage is regular and well presented, as the crowds for recent women's football World Cups have shown. Girls are also more likely to take up a sport when they see women playing it at the highest level. Publicly funded broadcasters, such as the BBC, have a particular duty here because they are not bound by ratings alone.\n\nHistory and money, rather than any lack of quality in women's competitions, explain why men's sport fills the schedules. In my view, channels should commit to a steadily rising share of women's sport instead of a fixed half, so that interest and coverage can grow together.",
"b556":"Sending money once meant visiting a bank, writing a cheque or handing over cash in person. A few taps on a phone now achieve the same result in seconds, and I believe this change is clearly a positive one, despite the new risks of fraud that have come with it.\n\nConvenience is the greatest benefit, and it reaches well beyond wealthy customers. Someone working in a city can support parents in a distant village the moment they need help, without paying an agent or travelling home with banknotes. In Kenya, for instance, the mobile service M-Pesa has allowed people with no bank account to save, pay bills and receive wages through a basic handset. Small traders gain as well, because they can accept payment from customers who carry no cash and keep an automatic record of their income. These advantages are largest in places where banks are few, but even in rich countries splitting a restaurant bill or paying a plumber has become simpler.\n\nSpeed, unfortunately, also helps criminals. A payment that arrives instantly is very difficult to reverse, so a person tricked by a false message or a fake website may lose savings within minutes. Criminals know this, and they often pretend to be a bank or a relative in trouble so that the victim acts before thinking. Older people, who are often less familiar with the technology, are particularly exposed. These problems are real, although they can be reduced rather than simply accepted: banks can delay unusual transfers, confirm the name of the recipient and refund victims of deception.\n\nBecause the dangers can be managed while the benefits are felt daily by millions, in my view the growth of digital payments is a welcome development. It has made financial services faster, cheaper and open to far more people.",
"b557":"In many cities the shopping mall has become the place where teenagers and young adults go when they have nothing particular to do. I agree that this habit carries some costs for them, but I believe the claim that it damages society as a whole is overstated.\n\nThe harm to young people themselves lies mainly in what the mall teaches them to want. A shopping centre is designed to keep visitors looking at things they do not own, and a teenager who spends every weekend there is likely to measure herself against what her friends can afford. Leisure becomes a matter of buying, or of wishing to buy, rather than of making or doing anything. This matters most for those with little money, who may feel excluded in a place where others their age are spending freely.\n\nThe wider social damage, however, is harder to demonstrate. Young people have gathered in public places in every generation, and a mall is often the safest one available to them. In a hot, crowded city such as Bangkok or Dubai, for example, it may be the only large space that is cool, well lit and free to enter. Most of those who go there are meeting friends rather than shopping, which is an ordinary and healthy thing to do. Although a society in which the young had nowhere else to go would be poorer for it, the fault would then lie with planners who built too few parks, libraries and sports grounds.\n\nSpending a great deal of free time in malls can narrow young people's interests and sharpen their appetite for possessions, and to that extent I agree with the statement. The remedy, in my view, is to offer better places to meet, not to blame those who use the one they have.",
"b558":"From detective dramas to documentaries about real murders, crime now fills the schedules of broadcasters and streaming services. Its appeal lies in a mixture of curiosity and safe excitement, and I believe its influence on society is more mixed than either its fans or its critics suggest.\n\nThe chief attraction of crime stories is that they let viewers experience danger without risk. Watching a killer being pursued produces real tension, while the audience remains comfortable at home and can expect order to be restored by the final episode. These programmes are also puzzles. Viewers enjoy weighing the evidence and guessing the culprit before the detective does, which makes the genre more involving than most drama. A series such as Sherlock, for example, invites the audience to notice the same small details as its hero.\n\nThe effects on society are harder to judge. One likely consequence is an exaggerated fear of crime. People who watch violent offences every evening may come to believe that such events are common, and become more suspicious of strangers, even where crime is falling. Some may keep their children indoors or avoid going out after dark as a result. There is a more positive side, however. Careful documentaries have drawn attention to wrongful convictions and unsolved cases, and some have led to investigations being reopened. Whether the influence is harmful therefore depends largely on how responsibly the material is made.\n\nCrime programmes are popular because they offer suspense, a puzzle and reassurance at once. Their social effect, in my view, is not uniform: they can spread unnecessary fear, although the best of them improve public understanding of how justice works and fails.",
"b559":"A play, a concert or a football match can now be watched from the sofa in excellent quality, yet tickets for live events still sell out. I agree that being present is generally the more enjoyable experience, although television has advantages that should not be dismissed.\n\nWhat a live event offers above all is the sense of taking part. A spectator in a stadium is surrounded by thousands of people who react at the same moment. That shared tension makes a late goal or a final song feel far more intense than it does at home. The occasion is also unrepeatable. An actor may deliver a line differently each night, and the audience knows that what it is seeing will not happen in quite the same way again. This gives even an ordinary evening a sense of occasion that people remember for years.\n\nTelevision, however, often provides the better view. Cameras bring the viewer closer than the most expensive seat, and replays and commentary explain details easily missed from the back of a hall. At a football match, for example, a fan high in the stands may struggle to see which player scored, while the viewer at home sees it from several angles. Comfort and cost matter as well, since a family can watch a broadcast for almost nothing, whereas tickets, travel and food make a single evening expensive. The viewer at home does not have to queue, travel home late or sit in the rain. For those who mainly want to follow the event rather than feel it, the screen may therefore be the more satisfying choice.\n\nThese are real advantages, but they concern information and convenience rather than enjoyment itself. On balance, I believe that the atmosphere of a live audience gives a pleasure that a broadcast, however well produced, can only partly reproduce.",
"b560":"Architects are often said to face a choice between making a building work well and making it look impressive. I agree that purpose should come first in design, although I would not dismiss appearance as readily as the statement implies.\n\nA building exists to be used, and one that fails its users has failed, however handsome it may be. In a hospital, for example, the distance between the emergency entrance and the operating theatres can affect whether patients survive, and no elegant facade compensates for a poor layout. The same applies in less dramatic ways to schools that need daylight and quiet, or to offices where staff must be able to move and meet easily. Mistakes of this kind are expensive to correct and are endured every day by the people inside. An unattractive exterior, by comparison, does little practical harm. For those who occupy a building, function is what counts.\n\nOccupants are not the only people a building affects, however. A structure may stand for a century, and during that time it is seen daily by thousands of passers-by who did not choose it. Dull or oppressive buildings lower the quality of a whole street, while a distinguished one can lift the reputation of a city, as the Sydney Opera House has done. Appearance therefore has a public value that the owner's needs alone do not capture. The two aims also conflict less often than is supposed. Good architects usually arrive at an attractive form by solving practical problems well rather than by adding decoration afterwards.\n\nIntended use should guide a design, since a building that does not serve its purpose has no justification. Because the public lives with the result for generations, I believe appearance deserves serious attention as well.",
"b561":"Teenagers care intensely about the opinion of their classmates and friends, often more than about that of parents or teachers. This influence can push them in good directions as well as bad, but I believe its drawbacks are the greater, because pressure of any kind discourages young people from thinking for themselves.\n\nThe benefits of peer pressure appear when the group's standards are high. A student surrounded by friends who work hard is likely to do the same, simply to keep up, and one whose friends play sport will probably join them. Wanting to belong also teaches adolescents to cooperate, to share and to consider how their behaviour appears to others. A shy boy who joins a debating club because his friends have done so, for instance, may gain a confidence he would not have found alone. These are real advantages, although they depend on the luck of falling in with the right company.\n\nWhen the company is less fortunate, the same mechanism does damage. Many young people first smoke, drink or skip school because refusing would mean being mocked or left out, and the fear of exclusion is strong enough to override what they know to be sensible. Habits formed in this way, such as smoking, can last long after the friendships themselves have ended. The second drawback is more general, because even helpful pressure trains a person to follow the crowd. An adolescent who studies only because friends do has not learned to choose well, and may choose badly when the friends change.\n\nPeers can encourage effort and build social skills, and their influence is a normal part of growing up. The risks are more serious and harder to undo, however, so in my view the disadvantages outweigh the advantages.",
"b562":"Most legal systems draw a line at eighteen, dealing with those below it in separate courts and under different rules. Treating young offenders exactly as adults would remove that line, and I disagree with the proposal, because the differences in procedure exist for good reasons.\n\nThe differences begin at the police station. A fifteen-year-old who is questioned alone, without a parent or lawyer, is far more likely than an adult to misunderstand his rights or to confess to something he did not do. Youth courts are less formal for a similar reason, since a defendant who cannot follow the proceedings cannot take part in his own defence. Although these safeguards may look like leniency, they do not excuse the offence. They exist to make sure that the outcome is accurate and fair, which is in the interest of victims as much as of the accused.\n\nThe consequences of a conviction also need to be different. An adult's criminal record is usually public and lasting, whereas a young offender's name is generally kept out of the press and his record may later be cleared. Without this protection, a single act of shoplifting at fourteen could prevent someone from being hired at twenty-five, for example as a teacher or nurse. A person with no prospects is more likely to offend again. It is true that some older teenagers commit crimes with full understanding. Courts should have the power to impose long sentences in such cases, but as an exception to the rule rather than as the standard treatment.\n\nTreating the young exactly like adults would be simpler, but it would produce less reliable verdicts and more repeat offenders. Separate procedures, with room for severity in the gravest cases, serve both justice and public safety better, and I think they should be kept."
};
